\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/Emerging Europe: Russia's Massive Capital Outflow Has Silver Lining\b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2012\par 17:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Emerging Europe blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh\par \par \uc2 Capital outflows are usually deemed to be a negative development for any economy, but in Russia's case they help avoid economic overheating and are natural considering the country's poor business climate, \uc2 Russia's finance ministry\uc2  and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  say.\par \par \uc2 Before the crisis that began in 2008, Russia was benefiting from rallying oil prices and risk appetite, attracting net capital inflows of $41.1 billion in 2006 and $81.7 billion in 2007, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the following four years, however, the world's largest oil producer saw nearly $305 billion leave and is on track to post around $60 billion in outflows this year.\par \par \uc2 "It is inevitable in the current state of the economy and, in some sense, it's not that bad as Russia's exports with current oil prices are generating more resources than it could be invested in Russia without creating bubbles," Deputy Finance Minister Alexei Moiseev told journalists on the sidelines of a banking conference in Moscow on Friday. "It's a big achievement that with a transition to the floating ruble the economy can balance itself. It digests exactly the same amount of money as it is able to do."\par \par \uc2 The IMF agrees that capital outflows from Russia are natural in the current global and domestic environment.\par \par \uc2 "With the switch towards a more flexible ruble exchange rate, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market have been scaled back, and the capital outflows that we see are to a large extent the counterpart to Russia's current account surpluses," said Odd Per Brekk, IMF's representative in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Moiseev said that the government's target now is to improve the country's business climate. According to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , Russia was ranked 120th out of 183 countries in the organization's 'Doing Business' report.\par \par \uc2 Russian leaders have repeatedly called for structural changes to make the country more investment-friendly and diversify the economy, cutting its dependence on global commodity prices, although no major breakthroughs have been made yet.\par \par \uc2 "The government calls for diversification but is not in a rush to spend on investments. Households and business can't see reasons to behave differently," said Evgeniy Nadroshin, chief economist with Russia's conglomerate Sistema.\par \par \uc2 Without diversification of the economy and reforms to improve the investment climate, capital outflows keep the ruble under pressure, which supports Russia's production, "far from the world's most competitive," said Mr. Nadorshin.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 26-10-12 1534GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121026e8aq0011w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
