\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Ruble Drops To 2009 Crisis Levels\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2012\par 17:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Ruble down as much as 2.8% vs US dollar\par \par \uc2 -- Analysts see further weakening\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps up support\par \par \uc2 (Rewrites throughout.)\par \par \uc2 By Ira Iosebashvili\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The Russian ruble plummeted to its lowest level in more than three years against the dollar on Thursday, bruised by falling oil prices and a fresh round of European sovereign debt worries, with market watchers predicting further weakness ahead despite \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened by as much as 2.8% against the greenback to RUB33.69, its lowest level since April 2009, when Russia was still fighting the initial waves of the global financial crisis amid plummeting oil prices and a 35% ruble decline.\par \par \uc2 After a strong start to the year, the ruble is now one of the world's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having shed 11.7% against the dollar in May alone.\par \par \uc2 Prices for oil, Russia's chief export, hovered near a six-month low of $103 per barrel for Brent crude as fears of a Greek exit from the euro eroded risk appetite across markets. Traders and fund managers have said that ruble selling may intensify if prices for oil slip below $100 per barrel and stay there.\par \par \uc2 "There is a recognition that Russia is far more vulnerable than ever if commodity prices crack on the back of weaker Chinese or European growth," said Tim Ash, emerging-markets economist at RBS. "It will not take much in terms of lower oil prices to cause serious stress to the Russia story."\par \par \uc2 While Russia's sovereign debt--which stands at just over 11% of gross domestic product--is enviably low compared with most of its European neighbors, successive waves of government spending have boosted the oil price needed to keep the budget in the black to an estimated $117 per barrel, from as low as $50 per barrel in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Turbulence on global markets and a lack of solid investment opportunities within the country have caused both foreign and domestic capital to flee Russia, with some $42 billion in capital outflows recorded in the first four months of the year and a total of $80.5 billion in capital flight in 2011. With markets continuing to look shaky and investors unimpressed by their first glimpses of the economic policies of President Vladimir Putin, who was sworn in to a third Kremlin term on May 7, analysts said the outflow is likely to continue.\par \par \uc2 Because oil prices remain at historically high levels, the ruble may still have more weakness ahead, said Kingsmill Bond, chief strategist at Citi in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 "The ruble is still not cheap," Bond said, noting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only 5% off its all-time high when adjusted for inflation. "It is noteworthy that the ruble is today more expensive in real terms that at its 2008 pre-crisis valuation peak."\par \par \uc2 While both global and domestic factors seem to be against it at the moment, the ruble is about to get support from at least one source--the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The regulator resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday for the first time since Jan 17. While those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have so far been small--totaling almost $90 million so far this week--they will grow steadily as the ruble gets weaker.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a six-ruble-wide band against a basket of dollars and euros, purchasing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the ruble strengthens towards the lower edges of the band, and selling when it weakens. The borders of the ruble's corridor against the basket are RUB32.15 to RUB38.15. The ruble closed at RUB37.05 against the basket Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the regulator is likely to stick with this policy of exchange-rate flexibility, rather than drawing a line in the sand beyond which it will defend the ruble--a move that cost it nearly a third of its reserves in 2008.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has learned its lessons well," said Alexander Morozov, chief economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 -By Ira Iosebashvili, Dow Jones Newswires, 7 495 232 9192; ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 31, 2012 11:56 ET (15:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120531e85v005w0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
