\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  to try to minimize  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  - official\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven C. Johnson   \par \uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2012\par 19:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail about conference)\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  intends to minimize \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a top \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on We dnesday, though excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility remains a risk.\par \par \uc2 The country also has a "good chance" to decrease its budget shortfall this year and should achieve a balanced budget in coming years, said Alexei Ulyukayev, first deputy chairman of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the VTB Capital Investment conference in New York, Ulyukayev said that with interest rates pinned at or near zero in the United States, Europe and other advanced economies, "exchange rate volatility is a real challenge for us."\par \par \uc2 But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to spend less each month to limit excess ruble strength against the dollar and euro. The bank bought $4.3 billion last month, and Ulyukayev said this month's total so far was below $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the past, he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often bought between $10 billion and $15 billion a month in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ruble last traded at 29.4750 per dollar and is up more than 8 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is expected to rise this year as government spending increases. During his election campaign, President-elect Vladimir Putin promised to increase public sector pay and social benefits.\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said he expects inflation to be within the central banks' 5 percent to 6 percent target by year end, up from 3.7 percent in the year to March.\par \par \uc2 Economists say a modestly stronger ruble could help keep price pressures in check. But while a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can ease inflation, it can also make a country's exports more expensive and hurt growth.\par \par \uc2 Despite the expected spending increases, Ulyukayev said Russia still had "a good chance to minimize the budget deficit."\par \par \uc2 Under its three-year fiscal plan, Russia forecasts a federal budget deficit this year of 1.5 percent of gross domestic product, rising to 1.6 percent in 2013 and falling to 0.7 percent of GDP in 2014.\par \par \uc2 He said the biggest risk to the Russian and global economies was slower growth in big emerging markets, led by China.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By Steven C. Johnson; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama and Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-ULYUKAYEV/ECONOMY(UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120418e84i00143\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
