 years in response to global\par supply and demand, general economic conditions, competition from other energy\par sources and other factors. In 2008, for example, the peak price of Urals oil\par was U.S.$140.80 per barrel and the average price per barrel was U.S.$94.37,\par compared to a peak price of U.S.$78.15 and an average price of U.S.$60.89 in\par 2009. In 2010, the peak price of Urals oil was U.S.$92.05 per barrel and the\par average price per barrel was U.S.$78.20, compared to a peak price of\par U.S.$123.38 and an average price of U.S.$109.33 in 2011. Russia's 2012 Budget\par Law assumes an average price per barrel of Urals oil of U.S.$100, U.S.$97 and\par U.S.$101 for 2012, 2013 and 2014, respectively. See "Public Finance-2012\par Federal Budget Law." Natural gas prices have likewise fluctuated significantly\par in recent years, in part because crude oil prices often provide a benchmark for\par natural gas prices. In 2008, the lowest monthly average export price of natural\par gas was U.S.$294.80 per thousand m3, the highest monthly average export price\par of natural gas was U.S.$417.10 per thousand m3 and the average export price of\par natural gas was U.S.$359.10 per thousand m3, compared to the lowest monthly\par average export price of U.S.$217.70 per thousand m3, a peak monthly average\par export price of U.S.$332.90 per thousand m3 and an average export price of\par U.S.$257.10 per thousand m3 in 2009. In 2010, the lowest monthly average export\par price of natural gas was U.S.$254.40 per thousand m3, the highest monthly\par average export price of natural gas was U.S.$287.60 per thousand m3 and the\par average export price of natural gas was U.S.$273 per thousand m3 (excluding\par trade with Kazakhstan from 1 July 2010), compared to the lowest monthly average\par export price of U.S.$328.3 per thousand m3, the peak monthly average export\par price of U.S.$436 per thousand m3 and the average export price of U.S.$381.48\par per thousand m3 in 2011. Any material reduction in the price of crude oil or\par natural gas will have a significant effect on Russia's budgetary revenues and\par foreign reserves, and, if sustained, may materially adversely affect the\par Russian Federation's financial condition.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation's economy, like many economies, is vulnerable to external shocks. A global economic crisis, significant future economic difficulties of the Russian Federation's major trading partners, and more general "contagion"effects all could have a material adverse effect on the Russian Federation's economic growth and the market for the Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation's economy and finances were adversely affected in\par 2008-09 by the global financial crisis. Real gross domestic product ("GDP"),\par for example, declined by 7.8% in 2009 compared to 2008, and the average\par unemployment rate (using the definition of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Labour\par Organisation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "ILO")) increased from 7.1% in the fourth quarter of 2008 to\par 8% in the fourth quarter of 2009. The value of the rouble against the dollar\par also declined in 2009, by 12.2% in real terms, as did the value of the real\par effective exchange rate, which fell by 5.6%.\par \par The Russian Federation's economy remains vulnerable to further external shocks.\par A significant decline in the economic growth of the EU or any of the Russian\par Federation's other major trading partners could have a material adverse effect\par on the Russian Federation's balance of trade and adversely affect the Russian\par Federation's economic growth.\par \par Events occurring in one geographic or financial market sometimes result in an\par entire region or class of investments being disfavoured by international\par investors-so-called "contagion effects." The Russian Federation has been\par adversely affected by contagion effects in the past, and it is possible that\par the market for Russian investments, including the Bonds, will be similarly\par affected in the future by negative economic or financial developments in\par countries whose economies or credit ratings are similar to those of the Russian\par Federation.\par \par There can be no assurance that a future external economic crisis will not have\par a negative effect on investors' confidence in Russia's markets or on the\par Russian Federation's economy or ability to raise capital in the international\par debt markets, all of which could have a material adverse effect on the trading\par price of the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No waiver of immunity; enforcement of liabilities\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation is a sovereign state and has not waived any rights to\par sovereign immunity it may have in any jurisdiction. Accordingly, the Russian\par Federation may be entitled to immunity from suit in any action or proceeding in\par any jurisdiction arising out of the Bonds, and the Russian Federation and its\par assets, properties and revenues may be entitled to immunity in any related\par enforcement action. The Russian Federation has also not submitted to the\par jurisdiction of any court, agreed that any disputes may be resolved in any\par forum or appointed any agent for service of process in any jurisdiction, in\par connection with any action or proceeding arising out of the Bonds. As a result\par of the foregoing, it may be difficult or impossible for an investor to obtain a\par judgment against the Russian Federation in a foreign court and/or have such\par judgment recognised and/or enforced in any jurisdiction.\par \par A final judgment rendered by a foreign court will generally be recognised and\par enforced in the Russian Federation if there is an international treaty in\par effect between the Russian Federation and the country where the judgment is\par rendered providing for the mutual recognition and enforcement of judgments.\par There are no international treaties in effect today providing for the mutual\par recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments rendered by courts in the\par Russian Federation and courts in most of the countries where Bond investors are\par likely to reside, including the United States and the United Kingdom.\par \par In the absence of an applicable treaty, enforcement of a final judgment\par rendered by a foreign court may still be recognised by a Russian court on the\par basis of reciprocity, if courts of the country where the foreign judgment is\par rendered have previously enforced judgments issued by Russian courts. While\par Russian courts have recently recognised and enforced English and Dutch court\par judgments on these grounds, the existence of reciprocity must be established at\par the time the recognition and enforcement of a foreign judgment is sought, and\par it is not possible to predict whether a Russian court will in the future\par recognise and enforce on the basis of reciprocity a judgment issued by a\par foreign court, including an English or Dutch court.\par \par Even if an applicable international treaty is in effect or a foreign judgment\par might otherwise be recognised and enforced on the basis of reciprocity, the\par recognition and enforcement of a foreign judgment will in all events be subject\par to exceptions and limitations provided for in Russian law. For example, a\par Russian court may refuse to recognise or enforce a foreign judgment if its\par recognition or enforcement would contradict Russian public policy.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, it may be difficult to obtain recognition or enforcement in the Russian Federation of a foreign judgment in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exchange rate risks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The depreciation of the rouble against the dollar or other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may\par adversely affect the financial condition of the Russian Federation, as well as\par the Russian Federation's ability to repay its debt denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par other than the rouble, including amounts due under the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks Relating to the Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit ratings may not reflect all risks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation has been assigned foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit\par ratings of Baa1 (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ), BBB (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) and BBB (Fitch), each with a\par stable outlook. A credit rating is not a recommendation to buy, sell or hold\par the Bonds, and is subject to revision or withdrawal at any time by the\par assigning rating agency. No assurance can be given that the Russian\par Federation's current or future sovereign ratings will not be downgraded or\par withdrawn entirely, if circumstances in the future so warrant in the judgment\par of the assigning rating agency. The Russian Federation has no obligation to\par inform Bondholders of any revision, downgrade or withdrawal of its current or\par future sovereign credit ratings. A suspension, downgrade or withdrawal at any\par time of a credit rating assigned to the Russian Federation may adversely affect\par the market price of the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Modification\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Each series of Bonds may generally be modified with the consent of persons\par holding or representing at least 662/3 per cent. of the votes cast at a meeting\par of Bondholders called for that purpose, so long as the meeting is attended by\par two or more persons holding or representing a clear majority of the aggregate\par principal amount of such outstanding Bonds (or by two or more persons being or\par representing Bondholders in the case of an adjourned meeting). The maturity,\par principal amount, interest rate and other payment terms of such series of Bonds\par may be modified with the consent of persons holding or representing at least\par 75 per cent. of the votes cast at a meeting of Bondholders called for that\par purpose, so long as the meeting is attended by persons holding or representing\par at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of such outstanding\par Bonds (or by persons holding or representing at least 25 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of such outstanding Bonds in the case of an\par adjourned meeting). Under these collective-action provisions, modifications to\par the terms of the Bonds that are so approved are binding on all Bondholders,\par including Bondholders that did not attend or vote at the Bondholders' meeting\par or that voted against the approved modifications.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Legal investment considerations may restrict certain investments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The investment activities of certain investors are subject to investment laws\par and regulations, or review or regulation by applicable authorities. Potential\par investors should consult their legal advisors to determine whether and to what\par extent (1) the Bonds are legal investments for them, (2) the Bonds can be used\par as collateral for various types of borrowing and (3) other restrictions apply\par to their purchase or pledge of any Bonds. Financial institutions should consult\par their legal advisors or the appropriate regulators to determine the appropriate\par treatment of the Bonds under any applicable risk-based capital or similar\par rules.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The secondary market generally\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A trading market for the Bonds of any series may not develop, and if a market does develop, it may not be liquid. Therefore, investors may not be able to sell their Bonds easily or at prices that will provide them with a yield comparable to similar investments that have a developed secondary market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                     TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF THE 2017 BONDS                     \par \par The following is the text of the terms and conditions of the 2017 Bonds which,\par subject to amendment, will be endorsed on each Bond Certificate (as defined\par below) and will be attached and (subject to the provisions thereof) apply to\par each Global Bond representing the 2017 Bonds.\par \par The U.S.$2,000,000,000 3.25 per cent. Bonds due 2017 (the "Bonds") (which\par expression includes any further Bonds issued pursuant to Condition 13 and\par forming a single series with the Bonds) of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ") acting on behalf of the Russian\par Federation (the "Russian Federation") were authorised pursuant to the\par provisions of Federal Law No. 136-FZ of 29 July 1998 (as amended) "On the\par Specifics of Issuance and Circulation of State and Municipal Securities";\par Federal Law No. 371-FZ of 30 November 2011 "On the Federal Budget for 2012 and\par for the Planning Period of 2013 and 2014"; Resolution of the Government of the\par Russian Federation dated 27 December 2011 No. 2390-r; Resolution of the\par Government of the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 217; Order of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 24 February 2012 No. 31n; and Order of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 30 March 2012 No. 144. The fiscal agency agreement dated\par 3 April 2012 (the "Fiscal Agency Agreement") has been entered into in relation\par to the Bonds by the Russian Federation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, as\par fiscal and paying agent (the "Fiscal Agent") and as registrar (the "Registrar")\par in London and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., New York Branch, as transfer agent (together with\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, the "Transfer Agents") and paying agent in New\par York (together with the Fiscal Agent and the Registrar, the "Paying Agents")\par and the transfer agents named therein (the "Transfer Agents").\par \par In these Conditions, "Fiscal Agent," "Registrar," "Paying Agent," and "Transfer\par Agent" shall include any successors appointed from time to time in accordance\par with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds, and any\par reference to an "Agent" or "Agents" shall mean any or all (as applicable) of\par such persons.\par \par Copies of the Fiscal Agency Agreement are available for inspection during usual\par business hours at the principal office of the Fiscal Agent (currently 13th\par Floor, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Centre\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Canada Square, London E14 5LB) and at the specified\par offices of each of the other Agents. The Bondholders (as defined in Condition 1\par (b)) are bound by, and are deemed to have notice of, the provisions of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds, including any provision of the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement which amends or supplements the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Form, Denomination and Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Form and Denomination\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds are in definitive fully registered form, without interest coupons\par attached, in the denomination of U.S.$200,000 (the "authorised denomination").\par A certificate (each a "Bond Certificate") will be issued to each Bondholder in\par respect of its registered holding or holdings of Bonds. Each Bond Certificate\par will be numbered serially with an identifying number which will be recorded in\par the register (the "Register") which the Russian Federation shall procure to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be\par kept by the Registrar.\par \par (b)Title\par \par Title to the Bonds will pass by and upon registration in the Register. In these\par Conditions, "Bondholder" and "holder" mean the person in whose name a Bond is\par registered in the Register (or, in the case of joint holders, the first-named\par thereof). The holder of any Bond will (except as otherwise requested by such\par holder in writing, or as otherwise ordered by a court of competent jurisdiction\par or required by law) be treated as its absolute owner for all purposes, whether\par or not it is overdue and regardless of any notice of ownership, trust or any\par interest therein, any writing thereon by any person (other than a duly executed\par transfer thereof in the form endorsed thereon) or any notice of any previous\par theft or loss thereof, and no person will be liable for so treating the holder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Transfer of Bonds and Issue of Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Transfer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subject to Condition 2(d), a Bond may be transferred in whole or in part in the\par authorised denomination upon the surrender of the Bond Certificate representing\par that Bond, together with the form of transfer (including any certification as\par to compliance with restrictions on transfer included in such form of transfer)\par endorsed thereon (the "Transfer Form") duly completed and executed, at the\par specified office of the Registrar or any Transfer Agent, together with such\par evidence as the Registrar or, as the case may be, such Transfer Agent may\par reasonably require to prove the title of the transferor and the authority of\par the persons who have executed the Transfer Form. In the case of a transfer of\par part only of the Bonds represented by a Bond Certificate, neither the part\par transferred nor the balance not transferred may be less than the authorised\par denomination and a new Bond Certificate in respect of the balance not so\par transferred will be issued to the transferor.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Delivery\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each new Bond Certificate to be issued upon a transfer of any Bonds will,\par within five business days of the request for transfer being duly made, be\par delivered at the specified office of the Registrar or, as the case may be, any\par Transfer Agent or (at the request and the risk of such transferee) be mailed by\par uninsured post to such address as the transferee entitled to the Bonds\par represented by such Bond Certificate may have specified. In this Condition 2\par (b), "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday) on which\par commercial banks are open for business (including dealings in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the cities in which the Registrar and any such Transfer Agent\par have their respective specified offices.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)No Charge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Registration or transfer of Bonds will be effected without charge to the holder\par or transferee thereof, but upon payment (or against such indemnity from the\par holder or the transferee thereof as the Registrar or the relevant Transfer\par Agent may reasonably require) in respect of any tax or other duty of whatever\par nature which may be levied or imposed in connection with such registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 or\par transfer.\par \par (d)Closed Periods\par \par No Bondholder may require the transfer of a Bond to be registered during the\par period of 15 calendar days ending on the due date for any payment of principal\par in respect of such Bond.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Regulations Concerning Transfer and Registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All transfers of Bonds and entries on the Register will be made subject to the\par detailed regulations concerning transfer of Bonds scheduled to the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement. The regulations may be changed by the Russian Federation in a\par manner which is reasonably required by the Russian Federation (after\par consultation with the Registrar) to reflect changes in legal requirements or in\par any other manner which is not prejudicial to the interests of the Bondholders.\par A copy of the current regulations will be sent by the Registrar to any\par Bondholder who so requests.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Status\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds constitute direct, unconditional, unsecured and unsubordinated\par obligations of the Russian Federation and the full faith and credit of the\par Russian Federation is pledged for the due and punctual payment of principal of,\par and interest on, the Bonds and for the performance of all other obligations of\par the Russian Federation pursuant to the Bonds. The Bonds shall at all times rank\par pari passu without any preference among themselves and pari passu in all\par respects with all other present and future unsecured and unsubordinated\par obligations of the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Negative Pledge and Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Negative Pledge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any of the Bonds remains outstanding (as defined in the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement) the Russian Federation will not create or permit to subsist\par any Lien upon the whole or any part of the International Monetary Assets to\par secure any Public External Indebtedness unless, at the same time or prior\par thereto, the obligations of the Russian Federation under the Bonds are secured\par equally and rateably therewith.\par (b)Covenant\par \par So long as any Bond remains outstanding the Russian Federation shall obtain,\par and do or cause to be done all things necessary to ensure the continuance of,\par all consents, licences, approvals and authorisations, and make or cause to be\par made all registrations, recordings and filings, which may at any time be\par required to be obtained or made in the Russian Federation for the execution,\par delivery or performance of the Bonds or for the validity or enforceability\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 thereof.\par \par (c)Definitions\par \par In these Conditions:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Excluded Indebtedness" means any obligation of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par of the U.S.S.R. (now known as state corporation "Bank for Development and\par Foreign Economic Affairs (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  )") or any other person that may arise\par from time to time representing restructured indebtedness originally incurred\par prior to 1 January 1992 by the government of the former Soviet Union or any of\par its legally authorised entities, other than the Russian Federation U.S. Dollar\par Denominated Bonds due 2007 to 2030.\par \par "External Indebtedness" means Indebtedness (i) which is not Excluded\par Indebtedness, (ii) which is denominated or payable, or at the option of the\par relevant creditor or holder thereof may be payable, in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the lawful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation and (iii) which was not\par originally incurred or assumed under an agreement or instrument made with or\par issued to creditors substantially all of whom were residents of the Russian\par Federation or entities having their head office or principal place of business\par within the territory of the Russian Federation. For the avoidance of doubt,\par External Indebtedness does not include Internal Government Hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bonds\par known as "OVVZs," "Taiga" bonds or "MinFins" or any bonds representing\par restructured internal hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds of the Government of the Russian\par Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Government of the Russian Federation" means the Government of the Russian Federation as provided in Article 110 of the Constitution of the Russian Federation, or any successor article, from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "IMF" means the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Indebtedness" means any legal obligation or any obligation intended by its\par terms to be a legal obligation (whether present or future, actual or\par contingent, secured or unsecured, incurred as principal, surety, guarantor or\par otherwise) for the payment or repayment of borrowed money created (or intended\par by its terms to have been created) under an agreement or instrument in which\par the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is designated as the obligor, or which by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par Russian law constitutes a legal obligation of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (it being\par understood that neither the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , nor any\par political subdivision, regional or municipal government, ministry (other than\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), department, authority or statutory corporation of the\par Russian Federation nor any joint stock company, enterprise or other entity\par organised or existing under the laws or regulations of the Russian Federation\par or any of the above, is considered to be part of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for purposes\par hereof).\par \par "International Monetary Assets" means all official holdings of gold, Special\par Drawing Rights, Reserve Positions in the Fund and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the Government of the Russian Federation from time to\par time (but not, for the avoidance of doubt, any such assets of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  at any time), and the terms "Special Drawing Rights,"\par "Reserve Positions in the Fund" and "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " have, as to the types of\par assets included, the meanings given to them in the IMF's publication entitled\par "International Financial Statistics" or such other meanings as shall be\par formally adopted by the IMF from time to time.\par \par "Lien" means any lien, pledge, hypothecation, mortgage, security interest, deed\par of trust, charge or any other encumbrance arising under a security agreement or\par arrangement.\par \par "Public External Indebtedness" means External Indebtedness which (i) is in the\par form of, or represented by, bonds, notes or other securities or any guarantee\par thereof and (ii) is, or may be, quoted, listed or ordinarily purchased and sold\par on any stock exchange, automated trading system or over-the-counter or other\par securities market (including, without prejudice to the generality of the\par foregoing, the market for securities eligible for resale pursuant to Rule 144A\par under the United States Securities Act of 1933, as amended (the "Securities\par Act")).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond bears interest on its outstanding principal amount from 4 April 2012\par at 3.25 per cent. per annum, payable semi-annually in arrear on 4 April and\par 4 October in each year until maturity, commencing on 4 October 2012 (each, an "\par Interest Payment Date"). Interest will be paid subject to and in accordance\par with the provisions of Condition 7. The period beginning on and including\par 4 April 2012 and ending on but excluding the first Interest Payment Date and\par each successive period beginning on and including an Interest Payment Date and\par ending on but excluding the next successive Interest Payment Date is called an\par "Interest Period".\par \par Each Bond will cease to bear interest from the due date for redemption unless,\par after surrender of such Bond, payment of principal is improperly withheld or\par refused, in which case the outstanding principal amount of such Bond will\par continue to bear interest at the rate specified above (after as well as before\par judgment) until whichever is the earlier of (a) the day on which all sums due\par in respect of such Bond up to that day are received by or on behalf of the\par relevant Bondholder and (b) the day which is seven days after notice has been\par given to the Bondholders that the Fiscal Agent has received all sums due in\par respect of the Bonds up to such seventh day (except, in the case of payment to\par the Fiscal Agent, to the extent that there is any subsequent default in payment\par in accordance with these Conditions).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If interest is required to be calculated for a period of less than a complete Interest Period, it will be calculated on the basis of a year of 360 days consisting of 12 months of 30 days each and, in the case of an incomplete month, the actual number of days elapsed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6 Redemption, Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Redemption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless previously redeemed, or purchased and cancelled, each Bond will be redeemed at its principal amount on 4 April 2017 (the "Maturity Date") subject as provided in Condition 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b)Purchase and Cancellation\par \par The Russian Federation and its affiliates may at any time purchase Bonds in the\par open market or otherwise at any price. Any Bonds so purchased may be cancelled\par or held and resold (provided that such resale is outside the United States, as\par defined in Regulation S under the Securities Act). Any Bonds so cancelled will\par not be reissued.\par \par 7 Payments\par \par (a)Principal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of principal in respect of each Bond will be made by transfer to a\par U.S. dollar account maintained by the Bondholder with a bank in New York City\par or (i) if it does not have such a U.S. dollar account or (ii) if the principal\par amount of Bonds held by such person is less than U.S.$300,000, by a U.S. dollar\par cheque drawn on a bank in New York City mailed to the registered address of the\par Bondholder by uninsured mail at the risk of the Bondholder. Such payment will\par be made only upon presentation and surrender of the relevant Bond Certificate\par at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents and will be rounded\par downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent.\par \par For the purposes of this Condition 7(a) the holder of such Bond will be deemed\par to be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on\par the Register at the Registrar's close of business on the fifteenth day before\par the due date for such payment of principal and the outstanding amount of each\par Bondholder's registered holding will be deemed to be the amount shown as such\par on the Register for such Bondholder at the same time on that date.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of each\par Bond will be made by U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New York City and\par mailed to the holder (or to the first-named of joint holders) of such Bond at\par the address appearing in the Register not later than the due date for such\par payment and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent. For\par the purposes of this Condition 7(b) the holder of such Bond will be deemed to\par be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on the\par Register on the fifteenth day before the due date for such payment of interest.\par \par Upon application by a Bondholder with respect to Bonds having a principal\par amount of U.S.$300,000 or more to the specified office of the Registrar not\par less than 15 days before the due date for the payment of any interest (other\par than interest due on redemption) in respect of such Bonds, such payment will be\par made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by the payee with a bank\par in New York City. Any such application for transfer to a U.S. dollar account\par shall be deemed to relate to all future payments of interest (other than\par interest due on redemption) in respect of the Bonds which become payable to the\par Bondholder who has made the initial application until such time as the\par Registrar is notified in writing to the contrary by such Bondholder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payment of interest due on redemption will be made in the same manner as payment of the principal of a Bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)Payments Subject to Fiscal Laws\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds are subject in\par all cases to any applicable fiscal or other laws and regulations, but without\par prejudice to the provisions of Condition 8.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)Commissions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No commissions or expenses shall be charged to the Bondholders in respect of any payments of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Payments on Business Days\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Where payment is to be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account, payment\par instructions (for value the due date or, if that is not a business day, for\par value the first following day which is a business day) will be initiated on the\par due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest due on\par redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate is\par presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Where payment is to be made by cheque, the cheque will be mailed on the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In these Conditions, "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or\par Sunday) on which commercial banks are open for business in New York City and,\par in the case of the surrender of a Bond Certificate, in the place where the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond\par Certificate is surrendered.\par \par (f)Delay in Payments\par \par Bondholders will not be entitled to any interest or other payment for any delay\par after the due date in receiving any amount due in respect of any Bond as a\par result of (i) the due date not being a business day, (ii) the Bondholder being\par late in presenting or surrendering its Bond Certificate (if required to do so)\par or (iii) a cheque mailed in accordance with this Condition 7 arriving after the\par due date for payment or being lost in the mail.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (g)Partial Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If at any time a partial payment is made in respect of any Bond, the Registrar\par shall endorse the Register with a statement indicating the amount and date\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of\par such payment.\par \par (h)Agents\par \par The initial Agents and their initial specified offices are listed below. Any of\par the Agents may resign in accordance with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement and the Russian Federation reserves the right at any time to vary or\par terminate the appointment of any Agent and appoint additional or other Agents,\par provided that while the Bonds are outstanding it will maintain (i) a Fiscal\par Agent, (ii) a Registrar and (iii) a Paying Agent and a Transfer Agent having a\par specified office in a major European city which will be in London, so long as\par the Bonds are listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London\par Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   so require. Notice of any change in the Agents or their\par specified offices will promptly be given to the Bondholders in accordance with\par Condition 14.\par \par 8 Taxation\par \par All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds by the Russian\par Federation shall be made free and clear of, and without withholding or\par deduction for, any taxes, duties, assessments or governmental charges of\par whatever nature imposed, levied, collected, withheld or assessed by the Russian\par Federation or any political subdivision or any authority thereof or therein\par having power to tax (together, "Taxes"), unless such withholding or deduction\par is required by law. If at any time any Taxes are withheld or deducted, the\par Russian Federation shall increase the payment of principal or interest, as the\par case may be, to such amount as will result in the receipt by the Bondholders of\par such amounts as would have been received by them had no such withholding or\par deduction been required, except that no such increased amount shall be payable:\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, if such holder is liable to such Taxes by reason of having some connection with the Russian Federation other than the mere holding of a Bond or the receipt of payments thereunder;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b) if the Bond Certificate representing a Bond is presented and surrendered\par for payment more than 30 days after the Relevant Date, except to the extent\par that the holder would have been entitled to such additional amounts on\par presentation and surrender of such Bond Certificate for payment on the last day\par of such period of 30 days;\par \par (c) where such withholding or deduction is imposed on a payment to an\par individual and is required to be made pursuant to European Council Directive\par 2003/48/EC or any other Directive implementing the conclusions of the ECOFIN\par Council meeting of 26-27 November 2000 on the taxation of savings income or any\par law implementing or complying with, or introduced in order to conform to, such\par Directive; or\par \par (d) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, who would have been\par able to avoid such withholding or deduction by presenting the relevant Bond\par Certificate representing a Bond to another Paying Agent in a Member State of\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par If, in relation to any payment under the Bonds (a "Payment") any Bondholder has\par received any increased amount as provided in this Condition 8, the Russian\par Federation shall have the right to retain any refund of any amount withheld or\par deducted in respect of such Payment, which refund may be available under a tax\par treaty or otherwise to such Bondholder. Notwithstanding the foregoing, no\par Bondholder makes any representation or warranty that the Russian Federation\par will be entitled to any such refund (or to make any claim in respect thereof)\par and no Bondholder shall incur any obligation with respect thereto (including,\par without limitation, incurring any expense or liability in connection\par therewith).\par \par For the avoidance of doubt, the Russian Federation acknowledges that it shall\par have no right to make any deduction or withholding in respect of (nor will it\par purport to set off any amount equal to) any such refund from any payment in\par respect of any Bond.\par \par In these Conditions, "Relevant Date" means whichever is the later of (i) the\par date on which the payment in question first becomes due and (ii) if the full\par amount payable has not been received in New York City by the Fiscal Agent on or\par prior to such due date, the date on which (the full amount having been so\par received) notice to that effect has been given to the Bondholders.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any reference in these Conditions to principal or interest in respect of the Bonds shall be deemed to include any increased amounts which may be payable under this Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Events of Default\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If any of the following occurs and is continuing (each an "Event of Default") in respect of the Bonds, as applicable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Non-payment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation fails to pay any amount of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds, as the case may be, when due and such failure continues for a period of 30 calendar days; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Breach of other obligations or undertakings\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation defaults in performance or observance of or compliance\par with any of its other obligations or undertakings in respect of the Bonds, as\par the case may be, which default (if capable of remedy) is not remedied within\par 60 days after written notice of such default shall have been given to the\par Russian Federation by any holder of the Bonds, as the case may be, it being\par understood that a default in respect of the undertaking set forth in\par Condition 4(a) shall be deemed capable of remedy for purposes hereof; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)Cross-acceleration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any Public External Indebtedness shall become due and payable prior to the\par stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the debtor following a\par default or the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall fail to make the final payment of principal in\par respect of any Public External Indebtedness on the date on which such final\par payment is due and payable or at the expiration of any grace period originally\par applicable thereto or, in the case of any guarantee which constitutes Public\par External Indebtedness, the underlying obligation in respect of which such\par guarantee has been given shall have become due and payable prior to the stated\par maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the debtor following a default\par or the debtor shall have failed to make the final payment of principal in\par respect of such underlying obligation on the date on which such final payment\par is due and payable or at the expiration of any grace period originally\par applicable thereto and the guarantee shall not be honoured when due and called\par upon; provided that the aggregate amount of the relevant Public External\par Indebtedness in respect of which one or more of the events mentioned in this\par Condition 9(c) shall have occurred equals or exceeds U.S.$75,000,000 (or its\par equivalent in any other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ); and provided, further, that\par any secured Public External Indebtedness that by its terms is fully\par non-recourse to the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian\par Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall not be counted as Public External\par Indebtedness for purposes of this Condition 9(c); or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)Moratorium\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A moratorium on the payment of principal of, or interest on, all or any part of Public External Indebtedness; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Unlawfulness or Invalidity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The validity of the Bonds is contested by the Russian Federation or any agency\par or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation or the Russian Federation\par or any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation shall deny\par any of the Russian Federation's obligations under the Bonds or it is or will\par become unlawful for the Russian Federation to perform or comply with any of its\par obligations under or in respect of the Bonds or under or in respect of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement or any of such obligations shall be or become\par unenforceable or invalid; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f)Consents etc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any regulation, decree, consent, approval, licence or other authority necessary\par to enable the Russian Federation to enter into or perform its obligations under\par the Bonds or under the Fiscal Agency Agreement or for the validity or\par enforceability thereof shall expire or be withheld, revoked or terminated or\par otherwise be void or cease to remain in full force and effect or shall be\par modified in a manner which adversely affects any rights or claims of any of the\par holders of the Bonds, as the case may be,\par \par then holders of 25 per cent. or more in aggregate outstanding principal amount\par of the Bonds may declare the Bonds to be immediately due and payable whereupon\par the Bonds shall become immediately due and payable at their principal amount,\par together with accrued interest, without any further formality.\par \par If the Russian Federation receives notice in writing from holders of at least\par 50 per cent. in aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds to the\par effect that the Event of Default or Events of Default giving rise to any above\par mentioned declaration of acceleration is or are cured following any such\par declaration and that such holders wish the relevant declaration to be\par withdrawn, the Russian Federation shall give notice thereof to the Bondholders\par (with a copy to the Fiscal Agent), whereupon the relevant declaration shall be\par withdrawn and shall have no further effect, but without prejudice to any rights\par or obligations which may have arisen before the Russian Federation gives such\par notice (whether pursuant to these Conditions or otherwise). No such withdrawal\par shall affect any other or any subsequent Event of Default or any right of any\par Bondholder in relation thereto.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Prescription\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Claims against the Russian Federation in respect of principal and interest shall become void unless made within a period of three years from the date following the Maturity Date or a relevant Interest Payment Date, as applicable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11 Replacement of Bond Certificates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If any Bond Certificate is lost, stolen, mutilated, defaced or destroyed, it\par may be replaced at the specified office of the Registrar or the Transfer Agent\par with its specified office in London subject to all applicable laws and stock\par exchange requirements, upon payment by the claimant of the expenses incurred in\par connection with such replacement and on such terms as to evidence, security,\par indemnity and otherwise as the Russian Federation may reasonably require.\par Mutilated or defaced Bond Certificates must be surrendered before replacements\par will be issued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions; Modification and Waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions for convening meetings of\par Bondholders to consider any matter affecting their interests, including the\par modification by Extraordinary Resolution of these Conditions. The quorum at any\par such meeting for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as provided\par in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all outstanding Bonds are held\par by one person) be two or more persons holding or representing a clear majority\par of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting\par that is adjourned two or more persons being or representing Bondholders\par (whatever the principal amount of the outstanding Bonds so held or\par represented), and the majority for passing an Extraordinary Resolution at any\par such meeting shall be at least 662/3 per cent. of the votes cast except that at\par any meeting the business of which includes consideration of proposals on\par Reserved Matters, including, inter alia, (i) to modify the maturity of the\par Bonds, (ii) to reduce or cancel the principal amount of, or interest on, the\par Bonds, (iii) to change the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of payment of the Bonds, (iv) to modify the\par provisions concerning the quorum required at any meeting of Bondholders or the\par majority required to pass an Extraordinary Resolution or (v) to modify the\par percentage required to pass any resolution, the necessary quorum for passing an\par Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as provided in the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement in the event that all outstanding Bonds are held by one person) be\par two or more persons holding or representing at least 75 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting that is\par adjourned at least 25 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, and the majority for passing an Extraordinary Resolution on\par a Reserved Matter at any such meeting shall be at least 75 per cent. of the\par votes cast. An Extraordinary Resolution duly passed at any meeting of\par Bondholders will be binding on all Bondholders whether or not they are present\par at the meeting.\par \par In addition, the Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions relating to\par Written Resolutions. A "Written Resolution" means a resolution in writing\par signed by or on behalf of holders of at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate\par principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in the case of a Reserved Matter, or\par 662/3 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in\par the case of a matter other than a Reserved Matter. Any Written Resolution may\par be contained in one document or several documents in like form, each signed by\par or on behalf of one or more Bondholders. Any Written Resolution shall be\par binding on all of the Bondholders, whether or not signed by them.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Modification and waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The parties to the Fiscal Agency Agreement may agree, without the consent of\par the Bondholders, to any modification of any provision of the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement or the Bonds which is of a formal, minor or technical nature or is\par made to correct a manifest error.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 Further Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation shall be at liberty from time to time, without the\par consent of the Bondholders, to create and issue further Bonds ranking equally\par in all respects (or in all respects save for payments made prior to the\par issuance of such further Bonds and, if applicable, the date and amount of the\par first payment on such further Bonds) so that the same shall be consolidated and\par form a single series with the Bonds, provided that such further issuance\par constitutes a qualified reopening for U.S. federal income tax purposes.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Notices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Notices to the Bondholders will be sent to them by mail (airmail if overseas)\par at their respective addresses on the Register, and will be deemed to have been\par given on the fourth day after the date of mailing. So long as the Bonds are\par listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par so require, notices with respect to the Bonds will also be published in a\par leading newspaper having general circulation in the United Kingdom (which is\par expected to be The Financial Times) or, if such publication is not practicable,\par in an English language newspaper having general circulation in Europe, and each\par such notice shall be deemed to have been given on the date of such publication,\par or if published more than once or on different dates, on the first date on\par which publication is made.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Indemnity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S. dollar is the sole \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of account and payment for all sums\par payable by the Russian Federation under or in connection with the Bonds,\par including damages. Any amount received or recovered in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the U.S. dollar (whether as a result of, or the enforcement of, a judgement or\par order of a court of any jurisdiction or otherwise) by any Bondholder in respect\par of any sum expressed to be due to it from the Russian Federation shall only\par constitute a discharge to the Russian Federation to the extent of the U.S.\par dollar amount which the recipient is able to purchase with the amount so\par received or recovered in that other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the date of that receipt or\par recovery (or, if it is not practicable to make that purchase on that date, on\par the first date on which it is practicable to do so). If that U.S. dollar amount\par is less than the U.S. dollar amount expressed to be due to the recipient under\par any Bond, the Russian Federation shall indemnify such recipient against any\par loss sustained by it as a result. In any event, the Russian Federation shall\par indemnify the recipient against the cost of making any such purchase. These\par indemnities constitute separate and independent obligations from the Russian\par Federation's other obligations, shall give rise to a separate and independent\par cause of action, shall apply irrespective of any indulgence granted by any\par Bondholder and shall continue in full force and effect despite any judgement,\par order, claim or proof for a liquidated amount in respect of any sum due under\par any Bond or any judgement or order.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No person shall have any right to enforce any term or condition of the Bonds under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Governing Law\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds and the Fiscal Agency Agreement and any non-contractual obligations\par arising out of or in connection with them shall be governed by and construed in\par accordance with the laws of England.\par \par                     TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF THE 2022 BONDS                     \par \par The following is the text of the terms and conditions of the 2022 Bonds which,\par subject to amendment, will be endorsed on each Bond Certificate (as defined\par below) and will be attached and (subject to the provisions thereof) apply to\par each Global Bond representing the 2022 Bonds.\par \par The U.S.$2,000,000,000 4.50 per cent. Bonds due 2022 (the "Bonds") (which\par expression includes any further Bonds issued pursuant to Condition 13 and\par forming a single series with the Bonds) of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ") acting on behalf of the Russian\par Federation (the "Russian Federation") were authorised pursuant to the\par provisions of Federal Law No. 136-FZ of 29 July 1998 (as amended) "On the\par Specifics of Issuance and Circulation of State and Municipal Securities";\par Federal Law No. 371-FZ of 30 November 2011 "On the Federal Budget for 2012 and\par for the Planning Period of 2013 and 2014"; Resolution of the Government of the\par Russian Federation dated 27 December 2011 No. 2390-r; Resolution of the\par Government of the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 217; Order of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 24 February 2012 No. 31n; and Order of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 30 March 2012 No. 145. The fiscal agency agreement dated\par 3 April 2012 (the "Fiscal Agency Agreement") has been entered into in relation\par to the Bonds by the Russian Federation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, as\par fiscal and paying agent (the "Fiscal Agent") and as registrar (the "Registrar")\par in London and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., New York Branch, as transfer agent (together with\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, the "Transfer Agents") and paying agent in New\par York (together with the Fiscal Agent and the Registrar, the "Paying Agents")\par and the transfer agents named therein (the "Transfer Agents").\par \par In these Conditions, "Fiscal Agent," "Registrar," "Paying Agent," and "Transfer\par Agent" shall include any successors appointed from time to time in accordance\par with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds, and any\par reference to an "Agent" or "Agents" shall mean any or all (as applicable) of\par such persons.\par \par Copies of the Fiscal Agency Agreement are available for inspection during usual\par business hours at the principal office of the Fiscal Agent (currently 13th\par Floor, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Centre\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Canada Square, London E14 5LB) and at the specified\par offices of each of the other Agents. The Bondholders (as defined in Condition 1\par (b)) are bound by, and are deemed to have notice of, the provisions of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds, including any provision of the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement which amends or supplements the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Form, Denomination and Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Form and Denomination\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds are in definitive fully registered form, without interest coupons\par attached, in the denomination of U.S.$200,000 (the "authorised denomination").\par A certificate (each a "Bond Certificate") will be issued to each Bondholder in\par respect of its registered holding or holdings of Bonds. Each Bond Certificate\par will be numbered serially with an identifying number which will be recorded in\par the register (the "Register") which the Russian Federation shall procure to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be\par kept by the Registrar.\par \par (b)Title\par \par Title to the Bonds will pass by and upon registration in the Register. In these\par Conditions, "Bondholder" and "holder" mean the person in whose name a Bond is\par registered in the Register (or, in the case of joint holders, the first-named\par thereof). The holder of any Bond will (except as otherwise requested by such\par holder in writing, or as otherwise ordered by a court of competent jurisdiction\par or required by law) be treated as its absolute owner for all purposes, whether\par or not it is overdue and regardless of any notice of ownership, trust or any\par interest therein, any writing thereon by any person (other than a duly executed\par transfer thereof in the form endorsed thereon) or any notice of any previous\par theft or loss thereof, and no person will be liable for so treating the holder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Transfer of Bonds and Issue of Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Transfer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subject to Condition 2(d), a Bond may be transferred in whole or in part in the\par authorised denomination upon the surrender of the Bond Certificate representing\par that Bond, together with the form of transfer (including any certification as\par to compliance with restrictions on transfer included in such form of transfer)\par endorsed thereon (the "Transfer Form") duly completed and executed, at the\par specified office of the Registrar or any Transfer Agent, together with such\par evidence as the Registrar or, as the case may be, such Transfer Agent may\par reasonably require to prove the title of the transferor and the authority of\par the persons who have executed the Transfer Form. In the case of a transfer of\par part only of the Bonds represented by a Bond Certificate, neither the part\par transferred nor the balance not transferred may be less than the authorised\par denomination and a new Bond Certificate in respect of the balance not so\par transferred will be issued to the transferor.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Delivery\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each new Bond Certificate to be issued upon a transfer of any Bonds will,\par within five business days of the request for transfer being duly made, be\par delivered at the specified office of the Registrar or, as the case may be, any\par Transfer Agent or (at the request and the risk of such transferee) be mailed by\par uninsured post to such address as the transferee entitled to the Bonds\par represented by such Bond Certificate may have specified. In this Condition 2\par (b), "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday) on which\par commercial banks are open for business (including dealings in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the cities in which the Registrar and any such Transfer Agent\par have their respective specified offices.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)No Charge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Registration or transfer of Bonds will be effected without charge to the holder\par or transferee thereof, but upon payment (or against such indemnity from the\par holder or the transferee thereof as the Registrar or the relevant Transfer\par Agent may reasonably require) in respect of any tax or other duty of whatever\par nature which may be levied or imposed in connection with such registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 or\par transfer.\par \par (d)Closed Periods\par \par No Bondholder may require the transfer of a Bond to be registered during the\par period of 15 calendar days ending on the due date for any payment of principal\par in respect of such Bond.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Regulations Concerning Transfer and Registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All transfers of Bonds and entries on the Register will be made subject to the\par detailed regulations concerning transfer of Bonds scheduled to the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement. The regulations may be changed by the Russian Federation in a\par manner which is reasonably required by the Russian Federation (after\par consultation with the Registrar) to reflect changes in legal requirements or in\par any other manner which is not prejudicial to the interests of the Bondholders.\par A copy of the current regulations will be sent by the Registrar to any\par Bondholder who so requests.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Status\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds constitute direct, unconditional, unsecured and unsubordinated\par obligations of the Russian Federation and the full faith and credit of the\par Russian Federation is pledged for the due and punctual payment of principal of,\par and interest on, the Bonds and for the performance of all other obligations of\par the Russian Federation pursuant to the Bonds. The Bonds shall at all times rank\par pari passu without any preference among themselves and pari passu in all\par respects with all other present and future unsecured and unsubordinated\par obligations of the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Negative Pledge and Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Negative Pledge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any of the Bonds remains outstanding (as defined in the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement) the Russian Federation will not create or permit to subsist\par any Lien upon the whole or any part of the International Monetary Assets to\par secure any Public External Indebtedness unless, at the same time or prior\par thereto, the obligations of the Russian Federation under the Bonds are secured\par equally and rateably therewith.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any Bond remains outstanding the Russian Federation shall obtain,\par and do or cause to be done all things necessary to ensure the continuance of,\par all consents, licences, approvals and authorisations, and make or cause to be\par made all registrations, recordings and filings, which may at any time be\par required to be obtained or made in the Russian Federation for the execution,\par delivery or performance of the Bonds or for the validity or enforceability\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 thereof.\par \par (c)Definitions\par \par In these Conditions:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Excluded Indebtedness" means any obligation of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par of the U.S.S.R. (now known as state corporation "Bank for Development and\par Foreign Economic Affairs (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  )") or any other person that may arise\par from time to time representing restructured indebtedness originally incurred\par prior to 1 January 1992 by the government of the former Soviet Union or any of\par its legally authorised entities, other than the Russian Federation U.S. Dollar\par Denominated Bonds due 2007 to 2030.\par \par "External Indebtedness" means Indebtedness (i) which is not Excluded\par Indebtedness, (ii) which is denominated or payable, or at the option of the\par relevant creditor or holder thereof may be payable, in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the lawful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation and (iii) which was not\par originally incurred or assumed under an agreement or instrument made with or\par issued to creditors substantially all of whom were residents of the Russian\par Federation or entities having their head office or principal place of business\par within the territory of the Russian Federation. For the avoidance of doubt,\par External Indebtedness does not include Internal Government Hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bonds\par known as "OVVZs," "Taiga" bonds or "MinFins" or any bonds representing\par restructured internal hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds of the Government of the Russian\par Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Government of the Russian Federation" means the Government of the Russian Federation as provided in Article 110 of the Constitution of the Russian Federation, or any successor article, from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "IMF" means the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Indebtedness" means any legal obligation or any obligation intended by its\par terms to be a legal obligation (whether present or future, actual or\par contingent, secured or unsecured, incurred as principal, surety, guarantor or\par otherwise) for the payment or repayment of borrowed money created (or intended\par by its terms to have been created) under an agreement or instrument in which\par the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is designated as the obligor, or which by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par Russian law constitutes a legal obligation of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (it being\par understood that neither the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , nor any\par political subdivision, regional or municipal government, ministry (other than\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), department, authority or statutory corporation of the\par Russian Federation nor any joint stock company, enterprise or other entity\par organised or existing under the laws or regulations of the Russian Federation\par or any of the above, is considered to be part of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for purposes\par hereof).\par \par "International Monetary Assets" means all official holdings of gold, Special\par Drawing Rights, Reserve Positions in the Fund and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the Government of the Russian Federation from time to\par time (but not, for the avoidance of doubt, any such assets of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  at any time), and the terms "Special Drawing Rights,"\par "Reserve Positions in the Fund" and "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " have, as to the types of\par assets included, the meanings given to them in the IMF's publication entitled\par "International Financial Statistics" or such other meanings as shall be\par formally adopted by the IMF from time to time.\par \par "Lien" means any lien, pledge, hypothecation, mortgage, security interest, deed\par of trust, charge or any other encumbrance arising under a security agreement or\par arrangement.\par \par "Public External Indebtedness" means External Indebtedness which (i) is in the\par form of, or represented by, bonds, notes or other securities or any guarantee\par thereof and (ii) is, or may be, quoted, listed or ordinarily purchased and sold\par on any stock exchange, automated trading system or over-the-counter or other\par securities market (including, without prejudice to the generality of the\par foregoing, the market for securities eligible for resale pursuant to Rule 144A\par under the United States Securities Act of 1933, as amended (the "Securities\par Act")).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond bears interest on its outstanding principal amount from 4 April 2012\par at 4.50 per cent. per annum, payable semi-annually in arrear on 4 April and\par 4 October in each year until maturity, commencing on 4 October 2012 (each, an "\par Interest Payment Date"). Interest will be paid subject to and in accordance\par with the provisions of Condition 7. The period beginning on and including\par 4 April 2012 and ending on but excluding the first Interest Payment Date and\par each successive period beginning on and including an Interest Payment Date and\par ending on but excluding the next successive Interest Payment Date is called an\par "Interest Period".\par \par Each Bond will cease to bear interest from the due date for redemption unless,\par after surrender of such Bond, payment of principal is improperly withheld or\par refused, in which case the outstanding principal amount of such Bond will\par continue to bear interest at the rate specified above (after as well as before\par judgment) until whichever is the earlier of (a) the day on which all sums due\par in respect of such Bond up to that day are received by or on behalf of the\par relevant Bondholder and (b) the day which is seven days after notice has been\par given to the Bondholders that the Fiscal Agent has received all sums due in\par respect of the Bonds up to such seventh day (except, in the case of payment to\par the Fiscal Agent, to the extent that there is any subsequent default in payment\par in accordance with these Conditions).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If interest is required to be calculated for a period of less than a complete Interest Period, it will be calculated on the basis of a year of 360 days consisting of 12 months of 30 days each and, in the case of an incomplete month, the actual number of days elapsed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6 Redemption, Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Redemption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless previously redeemed, or purchased and cancelled, each Bond will be redeemed at its principal amount on 4 April 2022 (the "Maturity Date") subject as provided in Condition 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b)Purchase and Cancellation\par \par The Russian Federation and its affiliates may at any time purchase Bonds in the\par open market or otherwise at any price. Any Bonds so purchased may be cancelled\par or held and resold (provided that such resale is outside the United States, as\par defined in Regulation S under the Securities Act). Any Bonds so cancelled will\par not be reissued.\par \par 7 Payments\par \par (a)Principal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of principal in respect of each Bond will be made by transfer to a\par U.S. dollar account maintained by the Bondholder with a bank in New York City\par or (i) if it does not have such a U.S. dollar account or (ii) if the principal\par amount of Bonds held by such person is less than U.S.$300,000, by a U.S. dollar\par cheque drawn on a bank in New York City mailed to the registered address of the\par Bondholder by uninsured mail at the risk of the Bondholder. Such payment will\par be made only upon presentation and surrender of the relevant Bond Certificate\par at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents and will be rounded\par downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent.\par \par For the purposes of this Condition 7(a) the holder of such Bond will be deemed\par to be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on\par the Register at the Registrar's close of business on the fifteenth day before\par the due date for such payment of principal and the outstanding amount of each\par Bondholder's registered holding will be deemed to be the amount shown as such\par on the Register for such Bondholder at the same time on that date.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of each\par Bond will be made by U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New York City and\par mailed to the holder (or to the first-named of joint holders) of such Bond at\par the address appearing in the Register not later than the due date for such\par payment and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent. For\par the purposes of this Condition 7(b) the holder of such Bond will be deemed to\par be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on the\par Register on the fifteenth day before the due date for such payment of interest.\par \par Upon application by a Bondholder with respect to Bonds having a principal\par amount of U.S.$300,000 or more to the specified office of the Registrar not\par less than 15 days before the due date for the payment of any interest (other\par than interest due on redemption) in respect of such Bonds, such payment will be\par made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by the payee with a bank\par in New York City. Any such application for transfer to a U.S. dollar account\par shall be deemed to relate to all future payments of interest (other than\par interest due on redemption) in respect of the Bonds which become payable to the\par Bondholder who has made the initial application until such time as the\par Registrar is notified in writing to the contrary by such Bondholder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payment of interest due on redemption will be made in the same manner as payment of the principal of a Bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)Payments Subject to Fiscal Laws\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds are subject in\par all cases to any applicable fiscal or other laws and regulations, but without\par prejudice to the provisions of Condition 8.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)Commissions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No commissions or expenses shall be charged to the Bondholders in respect of any payments of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Payments on Business Days\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Where payment is to be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account, payment\par instructions (for value the due date or, if that is not a business day, for\par value the first following day which is a business day) will be initiated on the\par due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest due on\par redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate is\par presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Where payment is to be made by cheque, the cheque will be mailed on the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In these Conditions, "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or\par Sunday) on which commercial banks are open for business in New York City and,\par in the case of the surrender of a Bond Certificate, in the place where the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond\par Certificate is surrendered.\par \par (f)Delay in Payments\par \par Bondholders will not be entitled to any interest or other payment for any delay\par after the due date in receiving any amount due in respect of any Bond as a\par result of (i) the due date not being a business day, (ii) the Bondholder being\par late in presenting or surrendering its Bond Certificate (if required to do so)\par or (iii) a cheque mailed in accordance with this Condition 7 arriving after the\par due date for payment or being lost in the mail.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (g)Partial Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If at any time a partial payment is made in respect of any Bond, the Registrar\par shall endorse the Register with a statement indicating the amount and date\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of\par such payment.\par \par (h)Agents\par \par The initial Agents and their initial specified offices are listed below. Any of\par the Agents may resign in accordance with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement and the Russian Federation reserves the right at any time to vary or\par terminate the appointment of any Agent and appoint additional or other Agents,\par provided that while the Bonds are outstanding it will maintain (i) a Fiscal\par Agent, (ii) a Registrar and (iii) a Paying Agent and a Transfer Agent having a\par specified office in a major European city which will be in London, so long as\par the Bonds are listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London\par Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   so require. Notice of any change in the Agents or their\par specified offices will promptly be given to the Bondholders in accordance with\par Condition 14.\par \par 8 Taxation\par \par All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds by the Russian\par Federation shall be made free and clear of, and without withholding or\par deduction for, any taxes, duties, assessments or governmental charges of\par whatever nature imposed, levied, collected, withheld or assessed by the Russian\par Federation or any political subdivision or any authority thereof or therein\par having power to tax (together, "Taxes"), unless such withholding or deduction\par is required by law. If at any time any Taxes are withheld or deducted, the\par Russian Federation shall increase the payment of principal or interest, as the\par case may be, to such amount as will result in the receipt by the Bondholders of\par such amounts as would have been received by them had no such withholding or\par deduction been required, except that no such increased amount shall be payable:\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, if such holder is liable to such Taxes by reason of having some connection with the Russian Federation other than the mere holding of a Bond or the receipt of payments thereunder;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b) if the Bond Certificate representing a Bond is presented and surrendered\par for payment more than 30 days after the Relevant Date, except to the extent\par that the holder would have been entitled to such additional amounts on\par presentation and surrender of such Bond Certificate for payment on the last day\par of such period of 30 days;\par \par (c) where such withholding or deduction is imposed on a payment to an\par individual and is required to be made pursuant to European Council Directive\par 2003/48/EC or any other Directive implementing the conclusions of the ECOFIN\par Council meeting of 26-27 November 2000 on the taxation of savings income or any\par law implementing or complying with, or introduced in order to conform to, such\par Directive; or\par \par (d) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, who would have been\par able to avoid such withholding or deduction by presenting the relevant Bond\par Certificate representing a Bond to another Paying Agent in a Member State of\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par If, in relation to any payment under the Bonds (a "Payment") any Bondholder has\par received any increased amount as provided in this Condition 8, the Russian\par Federation shall have the right to retain any refund of any amount withheld or\par deducted in respect of such Payment, which refund may be available under a tax\par treaty or otherwise to such Bondholder. Notwithstanding the foregoing, no\par Bondholder makes any representation or warranty that the Russian Federation\par will be entitled to any such refund (or to make any claim in respect thereof)\par and no Bondholder shall incur any obligation with respect thereto (including,\par without limitation, incurring any expense or liability in connection\par therewith).\par \par For the avoidance of doubt, the Russian Federation acknowledges that it shall\par have no right to make any deduction or withholding in respect of (nor will it\par purport to set off any amount equal to) any such refund from any payment in\par respect of any Bond.\par \par In these Conditions, "Relevant Date" means whichever is the later of (i) the\par date on which the payment in question first becomes due and (ii) if the full\par amount payable has not been received in New York City by the Fiscal Agent on or\par prior to such due date, the date on which (the full amount having been so\par received) notice to that effect has been given to the Bondholders.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any reference in these Conditions to principal or interest in respect of the Bonds shall be deemed to include any increased amounts which may be payable under this Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Events of Default\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If any of the following occurs and is continuing (each an "Event of Default") in respect of the Bonds, as applicable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Non-payment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation fails to pay any amount of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds, as the case may be, when due and such failure continues for a period of 30 calendar days; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Breach of other obligations or undertakings\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation defaults in performance or observance of or compliance\par with any of its other obligations or undertakings in respect of the Bonds, as\par the case may be, which default (if capable of remedy) is not remedied within\par 60 days after written notice of such default shall have been given to the\par Russian Federation by any holder of the Bonds, as the case may be, it being\par understood that a default in respect of the undertaking set forth in\par Condition 4(a) shall be deemed capable of remedy for purposes hereof; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)Cross-acceleration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any Public External Indebtedness shall become due and payable prior to the\par stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the debtor following a\par default or the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall fail to make the final payment of principal in\par respect of any Public External Indebtedness on the date on which such final\par payment is due and payable or at the expiration of any grace period originally\par applicable thereto or, in the case of any guarantee which constitutes Public\par External Indebtedness, the underlying obligation in respect of which such\par guarantee has been given shall have become due and payable prior to the stated\par maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the debtor following a default\par or the debtor shall have failed to make the final payment of principal in\par respect of such underlying obligation on the date on which such final payment\par is due and payable or at the expiration of any grace period originally\par applicable thereto and the guarantee shall not be honoured when due and called\par upon; provided that the aggregate amount of the relevant Public External\par Indebtedness in respect of which one or more of the events mentioned in this\par Condition 9(c) shall have occurred equals or exceeds U.S.$75,000,000 (or its\par equivalent in any other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ); and provided, further, that\par any secured Public External Indebtedness that by its terms is fully\par non-recourse to the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian\par Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall not be counted as Public External\par Indebtedness for purposes of this Condition 9(c); or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)Moratorium\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A moratorium on the payment of principal of, or interest on, all or any part of Public External Indebtedness; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Unlawfulness or Invalidity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The validity of the Bonds is contested by the Russian Federation or any agency\par or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation or the Russian Federation\par or any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation shall deny\par any of the Russian Federation's obligations under the Bonds or it is or will\par become unlawful for the Russian Federation to perform or comply with any of its\par obligations under or in respect of the Bonds or under or in respect of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement or any of such obligations shall be or become\par unenforceable or invalid; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f)Consents etc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any regulation, decree, consent, approval, licence or other authority necessary\par to enable the Russian Federation to enter into or perform its obligations under\par the Bonds or under the Fiscal Agency Agreement or for the validity or\par enforceability thereof shall expire or be withheld, revoked or terminated or\par otherwise be void or cease to remain in full force and effect or shall be\par modified in a manner which adversely affects any rights or claims of any of the\par holders of the Bonds, as the case may be,\par \par then holders of 25 per cent. or more in aggregate outstanding principal amount\par of the Bonds may declare the Bonds to be immediately due and payable whereupon\par the Bonds shall become immediately due and payable at their principal amount,\par together with accrued interest, without any further formality.\par \par If the Russian Federation receives notice in writing from holders of at least\par 50 per cent. in aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds to the\par effect that the Event of Default or Events of Default giving rise to any above\par mentioned declaration of acceleration is or are cured following any such\par declaration and that such holders wish the relevant declaration to be\par withdrawn, the Russian Federation shall give notice thereof to the Bondholders\par (with a copy to the Fiscal Agent), whereupon the relevant declaration shall be\par withdrawn and shall have no further effect, but without prejudice to any rights\par or obligations which may have arisen before the Russian Federation gives such\par notice (whether pursuant to these Conditions or otherwise). No such withdrawal\par shall affect any other or any subsequent Event of Default or any right of any\par Bondholder in relation thereto.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Prescription\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Claims against the Russian Federation in respect of principal and interest shall become void unless made within a period of three years from the date following the Maturity Date or a relevant Interest Payment Date, as applicable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11 Replacement of Bond Certificates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If any Bond Certificate is lost, stolen, mutilated, defaced or destroyed, it\par may be replaced at the specified office of the Registrar or the Transfer Agent\par with its specified office in London subject to all applicable laws and stock\par exchange requirements, upon payment by the claimant of the expenses incurred in\par connection with such replacement and on such terms as to evidence, security,\par indemnity and otherwise as the Russian Federation may reasonably require.\par Mutilated or defaced Bond Certificates must be surrendered before replacements\par will be issued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions; Modification and Waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions for convening meetings of\par Bondholders to consider any matter affecting their interests, including the\par modification by Extraordinary Resolution of these Conditions. The quorum at any\par such meeting for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as provided\par in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all outstanding Bonds are held\par by one person) be two or more persons holding or representing a clear majority\par of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting\par that is adjourned two or more persons being or representing Bondholders\par (whatever the principal amount of the outstanding Bonds so held or\par represented), and the majority for passing an Extraordinary Resolution at any\par such meeting shall be at least 662/3 per cent. of the votes cast except that at\par any meeting the business of which includes consideration of proposals on\par Reserved Matters, including, inter alia, (i) to modify the maturity of the\par Bonds, (ii) to reduce or cancel the principal amount of, or interest on, the\par Bonds, (iii) to change the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of payment of the Bonds, (iv) to modify the\par provisions concerning the quorum required at any meeting of Bondholders or the\par majority required to pass an Extraordinary Resolution or (v) to modify the\par percentage required to pass any resolution, the necessary quorum for passing an\par Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as provided in the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement in the event that all outstanding Bonds are held by one person) be\par two or more persons holding or representing at least 75 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting that is\par adjourned at least 25 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, and the majority for passing an Extraordinary Resolution on\par a Reserved Matter at any such meeting shall be at least 75 per cent. of the\par votes cast. An Extraordinary Resolution duly passed at any meeting of\par Bondholders will be binding on all Bondholders whether or not they are present\par at the meeting.\par \par In addition, the Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions relating to\par Written Resolutions. A "Written Resolution" means a resolution in writing\par signed by or on behalf of holders of at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate\par principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in the case of a Reserved Matter, or\par 662/3 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in\par the case of a matter other than a Reserved Matter. Any Written Resolution may\par be contained in one document or several documents in like form, each signed by\par or on behalf of one or more Bondholders. Any Written Resolution shall be\par binding on all of the Bondholders, whether or not signed by them.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Modification and waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The parties to the Fiscal Agency Agreement may agree, without the consent of\par the Bondholders, to any modification of any provision of the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement or the Bonds which is of a formal, minor or technical nature or is\par made to correct a manifest error.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 Further Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation shall be at liberty from time to time, without the\par consent of the Bondholders, to create and issue further Bonds ranking equally\par in all respects (or in all respects save for payments made prior to the\par issuance of such further Bonds and, if applicable, the date and amount of the\par first payment on such further Bonds) so that the same shall be consolidated and\par form a single series with the Bonds, provided that such further issuance\par constitutes a qualified reopening for U.S. federal income tax purposes.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Notices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Notices to the Bondholders will be sent to them by mail (airmail if overseas)\par at their respective addresses on the Register, and will be deemed to have been\par given on the fourth day after the date of mailing. So long as the Bonds are\par listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par so require, notices with respect to the Bonds will also be published in a\par leading newspaper having general circulation in the United Kingdom (which is\par expected to be The Financial Times) or, if such publication is not practicable,\par in an English language newspaper having general circulation in Europe, and each\par such notice shall be deemed to have been given on the date of such publication,\par or if published more than once or on different dates, on the first date on\par which publication is made.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Indemnity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S. dollar is the sole \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of account and payment for all sums\par payable by the Russian Federation under or in connection with the Bonds,\par including damages. Any amount received or recovered in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the U.S. dollar (whether as a result of, or the enforcement of, a judgement or\par order of a court of any jurisdiction or otherwise) by any Bondholder in respect\par of any sum expressed to be due to it from the Russian Federation shall only\par constitute a discharge to the Russian Federation to the extent of the U.S.\par dollar amount which the recipient is able to purchase with the amount so\par received or recovered in that other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the date of that receipt or\par recovery (or, if it is not practicable to make that purchase on that date, on\par the first date on which it is practicable to do so). If that U.S. dollar amount\par is less than the U.S. dollar amount expressed to be due to the recipient under\par any Bond, the Russian Federation shall indemnify such recipient against any\par loss sustained by it as a result. In any event, the Russian Federation shall\par indemnify the recipient against the cost of making any such purchase. These\par indemnities constitute separate and independent obligations from the Russian\par Federation's other obligations, shall give rise to a separate and independent\par cause of action, shall apply irrespective of any indulgence granted by any\par Bondholder and shall continue in full force and effect despite any judgement,\par order, claim or proof for a liquidated amount in respect of any sum due under\par any Bond or any judgement or order.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No person shall have any right to enforce any term or condition of the Bonds under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Governing Law\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds and the Fiscal Agency Agreement and any non-contractual obligations\par arising out of or in connection with them shall be governed by and construed in\par accordance with the laws of England.\par \par                     TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF THE 2042 BONDS                     \par \par The following is the text of the terms and conditions of the 2042 Bonds which,\par subject to amendment, will be endorsed on each Bond Certificate (as defined\par below) and will be attached and (subject to the provisions thereof) apply to\par each Global Bond representing the 2042 Bonds.\par \par The U.S.$3,000,000,000 5.625 per cent. Bonds due 2042 (the "Bonds") (which\par expression includes any further Bonds issued pursuant to Condition 13 and\par forming a single series with the Bonds) of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ") acting on behalf of the Russian\par Federation (the "Russian Federation") were authorised pursuant to the\par provisions of Federal Law No. 136-FZ of 29 July 1998 (as amended) "On the\par Specifics of Issuance and Circulation of State and Municipal Securities";\par Federal Law No. 371-FZ of 30 November 2011 "On the Federal Budget for 2012 and\par for the Planning Period of 2013 and 2014"; Resolution of the Government of the\par Russian Federation dated 27 December 2011 No. 2390-r; Resolution of the\par Government of the Russian Federation dated 8 April 2010 No. 217; Order of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 24 February 2012 No. 31n; and Order of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dated 30 March 2012 No. 146. The fiscal agency agreement dated\par 3 April 2012 (the "Fiscal Agency Agreement") has been entered into in relation\par to the Bonds by the Russian Federation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, as\par fiscal and paying agent (the "Fiscal Agent") and as registrar (the "Registrar")\par in London and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., New York Branch, as transfer agent (together with\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citibank, N.A \f28 \uc2 \f2 ., London Branch, the "Transfer Agents") and paying agent in New\par York (together with the Fiscal Agent and the Registrar, the "Paying Agents")\par and the transfer agents named therein (the "Transfer Agents").\par \par In these Conditions, "Fiscal Agent," "Registrar," "Paying Agent," and "Transfer\par Agent" shall include any successors appointed from time to time in accordance\par with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds, and any\par reference to an "Agent" or "Agents" shall mean any or all (as applicable) of\par such persons.\par \par Copies of the Fiscal Agency Agreement are available for inspection during usual\par business hours at the principal office of the Fiscal Agent (currently 13th\par Floor, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Centre\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Canada Square, London E14 5LB) and at the specified\par offices of each of the other Agents. The Bondholders (as defined in Condition 1\par (b)) are bound by, and are deemed to have notice of, the provisions of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement for the Bonds, including any provision of the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement which amends or supplements the Bonds.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Form, Denomination and Title\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Form and Denomination\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bonds are in definitive fully registered form, without interest coupons\par attached, in the denomination of U.S.$200,000 (the "authorised denomination").\par A certificate (each a "Bond Certificate") will be issued to each Bondholder in\par respect of its registered holding or holdings of Bonds. Each Bond Certificate\par will be numbered serially with an identifying number which will be recorded in\par the register (the "Register") which the Russian Federation shall procure to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be\par kept by the Registrar.\par \par (b)Title\par \par Title to the Bonds will pass by and upon registration in the Register. In these\par Conditions, "Bondholder" and "holder" mean the person in whose name a Bond is\par registered in the Register (or, in the case of joint holders, the first-named\par thereof). The holder of any Bond will (except as otherwise requested by such\par holder in writing, or as otherwise ordered by a court of competent jurisdiction\par or required by law) be treated as its absolute owner for all purposes, whether\par or not it is overdue and regardless of any notice of ownership, trust or any\par interest therein, any writing thereon by any person (other than a duly executed\par transfer thereof in the form endorsed thereon) or any notice of any previous\par theft or loss thereof, and no person will be liable for so treating the holder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Transfer of Bonds and Issue of Bonds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Transfer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subject to Condition 2(d), a Bond may be transferred in whole or in part in the\par authorised denomination upon the surrender of the Bond Certificate representing\par that Bond, together with the form of transfer (including any certification as\par to compliance with restrictions on transfer included in such form of transfer)\par endorsed thereon (the "Transfer Form") duly completed and executed, at the\par specified office of the Registrar or any Transfer Agent, together with such\par evidence as the Registrar or, as the case may be, such Transfer Agent may\par reasonably require to prove the title of the transferor and the authority of\par the persons who have executed the Transfer Form. In the case of a transfer of\par part only of the Bonds represented by a Bond Certificate, neither the part\par transferred nor the balance not transferred may be less than the authorised\par denomination and a new Bond Certificate in respect of the balance not so\par transferred will be issued to the transferor.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Delivery\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each new Bond Certificate to be issued upon a transfer of any Bonds will,\par within five business days of the request for transfer being duly made, be\par delivered at the specified office of the Registrar or, as the case may be, any\par Transfer Agent or (at the request and the risk of such transferee) be mailed by\par uninsured post to such address as the transferee entitled to the Bonds\par represented by such Bond Certificate may have specified. In this Condition 2\par (b), "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or Sunday) on which\par commercial banks are open for business (including dealings in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the cities in which the Registrar and any such Transfer Agent\par have their respective specified offices.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)No Charge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Registration or transfer of Bonds will be effected without charge to the holder\par or transferee thereof, but upon payment (or against such indemnity from the\par holder or the transferee thereof as the Registrar or the relevant Transfer\par Agent may reasonably require) in respect of any tax or other duty of whatever\par nature which may be levied or imposed in connection with such registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 or\par transfer.\par \par (d)Closed Periods\par \par No Bondholder may require the transfer of a Bond to be registered during the\par period of 15 calendar days ending on the due date for any payment of principal\par in respect of such Bond.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Regulations Concerning Transfer and Registration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All transfers of Bonds and entries on the Register will be made subject to the\par detailed regulations concerning transfer of Bonds scheduled to the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement. The regulations may be changed by the Russian Federation in a\par manner which is reasonably required by the Russian Federation (after\par consultation with the Registrar) to reflect changes in legal requirements or in\par any other manner which is not prejudicial to the interests of the Bondholders.\par A copy of the current regulations will be sent by the Registrar to any\par Bondholder who so requests.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Status\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds constitute direct, unconditional, unsecured and unsubordinated\par obligations of the Russian Federation and the full faith and credit of the\par Russian Federation is pledged for the due and punctual payment of principal of,\par and interest on, the Bonds and for the performance of all other obligations of\par the Russian Federation pursuant to the Bonds. The Bonds shall at all times rank\par pari passu without any preference among themselves and pari passu in all\par respects with all other present and future unsecured and unsubordinated\par obligations of the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Negative Pledge and Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Negative Pledge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any of the Bonds remains outstanding (as defined in the Fiscal\par Agency Agreement) the Russian Federation will not create or permit to subsist\par any Lien upon the whole or any part of the International Monetary Assets to\par secure any Public External Indebtedness unless, at the same time or prior\par thereto, the obligations of the Russian Federation under the Bonds are secured\par equally and rateably therewith.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Covenant\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par So long as any Bond remains outstanding the Russian Federation shall obtain,\par and do or cause to be done all things necessary to ensure the continuance of,\par all consents, licences, approvals and authorisations, and make or cause to be\par made all registrations, recordings and filings, which may at any time be\par required to be obtained or made in the Russian Federation for the execution,\par delivery or performance of the Bonds or for the validity or enforceability\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 thereof.\par \par (c)Definitions\par \par In these Conditions:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Excluded Indebtedness" means any obligation of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par of the U.S.S.R. (now known as state corporation "Bank for Development and\par Foreign Economic Affairs (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  )") or any other person that may arise\par from time to time representing restructured indebtedness originally incurred\par prior to 1 January 1992 by the government of the former Soviet Union or any of\par its legally authorised entities, other than the Russian Federation U.S. Dollar\par Denominated Bonds due 2007 to 2030.\par \par "External Indebtedness" means Indebtedness (i) which is not Excluded\par Indebtedness, (ii) which is denominated or payable, or at the option of the\par relevant creditor or holder thereof may be payable, in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the lawful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation and (iii) which was not\par originally incurred or assumed under an agreement or instrument made with or\par issued to creditors substantially all of whom were residents of the Russian\par Federation or entities having their head office or principal place of business\par within the territory of the Russian Federation. For the avoidance of doubt,\par External Indebtedness does not include Internal Government Hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bonds\par known as "OVVZs," "Taiga" bonds or "MinFins" or any bonds representing\par restructured internal hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds of the Government of the Russian\par Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Government of the Russian Federation" means the Government of the Russian Federation as provided in Article 110 of the Constitution of the Russian Federation, or any successor article, from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "IMF" means the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Indebtedness" means any legal obligation or any obligation intended by its\par terms to be a legal obligation (whether present or future, actual or\par contingent, secured or unsecured, incurred as principal, surety, guarantor or\par otherwise) for the payment or repayment of borrowed money created (or intended\par by its terms to have been created) under an agreement or instrument in which\par the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is designated as the obligor, or which by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par Russian law constitutes a legal obligation of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (it being\par understood that neither the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , nor any\par political subdivision, regional or municipal government, ministry (other than\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), department, authority or statutory corporation of the\par Russian Federation nor any joint stock company, enterprise or other entity\par organised or existing under the laws or regulations of the Russian Federation\par or any of the above, is considered to be part of the Russian Federation, the\par Government of the Russian Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for purposes\par hereof).\par \par "International Monetary Assets" means all official holdings of gold, Special\par Drawing Rights, Reserve Positions in the Fund and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the Government of the Russian Federation from time to\par time (but not, for the avoidance of doubt, any such assets of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par of the Russian Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2  at any time), and the terms "Special Drawing Rights,"\par "Reserve Positions in the Fund" and "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " have, as to the types of\par assets included, the meanings given to them in the IMF's publication entitled\par "International Financial Statistics" or such other meanings as shall be\par formally adopted by the IMF from time to time.\par \par "Lien" means any lien, pledge, hypothecation, mortgage, security interest, deed\par of trust, charge or any other encumbrance arising under a security agreement or\par arrangement.\par \par "Public External Indebtedness" means External Indebtedness which (i) is in the\par form of, or represented by, bonds, notes or other securities or any guarantee\par thereof and (ii) is, or may be, quoted, listed or ordinarily purchased and sold\par on any stock exchange, automated trading system or over-the-counter or other\par securities market (including, without prejudice to the generality of the\par foregoing, the market for securities eligible for resale pursuant to Rule 144A\par under the United States Securities Act of 1933, as amended (the "Securities\par Act")).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Each Bond bears interest on its outstanding principal amount from 4 April 2012\par at 5.625 per cent. per annum, payable semi-annually in arrear on 4 April and\par 4 October in each year until maturity, commencing on 4 October 2012 (each, an "\par Interest Payment Date"). Interest will be paid subject to and in accordance\par with the provisions of Condition 7. The period beginning on and including\par 4 April 2012 and ending on but excluding the first Interest Payment Date and\par each successive period beginning on and including an Interest Payment Date and\par ending on but excluding the next successive Interest Payment Date is called an\par "Interest Period".\par \par Each Bond will cease to bear interest from the due date for redemption unless,\par after surrender of such Bond, payment of principal is improperly withheld or\par refused, in which case the outstanding principal amount of such Bond will\par continue to bear interest at the rate specified above (after as well as before\par judgment) until whichever is the earlier of (a) the day on which all sums due\par in respect of such Bond up to that day are received by or on behalf of the\par relevant Bondholder and (b) the day which is seven days after notice has been\par given to the Bondholders that the Fiscal Agent has received all sums due in\par respect of the Bonds up to such seventh day (except, in the case of payment to\par the Fiscal Agent, to the extent that there is any subsequent default in payment\par in accordance with these Conditions).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If interest is required to be calculated for a period of less than a complete Interest Period, it will be calculated on the basis of a year of 360 days consisting of 12 months of 30 days each and, in the case of an incomplete month, the actual number of days elapsed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6 Redemption, Purchase and Cancellation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Redemption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless previously redeemed, or purchased and cancelled, each Bond will be redeemed at its principal amount on 4 April 2042 (the "Maturity Date") subject as provided in Condition 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b)Purchase and Cancellation\par \par The Russian Federation and its affiliates may at any time purchase Bonds in the\par open market or otherwise at any price. Any Bonds so purchased may be cancelled\par or held and resold (provided that such resale is outside the United States, as\par defined in Regulation S under the Securities Act). Any Bonds so cancelled will\par not be reissued.\par \par 7 Payments\par \par (a)Principal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of principal in respect of each Bond will be made by transfer to a\par U.S. dollar account maintained by the Bondholder with a bank in New York City\par or (i) if it does not have such a U.S. dollar account or (ii) if the principal\par amount of Bonds held by such person is less than U.S.$300,000, by a U.S. dollar\par cheque drawn on a bank in New York City mailed to the registered address of the\par Bondholder by uninsured mail at the risk of the Bondholder. Such payment will\par be made only upon presentation and surrender of the relevant Bond Certificate\par at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents and will be rounded\par downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent.\par \par For the purposes of this Condition 7(a) the holder of such Bond will be deemed\par to be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on\par the Register at the Registrar's close of business on the fifteenth day before\par the due date for such payment of principal and the outstanding amount of each\par Bondholder's registered holding will be deemed to be the amount shown as such\par on the Register for such Bondholder at the same time on that date.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payments of interest (other than interest due on redemption) in respect of each\par Bond will be made by U.S. dollar cheque drawn on a bank in New York City and\par mailed to the holder (or to the first-named of joint holders) of such Bond at\par the address appearing in the Register not later than the due date for such\par payment and will be rounded downwards, if necessary, to the nearest cent. For\par the purposes of this Condition 7(b) the holder of such Bond will be deemed to\par be the person shown as the holder (or the first-named of joint holders) on the\par Register on the fifteenth day before the due date for such payment of interest.\par \par Upon application by a Bondholder with respect to Bonds having a principal\par amount of U.S.$300,000 or more to the specified office of the Registrar not\par less than 15 days before the due date for the payment of any interest (other\par than interest due on redemption) in respect of such Bonds, such payment will be\par made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account maintained by the payee with a bank\par in New York City. Any such application for transfer to a U.S. dollar account\par shall be deemed to relate to all future payments of interest (other than\par interest due on redemption) in respect of the Bonds which become payable to the\par Bondholder who has made the initial application until such time as the\par Registrar is notified in writing to the contrary by such Bondholder.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Payment of interest due on redemption will be made in the same manner as payment of the principal of a Bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)Payments Subject to Fiscal Laws\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds are subject in\par all cases to any applicable fiscal or other laws and regulations, but without\par prejudice to the provisions of Condition 8.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)Commissions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No commissions or expenses shall be charged to the Bondholders in respect of any payments of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Payments on Business Days\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Where payment is to be made by transfer to a U.S. dollar account, payment\par instructions (for value the due date or, if that is not a business day, for\par value the first following day which is a business day) will be initiated on the\par due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest due on\par redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate is\par presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Where payment is to be made by cheque, the cheque will be mailed on the due date for payment or, if later, in the case of principal and interest due on redemption, on the business day on which the relevant Bond Certificate is presented and surrendered at the specified office of any of the Paying Agents.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In these Conditions, "business day" means a day (other than a Saturday or\par Sunday) on which commercial banks are open for business in New York City and,\par in the case of the surrender of a Bond Certificate, in the place where the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond\par Certificate is surrendered.\par \par (f)Delay in Payments\par \par Bondholders will not be entitled to any interest or other payment for any delay\par after the due date in receiving any amount due in respect of any Bond as a\par result of (i) the due date not being a business day, (ii) the Bondholder being\par late in presenting or surrendering its Bond Certificate (if required to do so)\par or (iii) a cheque mailed in accordance with this Condition 7 arriving after the\par due date for payment or being lost in the mail.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (g)Partial Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If at any time a partial payment is made in respect of any Bond, the Registrar\par shall endorse the Register with a statement indicating the amount and date\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of\par such payment.\par \par (h)Agents\par \par The initial Agents and their initial specified offices are listed below. Any of\par the Agents may resign in accordance with the provisions of the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement and the Russian Federation reserves the right at any time to vary or\par terminate the appointment of any Agent and appoint additional or other Agents,\par provided that while the Bonds are outstanding it will maintain (i) a Fiscal\par Agent, (ii) a Registrar and (iii) a Paying Agent and a Transfer Agent having a\par specified office in a major European city which will be in London, so long as\par the Bonds are listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London\par Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   so require. Notice of any change in the Agents or their\par specified offices will promptly be given to the Bondholders in accordance with\par Condition 14.\par \par 8 Taxation\par \par All payments of principal and interest in respect of the Bonds by the Russian\par Federation shall be made free and clear of, and without withholding or\par deduction for, any taxes, duties, assessments or governmental charges of\par whatever nature imposed, levied, collected, withheld or assessed by the Russian\par Federation or any political subdivision or any authority thereof or therein\par having power to tax (together, "Taxes"), unless such withholding or deduction\par is required by law. If at any time any Taxes are withheld or deducted, the\par Russian Federation shall increase the payment of principal or interest, as the\par case may be, to such amount as will result in the receipt by the Bondholders of\par such amounts as would have been received by them had no such withholding or\par deduction been required, except that no such increased amount shall be payable:\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, if such holder is liable to such Taxes by reason of having some connection with the Russian Federation other than the mere holding of a Bond or the receipt of payments thereunder;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (b) if the Bond Certificate representing a Bond is presented and surrendered\par for payment more than 30 days after the Relevant Date, except to the extent\par that the holder would have been entitled to such additional amounts on\par presentation and surrender of such Bond Certificate for payment on the last day\par of such period of 30 days;\par \par (c) where such withholding or deduction is imposed on a payment to an\par individual and is required to be made pursuant to European Council Directive\par 2003/48/EC or any other Directive implementing the conclusions of the ECOFIN\par Council meeting of 26-27 November 2000 on the taxation of savings income or any\par law implementing or complying with, or introduced in order to conform to, such\par Directive; or\par \par (d) to a holder, or to a third party on behalf of a holder, who would have been\par able to avoid such withholding or deduction by presenting the relevant Bond\par Certificate representing a Bond to another Paying Agent in a Member State of\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par If, in relation to any payment under the Bonds (a "Payment") any Bondholder has\par received any increased amount as provided in this Condition 8, the Russian\par Federation shall have the right to retain any refund of any amount withheld or\par deducted in respect of such Payment, which refund may be available under a tax\par treaty or otherwise to such Bondholder. Notwithstanding the foregoing, no\par Bondholder makes any representation or warranty that the Russian Federation\par will be entitled to any such refund (or to make any claim in respect thereof)\par and no Bondholder shall incur any obligation with respect thereto (including,\par without limitation, incurring any expense or liability in connection\par therewith).\par \par For the avoidance of doubt, the Russian Federation acknowledges that it shall\par have no right to make any deduction or withholding in respect of (nor will it\par purport to set off any amount equal to) any such refund from any payment in\par respect of any Bond.\par \par In these Conditions, "Relevant Date" means whichever is the later of (i) the\par date on which the payment in question first becomes due and (ii) if the full\par amount payable has not been received in New York City by the Fiscal Agent on or\par prior to such due date, the date on which (the full amount having been so\par received) notice to that effect has been given to the Bondholders.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any reference in these Conditions to principal or interest in respect of the Bonds shall be deemed to include any increased amounts which may be payable under this Condition 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Events of Default\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If any of the following occurs and is continuing (each an "Event of Default") in respect of the Bonds, as applicable:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Non-payment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian Federation fails to pay any amount of principal or interest in respect of the Bonds, as the case may be, when due and such failure continues for a period of 30 calendar days; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Breach of other obligations or undertakings\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation defaults in performance or observance of or compliance\par with any of its other obligations or undertakings in respect of the Bonds, as\par the case may be, which default (if capable of remedy) is not remedied within\par 60 days after written notice of such default shall have been given to the\par Russian Federation by any holder of the Bonds, as the case may be, it being\par understood that a default in respect of the undertaking set forth in\par Condition 4(a) shall be deemed capable of remedy for purposes hereof; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)Cross-acceleration\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any Public External Indebtedness shall become due and payable prior to the\par stated maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the debtor following a\par default or the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian Federation or\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall fail to make the final payment of principal in\par respect of any Public External Indebtedness on the date on which such final\par payment is due and payable or at the expiration of any grace period originally\par applicable thereto or, in the case of any guarantee which constitutes Public\par External Indebtedness, the underlying obligation in respect of which such\par guarantee has been given shall have become due and payable prior to the stated\par maturity thereof otherwise than at the option of the debtor following a default\par or the debtor shall have failed to make the final payment of principal in\par respect of such underlying obligation on the date on which such final payment\par is due and payable or at the expiration of any grace period originally\par applicable thereto and the guarantee shall not be honoured when due and called\par upon; provided that the aggregate amount of the relevant Public External\par Indebtedness in respect of which one or more of the events mentioned in this\par Condition 9(c) shall have occurred equals or exceeds U.S.$75,000,000 (or its\par equivalent in any other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ); and provided, further, that\par any secured Public External Indebtedness that by its terms is fully\par non-recourse to the Russian Federation, the Government of the Russian\par Federation or the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shall not be counted as Public External\par Indebtedness for purposes of this Condition 9(c); or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)Moratorium\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A moratorium on the payment of principal of, or interest on, all or any part of Public External Indebtedness; or\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (e)Unlawfulness or Invalidity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The validity of the Bonds is contested by the Russian Federation or any agency\par or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation or the Russian Federation\par or any agency or entity acting on behalf of the Russian Federation shall deny\par any of the Russian Federation's obligations under the Bonds or it is or will\par become unlawful for the Russian Federation to perform or comply with any of its\par obligations under or in respect of the Bonds or under or in respect of the\par Fiscal Agency Agreement or any of such obligations shall be or become\par unenforceable or invalid; or\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (f)Consents etc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Any regulation, decree, consent, approval, licence or other authority necessary\par to enable the Russian Federation to enter into or perform its obligations under\par the Bonds or under the Fiscal Agency Agreement or for the validity or\par enforceability thereof shall expire or be withheld, revoked or terminated or\par otherwise be void or cease to remain in full force and effect or shall be\par modified in a manner which adversely affects any rights or claims of any of the\par holders of the Bonds, as the case may be,\par \par then holders of 25 per cent. or more in aggregate outstanding principal amount\par of the Bonds may declare the Bonds to be immediately due and payable whereupon\par the Bonds shall become immediately due and payable at their principal amount,\par together with accrued interest, without any further formality.\par \par If the Russian Federation receives notice in writing from holders of at least\par 50 per cent. in aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds to the\par effect that the Event of Default or Events of Default giving rise to any above\par mentioned declaration of acceleration is or are cured following any such\par declaration and that such holders wish the relevant declaration to be\par withdrawn, the Russian Federation shall give notice thereof to the Bondholders\par (with a copy to the Fiscal Agent), whereupon the relevant declaration shall be\par withdrawn and shall have no further effect, but without prejudice to any rights\par or obligations which may have arisen before the Russian Federation gives such\par notice (whether pursuant to these Conditions or otherwise). No such withdrawal\par shall affect any other or any subsequent Event of Default or any right of any\par Bondholder in relation thereto.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Prescription\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Claims against the Russian Federation in respect of principal and interest shall become void unless made within a period of three years from the date following the Maturity Date or a relevant Interest Payment Date, as applicable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11 Replacement of Bond Certificates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par If any Bond Certificate is lost, stolen, mutilated, defaced or destroyed, it\par may be replaced at the specified office of the Registrar or the Transfer Agent\par with its specified office in London subject to all applicable laws and stock\par exchange requirements, upon payment by the claimant of the expenses incurred in\par connection with such replacement and on such terms as to evidence, security,\par indemnity and otherwise as the Russian Federation may reasonably require.\par Mutilated or defaced Bond Certificates must be surrendered before replacements\par will be issued.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions; Modification and Waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)Meetings of Bondholders, Written Resolutions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions for convening meetings of\par Bondholders to consider any matter affecting their interests, including the\par modification by Extraordinary Resolution of these Conditions. The quorum at any\par such meeting for passing an Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as provided\par in the Fiscal Agency Agreement in the event that all outstanding Bonds are held\par by one person) be two or more persons holding or representing a clear majority\par of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting\par that is adjourned two or more persons being or representing Bondholders\par (whatever the principal amount of the outstanding Bonds so held or\par represented), and the majority for passing an Extraordinary Resolution at any\par such meeting shall be at least 662/3 per cent. of the votes cast except that at\par any meeting the business of which includes consideration of proposals on\par Reserved Matters, including, inter alia, (i) to modify the maturity of the\par Bonds, (ii) to reduce or cancel the principal amount of, or interest on, the\par Bonds, (iii) to change the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of payment of the Bonds, (iv) to modify the\par provisions concerning the quorum required at any meeting of Bondholders or the\par majority required to pass an Extraordinary Resolution or (v) to modify the\par percentage required to pass any resolution, the necessary quorum for passing an\par Extraordinary Resolution shall (subject as provided in the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement in the event that all outstanding Bonds are held by one person) be\par two or more persons holding or representing at least 75 per cent. of the\par aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, or at any meeting that is\par adjourned at least 25 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the\par outstanding Bonds, and the majority for passing an Extraordinary Resolution on\par a Reserved Matter at any such meeting shall be at least 75 per cent. of the\par votes cast. An Extraordinary Resolution duly passed at any meeting of\par Bondholders will be binding on all Bondholders whether or not they are present\par at the meeting.\par \par In addition, the Fiscal Agency Agreement contains provisions relating to\par Written Resolutions. A "Written Resolution" means a resolution in writing\par signed by or on behalf of holders of at least 75 per cent. of the aggregate\par principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in the case of a Reserved Matter, or\par 662/3 per cent. of the aggregate principal amount of the outstanding Bonds, in\par the case of a matter other than a Reserved Matter. Any Written Resolution may\par be contained in one document or several documents in like form, each signed by\par or on behalf of one or more Bondholders. Any Written Resolution shall be\par binding on all of the Bondholders, whether or not signed by them.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)Modification and waiver\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The parties to the Fiscal Agency Agreement may agree, without the consent of\par the Bondholders, to any modification of any provision of the Fiscal Agency\par Agreement or the Bonds which is of a formal, minor or technical nature or is\par made to correct a manifest error.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 Further Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation shall be at liberty from time to time, without the\par consent of the Bondholders, to create and issue further Bonds ranking equally\par in all respects (or in all respects save for payments made prior to the\par issuance of such further Bonds and, if applicable, the date and amount of the\par first payment on such further Bonds) so that the same shall be consolidated and\par form a single series with the Bonds, provided that such further issuance\par constitutes a qualified reopening for U.S. federal income tax purposes.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Notices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Notices to the Bondholders will be sent to them by mail (airmail if overseas)\par at their respective addresses on the Register, and will be deemed to have been\par given on the fourth day after the date of mailing. So long as the Bonds are\par listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par so require, notices with respect to the Bonds will also be published in a\par leading newspaper having general circulation in the United Kingdom (which is\par expected to be The Financial Times) or, if such publication is not practicable,\par in an English language newspaper having general circulation in Europe, and each\par such notice shall be deemed to have been given on the date of such publication,\par or if published more than once or on different dates, on the first date on\par which publication is made.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Indemnity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The U.S. dollar is the sole \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of account and payment for all sums\par payable by the Russian Federation under or in connection with the Bonds,\par including damages. Any amount received or recovered in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than\par the U.S. dollar (whether as a result of, or the enforcement of, a judgement or\par order of a court of any jurisdiction or otherwise) by any Bondholder in respect\par of any sum expressed to be due to it from the Russian Federation shall only\par constitute a discharge to the Russian Federation to the extent of the U.S.\par dollar amount which the recipient is able to purchase with the amount so\par received or recovered in that other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the date of that receipt or\par recovery (or, if it is not practicable to make that purchase on that date, on\par the first date on which it is practicable to do so). If that U.S. dollar amount\par is less than the U.S. dollar amount expressed to be due to the recipient under\par any Bond, the Russian Federation shall indemnify such recipient against any\par loss sustained by it as a result. In any event, the Russian Federation shall\par indemnify the recipient against the cost of making any such purchase. These\par indemnities constitute separate and independent obligations from the Russian\par Federation's other obligations, shall give rise to a separate and independent\par cause of action, shall apply irrespective of any indulgence granted by any\par Bondholder and shall continue in full force and effect despite any judgement,\par order, claim or proof for a liquidated amount in respect of any sum due under\par any Bond or any judgement or order.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No person shall have any right to enforce any term or condition of the Bonds under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Governing Law\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bonds and the Fiscal Agency Agreement and any non-contractual obligations\par arising out of or in connection with them shall be governed by and construed in\par accordance with the laws of England.\par \par                                 USE OF PROCEEDS                                \par \par The proceeds of the issue of the 2017 Bonds (net of commissions but before\par deduction of expenses) are expected to amount to approximately\par U.S.$1,991,740,000, the proceeds of the issue of the 2022 Bonds (net of\par commissions but before deduction of expenses) are expected to amount to\par approximately U.S.$1,984,140,000 and the proceeds of the issue of the 2042\par Bonds (net of commissions but before deduction of expenses) are expected to\par amount to approximately U.S.$2,924,490,000. The proceeds of each issue will be\par used for general governmental purposes. Total commissions and expenses payable\par by the Russian Federation in connection with the 2017 Bonds are expected to be\par approximately U.S.$1,400,000, total commissions and expenses payable by the\par Russian Federation in connection with the 2022 Bonds are expected to be\par approximately U.S.$1,400,000 and total commissions and expenses payable by the\par Russian Federation in connection with the 2042 Bonds are expected to be\par approximately U.S.$2,100,000.\par \par                               RUSSIAN FEDERATION                               \par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Territory, Population and Natural Resources\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian Federation is a sovereign and democratic federal state, consisting\par of 83 sub-federal political units. Russia is the largest country in the world\par by land mass, covering 17.1 million square kilometres. Russia borders 16\par countries and spans nine time zones, extending some 9,000 kilometres from the\par Baltic Sea in the west to the Pacific Ocean in the east and some 4,000\par kilometres from its southern border on the Black and Caspian Seas to its\par northern limits on the Arctic Ocean.\par \par Russia is rich in natural resources. It is the world's second-largest producer\par of natural gas, the world's leading producer of oil and a significant producer\par of coal, uranium, nickel, palladium and platinum. As of 1 January 2009,\par Russia's A, B and C1 natural gas reserves (which include explored reserves\par considered to be fully extractable pursuant to the Russian reserves system)\par were estimated at 47.8 trillion cubic metres. The country also has substantial\par gold deposits (located mainly in Eastern Siberia and the Russian Far East) as\par well as significant deposits of zinc, lead, tin, silver, other rare metals and\par precious and semiprecious stones. Russia is among the world's leading producers\par of electricity, steel, fertilisers, cotton textiles and other goods. Forests\par cover approximately 52% of Russia's total land area, and Russia's timber\par reserves, the largest of any country, are estimated at 83.3 billion cubic\par metres.\par \par Russia is home to approximately 143 million people, ranks ninth in the world by\par population and has a population density of approximately 8.3 persons per square\par kilometre. Roughly 79% of the population lives in European Russia, and\par approximately the same percentage of Russia's population lives in cities.\par Russia's capital and largest city is Moscow with 11.5 million inhabitants, and\par 11 other Russian cities have a population of more than one million residents.\par \par According to the preliminary results of the 2010 general census, ethnic\par Russians are the largest demographic group and account for approximately 81% of\par the total population. Other ethnic groups include Tatars (approximately 3.9% of\par the total population), Ukrainians (1.4%), Bashkirs (1.2%), Chuvashs (1.1%) and\par Chechens (1%). No other ethnic group accounts for more than 1% of Russia's\par total population.\par \par Russia has a well-developed system of education, with a literacy rate exceeding\par 99%, and grammar and middle school education is compulsory. Russia had\par approximately 1,115 institutions of higher education, with approximately\par 7.4 million students, during the 2009-10 educational year, and an estimated\par labour force of 75.4 million people in December 2009. As of year-end 2010, over\par 99% of the labour force had at least eight years of schooling, 95% had at least\par ten years, 28.9% had received higher education and 45.9% had received\par professional training.\par \par Political System\par \par Constitution\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Constitution of Russia provides for a tripartite governmental structure in\par which the power of the state is divided among the executive, legislative and\par judicial branches, each independent of the others. The Constitution also\par establishes a federal system, allocating responsibilities between federal,\par sub-federal and local authorities.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Constitution protects certain fundamental "rights and freedoms of the person and the citizen," and charges the state with guaranteeing the equal treatment of people of all races, nationalities and beliefs. Under the Russian Constitution all forms of property (including private property) are equal before the law, and ideological diversity and a multi-party system are expressly recognised.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In general, the Constitution may be amended through passage of a special\par federal constitutional law, and its ratification by the legislatures of at\par least two-thirds of the Federation subjects. See "-Federal Structure and\par Regional Issues" for more information on Federation subjects. Passage of such a\par law requires the vote of a two-thirds majority of the State Duma, a\par three-fourths majority of the Federation Council (the upper house of the\par Federal Assembly) and signature by the President. The provisions of the\par Constitution that govern the nature of the constitutional system, individual\par rights of citizens and the amendment process, however, can be changed only by\par convening a Constitutional Assembly. A proposed new constitution may be adopted\par either by the vote of a two-thirds majority of the Constitutional Assembly or\par by a simple majority in a national referendum in which more than half of the\par eligible voters participate.\par \par President\par \par The President of the Russian Federation is currently Dmitry Medvedev, who was\par elected President in March 2008, receiving 70.28% of the vote. On 4 March 2012,\par Vladimir Putin was elected to be the next President of Russia, receiving 63.60%\par of the vote. Mr Putin will assume the office of President on 7 May 2012.\par Mr. Putin has already served two terms as President and was acting President\par between January 2000, following Boris Yeltsin's resignation, and March 2000. In\par March 2000, Mr. Putin was elected to his first term in office as President,\par receiving approximately 53% of the vote, and in March 2004 Mr. Putin was\par re-elected with approximately 71% of the vote. From May 2008, Mr. Putin was\par Prime Minister under the presidency of Mr. Dmitry Medvedev. Mr. Putin will\par cease to hold that office when he assumes the office of President. See\par "-Political Parties, Recent Elections and Political Developments."\par \par The President is the Head of State and the Supreme Commander of the Armed\par Forces. The President has broad powers, including the authority to declare a\par state of emergency, subject to approval of the Federation Council, the ability\par to commence military engagements, subject to immediate notification of the\par State Duma and Federation Council, the power to issue decrees and orders that\par have the force of law (although these may not contravene the Constitution or\par federal legislation), to suspend acts of sub-federal and local executive\par authorities, and to call national referenda. In addition, the President is\par empowered to arbitrate disputes between the federal authorities and the\par Federation subjects. The President is also responsible for foreign policy.\par \par The President has the power to veto bills passed by the Federal Assembly and,\par under certain circumstances, to dissolve the State Duma. The President may\par dissolve the State Duma if (i) the State Duma fails to accept the President's\par proposed candidate for Prime Minister in three successive votes, (ii) the State\par Duma twice within three months passes a motion of no confidence in the\par Government or (iii) the Government loses a confidence motion put before the\par State Duma by the Prime Minister. The State Duma may not, however, be dissolved\par at any time during the last six months of a presidential term, during the\par period between passage by the State Duma of an accusation initiating\par impeachment proceedings against the President and action by the Federation\par Council on such accusation, while a state of emergency covering all of Russia\par is in effect, or, in the case of (ii) or (iii) above, within the first year\par after State Duma elections. In the event the State Duma is dissolved, the\par President must schedule elections and a newly elected State Duma must be\par convened within four months.\par \par The President has significant appointment powers, including the power to\par appoint the Prime Minister (with the consent of the State Duma) and the other\par members of the Government (upon the nomination of the Prime Minister). The\par President may also dismiss deputy prime ministers and federal ministers at any\par time and may dismiss the Prime Minister, which would simultaneously lead to a\par dismissal of the Government. In addition, the President nominates candidates\par for governor of the Bank of Russia (for appointment by the State Duma),\par Prosecutor General (for appointment by the Federation Council) and judges to\par the Constitutional Court, the Supreme Court and the Supreme Arbitration Court\par (for appointment by the Federation Council). The President also has the power\par to dismiss the legislative and executive authorities of Federation subjects\par under certain circumstances. Beginning in December 2004, the President's powers\par were expanded to include nominating the heads of Federation subjects, who are\par now appointed by the legislatures of the respective Federation subjects. See\par "-Federal Structure and Regional Issues."\par \par The President is elected in a national election for a term of six years. Under\par Russia's Constitution, the President may not serve more than two consecutive\par terms. The Constitution also provides for the early termination of the\par President's term of office in the event of the President's death, resignation,\par impeachment, or persistent inability to exercise his powers for health reasons.\par New presidential elections must be held within three months of an early\par termination. Impeachment of the President requires an accusation supported by\par the vote of a two-thirds majority of the State Duma, followed by a vote in\par favour of impeachment by a two-thirds majority of the Federation Council, with\par the subsequent confirmation by the Supreme Court of the legality of the\par accusation and by the Constitutional Court of the observance of due process.\par Under the Constitution, whenever the President is incapable for any reason of\par carrying out his duties, the obligations of the office are temporarily assumed\par by the Prime Minister, except that the Prime Minister, as acting President, may\par not dissolve the State Duma or propose any national referendum or changes to\par the Constitution.\par \par Government\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government consists of the Prime Minister, deputy prime ministers and federal ministers, all of whom are appointed by the President as described above. The Government is automatically dissolved after each presidential election in order to permit the President to form a new Government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government is responsible for implementing federal laws, presidential decrees and international agreements. In particular, the Government is responsible for preparing and implementing the federal budget, establishing a unified financial, credit and monetary policy, carrying out social policy, preserving public order and defending the rights and freedoms of citizens.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Prime Minister of the Russian Federation is currently Vladimir Putin, the incoming President of Russia, who took office in May 2008 following his nomination by President Medvedev.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Legislative Branch\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The legislative branch consists of the Federal Assembly, which comprises a lower chamber, known as the State Duma, and an upper chamber, known as the Federation Council.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The State Duma consists of 450 deputies, elected to a five-year term by a\par system of proportional representation. In accordance with the Federal Law on\par the Elections of Deputies of the State Duma, which became effective in December\par 2006, deputies are chosen from "party lists" on the basis of a nationwide\par election, with seats allocated in proportion to the number of votes received by\par the party, if the party receives 7% or more of the vote (5% or more of the vote\par beginning with the December 2016 elections). Currently, parties that garner\par between 6% and 7% of the vote are allocated two seats in the State Duma, and\par parties that collect between 5% and 6% of the vote are allocated one seat in\par the State Duma. Deputies are not able to change party affiliation during their\par term of office without surrendering their seat. No person may simultaneously\par serve as a State Duma deputy and hold a position in the Government.\par \par The Federation Council, with 166 deputies, represents Russia's 83 Federation\par subjects. See "-Federal Structure and Regional Issues." Each Federation subject\par appoints two members of the Federation Council, one chosen by the legislative\par body of the Federation subject and the other by its executive body. The members\par of the Federation Council have to work on a full-time basis and cannot occupy\par any other office.\par \par For a bill to become federal law, it must first be passed by a majority vote in\par the State Duma, then be approved by a majority vote in the Federation Council\par and finally be signed by the President. Rejection of a bill by the Federation\par Council can be overridden by a two-thirds majority vote in the State Duma.\par Rejection of a bill by the President can be overridden by a two-thirds majority\par of each of the Federation Council and the State Duma.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Judicial Branch\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has three courts of final appeal. The Constitutional Court has\par jurisdiction over matters relating to the interpretation of the Constitution,\par including the constitutionality of federal laws, decrees of the President,\par resolutions of the Government, resolutions of the State Duma and the Federation\par Council, laws and legal documents of Federation subjects and agreements between\par federal and sub-federal authorities. The Supreme Arbitration Court and lower\par arbitration courts have jurisdiction over economic disputes. Judicial authority\par is otherwise vested in the Supreme Court and lower courts of general\par jurisdiction over civil, criminal, administrative and other matters.\par \par Judges of the Constitutional Court, Supreme Arbitration Court and Supreme Court\par are nominated by the President and appointed by the Federation Council. Judges\par of lower federal courts are appointed by the President in accordance with\par procedures established by federal legislation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Political Parties, Recent Elections and Political Developments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under the Constitution and federal law, Russian citizens who are at least 18 years old have the right to vote in presidential and State Duma elections, regional and local elections and regional and local referenda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russian legislation contains a number of provisions designed to encourage the\par development of a stable party system. First, candidates must collect a\par specified number of signatures to qualify for elections. In the case of\par presidential elections, a candidate must collect at least two million\par signatures of the electorate. In order to participate in elections to the State\par Duma, a political party must also obtain a specified number of signatures\par supporting its party-list (150,000 signatures for the December 2011 elections,\par and 120,000 signatures for elections thereafter), unless such party has seats\par in the current State Duma, in which case the requirement to collect signatures\par does not apply. Second, political parties must garner at least 7% (5% beginning\par with the next State Duma elections in December 2016) of the vote in party-list\par elections to qualify for seats allocated on the basis of proportional\par representation in the State Duma, though parties are currently awarded one seat\par for collecting between 5% and 6% of the vote and two seats for collecting\par between 6% and 7% of the vote. See "-Political System-Legislative Branch."\par Third, the rules for presidential elections, which call for a run-off election\par between the first and second place candidates if no candidate wins more than\par 50% in the initial round of voting, discourage fragmentation of the vote.\par \par In the most recent State Duma election, held in December 2011, the party\par Edinaya Rossiya (United Russia), which is currently led by Prime Minister Putin\par and is the product of the 2001 merger of two large parties, Yedinstvo (Unity)\par and Otechestvo-Vsya Rossiya (Fatherland-All Russia), received 49.3% of the\par votes cast, down from 64.3% of the votes cast in December 2007, and currently\par holds 238 State Duma seats. As a result of the election, United Russia lost the\par two-thirds constitutional majority it had held in the State Duma since 2007.\par The Communist Party received 19.2% of the votes cast in 2011 and now holds 92\par State Duma seats. Two other parties are also represented in the State Duma: the\par Liberal Democratic Party (11.7% of the votes cast and a total of 56 seats) and\par Fair Russia (13.2% of the votes cast and a total of 64 seats). Parties that\par received votes in the December 2011 election, but did not pass the 7% threshold\par required to seat members in the State Duma, include the Russian Democratic\par Party "Yabloko" (3.4% of the votes cast) and several other parties, none of\par which received more than 1% of the vote. Currently, the Ministry of Justice has\par registered seven political parties.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the December 2011 State Duma elections, several large-scale demonstrations have taken place across Russia, precipitated in large part by allegations of voting irregularities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Structure and Regional Issues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia consists of 83 Federation subjects, comprising 21 republics, nine krais,\par 46 oblasts, two cities of federal importance (Moscow and St. Petersburg), one\par autonomous oblast and four autonomous okrugs.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many of the republics and autonomous regions contain a substantial number of ethnic non-Russians, with non-Russians constituting a majority in certain regions (e.g., the Republic of Tatarstan and the Republic of Kalmykia).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Federation subjects form the intermediate level of government, and are further\par subdivided into approximately 23,500 municipalities, comprising cities or\par geographically separated county territories (raions). Lower-level governments\par at the county (raion), city and village levels are subordinate to their\par respective Federation subjects.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Throughout its history, Russia has generally been a unitary state with a single central government. Since the dissolution of the Soviet Union, however, Federation subjects have obtained greater decision-making authority and financial autonomy, and Russia is now a federal state.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Federation Treaty of 31 March 1992, signed by 87 Federation subjects (all\par but the Chechen Republic and the Republic of Tatarstan), initially granted each\par Federation subject a measure of control over budgetary and external policy, as\par well as local natural resources. The Constitution subsequently refined the\par division of authority between Russia and the Federation subjects, expressly\par incorporating the provisions of the Federation Treaty to the extent they were\par not inconsistent with other provisions of the Constitution. Certain areas of\par governance are reserved by the Constitution exclusively to the federal\par authorities, including management of federal property, the issuance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulation, customs policy, foreign relations, military defence,\par atomic energy and space exploration. Joint federal-regional authority is\par prescribed in a number of other areas, including tax administration, ownership\par and use of land and natural resources, environmental protection, social\par assistance, education, health, science and cultural facilities, and the\par selection of certain court and law enforcement officials. Responsibility for\par all matters not reserved to the federal authorities or to joint\par federal-regional competence is reserved for Federation subjects. The\par Constitution prohibits any sub-federal barriers to the free movement of goods,\par capital and labour throughout Russia.\par \par In May 2000, President Putin signed a presidential decree creating seven\par federal circuits, each comprising several Federation subjects, and authorising\par the President to appoint a representative to each federal circuit. The\par representatives' responsibilities include facilitating the implementation of\par the President's domestic and foreign policies, supervising the implementation\par of federal decisions in the Federation subjects and reporting to the President\par on the state of affairs in the Federation subjects. In January 2010, pursuant\par to Presidential decree, a separate North Caucasus federal circuit was created,\par bringing the total number of federal circuits to eight.\par \par In August 2000, a law aimed at improving management of the Federation subjects\par and lower-level authorities came into effect. The law grants the President the\par power to dismiss the chief legislative and executive authorities of a\par Federation subject if the authorities refuse to repeal local legislation held\par by a court to be contrary to the Constitution or federal law and harmful to the\par fundamental rights and freedoms of Russian citizens or that prevent federal\par authorities from performing their statutory functions.\par \par A new law abolishing the direct election of executives of the Federation\par subjects was adopted in December 2004. Under this law, the President may\par nominate and dismiss the executives of a Federation subject, but Presidential\par nominations must still be approved by the Federation subject's legislative\par body. This law was intended to strengthen central control of the country and\par facilitate the Government's efforts in combating domestic terrorism.\par \par In general, disputes between the federal authorities and Federation subjects\par have been resolved peacefully through the political process, with the notable\par exception of the military confrontation in the Chechen Republic and periodic\par unrest in the other republics of the North Caucasus, including Daghestan and\par Ingushetia.\par \par International Relations\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's Position in the International Community\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia has been recognised by the international community as the successor to\par the Soviet Union. Russia is currently a member of many international\par organisations, including the United Nations, where it is a permanent member of\par the Security Council and, accordingly, plays an active role in maintaining\par international peace and security.\par \par Russia is a member of the G8 group of industrial nations, having chaired that\par group in 2006, and the G20. Since 1992, Russia has been a member of the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("IMF") and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Bank for\par Reconstruction and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 "). See "Public Debt and Related\par Matters-Relations with International Financial Institutions." Russia is also a\par member of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Finance Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2   and participates as a donor in\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Development Association\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In December 1996, the Bank of Russia\par became a member of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and in September\par 1997 Russia became a member of the Paris Club of creditor nations.\par \par In June 1993, Russia applied for membership in the General Agreement on Tariffs\par and Trade, which later became the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Trade Organisation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "WTO"), and in\par December 2011 concluded all accession agreements required for membership in the\par WTO. Formal membership in the WTO is subject to ratification by the State Duma,\par which should take place within 220 days from 16 December 2011.\par \par In 1994, Russia concluded a Declaration on Cooperation with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organisation\par for Economic Cooperation and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 "), and, in May 1996, Russia\par formally applied for membership in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In 2007, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  on a ministerial\par level made the decision to invite Russia to begin negotiations regarding its\par accession. Such negotiations are currently underway. Russia is currently\par participating on a permanent basis in the activities of more than 20 working\par groups of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par In 1997, President Yeltsin and the leaders of 16 NATO nations signed the\par "Founding Act on Mutual Cooperation," establishing new principles to govern\par their relationship and providing for consultation between Russia and NATO. In\par December 2001, Russia and NATO agreed upon a process aimed at enhancing future\par cooperation. In May 2002, a NATO-Russia Council was established, under which\par more than 20 working groups have been created to enhance political contacts and\par develop cooperation between Russia and NATO members on issues of mutual\par interest, including terrorism, crisis management, missile defence, arms control\par and civil defence.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia and Regional Cooperation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia has wide-ranging contacts with the EU, its most significant economic\par partner and one of its most important political partners. The Agreement on\par Partnership and Cooperation between Russia and the EU, in effect since December\par 1997, was amended in April 2004 and March 2008 to allow new EU members to\par accede to it. This agreement establishes a framework for the parties' economic\par and political relationship.\par \par On 15 June 2001, Russia, China, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and\par Uzbekistan signed a declaration on the creation of the Shanghai Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par Organisation (SCO). Russia chaired the SCO in 2008-2009. Russia is a member of\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Bank for Reconstruction and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "\f28 \uc2 \f2 EBRD\f28 \uc2 \f2 ") and the Black\par Sea Bank for Trade and Development. Russia also participates in a number of\par other regional cooperation forums, including the Association of South-East\par Asian Nations (ASEAN).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia, the Former Soviet Union and the CIS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par After the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Russia concluded separate agreements\par ("zero-option agreements") with all of the other republics of the former Soviet\par Union except the three Baltic republics. All of the zero-option agreements have\par been mutually ratified, except the agreement with Ukraine, which has not yet\par been ratified by Ukraine. Each zero-option agreement provides that, as between\par Russia and the other former Soviet republic, Russia is responsible for\par virtually all the external debt contracted on behalf of the authorities of the\par former Soviet Union and receives in return all claims on former Soviet Union\par assets located outside the territory of the other former Soviet republic. The\par Government regularised its relations with virtually all the external creditors\par of the former Soviet Union and agreed to a schedule for the repayment of former\par Soviet Union debt.\par \par In April 1996, the Government entered into an agreement rescheduling\par approximately U.S.$33 billion of indebtedness to the Paris Club of official\par creditors. In August 1999, the Government concluded a further rescheduling\par agreement providing for the deferral of approximately U.S.$8.3 billion of\par indebtedness to Paris Club creditors. In the summer of 2005, Russia prepaid\par U.S.$15 billion of its Paris Club indebtedness. In August 2006, Russia prepaid\par the remaining balance of approximately U.S.$21.6 billion owed to its Paris Club\par creditors.\par \par In December 1997, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   restructured the Soviet-era debt owed to the\par London Club of commercial creditors. In the wake of the events of 17 August\par 1998, when the Government announced that it would not meet its obligations\par under its short-term rouble-denominated treasury bills, i.e., GKOs and OFZs,\par Russia, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   and a committee of London Club creditors agreed, in\par February 2000, on terms for the further restructuring of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's\par London Club debt. The agreed restructuring terms have been implemented through\par a series of exchange offers. In December 2002, December 2006 and December 2009,\par Russia restructured most of the Soviet-era debt owed to uninsured trade\par creditors, and in March 2004 Russia restructured most of the Soviet-era debts\par owed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  for Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par ("IBEC") and to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Investment Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2   ("IIB"). See "Public Debt and\par Related Matters-External Debt Restructuring" for a further discussion of\par Russia's indebtedness to the Paris Club, the London Club, Soviet-era uninsured\par trade creditors, IBEC and the IIB.\par \par Russia is a member of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth of Independent States\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "CIS"), which\par was founded in December 1991 to promote mutual cooperation among its members.\par Currently, 11 of the 15 former Soviet Republics are members of the CIS. The\par three Baltic states never joined the organisation, and Georgia withdrew from\par the organisation, effective August 2009.\par \par In 1995, Russia, Belarus and Kazakhstan signed a framework agreement to form a\par customs union. Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan acceded to this agreement in 1996 and\par 1999, respectively. In October 2007, Russia, Belarus and Kazakhstan signed a\par tripartite agreement on the creation of a customs union, which entered into\par force in October 2008. In November 2009, Russia, Belarus and Kazakhstan\par concluded several agreements that unified customs tariff and non-tariff\par regulations among the three nations effective 1 January 2010 and established a\par Customs Code to harmonise customs procedures effective July 2010. The process\par of finalising customs integration among the three parties is expected to\par continue through 2012. See "The Russian Economy-Civil and Commercial Law-" for\par a discussion of the Customs Code. Effective 1 January 2012, a Common Economic\par Space among Russia, Belarus and Kazakhstan went into effect. The commission of\par the Common Economic Space is expected to begin work on 1 July 2012 and to\par assume the responsibilities currently handled by the commission of the customs\par union.\par \par Since 1995, Russia and Belarus have pursued closer economic and political\par integration. In December 1999, Russia and Belarus signed a treaty on the\par creation of a unified state and a programme for its implementation. Under the\par treaty, Russia and Belarus are to preserve their independence and sovereignty,\par while developing a single economic space, agreed social policies and\par coordinated foreign and defence policies. In November 2011, Russia, Belarus and\par Kazakhstan signed the Declaration on the Establishment of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurasian Economic\par Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which calls for the creation of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurasian Economic Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  by 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia is also a member of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Collective Security Treaty Organisation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , founded in 2002 on the basis of the 1992 Collective Security Treaty, to promote collective security among its members, which include Armenia, Belarus, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Uzbekistan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In August 2008, Russia and Georgia fought a brief war over the territories of\par South Ossetia and Abkhazia. Both territories are now de facto independent from\par Georgia and have been legally recognised by Russia, Nicaragua, Venezuela and\par the Pacific island states of Nauru and Tuvalu. In the aftermath of the August\par 2008 hostilities, Georgia severed diplomatic relations with Russia, and Russian\par troops are currently stationed in both South Ossetia and Abkhazia.\par \par On several occasions since the collapse of the Soviet Union, disputes have\par arisen with Ukraine concerning the supply and transit of natural gas and with\par Belarus concerning the supply and transit of both natural gas and oil. Russia\par is dependent on pipelines in Ukraine and Belarus to deliver a significant\par portion of the natural gas it exports to western Europe (Russia supplies\par approximately one-quarter of the natural gas consumed in the EU), and Ukraine\par and Belarus are, in turn, dependent on Russian gas to meet their domestic\par requirements. In 2006, Russia and Belarus reached agreement on the supply of\par Russian gas to Belarus through 2011, and in 2011 they reached a similar\par agreement for the period 2012-2014. In addition, at the end of 2011,\par State-controlled \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   purchased a 50% stake in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Beltransgaz\f28 \uc2 \f2  , making it the\par sole shareholder in Belarus' principle gas transportation company. On\par 19 January 2009, following a dispute regarding Russian gas supplies to Ukraine,\par Russia and Ukraine agreed to a 10-year transit and supply contract. Under both\par of these agreements, prices for gas supplies are set pursuant to a European\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 pricing formula.\par \par                               THE RUSSIAN ECONOMY                              \par \par Overview\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's initial economic reform programme, first outlined by President Yeltsin in October 1991, set out to achieve three principal goals: liberalisation, privatisation and financial stabilisation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Liberalisation of the Russian economy required a fundamental redefinition of\par the role of the state, which had in the Soviet era regulated virtually all\par economic and financial activities. The subsequent liberalisation of most prices\par has permitted market forces to determine prices to a significant extent and to\par play an increased role in the allocation of resources and output. At present,\par only certain public services and legislatively defined "natural monopolies"\par (for example, pipeline transport of oil and gas, electricity transmission and\par production, railway transport, terminal, port and airport services, and postal\par services) are subject to price regulation. Internationally, liberalisation of\par Russia's trade regime has helped redirect trade flows away from historically\par important former socialist markets towards \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries and developing\par nations and contributed to an overall rise in exports. See "Balance of Payments\par and Foreign Trade-Foreign Trade."\par \par Beginning in January 1992, the Government implemented a privatisation\par programme, made possible by the legal recognition of private property and the\par creation of an economic and legal environment more conducive to the development\par of private economic activity.\par \par Russia's economic programme came under severe pressure in the second half of\par 1997 and the first half of 1998, following the Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial\par crisis and a sharp fall in world prices for oil and other commodities, which\par adversely affected the Government's ability to continue to finance its budget\par deficit and to maintain the value of the rouble against the dollar. In 1999,\par Russia was assigned sovereign credit ratings of CCC (Fitch), B3 (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) and\par CCC\u65293\'FF\'0D (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ). Russia's current foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit\par ratings are Baa1 (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ), BBB (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) and BBB (Fitch), each with\par a stable outlook.\par \par In 2004, the responsibilities of federal Governmental structures were reformed.\par A new three-tiered system of federal executive bodies was established, which\par assigned responsibility to federal ministries for the elaboration of state\par policy and legal regulation, to federal services for the supervision, control\par and protection of state security and state borders and to federal agencies for\par the provision of public services and the management of state property and law\par enforcement (other than supervision and control).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In January 2006, the Government adopted a Medium-Term Programme for the period 2006-2008. The principal priorities of the program were improving the investment climate, diversifying the economy, modernising the industrial sector, reducing Government involvement in the economy, stimulating Russian innovation, developing the housing and mortgage markets and improving the quality of the healthcare and education systems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In November 2008, the Government adopted a Long-Term Programme for Social and\par Economic Development through 2020 (the "Development Programme through 2020"),\par the primary objectives of which are to stimulate Russian innovation, including\par in high-technology industries, diversify the economy, promote regional economic\par development and reform the country's natural monopolies.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Anti-Crisis Measures\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In response to the global financial crisis that began in the fall of 2008,\par Russia enacted a set of measures designed to restore investor confidence and\par support the medium-term economic growth of the country. Many of these measures\par were aimed at bolstering the liquidity of the Russian banking sector and\par facilitating the flow of credit within the economy. See "Monetary and Financial\par System-Banking-Anti-Crisis Measures" for a discussion of the policies\par specifically related to the banking sector. However, the Government also\par implemented policies to support the real sector of the economy, the labour\par market and households.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In April 2009, the Government adopted its Anti-Crisis Programme for 2009, allocating to it approximately 1.2 trillion roubles in federal budget financing. In addition to policies aimed at the banking sector, the programme included the following principal measures:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of 300 billion roubles for federal budget loans and transfers to sub-federal governments. In 2009, approximately 306 billion roubles were spent on federal budget loans and transfers to sub-federal governments, including 150 billion roubles in financial aid and 87 billion roubles in subsidies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of funds to support approximately 300 key enterprises with\par supplemental recapitalisations, state loan guarantees (for which the Government\par has allocated 300 billion roubles) and direct state support. In 2009,\par enterprises requested state loan guarantees amounting to approximately\par 276.6 billion roubles, and the Government issued state loan guarantees\par totalling 145.9 billion roubles.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 The increase of federal support for the defence-industrial complex, including\par (i) the allocation of (x) up to 100 billion roubles in state guarantees of\par previously issued loans to companies in the defence industry, and (y) an\par increase of three billion roubles in payments to certain strategic companies of\par the defence-industrial complex; and (ii) equity injections into certain key\par companies, including 15 billion roubles into Russian Aircraft Company "MIG"\par ("MIG") and eight billion roubles into the Khrunichev Space Center. In 2009,\par the Government made disbursements to certain defence companies for the\par repayment of interest under existing loan facilities and injected over\par 100 billion roubles of capital into certain key companies, including 15 billion\par roubles into MIG and 13.5 billion roubles into the Open Joint Stock Company\par "Unified Aviation Construction Corporation.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of over 200 billion roubles to support development of the\par agricultural and fishing industries. In 2009, approximately 205 billion roubles\par were spent on the development of these industries. An additional 95 billion\par roubles from regional budgets were earmarked for the same purpose.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 The initial allocation of 39 billion roubles to support the automotive industry. In 2009, approximately 78 billion roubles were spent in support of the automotive industry, which includes the disbursement of a 37 billion rouble, interest-free loan to \f28 \uc2 \f2 AVTOVAZ\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \u8226\'20\'22 To support the transport industry, the initial allocation of (i) 560 billion\par roubles for general development, including approximately 342 billion roubles to\par improve roads; (ii) 50 billion roubles to subsidise losses incurred by Open\par Joint Stock Company "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  " ("\f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  ") in connection with\par state regulation of tariffs for railway shipments in 2009; and (iii)\par  11.3 billion roubles for additional contributions to the share capital of\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  . In 2009, 570 billion roubles were spent on general\par development of the transport industry, including 194 billion roubles to improve\par roads (122.5 billion roubles of which were spent on improving federal roads),\par 40.7 billion roubles were spent to subsidise losses incurred by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian\par Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2   as a result of lower tariffs on freight transport, 36.4 billion\par roubles were spent to subsidise falling revenues incurred by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par due to state regulation of tariffs on passenger transport and three billion\par roubles were spent on additional contributions to the share capital of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian\par Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Within the framework of the Anti-Crisis Programme for 2009, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian\par Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2   also carried out the issuance and placement of 145 billion roubles of\par corporate bonds in 2009. The Government also created a new transport leasing\par company, injecting 10 billion roubles into its charter capital, to finance road\par construction companies.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 The investment of 343.5 billion roubles in housing construction and development.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In December 2009, the Government passed its Anti-Crisis Programme for 2010, which, in its implementation, combines support for enterprises adversely affected by the crisis with measures to modernise the economy. The key elements of the Anti-Crisis Programme for 2010 include the following:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of approximately 36 billion roubles to support the labour\par market, which amount was subsequently increased to 40.5 billion roubles as a\par result of a February 2010 Government resolution. In the first half of 2010,\par approximately 33 billion roubles were spent on such measures.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of 40 billion roubles for the support of certain strategic\par enterprises. In the first half of 2010, approximately 34 billion roubles were\par spent on measures to support certain strategic enterprises.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 To support the automotive industry, the allocation of (i) 21 billion roubles\par in subsidies to trade organisations for losses incurred in Russia's "cash for\par clunkers" programme; (ii) 20 billion roubles for the purchase by federal\par authorities of various automotive products and machinery for road repairs; and\par (iii) 10 billion roubles in subsidies from the federal budget to the Federation\par subjects for the purchase of vehicles and automotive parts. In 2010,\par approximately 11.6 billion roubles were spent on subsidies to trade\par organisations for losses incurred in connection with the "cash for clunkers"\par programme, approximately 8.4 billion roubles were spent on the purchase of\par automotive products and machinery for road repairs and 28.7 billion roubles\par were spent on federal subsidies to the Federation subjects for the purchase of\par vehicles and automotive parts. In addition, the Government has allocated\par 38 billion roubles to the state corporation "Rostekhnologiya" to provide\par financial support to OAO "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Avtovaz\f28 \uc2 \f2  ."\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of 27 billion roubles to support modernisation measures in\par single-industry towns, including 10 billion roubles in interbudgetary transfers\par for the implementation of investment projects and 10 billion roubles in\par budgetary credits.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 The allocation of funds for investment in housing construction and development. In the first half of 2010, 122.8 billion roubles were spent on such investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government did not adopt an anti-crisis programme for 2011 and has not adopted an anti-crisis programme for 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As the Russian economy continues to recover from the global financial crisis,\par the Government plans to prioritise the modernisation initiative that forms a\par central part of its Development Programme through 2020. The modernisation\par initiative is based on several key priority areas, including the following:\par diversifying the Russian economy, including developing certain high-tech\par industries; stimulating innovation; improving regulation of the domestic\par financial markets; and reducing the share of state ownership in the economy.\par The Government also intends to improve the investment climate, in particular,\par by taking steps to strengthen the financial sector, the legal system and\par corporate governance.\par \par Civil and Commercial Law\par Since 1992, Russia has sought to establish a legal framework for economic\par relations between independent legal persons and entities. The rule of law in\par Russia nonetheless continues to be undermined by persistent gaps in\par legislation, inconsistencies between legal norms at the federal level as well\par as between norms at the federal and regional levels, the significant degree of\par discretion given to state officials in many areas and the inexperience of some\par Russian judges and their susceptibility to outside influences, especially at\par the regional and local levels.\par \par The Constitution protects the right of Russian citizens to hold private\par property. Property rights and basic rules for commercial relations are set out\par in Russia's Civil Code. Part I of the Civil Code, passed in 1994, establishes\par the principles of contract and property law. The Civil Code also specifies the\par forms that private enterprises may take, including corporations, partnerships\par and limited liability companies. Part II of the Civil Code, passed in 1996,\par regulates particular types of contractual relationships, including sales\par contracts, leases, credit agreements and insurance policies. Part III of the\par Civil Code came into effect in 2002 and covers inheritance law and private\par international law. The fourth and final part of the Civil Code, which came into\par effect on 1 January 2008, codifies rules on intellectual property matters.\par \par The Federal Law on the Enactment of the Land Code (the "Land Code"), enacted in\par 2001, regulates the ownership of non-agricultural real estate and establishes\par general principles of private land ownership. The law in this area remains\par incomplete, however, and there are many local rules and standards that limit\par the purchase and ownership of land. As a result, private ownership of land is\par not widespread in Russia, and a land market does not exist to the same extent\par as is common in countries with more developed market economies. According to\par the Land Code, legal entities that are currently using their land based on the\par right of permanent (perpetual) use are required to purchase or lease such land\par before 1 July 2012, with the exception of land on which power transmission\par lines, telephone lines, pipelines, roads, railways and other similar facilities\par are located, which must be purchased or leased before 1 January 2015.\par Agricultural land is regulated by the Land Code and the Federal Law on the\par Circulation of Agricultural Land (the "Agricultural Land Code"). The\par Agricultural Land Code, which was enacted in July 2002, creates a unified\par market framework at the federal level for the purchase and sale of farmland.\par Under the Agricultural Land Code, foreigners are not allowed to purchase\par farmland.\par \par The Law on Privatisation of State and Municipal Property dated 21 December 2001\par (the "Privatisation Law") sets forth the procedures that govern the\par privatisation of state and municipal property, including the requirements for\par potential buyers and other related matters. Under the Privatisation Law, a\par privatisation programme, including a list of enterprises to be sold, is adopted\par \par for a one to three year period. In accordance with the privatisation programme\par for 2010, the Government sold off its 13.1% stake in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosgosstrakh\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a leading\par Russian insurance company, in an auction held in September 2010. Proceeds from\par the sale amounted to approximately 8.7 billion roubles, bringing overall\par proceeds from privatisations in 2010 to approximately 22.65 billion roubles. In\par November 2010, the Government published the list of enterprises to undergo\par privatisation in 2011, 2012 and 2013, which includes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , Rosneft,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sovcomflot\f28 \uc2 \f2  , VTB, the Federal Grid Company and \f28 \uc2 \f2 RusHydro\f28 \uc2 \f2  . In February 2011, the\par Government sold a 10% stake in VTB, generating U.S.$3.3 billion in proceeds.\par According to the Ministry of Economic Development, overall privatisation\par proceeds in 2011 totalled 120 billion roubles. Based on the Principal\par Priorities of the Budget Policy for 2012 and the Planning Period 2013 and 2014,\par prepared by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Government expects to generate\par approximately 885 billion roubles in privatisation proceeds from 2012 through\par 2014.\par \par The Law on Joint-Stock Companies dated 26 December 1995 came into effect in\par 1996 and was subsequently amended on several occasions between 1999 and 2010.\par The Law on Joint-Stock Companies sets forth the basic framework for corporate\par organisation and corporate governance, including the formation of companies,\par shareholder rights and liabilities (including the ability to enter into\par shareholder agreements), the role of directors, interested party transactions,\par mergers and acquisitions, shareholder buy-outs and share capital and dividends.\par The Law on the Securities Market, adopted in 1996 and subsequently amended,\par regulates the issuance and circulation of securities. Pursuant to this law,\par companies that issue securities must, with limited exceptions, register the\par issuance by filing a prospectus and publicly reporting certain information on a\par quarterly basis. The law also regulates the activities of professional\par participants in the securities market, aims to strengthen the protection of\par investors by imposing rules on market professionals and contains framework\par provisions on the listing and public trading of securities of foreign issuers\par in Russia.\par \par The Law on Limited Liability Companies dated 8 February 1998, as amended, sets\par forth the basic framework for the organisation and governance of limited\par liability companies. On 1 July 2009, substantial amendments to this law came\par into effect, the primary purposes of which were to streamline procedures for\par establishing limited liability companies, to improve the regulation of the\par purchase and sale of interests in limited liability companies and to make the\par ownership structure of limited liability companies more transparent.\par \par The Federal Law "On Insolvency (Bankruptcy)" (the "Bankruptcy Law"), which\par replaced the prior law adopted in 1998, came into effect in November 2002 and\par was subsequently amended. The Bankruptcy Law provides basic rules for declaring\par an enterprise bankrupt, for managing and liquidating an enterprise after it has\par been declared bankrupt, for satisfying creditors' claims and for the bankruptcy\par process generally. The Bankruptcy Law permits an enterprise to be declared\par bankrupt if it is unable to make payments to its creditors (including tax\par authorities) in excess of 100,000 roubles within three months of such payments\par becoming due. The Bankruptcy Law also expands the types of legal entities that\par are subject to the Bankruptcy Law and clarifies the procedure for appointing\par managers of bankrupt enterprises and for initiating insolvency proceedings. In\par July 2010, amendments to the Bankruptcy Law went into effect, setting forth\par certain procedures related to the bankruptcy of financial organisations,\par including credit institutions, insurance companies, professional participants\par on the securities markets and non-state pension funds.\par \par The legislative framework for the restructuring of credit institutions is\par further set out in the Law on Insolvency (Bankruptcy) of Credit Institutions,\par which was adopted in February 1999 and subsequently amended between 2000 and\par 2009. This legislation details the standards for the appointment by the Bank of\par Russia of temporary bank administrators, provides an expedited liquidation\par procedure for banks and gives the Bank of Russia the power to accredit the\par receivers of bankrupt banks and to propose receivers for approval by\par arbitration courts.\par \par Russia's Strategic Investments Law, which came into force in May 2008,\par regulates foreign investments (whether direct or indirect) in Russian\par businesses having strategic importance in matters of state defence and security\par and, inter alia, requires foreign investors to receive prior consent of a\par special government commission before acquiring control (including through the\par purchase of certain percentages of voting shares or certain management rights)\par of such strategic companies.\par \par Under Russia's Foreign Investment Law, adopted in July 1999, foreign investors\par are ensured equal treatment with domestic investors, with some exceptions in\par the area of land ownership and leasing, access to natural resources and\par participation in privatisations. The law provides protections against\par nationalisation and expropriation without compensation, ensures the free\par transfer of investment proceeds in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the reinvestment of\par profits in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and provides recourse to Russian courts. The Foreign\par Investment Law provides that any acquisition (whether direct or indirect) by a\par foreign state or international organisation or entities controlled by them of\par (i) more than 25% of the voting shares of a Russian company; or (ii) any power\par to block decisions of the management bodies of a Russian company requires prior\par approval of the special government commission established by the Strategic\par Investments Law.\par \par Russia's Federal Law "On Protection of Competition" (the "Competition Law")\par came into force in October 2006. The Competition Law generally prohibits any\par concerted action, agreement or coordination of business activity that results\par or may result in the restriction of competition, including (a) price fixing;\par (b) coordination of auction bids; (c) partition of a commodity market by\par territory, volume of sales or purchases, types of goods, customers or\par suppliers; (d) fixing of disparate prices for the same goods for reasons other\par than economic or technical reasons; and (e) creation of barriers to entering or\par exiting a market. The Competition Law also regulates persons (or a group of\par affiliated persons) who have a dominant position in a particular market. A\par dominant position may arise in circumstances when (a) the person has a market\par share in a particular market in excess of 50%, unless it is specifically\par established that the person does not have a dominant position; (b) the person\par has a market share in a particular market in excess of 35% but less than 50%,\par and it is specifically established by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Antimonopoly Service\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the\par "FAS") that the person has a dominant position based on certain factors; or (c)\par  in certain circumstances, the person has a market share in a particular market\par less than 35% but exceeding shares of competitors in the same market, which can\par have a crucial impact on general conditions of commodity circulation in the\par market. The FAS is authorised to employ a variety of means to restrain\par anti-competitive behaviour and can in extreme cases compel the break-up of\par enterprises. In addition, the Competition Law establishes a merger control\par regime, pursuant to which FAS consent is required prior to closing in\par particular circumstances, including the following: (a) acquisitions of voting\par rights in a joint stock company or assets of a person above a certain\par threshold; (b) mergers and consolidations that result in an aggregate asset\par value or aggregate annual revenues above a certain amount; and (c) creation of\par a company, if its share capital or the contributors of such share capital meet\par certain specifications. The Competition Law also provides for a mandatory\par post-transactional notification (within 45 days of the closing) to FAS in\par certain circumstances.\par \par The Customs Code of the Customs Union among Russia, Belarus and Kazakhstan took\par effect in July 2010, replacing Russia's prior Customs Code passed in\par January 2004. The new Customs Code seeks to harmonise customs procedures,\par reduce the volume of required documentation and shorten customs clearance\par times, reflecting a shift in policy to a focus on developing foreign economic\par activity. This law strives to bring current legislation into line with\par international standards.\par \par In an attempt to maximise value and increase the efficiency of state assets, a\par law on concession agreements, enacted in July 2005, establishes a framework for\par the Government to transfer the management of state-owned property to private\par companies for a term provided for in the relevant concession agreement. Under\par the law, the private company is entitled to retain the profits generated from\par the property in exchange for investing in and managing it.\par \par A law on special economic zones in the Russian Federation, adopted in July\par 2005, aims to create technical, industrial, production, tourist and health\par resort zones. Commercial and industrial residents of these zones enjoy\par reductions in property and land tax and import duties. They also benefit from\par greater depreciation allowances (except with respect to technical zones) and\par are permitted to write-off research and development costs within the periods\par when such costs were incurred without limitation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gross Domestic Product\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The following table sets forth certain information regarding Russia's GDP for\par the periods indicated:\par \par                            Gross Domestic Product(1)                           \par \par                             For the                                               \par                           year ended                                              \par                           31 December                                             \par                                                                                   \par                               2007      2008    2009    2010    2011              \par                                                                                   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nominal GDP (billions of        33,248  41,277  38,809  45,166  54,369     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       \par roubles)                                                                          \par                                                                                   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Real GDP index (%) (2003         133.8   140.9   129.8   135.4   141.2     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       \par prices=100%)                                                                      \par                                                                                   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Real GDP growth/(decline)          8.5     5.2   (7.8)     4.3     4.3     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       \par (%, period-on-period)                                                             \par                                                                                   \par Nominal GDP per capita         233,948 290,771 273,318 314,396 382,035            \par (roubles)                                                                         \par                                                                                   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Real GDP per capita index        136.1   143.4   132.3   137.0   142.8     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       \par (%) (2003 prices=100%)                                                            \par                                                                                   \par Real GDP per capita                8.8     5.4   (7.8)     3.5     4.3            \par growth/(decline)                                                                  \par (%, period-on-period)                                                             \par                                                                                   \par GDP deflator (%,                  13.8    18.0     2.0    11.6    15.4            \par period-on-period)                                                                 \par                                                                                   \par Memo:                                                                             \par                                                                                   \par Total population,                142.0   141.9   142.9   142.9   143.0            \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 millions (end of period)                                                   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       \par \par \par Note:\par \par (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data\par due to regular revisions by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Figures in this table are current as of\par 31 January 2012.\par \par Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par Between 2007 and 2011, real GDP grew by 14.5%, with significant economic growth\par recorded through the first three quarters of 2008. Real GDP grew by 8.5% in\par 2007. Despite substantial growth in the first nine months of 2008 (9.1%, 7.7%\par and 6.4% in the first, second and third quarters, respectively, against the\par relevant periods in the previous year), the effects of the global financial\par crisis contributed to a slowdown in overall growth in 2008 (5.2%), which was\par until that time the slowest rate of growth recorded since 2002. As a result of\par the global financial crisis, the economy contracted by 7.8% in 2009 compared to\par 2008. In 2010 and 2011, the Russian economy began to recover from the global\par financial crisis, growing by 4.3% in each year.\par \par Economic growth in 2007 and 2008 resulted mainly from improved labour\par productivity due to substantial increases in investment and improved management\par and from a rapid expansion in private consumption. The surge in investments and\par private consumption was, in turn, fuelled by high commodity prices and a\par significant expansion in credit. Overall consumption as a percentage of GDP\par remained roughly constant during this period because of relatively flat growth\par in public consumption, which, in turn, was the result of strong fiscal\par discipline on the part of the Government.\par \par Strong real appreciation of the rouble and production growth contributed to an\par aggregate increase in the dollar value of Russia's GDP in 2007 and 2008. From\par 1999 through 2008, Russia registered an average GDP growth rate of\par approximately 6.9%, and, by 2008, based on purchasing power parity, Russia's\par economy was the world's sixth largest.\par \par Russia experienced particularly adverse economic and financial effects as a\par result of the global financial crisis. In the fourth quarter of 2008, due to a\par decline in gross investment and net exports and despite a nominal increase in\par both private and public consumption, real GDP fell by 1.1% relative to the\par fourth quarter of 2007. In 2009, real GDP decreased by 7.8% compared to 2008,\par primarily because of a contraction in private-sector consumption and gross\par investment. The decline in real GDP in 2009 was slightly offset by a rise in\par net exports that was, in turn, caused by a disproportionate drop in imports, a\par result of falling domestic demand and depreciation of the rouble (the average\par rouble rate fell by 27.4% in 2009 compared to 2008). The rouble depreciation as\par well as the overall decline in the Russian economy led to a decrease in the\par dollar value of Russia's GDP in 2009 relative to 2008.\par \par The Russian economy began to recover from the global financial crisis in the\par first quarter of 2010, when it registered real GDP growth of 3.1% compared to\par the first quarter of 2009. As a result of an increase in domestic demand,\par including consumption and gross investment, as well as an increase in net\par exports, supported, in turn, by higher prices for Russia's principal commodity\par exports, real GDP grew by 4.3% in 2010 compared to 2009. Appreciation of the\par rouble as well as the overall growth in the Russian economy resulted in an\par increase in the dollar value of Russia's GDP in 2010 relative to 2009.\par \par The Russian economy continued to grow in 2011, sustaining real GDP growth of\par 4.3% compared to 2010. Growth in 2011 was largely driven by an increase in\par domestic demand, including consumption, which grew by 4.8%, and gross\par investment, which grew by 22.4%. The dollar value of Russia's GDP also\par increased in 2011 compared to 2010 due mainly to the continued growth in the\par Russian economy and, to a lesser degree, the strengthening of the average\par rouble/dollar exchange rate.\par \par In September 2011, the Ministry of Economic Development officially published\par several estimates of anticipated real GDP growth, each based on a different oil\par price assumption. Under its "moderately optimistic" estimate, which assumes an\par average price per barrel of Urals oil of U.S.$108 in 2011, U.S.$100 in 2012,\par U.S.$97 in 2013 and U.S.$101 in 2014, real GDP was expected to grow by 4.1% in\par 2011 and is expected to grow by 3.7% in 2012, 4% in 2013 and 4.6% in 2014. The\par projected real GDP figures are used by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in preparing the\par annual budget. There can be no assurance that such figures will prove to be\par accurate. See "Forward-Looking Statements" for a description of the various\par external and domestic factors that may cause estimates of real GDP to differ\par from actual results.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GDP and Gross Added Value by Sector\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table illustrates Russia's GDP and gross added value by economic sector for the periods indicated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par          Gross Domestic Product and Gross Added Value by Sector(1)(2)          \par \par                   For the                                                         \par                 year ended                                                        \par                 31 December                                                       \par                                                                                   \par                    2007            2008        2009        2010        2011       \par                                                                                   \par                      %        %     %     %     %     %     %     %     %     %   \par                             change      change      change      change      change\par                              (3)         (3)         (3)         (3)         (3)  \par                                                                                   \par GDP total                 -    8.5    -    5.2    -  (7.8)    -    4.3    -    4.3\par                                                                                   \par Gross added             100    8.4  100    5.2  100  (6.7)  100    3.7  100    4.0\par value (in base                                                                    \par prices)                                                                           \par                                                                                   \par Agriculture(4)          4.2    1.3  4.2    6.4  4.4    1.5  3.8  (9.7)  4.1   16.1\par                                                                                   \par Fishery                 0.2  (0.9)  0.2  (5.6)  0.2    5.6  0.2 (12.6)  0.2   13.2\par                                                                                   \par Mining(5)              10.1  (2.2)  9.3    1.0  8.5  (2.4)  9.8    7.1 10.7    1.7\par                                                                                   \par Manufacturing          17.6    7.5 17.5  (2.1) 14.9 (14.6) 15.0    8.3 16.1    6.1\par                                                                                   \par Electricity,            3.0  (3.4)  2.9    0.7  4.1  (4.7)  3.9    3.5  3.8    0.4\par water and gas                                                                     \par production and                                                                    \par distribution                                                                      \par                                                                                   \par Construction            5.7   13.0  6.3   11.1  6.2 (14.7)  6.7    3.2  6.6    4.8\par                                                                                   \par Trade(6)               20.3   11.7 20.3    9.9 17.9  (5.8) 18.9    6.3 19.0    5.0\par                                                                                   \par Hotels and              1.0   13.6  1.0   10.1  1.0 (14.9)  1.0    2.6  1.0    4.8\par restaurants                                                                       \par                                                                                   \par Transportation          9.7    4.8  9.3    5.2  9.6  (8.6)  9.6    4.8  8.9    2.9\par and                                                                               \par communications                                                                    \par                                                                                   \par Financial               4.4   29.1  4.4   13.5  5.0    1.5  4.5  (2.1)  4.1    3.4\par services                                                                          \par                                                                                   \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with        10.9   20.8 11.3   10.9 12.6  (4.5) 12.4    5.7 11.6    2.6\par real estate,                                                                      \par including rent                                                                    \par and services                                                                      \par                                                                                   \par Public                  5.1    3.9  5.4    3.0  6.5  (0.1)  6.0    0.1  5.8    2.8\par administration,                                                                   \par defence and                                                                       \par mandatory                                                                         \par social security                                                                   \par                                                                                   \par Education               2.7    1.1  2.8  (0.1)  3.4  (1.4)  3.0  (1.9)  2.9  (0.8)\par                                                                                   \par Public health           3.3    1.1  3.4    0.9  4.0  (0.2)  3.7  (0.3)  3.7    3.1\par and social                                                                        \par services                                                                          \par                                                                                   \par Communal and            1.8    8.6  1.8    1.4  1.7 (20.0)  1.5  (6.4)  1.5    1.0\par other services                                                                    \par \par \par Notes:\par \par (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data\par due to regular revisions by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Figures in this table are current as of\par 31 January 2012.\par \par (2) Sectoral figures in this table are calculated pursuant to gross added\par value, which does not take into account taxes on products, but does take into\par account subsidies on products. For this reason, sectoral contributions for each\par period total 100% of gross added value, but do not total 100% of GDP for that\par period. For the same reason, GDP growth rates and gross added value growth\par rates over the same period may also differ.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) In constant prices. Percent changes reflect period-on-period changes, based on figures for gross added value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes hunting and forestry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) Includes extraction of oil, gas, coal, metal ores and other minerals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (6) Includes wholesale and retail trade and repairs of motor vehicles and personal and household goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par From 2007 through 2010, the major trend in the composition of gross added value\par by sector was an increase in the contribution of real estate and a decrease in\par the share of the manufacturing and trade sectors. As a share of gross added\par value, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with real estate rose from 10.9% in 2007 to 12.4% in 2010.\par Manufacturing decreased from 17.6% of gross added value in 2007 to 15% of gross\par added value in 2010, and trade declined from 20.3% of gross added value in 2007\par to 18.9% of gross added value in 2010. In 2011, manufacturing experienced a\par recovery, with its share of gross added value increasing to 16.1% in 2011,\par whereas the share of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with real estate decreased to 11.6% of gross\par added value in 2011. From 2007 through 2011, mining, electricity, water and gas\par production and distribution, construction, public administration, defence and\par mandatory social security, education and public health and social services\par increased their respective contributions to overall gross added value whereas\par the share of overall gross added value attributable to agriculture,\par transportation and communications, financial services and communal services\par declined.\par \par These shifts in the composition of Russia's gross added value resulted from\par changes in performance in real terms and from changes in relative prices.\par Between 2007 and 2011, the fastest rate of growth among Russia's large sectors\par was recorded in financial services (50.6%), real estate (38.7%), trade (28.9%)\par construction (15.8%) and agriculture (14.6%), all of which exceeded the GDP\par growth rate during this period of 14.5%. Buoyant economic growth and the real\par appreciation of the rouble between 2006 and 2008, together with the economic\par recovery in 2010 and 2011, were the principal reasons for the improvement in\par these sectors. From 2007 through 2011, growth was recorded in all sectors\par except for the fishery sector, which contracted by 2.4%, education, which\par declined by 3.1%, and communal and other services, which declined by 16.7%.\par \par Of the country's largest sectors, manufacturing, construction, trade, real\par estate and transportation and communications were the worst affected by the\par global financial crisis in 2009. Compared to 2008, manufacturing declined by\par 14.6%, construction by 14.7%, trade by 5.8%, hotels and restaurants by 14.9%,\par transportation and communications by 8.6% and real estate by 4.5%. Every\par industry with the exception of agriculture, fishery and financial services\par experienced a decline in 2009 compared to 2008.\par \par Due to improved economic conditions following the global financial crisis, key\par sectors of the Russian economy expanded in 2010 compared to 2009. For example,\par manufacturing grew by 8.3%, mining by 7.1%, transportation and communications\par by 4.8%, construction by 3.2% and trade by 6.3%. In contrast, agriculture and\par financial services experienced year-on-year declines of 9.7% and 2.1%,\par respectively.\par \par In 2011, the continued economic recovery was broadly reflected across Russia's\par key sectors. At 16.1%, agriculture experienced the highest year-on-year growth,\par with manufacturing recording 6.1% growth and trade recording 5% growth.\par Education was the only sector to contract in 2011, declining by 0.8%.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GDP by Use\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The following table illustrates Russia's GDP by use for the periods indicated:\par \par                        Gross Domestic Product by Use(1)                        \par \par                For the                                                          \par              year ended                                                         \par              31 December                                                        \par                                                                                 \par                 2007            2008        2009         2010        2011       \par                                                                                 \par                   %        %     %     %      %     %     %     %     %     %   \par                          change      change       change      change      change\par                           (2)         (2)          (2)         (2)         (2)  \par                                                                                 \par GDP                  100    8.5  100    5.2   100  (7.8)  100    4.3  100    4.3\par                                                                                 \par Consumption         66.8   11.2 65.9    8.6  74.1  (3.9) 69.5    3.3 66.5    4.8\par (3)                                                                             \par                                                                                 \par Private             48.8   14.3 47.8   10.6  53.1  (5.1) 50.8    5.1 48.9    6.4\par                                                                                 \par Public              17.5    2.7 17.6    3.4  20.4  (0.6) 18.2  (1.4) 17.2    0.8\par                                                                                 \par Non-profit           0.5    2.7  0.5  (1.4)   0.6  (8.0)  0.5  (0.5)  0.4  (2.9)\par institutions                                                                    \par                                                                                 \par Gross               24.4   22.0 25.1   10.5  18.6 (41.0) 22.4   28.9 25.0   22.4\par investment                                                                      \par                                                                                 \par Fixed assets        21.2   21.0 22.0   10.6  21.7 (14.4) 21.4    5.8 20.6    6.0\par accumulation                                                                    \par                                                                                 \par Inventory            3.2   29.3  3.1    9.5 (3.1)    n/a  1.0    n/a  4.4    n/a\par accumulation                                                                    \par                                                                                 \par Net exports          8.8 (26.6)  9.0 (34.8)   7.3    n/a  8.1    n/a  8.5    n/a\par                                                                                 \par Exports             30.6    6.3 30.8    0.6  27.4  (4.7) 29.4    7.0 30.5    1.0\par                                                                                 \par Imports             21.8   26.2 21.8   14.8  20.1 (30.4) 21.3   26.1 22.0   21.5\par \par \par Notes:\par \par n/a = not available\par \par (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data\par due to regular revisions by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Figures in this table are current as of\par 31 January 2012.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) In constant prices. Percent changes reflect period-on-period changes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (3) Represents expenses on final consumption.\par Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The composition of GDP by use was affected by the overall condition of the Russian economy-from its growth in 2007 and 2008, on the back of high commodity prices, to its contraction, particularly in 2009, as a result of the global financial crisis, and to its subsequent recovery in 2010 and 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From 2007 through 2011, domestic demand, i.e., consumption and gross investment, accounted for an average of 91.7% of annual GDP and net exports accounted for an average of 8.3% of annual GDP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Domestic consumption's contribution to GDP decreased slightly between 2007 and\par 2008, before expanding to 74.1% of GDP in 2009. In 2010, domestic consumption\par grew by 3.3% relative to 2009, accounting for 69.5% of GDP, and, in 2011,\par domestic consumption grew by 4.8% relative to 2010, accounting for 66.5% of\par GDP. The decline in domestic consumption's contribution to GDP in 2010 and 2011\par was primarily due to a more rapid expansion of gross investment over the same\par period. Overall, between 2007 and 2011, domestic consumption grew by\par approximately 25.6%, despite a 3.9% decline in 2009 compared to 2008.\par \par Between 2007 and 2011, overall private consumption rose in real terms by 34.2%,\par outpacing the overall growth rate of GDP during the period, whereas public\par consumption increased in real terms by only 4.9%, which reflected the country's\par relatively conservative fiscal policy during the period. In 2007 and 2008,\par private consumption increased in real terms by 26.4% due to the overall\par strength of the Russian economy during that period, including high oil prices\par and a rapid expansion of available credit. However, as a result of the global\par financial crisis and its negative effects on Russian consumers, private\par consumption contracted by 5.1% in 2009 compared to 2008. Private consumption\par grew by 5.1% in 2010 relative to 2009 and by 6.4% in 2011 relative to 2010, as\par the economy began to recover from the global financial crisis. The contribution\par to GDP made by private consumption fluctuated slightly during this period,\par averaging 48.3% per year in 2007 and 2008, before increasing to 53.1% in 2009\par and then decreasing to 50.8% in 2010 and 48.9% in 2011. The contribution to GDP\par made by public consumption remained roughly the same in 2007 and 2008 (17.5% in\par 2007, compared to 17.6% in 2008), before increasing to 20.4% in 2009 and 18.2%\par in 2010. In 2011, public consumption accounted for 17.2% of GDP. The rise in\par the share of public consumption as a percentage of GDP in 2009 and 2010 was\par mainly a function of the Government's fiscal stimulus package.\par \par Between 2007 and 2011, gross investment grew by 25.5% in real terms. In 2007\par and 2008, gross investment grew by 34.8%. In 2009, gross investment contracted\par by 41% due to the adverse effects of the global financial crisis on the Russian\par economy. Gross investment returned to strong growth following the crisis,\par expanding by 28.9% in 2010 and 22.4% in 2011. Between 2007 and 2011,\par investments in fixed assets grew by 28.5% in real terms, which included a 14.4%\par contraction in 2009 compared to 2008. The contribution of fixed asset\par investments to GDP increased from 21.2% in 2007 to 22% in 2008 before declining\par to 21.7% in 2009, 21.4% in 2010 and 20.6% in 2011.\par \par Between 2007 and 2011, exports grew at a slower pace than overall GDP, while\par imports accelerated at a faster rate than GDP. During this period, the real\par value of exports grew by 10.1%, and the real value of imports grew by 54.5%.\par Exports as a share of GDP contracted in 2009 and 2010, when they stood at 27.4%\par and 29.4%, respectively, compared to over 30% in each of 2007, 2008 and 2011.\par Exports declined by 4.7% in 2009 compared to 2008 due mainly to lower commodity\par prices and reduced global demand for Russian exports. However, exports\par increased by 7% in 2010 and 1% in 2011 due mainly to an increase in the price\par and volume of Russia's principal exports. The high growth in imports in 2007\par and 2008 (26.2% and 14.8%, respectively) was a result, generally, of high rates\par of economic growth in Russia during this period, and, in particular, of rising\par consumer incomes. The contraction in imports in 2009 compared to 2008 (30.4%)\par as well as the decline in imports as a share of GDP (from 21.8% in 2008 to\par 20.1% in 2009) was mainly due to the negative effects of the global financial\par crisis on the Russian economy. Imports returned to strong growth in 2010 and\par 2011, increasing by 26.1% and 21.5%, respectively, due mainly to an expansion\par in import volumes, which, in turn, was the result of an improvement in domestic\par demand, as well as to a moderate rise in the price of Russia's principal\par imports. See "Balance of Payments and Foreign Trade-Balance of Payments-Current\par Account."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Principal Sectors of the Economy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industry\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia is highly industrialised, with a large share of its industrial activity\par concentrated in manufacturing. Russia is the world leader in oil extraction and\par produces significant amounts of natural gas, electricity, iron, steel, rolled\par products, mineral fertilisers and coal. Manufacturing activity is heavily\par concentrated in metallurgy, coke and petrochemicals, food processing, machine\par and equipment building, the production of electrical equipment and the\par production of transport vehicles and equipment.\par \par The following table illustrates the structure of industrial output and\par period-on-period changes in real industrial output by sector for the periods\par indicated:\par \par                         Industrial Output by Sector(1)                         \par \par                  For the                                                           \par                year ended                                                          \par                31 December                                                         \par                                                                                    \par                   2007            2008         2009         2010        2011       \par                                                                                    \par                   %(2)       %    %(2)    %    %(2)    %    %(2)   %    %(2)   %   \par                            change       change       change      change      change\par                             (3)          (3)          (3)         (3)         (3)  \par                                                                                    \par Industrial           100.0    6.8 100.0    0.6 100.0  (9.3)  100    8.2  100    4.7\par Output                                                                             \par                                                                                    \par Extraction            21.8    3.3  21.3    0.4  22.7  (0.6) 22.1    3.6 23.4    1.9\par                                                                                    \par Of which                                                                           \par                                                                                    \par Fuels                 19.3    2.7  18.8    0.1  20.2    0.4 19.4    3.1 20.5    1.3\par                                                                                    \par Other minerals         2.5    4.0   2.5    1.1   2.5  (7.4)  2.7    7.3  2.9    4.8\par                                                                                    \par Manufacturing         67.8   10.5  68.3    0.5  63.9 (15.2) 65.0   11.8 65.0    6.5\par                                                                                    \par Of which                                                                           \par                                                                                    \par Foods                 10.4    7.3  10.7    1.9  12.6  (0.6) 11.4    5.4 10.6    1.0\par                                                                                    \par Paper products         2.4    8.3   2.2    0.3   2.2 (14.3)  2.1    5.9  1.9    1.8\par                                                                                    \par Coke and              11.0    2.8  12.1    2.8  11.8  (0.6) 12.4    5.0 13.0    2.9\par petrochemicals                                                                     \par                                                                                    \par Chemicals              4.6    6.6   5.3  (4.6)   4.7  (6.9)  5.0   14.6  5.2    5.2\par                                                                                    \par Metallurgy            14.3    4.5  13.3  (2.2)  10.6 (14.7) 11.9   12.4 11.7    2.9\par                                                                                    \par Machines and           3.9   26.7   4.0  (0.5)   3.6 (31.5)  3.3   12.2  3.2    9.5\par equipment                                                                          \par                                                                                    \par Electrical             4.0   10.9   3.7  (7.4)   3.6 (32.2)  3.7   22.8  3.7    5.1\par equipment                                                                          \par                                                                                    \par Transport              6.2    7.8   6.1    0.4   5.0 (37.2)  5.9   32.2  6.5   24.6\par vehicles and                                                                       \par equipment                                                                          \par                                                                                    \par Textiles and           0.7  (0.5)   0.6  (5.4)   0.7 (16.2)  0.6   12.1  0.6    2.6\par clothing                                                                           \par                                                                                    \par Leather and            0.1    2.3   0.1  (0.3)   0.2  (0.1)  0.2   18.7  0.1    8.6\par footwear                                                                           \par                                                                                    \par Wood products          1.1    7.9   1.0  (0.1)   1.0 (20.7)  0.9   11.4  0.8    4.0\par                                                                                    \par Rubber and             1.7   25.5   1.6   22.8   1.7 (12.6)  1.7   21.5  1.7   13.1\par plastics                                                                           \par                                                                                    \par Other                  4.1    8.3   4.1  (2.9)   3.0 (27.5)  2.9   10.7  2.9    9.3\par non-metal                                                                          \par mineral                                                                            \par products                                                                           \par                                                                                    \par Other                  1.8    4.6   1.8    1.7   1.4 (20.7)  1.5   17.7  1.4    4.5\par                                                                                    \par Electricity,          10.4  (0.6)  10.4    0.6  13.5  (3.9) 12.9    4.1 11.6    0.1\par water and gas                                                                      \par production and                                                                     \par distribution                                                                       \par \par \par Notes:\par \par (1) Figures are based on sales of industrial output and therefore include sales\par from inventories, but exclude output that is produced but not sold. Figures in\par this table are current as of 31 January 2012. Figures for 2011 are preliminary.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) In prices prevailing at time of sale.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Percent changes in this column are calculated pursuant to production indices and reflect period-on-period changes. Figures adjusted for shadow economic activity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Industrial output grew by 6.8% in 2007 and 0.6% in 2008, mainly due to growth\par in manufacturing, which, in turn, was the result of improved competitiveness of\par Russian products in both domestic and international markets, improved\par management, growth in private investment and strong global commodity prices.\par Growth in industrial production decelerated in 2008 because of lower commodity\par prices and the onset of the global financial crisis, which led to a contraction\par in industrial output in the fourth quarter of the year (a contraction of 9.1%\par compared to the fourth quarter of 2007) and reduced investment.\par \par In 2007, growth was recorded in all major industrial sectors. The greatest\par growth was recorded in machines and equipment (26.7%) rubber and plastics\par (25.5%), and electrical equipment (18.1%), as these industries benefited from\par high levels of investment and external demand. Growth in the extraction\par industry amounted to 3.3%. The two sectors that experienced a contraction in\par output in 2007 were textiles and clothing, which contracted by 0.5%, and\par electricity, water and gas production and distribution, which fell by 0.6%, due\par in part to government-mandated price caps.\par \par In 2008, Russia's total industrial output decelerated, increasing by 0.6% in\par real terms compared to 2007. Overall annual growth was recorded in several\par industries, including rubber and plastics (22.8%), coke and petrochemicals\par (2.8%) and foods (1.9%). Electrical equipment, textiles and clothing and\par chemicals experienced the largest contractions in output in 2008, falling by\par 7.4%, 5.4% and 4.6%, respectively, relative to 2007. Overall industrial growth\par decelerated in 2008 due to the global financial crisis, which began to have a\par significant negative impact on Russia's real economy in the fourth quarter of\par 2008. While industrial output grew in each of the first three quarters of 2008\par (6%, 4.3% and 1.9%, respectively, against the corresponding quarters of 2007),\par output contracted in the fourth quarter by 9.1% against the fourth quarter of\par 2007.\par \par In 2009, Russia's total industrial output decreased by 9.3% in real terms\par compared to 2008. Industrial output contracted in all major industries, with\par transport vehicles and equipment (37.2%), electrical equipment (32.2%) and\par machines and equipment (31.5%) experiencing the sharpest declines. The overall\par contraction was due to the effects of the global financial crisis, including a\par substantial decline in the overall demand for industrial products and a\par decrease in global commodity prices.\par \par In 2010, industrial output increased by 8.2% compared to 2009. Growth was\par recorded in all major industrial sectors due to the overall improvement of the\par economy following the global financial crisis, and, in particular, an increase\par in both foreign and domestic demand for industrial products. The transport\par vehicles and equipment, electrical equipment, rubber and plastics, leather and\par footwear and chemicals sectors experienced the most significant year-on-year\par growth, rising by 32.2%, 22.8%, 21.5%, 18.7% and 14.6%, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2011, according to preliminary estimates, industrial output increased by 4.7% in real terms compared to 2010. As in 2010, growth was recorded in all major industries. The strongest growth was in the transport vehicles and equipment sector (24.6%) and in rubbers and plastics (13.1%), whereas the slowest growth was recorded in extraction (1.9%), foods (1%), paper products (1.8%) and electricity, water and gas production and distribution (0.1%).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2009 and 2010, energy (including oil, petroleum products, natural gas, coal\par and electricity) accounted for approximately 67.4% and 68.4%, respectively, of\par Russia's exports. In 2009, oil and petroleum products accounted for 49.3% of\par total exports, while natural gas accounted for 13.9%, coal for 2.4% and\par electricity approximately 0.3%. In 2010, oil and petroleum products accounted\par for 51.6% of total exports, while natural gas accounted for 12%, coal for 2.3%\par and electricity for approximately 0.3%.\par \par Domestic energy prices were heavily subsidised in the Soviet Union (amounting\par to only 10-20% of world market prices in 1992). By 1996, however, domestic\par energy prices had risen relative to other prices as a result of export\par liberalisation and the real appreciation of the rouble against foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The devaluation of the rouble in 1998 led to a sharp fall in\par domestic energy prices in dollar terms. Despite a recovery in prices in\par subsequent years, domestic prices for gas, electricity and crude oil remain\par lower than international prices. Domestic prices for petroleum products,\par however, are more similar to international prices after deducting export duties\par and transportation costs. Federal budget revenues and Russia's balance of\par payments are affected to a significant extent by world prices for oil and gas.\par See "Balance of Payments and Foreign Trade" and "Public Finance."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil and Natural Gas\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russian methodologies for calculating oil and gas reserves and Russian reserves\par classifications differ from standard international methodologies and\par classifications, in particular with respect to the manner and extent to which\par commercial factors affecting exploration, development and production are taken\par into account. Generally, Russian methodologies classify oil and gas deposits as\par reserves if such deposits are technically recoverable, even if the recovery of\par a portion of such reserves, using currently available technology, is\par uneconomic. In contrast, most international methodologies classify oil and gas\par deposits as reserves only if such deposits are economically extractable on the\par basis of existing technologies, prices and costs. Accordingly, the gas reserves\par information contained in this Prospectus is not comparable, and cannot be made\par comparable, to gas reserves information provided on the basis of standard\par international methodologies and classifications. The gas reserves information\par contained in this Prospectus represents total "explored" reserves, consisting\par of gas reserves in categories A, B and C1 of the Russian classification system.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Oil output declined significantly following the dissolution of the Soviet Union\par but has been growing in recent years. Output in 2010 was approximately\par 505 million tonnes, an increase of 5.6% over production in 2009, and output in\par 2011 equalled 509.4 million tonnes. Between 2006 and 2010, oil output increased\par by 105.2%. In 2009 and 2010, oil exports to non-CIS countries were\par 211.0 million tonnes and 224.1 million tonnes, respectively, while exports to\par the CIS were 36.5 million tonnes and 22.9 million tonnes, respectively.\par \par Beginning in 1993, the state oil industry was split into several holding\par companies and operating subsidiaries. This resulted in the creation of a number\par of large vertically integrated oil groups, with each holding company managing\par and controlling a stake in many operating subsidiaries. The Government\par privatised its shareholdings in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Slavneft\f28 \uc2 \f2  (75%) and Lukoil (13.5%) between 2002\par and 2003. In December 2004, a 76.79% stake in Yuganskneftegaz , the main\par production subsidiary of what was then Russia's largest oil company, Yukos, was\par sold at auction for U.S.$9.35 billion, in partial settlement of Yukos' tax\par obligations, to Baikalfinancegroup, which was in turn acquired by Rosneft, then\par a wholly state-owned entity. In July 2006, the Government privatised\par approximately 15% of its shareholdings in Rosneft as part of the company's\par initial public offering.\par \par In 2005, three Yukos shareholders commenced three related arbitrations under\par the Energy Charter Treaty. The claimants originally sought in the aggregate\par U.S.$33.1 billion, and are currently seeking in the aggregate\par U.S.$103.6 billion, as compensation for the Russian Federation's alleged breach\par of its treaty obligations. Other Yukos-related claims have also been asserted\par before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Court of Human Rights\f28 \uc2 \f2  and under the Russian Federation's\par bilateral investment treaty with Spain. In addition, an arbitration tribunal\par has awarded another Yukos shareholder U.S.$3.5 million in damages in respect of\par a claim asserted under Russia's bilateral investment treaty with the United\par Kingdom. The Russian Federation has challenged the jurisdiction of the\par arbitration tribunal to hear that claim under the Russian Federation's\par bilateral investment treaty with the United Kingdom.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Natural Gas\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's A, B and C1 natural gas reserves (which include explored reserves\par considered to be fully extractable pursuant to the Russian reserves system)\par were estimated at 47.8 trillion cubic metres as of 1 January 2009. Natural gas\par output stabilised after declining immediately following the dissolution of the\par Soviet Union. Between 2005 and 2008, natural gas output increased by 3.9% (from\par 640.8 billion cubic metres in 2005 to 665.6 billion cubic metres in 2008).\par Then, in 2009, output decreased to 582.7 billion cubic metres, or by 12.5%\par compared to 2008. Natural gas output in 2010 was 652 billion cubic metres, an\par increase of 11.9% over production in 2009. In 2009 and 2010, natural gas\par exports to non-CIS countries were approximately 120.5 billion cubic metres and\par 107.4 billion cubic metres, respectively, while exports to the CIS were\par 47.9 billion cubic metres and 66.9 billion cubic metres, respectively.\par \par Russia's natural gas industry is dominated by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which has an effective\par monopoly on gas transmission, storage and export, and accounts for most of\par Russia's natural gas reserves and production. Approximately 38.4% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  's\par shares are owned directly by the Russian Government, although through its\par ownership of other \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   shareholders, the Government controls a majority\par stake in the company. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   held production licences with respect to\par approximately 70% of Russia's natural gas reserves at year-end 2010, accounted\par for approximately 78% of Russia's natural gas production in 2010 and owns and\par operates the Unified Gas Supply System, which includes approximately 162,000\par kilometres of high pressure pipelines.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coal\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's A, B and C1 coal reserves were estimated at 194 billion tonnes as of\par 1 January 2011. Currently, nearly all of Russia's coal is extracted by\par companies in the private sector. As a result of the restructuring of the coal\par industry following the dissolution of the Soviet Union, together with growing\par demand, coal production increased by 10.1% between 2005 and 2008. In 2009, coal\par production nearly returned to 2005 levels. Approximately 301.3 million tonnes\par of coal were produced in Russia in 2009, a decrease of approximately 8.3%\par compared to 2008 caused mainly by the global financial crisis. 322 million\par tonnes of coal were produced in Russia in 2010, an increase of approximately\par 6.6% compared to 2009, and 333.8 million tonnes of coal were produced in 2011,\par an increase of approximately 3.8% compared to 2010. In 2011, coal exports\par amounted to approximately 110.6 million tonnes, compared to 115.7 million\par tonnes in 2010. Columbia, the United States and Ukraine are the primary\par destinations for Russia's coal exports.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Electricity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2010, Russia's electricity output was 1,038 billion kWh, an increase of 4.6%\par compared to 2009. Of the total output in 2010, thermal power plants accounted\par for 699.2 billion kWh (67.4%), hydrogenation for 168.4 billion kWh (16.2%) and\par nuclear generation for 170.4 billion kWh (16.4%). In 2011, Russia's electricity\par output equalled 1,050.2 billion kWh, an increase of 1.2% compared to 2010. Of\par the total output in 2011, thermal power plants accounted for 713 billion kWh\par (67.8%), hydrogenation for 165 billion kWh (15.7%) and nuclear generation for\par 173 billion kWh (16.5%).\par \par As at 1 January 2010, total Russian generating capacity was 211.8 GW, 7.0% of\par which was attributable to thermal power plants, 20% to hydro power plants and\par 10% to nuclear power plants. Electricity is exported primarily to CIS\par countries, China, Finland, Latvia, Lithuania and Norway.\par \par Following the collapse of the Soviet Union, in 1992, the Russian electricity\par industry was initially restructured and split into RAO Unified Energy Systems\par ("UES"), 72 regional energy companies (energos), and a separate nuclear power\par industry operated by state-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosenergoatom\f28 \uc2 \f2   (re-named the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Energoatom Concern\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par in 2008).\par \par Until the restructuring of UES, described below, UES was the largest producer\par of electricity in Russia. At year-end 2007, UES owned more than 72.2% of\par Russia's generating capacity and was the monopoly high voltage distributor and\par wholesale purchaser and seller of electricity. Until its sell-off, the Russian\par Government owned approximately 53% of UES.\par \par In recent years, the Russian electricity industry has undergone a major\par restructuring, including the break-up of almost all vertically-integrated power\par companies controlled by UES and the formation of new mono-profile companies\par with the following activity types: generation, transmission, distribution,\par retail sales and repairs and servicing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The restructuring process\par culminated on 1 July 2008 in the de-merging of UES into more than 20\par independent companies, each engaged in either the competitive sector (power\par generation) or the non-competitive sector (transmission and distribution).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The electricity generation sector is currently principally comprised of thermal power plants, hydro power plants and nuclear generation complexes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The thermal power plants, which are powered by fossil fuels, are owned\par primarily by OGKs (wholesale generating companies, of which there are six),\par TGKs (regional generating companies, of which there are 14) and several other\par energy companies, such as OJSC \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bashkirenergo\f28 \uc2 \f2   and OJSC \f28 \uc2 \f2 Irkutskenergo\f28 \uc2 \f2  . The OGKs\par are the largest generators in the wholesale electricity (capacity) market\par except for \f28 \uc2 \f2 RusHydro\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mosenergo\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Each OGK owns plants in different regions in\par order to minimise competition abuse. With the exception of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mosenergo\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the TGKs\par are smaller than the OGKs (as measured by capacity and output) and operate\par principally on a regional level.\par \par State-controlled \f28 \uc2 \f2 RusHydro\f28 \uc2 \f2   owns more than 50% of the installed capacity of hydro\par power plants, with the remainder owned by several TGKs and other regional\par energy companies. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Energoatom Concern\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a wholly State-owned company, operates\par all of Russia's nuclear plants.\par \par Russia's transmission network of high-voltage power lines is operated by the\par state-owned Federal Grid Company. Most of the country's medium- and low-voltage\par distribution networks are consolidated into 11 inter-regional distribution\par companies, which are indirectly controlled by the Russian Federation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail electricity sales are made by the electricity companies that were spun-off from the energos and sold by UES in public auctions and other independent electricity retail companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2004, all dispatching functions of the Russian electricity sector were transferred from the energos to the System Operator of the Unified Energy System, which is 100% state-owned.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Russian electricity market is divided into a wholesale electricity market,\par a capacity market and a retail market. On 1 September 2006, new rules governing\par the wholesale electricity market took effect pending full liberalisation of the\par sector. Under these rules, trading in electricity is conducted on the basis of\par the following mechanisms: regulated contracts; unregulated bilateral contracts;\par the "one-day-ahead" market; and the balancing market. Regulated contracts are\par effectively take-or-pay obligations at a regulated tariff for electricity and\par capacity volumes. Each generator (except those facilities commissioned after\par 2007) is required to enter into regulated contracts covering a certain\par percentage of its planned 2007 annual output. This percentage, which was\par reduced semi-annually, was 35-40% for the six months ended 30 June 2010 and\par 15-20% for the six months ended 31 December 2010. Since 1 January 2011,\par wholesale electricity generators are no longer obligated to sell electricity\par under regulated contracts. The volumes of planned generation that are not\par traded under regulated contracts may be sold pursuant to unregulated bilateral\par contracts or traded on the one-day-ahead market or balancing market. The\par one-day-ahead market is effectively a spot market for those who wish to trade\par electricity at unregulated prices. Participants on the one-day-ahead market\par submit bids for the purchase and sale of electricity for a certain volume and\par price for each hour of the following day. The balancing market was established\par as a real-time platform to allow the trading of electricity in order to cover\par deviations on the one-day-ahead market between planned power volumes and\par volumes actually generated or consumed.\par \par Since 1 September 2006, electricity capacity has been sold separately from\par electricity. Currently, capacity is sold on the wholesale market pursuant to\par both regulated and unregulated contracts. Beginning in 2008, there has been a\par gradual liberalisation of the capacity market similar to the liberalisation\par underway on the wholesale electricity market. From 1 July to 31 December 2010,\par at least 15-20% of a generator's capacity must be sold under regulated\par contracts. The remaining capacity (and any capacity from power plants\par commissioned in 2008 or later) can be sold at unregulated prices. Pursuant to\par resolutions adopted in 2010, new rules governing the capacity market went into\par effect on 1 January 2011. Under these new rules, capacity is divided into two\par types: (i) capacity to be sold pursuant to capacity auctions, which includes\par existing capacity, and (ii) capacity to be constructed under 10-year capacity\par supply agreements, which is expected to be sold at tariffs indexed to inflation\par and designed to compensate the generating company for standard capital\par expenses, operating expenses, property tax, gas and electricity grid connection\par costs as well as allow a certain rate of return on investments and a payback\par period of 15 years.\par \par New rules governing the retail electricity market also came into effect on\par 1 September 2006. Purchasers on the retail market include (i) large industrial\par consumers, which pay for electricity supplies pursuant to a formula that takes\par into account the cost of electricity in the competitive wholesale electricity\par market; and (ii) households, which pay according to below-market tariffs set by\par a state regulator. Therefore, until full liberalisation of the retail market,\par which is expected to occur in conjunction with deregulation of the wholesale\par market, industrial consumers will continue to, in effect, subsidise retail\par consumers.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Agriculture\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia's agricultural sector has faced significant challenges in recent years,\par as major structural changes have been implemented under difficult conditions.\par Since the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Government subsidies to the\par agricultural sector have decreased significantly, the state food purchasing\par monopoly has been replaced by a decentralised market system and the share of\par agricultural output purchased by the state has fallen steadily.\par \par The agricultural sector consists primarily of large agricultural concerns\par (estimated to account for approximately 66% of arable land and 44.5% of\par agricultural output in monetary terms) and private plots, including both\par private farm collectives and individual farms (estimated to account for 34% of\par arable land and 55.5% of agricultural output in monetary terms). The\par Agricultural Land Code, which came into effect in January 2003, created a\par unified market framework at the federal level for the purchase and sale of\par farmland. It also prohibits the non-agricultural use of farmland, regulates the\par amount of farmland any one owner may hold and prohibits foreign ownership of\par farmland.\par \par Agricultural output declined by 9.7% in 2010 compared to 2009. In the summer of\par 2010, due to uncharacteristically hot weather, Russia suffered a severe drought\par that caused tens of billions of roubles in damage, led to an increase in prices\par for many agricultural products and set off thousands of wildfires. As a result\par of the drought, Russia's 2010 grain harvest was 61 million tonnes, 37.3% lower\par than the harvest in 2009, and Russia imposed a temporary ban on the export of\par grain, which was rescinded in July 2011. Russia's grain harvest recovered in\par 2011, equaling 94.2 million tonnes. See "-Environment," below, for a discussion\par of the environmental impact of the drought and wildfires. In 2011, agricultural\par output increased by 16.1%. While Russia still imports substantial amounts of\par food and agricultural products, foodstuffs and agricultural products amounted\par to 2.2% and 2.3% of total exports in 2010 and 2011, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Construction\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The construction sector accounted for approximately 6.7% of gross added value\par in 2010 and 6.6% of gross added value in 2011. Following a steep decline after\par the dissolution of the Soviet Union and until the onset of the global financial\par crisis, output in the construction industry grew significantly. In 2007 and\par 2008, the construction sector grew by 13% and 11.1%, respectively. As a result\par of the global financial crisis, the construction sector experienced a 14.7%\par decline in output in 2009 compared to 2008, including a contraction in new\par housing construction from 64.1 million square metres in 2008 to 59.9 million\par square metres in 2009. Construction output expanded again in 2010 and 2011,\par increasing by 3.2% and 4.8%, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Transport and Communications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russia has a railway network, a large merchant fleet, a large number of\par airports, a developed system of municipal transport, a comprehensive road\par network, modern telecommunications infrastructure and a major space industry.\par The market for the provision of transport and communication services has been\par liberalised and restructured, particularly in automotive transport, air\par transport, water transport and communications. Despite a 8.6% decline in 2009\par compared to 2008, the transportation and communications sector grew by 8.7%\par between 2007 and 2011, supported by a 4.8% increase in 2010 compared to 2009\par and a 2.9% increase in 2011 relative to 2010. In 2010 and 2011, the\par transportation and communications sector accounted for 9.6% and 8.9% of gross\par added value, respectively.\par \par Railways\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At year-end 2008, there were approximately 86,000 kilometres of railways in Russia for general passenger and freight transportation. In 2010 and 2011, railways accounted for approximately 85% of all freight transport in tonne-kilometres (excluding pipeline transport) and for over 30% of all passenger journeys in passenger-kilometres, making it the country's most important form of transport.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Tariffs for passenger transport are currently subsidised from the railway\par freight profits of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2   and from the federal budget. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian\par Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which is wholly-state owned and was founded in 2003, plays a\par significant role in the Russian railway sector. Among other activities, it is\par the owner and operator of the Russian Federation's integrated passenger and\par freight network and related infrastructure, owns and operates nearly all of the\par locomotives in Russia, is the largest Russian owner, operator and lessor of\par freight rolling stock and the largest Russian freight rail operator and,\par through its subsidiary the Federal Passenger Company, carries nearly all\par suburban and long-haul rail passengers. Substantial funding has been allocated\par to the renovation and maintenance of railway track and locomotive parks, as\par well as to the replacement of rolling stock.\par \par The Government has developed a multi-stage programme of structural reforms in\par the railway sector. Key aspects of the reforms include (i) increasing the\par safety of railway transport as well as the quality of services offered by the\par railway sector; (ii) meeting the increasing demand for railway transport; and\par (iii) enhancing the overall efficiency of the railway sector. As part of these\par reforms, and in an effort to foster competition and increase private investment\par in the industry, control and management functions of the railway sector were\par separated, including through the establishment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the\par spin-off of non-core assets of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the creation of new\par independent operating companies. Currently, more than 65% of freight cars are\par under the control of such private operating companies. The Government has also\par taken steps toward reducing or partially eliminating cross-subsidies, including\par the creation of a federal passenger company in April 2010. The third stage of\par the reform programme, currently underway, also envisages the privatisation of\par subsidiaries of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the approval of a market model for freight\par transport through 2015 and the transition to a new system of tariff regulation.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Roads\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As of 1 January 2011, there were approximately 1,004,000 kilometres of roads in\par Russia (including both public- and private-use roads), approximately 78% of\par which have hard surfaces. Some 83% of the hard-surface roads are in public use,\par 70% of which are under regional or municipal ownership, 23% of which are under\par local ownership and some 8% of which are under federal control. 17%, or\par approximately 128,905 kilometres, of the hard-surface roads are not in public\par use, are controlled by legal entities and generally are used to connect with\par the public road network. Roads falling under federal jurisdiction are renovated\par and maintained by means of the federal budget, while roads falling under the\par jurisdiction of Federation subjects are renovated and maintained by the\par Federation subjects in which they are located through the respective regional\par and local budget as well as transfers from the federal budget.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ports and Shipping\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Currently, Russia has 64 seaports. In the first 11 months of 2011, the volume\par of freight shipments in Russian seaports amounted to 489.3 million tonnes, an\par increase of 1.5% compared to the first 11 months of 2010. In 2011, freight\par turnover by means of sea transport equalled 77.5 billion tonne-kilometres, a\par decrease of 22.8% compared to 2010. In November 2007, a new federal law took\par effect that regulates the activities of seaports and intends to promote the\par growth of commercial transport by sea. Substantial resources are being invested\par to improve the competitiveness of Russian ports.\par \par As of 1 January 2010, Russia had approximately 101,500 kilometres of internal\par navigable waterways with 723 hydraulic installations, including 110 locks as\par well as several pumping stations, pressure dikes and dams and canals. In 2011,\par freight turnover on Russia's internal waterways equalled approximately\par 61 billion tonne-kilometres, representing 2.4% of Russia's total freight\par turnover (excluding pipeline transport), an increase of 13% compared to freight\par turnover in 2010.\par \par Air Transport\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At year-end 2011, Russia had 236 airports, 69 of which were international. The largest international airports are located in Moscow, St. Petersburg, Yekaterinburg and Novosibirsk. According to preliminary estimates, in 2011, turnover of passengers traveling by air equalled approximately 167 billion passenger-kilometres, a 13.4% increase compared to 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aeroflot\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the monopoly carrier until 1991. Following the dissolution of the\par Soviet Union, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aeroflot\f28 \uc2 \f2  was split into 140 regional airlines and an\par international carrier, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aeroflot-Russian International Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Most of the\par regional airlines were subsequently privatised. In 1995, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aeroflot-Russian\par International Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2   was partially privatised, although the state continues\par to own 51% of its shares. As of July 2011, 151 airlines were registered to\par operate in Russia, the 10 largest of which accounted for roughly 80.5% of\par passenger turnover. In 2011, the largest airlines in terms of market share were\par Open Joint Stock Company "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Aeroflot-Russian Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2  ," Open Joint Stock Company\par "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Transaero Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2  " and Open Joint Stock Company "\f28 \uc2 \f2 UTair Aviation\f28 \uc2 \f2 ."\par \par The largest Russian airports are those situated in Moscow, which handled more\par than 51% of airline passenger throughput in 2010. Domodedovo Airport,\par Sheremyetevo Airport and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnukovo Airport\f28 \uc2 \f2   were responsible for 22.4%, 19% and\par 9.5%, respectively, of passenger throughput in 2010. The largest regional air\par traffic hubs in Russia include St. Petersburg, Ekaterinburg and Novosibirsk.\par Airport infrastructure in Russia is developing rapidly. At \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sheremetyevo\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par Airport, a new Terminal C opened in March 2007, a new Terminal D opened in\par November 2009, a new Terminal E opened in April 2010, the planned transit\par capacity of which is expected to be seven million passengers per year, and a\par new Terminal A for business travelers opened in January 2012. In February 2009,\par Joint Stock Company "International Airport \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sheremetyevo\f28 \uc2 \f2 " adopted a master plan\par for development of the airport through 2030. Pursuant to this plan, a new\par luggage sorting system was installed at the airport, and Terminal F (formerly\par known as Terminal 2) was expanded by 4,000 square metres. According to the\par master plan, which also envisages modernisation of Terminal B (formerly known\par as Terminal 1), overall transit capacity of the airport is expected to reach\par 64 million passengers per year.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pipelines\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of the end of 2010, Russia had approximately 233,000 kilometres of trunk pipelines, consisting of approximately 168,000 kilometres of natural gas pipelines, 49,000 kilometres of oil pipelines and 16,000 kilometres of oil product pipelines. In 2011, freight turnover by means of pipeline transport totalled 2,421.9 billion tonne-kilometres, an increase of 1.7% compared with 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Pipelines from major oil- and gas-producing areas in Russia are generally\par connected to pipelines in the CIS and former Soviet bloc countries. A number of\par significant new pipeline projects are planned or under construction. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  ,\par together with international partners, has completed the Yamal-Europe project,\par building new trunk pipelines that extend nearly 6,000 kilometres from the\par northern part of the Tyumen region of Russia through Belarus and Poland to\par Germany. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   is also participating in the Nord Stream pipeline\par construction, expected to extend 1,200 kilometres from Vyborg, Russia to\par Griefswald, Germany. Nord Stream's first pipeline went into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par November 2011. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   has also constructed a trunk pipeline to Turkey (the\par "Blue Stream project") that crosses the Black Sea, significantly increasing\par Russia's gas export capacity. Initial gas flows through the undersea portion of\par this pipeline commenced in December 2002, with the first commercial delivery to\par Turkey in February 2003. The pipeline for the Blue Stream project has an annual\par projected capacity of 16 billion cubic metres. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   is considering supplying\par gas to Europe by other export routes, including under the Black Sea to the\par coast of Bulgaria (the South Stream project).\par \par New pipeline-related projects intended to increase the export capacity of the\par oil industry are also being implemented or planned in Europe and Asia,\par including the Baltic pipeline system, which connects West Siberia with a major\par new harbour at St. Petersburg. The project was completed in 2006 and increased\par annual pipeline capacity to 65 million tonnes of crude oil. Other programmes\par currently underway include the Baltic pipeline system-2, the construction of\par which began in June 2009 and which is expected to have an annual pipeline\par capacity of up to 50 million tonnes of crude oil, upgrading the Druzhba oil\par pipeline through Eastern Europe by constructing by-passes and extensions and\par the construction of the East Siberia-Pacific Ocean pipeline, which is expected\par to have a capacity of 80 million tonnes and will extend from Taishet to\par Primorski Krai, connecting West and East Siberia with the Asia-Pacific region.\par The first section of the East Siberia-Pacific pipeline came on line in December\par 2009. Work on the second, and final, phase began in January 2010.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A significant portion of Russia's existing oil pipeline network has been in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for at least 20 years. The gas pipeline network is also ageing. Repair and maintenance costs are accordingly high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The crude oil and oil product trunk pipeline networks are owned and managed by\par state-owned monopolies, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transneft\f28 \uc2 \f2   and Transnefteprodukt, respectively.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transneft\f28 \uc2 \f2  's pipeline capacity, including its export pipeline capacity, is\par allocated quarterly to oil producers, generally in proportion to the amount of\par oil produced and delivered to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transneft\f28 \uc2 \f2  's pipeline network in the prior\par quarter. Generally, a Russian oil company is given an allocation for export to\par non-CIS countries equal to approximately one-third of the total crude oil it\par produces and delivers to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transneft\f28 \uc2 \f2  . The gas trunk pipeline network is owned and\par managed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Third-party access to the gas trunk pipeline network is\par determined generally on the basis of available spare transport capacity, the\par quality and technical parameters of the natural gas supplies, the availability\par of supplier input connections and customer output connections and customer\par demand.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Telecommunications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2011, the value of the information technology market was estimated to be\par 649 billion roubles. The value of telephone services in 2011 was approximately\par 1.4 trillion roubles, an increase of 6.1% compared to 2010. There are over\par 4,500 telephone operators on the local market. By November 2011, there were\par approximately 226.4 million mobile telephone subscribers, a market penetration\par rate of 156%. Mobile phone services are available across the entirety of the\par Russian Federation.\par \par In recent years, in an effort to lower administrative barriers to entry as well\par as enhance customer choice, the market for intercity and international\par telephone services began to undergo a process of liberalisation. Over 65\par licences have already been issued for the provision of intercity and\par international telephone services. Recipients of these licences include\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rostelecom\f28 \uc2 \f2  , Transtelecom, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Interregional Transit Telecom\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vimpelcom\f28 \uc2 \f2  , Megafon,\par Sinterra, Comstar-OTS and others.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategic Enterprises\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Decree of the President of the Russian Federation No. 1009 dated 4 August\par 2004 established a list of strategic enterprises that are strategically\par important to state defence and security or to the public morals, health or\par rights of the citizens of the Russian Federation. In June 2010, as part of the\par country's modernisation policy, President Medvedev signed a decree, reducing\par the number of federal state unitary enterprises on this list from 295 to 165\par and the number of joint stock companies considered to be strategic enterprises\par from 214 to 48. Currently, there are approximately 150 federal state unitary\par enterprises and 50 joint stock companies on the list of strategic enterprises.\par Strategic enterprises include, among others, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aeroflot\f28 \uc2 \f2  and\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transneft\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which operates an extensive crude oil pipeline network in the\par country. Such companies, which may be privatised only upon their removal from\par the list by decision of the President, are subject to specific rules of\par corporate governance. For example, the Government may retain operating control\par over these companies through the exercise of a "golden share," and dilution of\par the Government's stake in such companies remains subject to approval by the\par President.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sochi Winter Olympics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The southern city of Sochi will host the XXII Olympic Winter Games in 2014, the\par first time that the Winter Olympics will be held in the Russian Federation. In\par preparation for the Games, the Government spent approximately 197.8 billion\par roubles from the federal budget in 2010. The total costs of preparing for the\par Games is expected to reach one trillion roubles. A substantial portion of the\par financing will be spent on improving the power, transportation and\par telecommunications infrastructure in and around the Sochi region and on\par building and modernising sports venues and hotels. The Government created the\par state corporation Olympstroi to oversee preparations for the Games.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Environment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Following a 2005 Government restructuring, environmental protection in Russia\par has primarily been the responsibility of the Federal Service for Ecological,\par Technological and Atomic Supervision. Environmental regulations require\par enterprises to pay fees for emissions or discharges of most pollutants. These\par fees, which are low by international standards, may be used to fund investment\par to improve the environment. Russia's environmental protection programme has\par focused on replanting forests, constructing spent gas treatment plants,\par installing water recycling systems and constructing sewage purification plants.\par A major component of the country's Development Programme through 2020 is to\par establish priority measures for protecting the environment, including tax\par incentives for enterprises using ecologically clean technology.\par \par In the summer of 2010, due to uncharacteristically hot weather, Russia suffered\par a severe drought that led to thousands of wildfires and caused tens of billions\par of roubles in damage. As a result of the fires, thousands of homes were\par destroyed across European Russia, and, in August, toxic emissions in the\par atmosphere over European Russia, including carbon monoxide, reached\par significantly higher levels. In August 2010, the Government announced that, in\par addition to expenses connected with battling the fires, it had spent\par approximately five billion roubles on compensating victims and repairing or\par replacing homes. See "-Principal Sectors of the Economy-Agriculture," above,\par for a discussion of the agricultural impact of the drought and wildfires.\par \par In November 2004, President Putin signed the Kyoto Protocol to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 United\par Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which became effective in\par February 2005. Under the terms of the Kyoto Protocol, Russia is to maintain its\par emissions of greenhouse gases at a level no higher than the level prevalent\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in\par 1990.\par \par Employment\par \par Overall employment declined in Russia from the dissolution of the Soviet Union\par through 1999. Beginning in 2000 and until the onset of the global financial\par crisis in the second half of 2008, employment was increasing, in large part due\par to the rise in export volumes and import substitution that began in 1999. The\par rate of increase in employment was less than the rate of increase in real GDP,\par however, due to gains in labour productivity. Based on the methodology of the\par ILO, the average number of unemployed workers in 2006 and 2007 was 5.3 million,\par or 7.2% of the economically active population, and 4.6 million, or 6.1% of the\par economically active population, respectively. The decline in unemployment\par levels during this period was primarily due to strong domestic demand and a\par positive economic outlook. In late 2008, unemployment began to rise. In the\par fourth quarter of 2008, the average number of unemployed workers rose to\par 5.4 million, or 7.1% of the economically active population, from an average of\par 4.4 million, or 5.9% of the economically active population, in the third\par quarter of 2008. Unemployment continued to rise in 2009, with the average\par number of unemployed workers in 2009 amounting to 6.4 million, or 8.4% of the\par economically active population. The rise in unemployment in the second half of\par 2008 and in 2009 was primarily the result of the global financial crisis, and,\par in particular, of a contraction both in domestic demand and in worldwide demand\par for Russian products. As the Russian economy began to recover from the global\par financial crisis, the average number of unemployed workers fell to 5.7 million,\par or 7.5% of the economically active population, in 2010, and to 5 million, or\par 6.6% of the economically active population, in 2011.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth quarterly information regarding unemployment in Russia for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Unemployment (quarterly average)                        \par \par                           Unemployed                          Registered             \par                        (ILO definition)                       unemployed             \par                              (1)                                 (2)                 \par                                                            