      \par                                                                                      \par Fourth Quarter                      4.4         5.8%     1.5         2.0%            \par                                                                                      \par 2008                                                                                 \par                                                                                      \par First Quarter                       5.1         6.7%     1.6         2.1%            \par                                                                                      \par Second Quarter                      4.3         5.6%     1.4         1.9%            \par                                                                                      \par Third Quarter                       4.4         5.9%     1.3         1.7%            \par                                                                                      \par Fourth Quarter                      5.4         7.1%     1.4         1.8%            \par                                                                                      \par 2009                                                                                 \par                                                                                      \par First Quarter                       6.8         9.1%     2.0         2.6%            \par                                                                                      \par Second Quarter                      6.5         8.6%     2.2         2.9%            \par                                                                                      \par Third Quarter                       6.0         7.8%     2.1         2.7%            \par                                                                                      \par Fourth Quarter                      6.1         8.0%     2.1         2.7%            \par                                                                                      \par 2010                                                                                 \par                                                                                      \par First Quarter                       6.6         8.8%     2.2         3.0%            \par                                                                                      \par Second Quarter                      5.6         7.4%     2.0         2.7%            \par                                                                                      \par Third Quarter                       5.2         6.8%     1.7         2.2%            \par                                                                                      \par Fourth Quarter                      5.2         6.9%     1.5         2.1%            \par                                                                                      \par 2011                                                                                 \par                                                                                      \par First Quarter                       5.6         7.5%     1.6         2.2%            \par                                                                                      \par Second Quarter                      5.0         6.6%     1.5         2.0%            \par                                                                                      \par Third Quarter                       4.8         6.2%     1.3         1.7%            \par                                                                                      \par Fourth Quarter                      4.7         6.3%     1.2         1.6%            \par \par \par Notes:\par \par (1) Numbers through the second quarter of 2009 are based on data from surveys\par carried out quarterly. Subsequent numbers are based on surveys carried out\par monthly. Persons between the ages of 15 and 72 not working, looking for a job\par and ready to start work are counted as unemployed.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Based on number of individuals who choose to register as unemployed with the various employment agencies, as recorded by the Federal Labour and Employment Service.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par These figures do not take into account certain "hidden unemployment" resulting\par from shortened workdays and temporary lay-offs. Based on monthly averages,\par workers working reduced hours totaled 1,033.4 thousand in 2009, 402.7 thousand\par in 2010 and 283.5 thousand in 2011, and workers put on leave without pay\par equalled 886.6 thousand in 2009, 621.3 thousand in 2010 and 627.3 thousand in\par 2011. Beginning in March 2010, the number of workers working reduced hours or\par put on leave without pay began to decrease due, in part, to the various\par economic and social measures adopted in support of domestic industries.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wages and Income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Real wages increased by 17.2% in 2007. In 2008, primarily due to higher\par inflation and the effects of the financial crisis on the labour market in the\par fourth quarter of 2008, the growth in real wages decelerated to 11.5%. In 2009,\par real wages declined by 3.5% compared to 2008 primarily due to inflation and the\par negative impact of the global financial crisis on private-sector nominal wage\par levels. As the economy began to recover after the global financial crisis, real\par wages began to increase, growing by 5.2% in 2010 compared to 2009 and by 3.5%\par (according to preliminary estimates) in 2011 compared to 2010.\par \par The minimum wage has remained relatively low, amounting to just 19.9% of the\par average monthly wage as of June 2010. The minimum wage, established by the\par State Duma, serves as a benchmark in setting the level of federal benefits and\par also plays a role in determining certain types of budget expenditures and the\par size of unemployment benefits. The minimum wage was increased by approximately\par 37.5% in May 2006 (to 1,100 roubles/month), by an additional 109.1% in\par September 2007 (to 2,300 roubles/month), by an additional 88.3% in January 2009\par (to 4,330 roubles/month) and then once again in June 2011 by 6.5% (to its\par current level of 4,611 roubles/month). Beginning 1 September 2007, each\par Federation subject has discretion to set the minimum wage in its territory,\par provided it is not lower than the amount set by the federal Government.\par \par Approximately 13.3% and 13.4% of the population had income below the official\par subsistence level in 2007 and 2008, respectively, compared to 15.2% in 2006.\par This decline was the result primarily of consistently high income growth. In\par the first nine months of 2009, the percentage of the population falling below\par the subsistence level increased slightly to 14% due mainly to the negative\par effects of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy, including the\par incomes of Russian citizens. By year-end 2009, however, this percentage dropped\par to 13.2%, due, in part, to a decline in the unemployment rate in the fourth\par quarter of 2009. In 2010 and the first nine months of 2011, the percentage of\par people living beneath the subsistence level equalled 12.6% and 14.3%,\par respectively. Between 2005 and 2007, real disposable income increased by\par approximately 43%. In 2008 and 2009, growth in real disposable income\par decelerated, increasing by 2.4% in 2008 compared to 2007 and 3.1% in 2009\par compared to 2008, due mainly to the global financial crisis. Real disposable\par income increased by 5.1% in 2010 compared to 2009 and by 0.8% in 2011 compared\par to 2010.\par \par Organised labour does not play a prominent role in the Russian economy. Whereas\par nearly 100% of workers in the Soviet Union were unionised, approximately 50% of\par Russian workers are now members of labour organisations.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Social Benefits and Expenditure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Social security reforms were introduced in Russia beginning in 1991, with new institutions founded to deliver social benefits, including unemployment insurance and benefits for the very poor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Total social expenditure (other than housing subsidies), based on the enlarged\par budget, amounted to approximately 17.6% of GDP in 2007, 17.7% of GDP in 2008,\par 21.3% of GDP in 2009, 22.6% of GDP in 2010 and 20.7% of GDP in 2011.\par \par Most social expenditure is provided through state extra-budgetary funds or by\par sub-federal authorities. In 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, 43.2%, 44.1%, 46%,\par 51.7% and 47.2%, respectively, of social expenditure was provided by state\par extra-budgetary funds, and 43.5%, 42%, 39.5%, 36% and 40.4%, respectively, was\par provided by sub-federal authorities. Direct payments from the federal budget\par accounted for approximately 13.3%, 13.9%, 14.5%, 12.3% and 12.4% of social\par expenditure in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. The state\par extra-budgetary funds finance expenditure on health, certain social benefits\par and pensions. Sub-federal budgets finance housing and transportation subsidies,\par most education and health expenditures and certain social benefits, while the\par federal budget is responsible for a portion of the expenditure on education,\par health, culture and social benefits. See "Public Finance-Federal-Sub-Federal\par Fiscal Relations."\par \par In 2005, several benefit-related fiscal measures took effect. These measures\par eliminated unfunded benefit entitlements and replaced social privileges\par financed by the federal budget, e.g., free passenger transportation and free\par medical drugs, with targeted social benefits largely in the form of monthly\par cash compensation of between 50 roubles and 2,000 roubles, adjusted for\par inflation. This monetisation of federal social benefits is available to the\par disabled, World War II veterans, survivors of the siege of Leningrad, Chernobyl\par cleanup workers, heroes of the Soviet Union and Russia and certain other\par groups.\par \par Decisions related to the eligibility for and the provision of social benefits\par and compensation to other groups is delegated to regional authorities. Regional\par beneficiaries include labour veterans, victims of Soviet-era political\par repression, people with dependent children and students.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pension Reform\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Pension reform legislation was enacted in 2001, and a new pension system came\par into effect on 1 January 2002. Under this system, which remained in effect\par through 2009, a retiree's pension consisted of a basic pension, an insurance\par pension and a funded pension. Amendments to the pension legislation were passed\par in 2009, according to which a retiree's pension, effective 1 January 2010,\par consists of an insurance pension and a funded pension.\par \par The insurance pension is made up of two components: a fixed amount, which is\par set by law, and a variable amount, which is dependent on past contributions by\par the pensioner and length of employment. The fixed amount, in effect, replaces\par the pre-2010 basic pension. The basic pension was funded from the budget\par through payments of the Unified Social Tax (the "UST"). The UST was abolished\par as of 1 January 2010, and as a result both elements of the insurance pension\par come from the Pension Fund of the Russian Federation (the "Pension Fund").\par \par The funded pension is based on the amount of funds accumulated in a pensioner's\par individual pension account, which in turn depends on the beneficiary's year of\par birth. For those born before 1967, all mandatory contributions to the pension\par system are made to the insurance pension. For those born in 1967 or after, the\par share of contributions accumulated in funded pensions now must equal 6% of the\par first 512,000 roubles of the employee's annual salary. Regardless of age, an\par employee may elect to make funded pension contributions in excess of his\par mandatory pension contributions. For voluntary contributions between 2,000\par roubles and 12,000 roubles per year, the Government will make a matching\par contribution, provided the employee files the appropriate application before\par 1 October 2013.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Pension Law envisages that the size of pensions will be adjusted for inflation and growth in average salary levels. The most recent indexation took place in February 2012, when the average pension amount was increased by 7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Contributions to the funded pension system are collected by the Pension Fund\par and are managed by state and private trust companies and private pension funds.\par According to the Law On Investments for Financing the Funded Part of Pensions,\par enacted in 2002, individuals may choose once a year a private trust manager\par from those selected on the basis of a public tender or a private pension fund.\par The initial tender for private trust managers was held in September 2003, and\par 55 private trust companies were selected. Individuals were required to choose a\par trust manager by 31 December 2003, and funds were transferred to private trust\par companies in the beginning of 2004. As of January 2012, there were 116 private\par pension funds and 52 private trust companies. Only a limited number of\par individuals choose private trust companies or private pension funds, leaving\par most pension savings to be managed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which was appointed as\par the State Trust Management Company. As the State Trust Management Company,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   is responsible for the management of funds held by individuals\par who selected \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   as their pension fund manager, as well as funds\par held by those citizens who did not select a trust company or private pension\par fund. Private trust companies and private pension funds may invest pension\par savings in various assets, including securities of Russian private and public\par issuers, rouble and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bank deposits and shares in foreign\par securities index funds. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   may invest pension savings only in\par certain types of assets, including Russian state securities, securities issued\par by subfederal political units, certain types of bonds issued by Russian\par issuers, certain types of mortgage securities, rouble and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bank\par deposits and rouble and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  accounts with credit organisations.\par See "Public Finance-State Extra-Budgetary Funds" for further discussion of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the\par Pension Fund.\par \par                      BALANCE OF PAYMENTS AND FOREIGN TRADE                     \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Balance of Payments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth the balance of payments of the Russian Federation for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par               Balance of Payments of the Russian Federation(1)(2)              \par \par                              (billions of dollars)                             \par \par                                       For the                     For the nine        \par                                      year ended                   months ended        \par                                      31 December                  30 September        \par                                                