                                                                                  \par import advances                                                                                     \par not repaid in                                                                                       \par time(4)                                                                                             \par                                                                                                     \par Other assets             (0.8)   (1.0)   (5.3)   (0.9)   (0.4)   (1.9)   (3.2)                      \par                                                                                                     \par Liabilities               30.3   139.8    63.2  (25.2)    10.0     4.6    24.5                      \par                                                                                                     \par Cash domestic              0.3     0.9     1.4     0.7     0.0     0.0   (0.2)                      \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                                                             \par                                                                                                     \par Demand and time           14.6    11.6   (4.2)   (2.3)    10.2    12.1     5.5                      \par deposits                                                                                            \par                                                                                                     \par Trade credits and            -       -       -     0.6     0.1     0.2     0.2                      \par advances received                                                                                   \par (3)                                                                                                 \par                                                                                                     \par Loans received            18.3   127.3    63.5  (32.0)   (0.8)  (11.0)    16.0                      \par (excl. arrears)                                                                                     \par                                                                                                     \par Arrears                  (3.6)   (1.3)     0.9   (0.7)   (0.2)     0.4     0.6                      \par                                                                                                     \par Other liabilities          0.7     1.3     1.6     8.5     0.7     2.9     2.4                      \par                                                                                                     \par Reserve Assets(5)      (107.5) (148.9)    38.9   (3.4)  (36.8)  (45.4)  (21.2)                      \par                                                                                                     \par Net errors and             9.5  (13.3)  (11.3)   (1.7)   (8.0)   (3.2)   (5.4)                      \par omissions                                                                                           \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Figures in this table are current as of 11 January 2012. Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by the Bank of Russia. For those transactions denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than the dollar, conversions into dollars are made at the exchange rate prevailing on the date of the relevant transaction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (2) Precise information on the volume of Russia's foreign trade is difficult to\par obtain because of the importance of so-called "unregistered trade," consisting\par of commercial (in contrast to private) transactions that escape customs records\par or are undervalued by customs authorities. Value adjustments for unregistered\par trade are made in the official balance of payments accounts. These adjustments\par amounted to approximately U.S.$30 billion in 2006, U.S.$29 billion in 2007,\par U.S.$32 billion in 2008, U.S.$28 billion in 2009, U.S.$23 billion in 2010 and\par U.S.$16 billion in the first nine months of 2011 (on a non-annualised basis).\par \par (3) Through 2008, received trade credits and advances in the export and import\par of services were reflected in the line item "trade credits and advances." For\par 2009, 2010 and the first nine months of 2010 and 2011, received trade credits\par and advances were reflected in the separate line item "trade credits and\par advances received."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes the estimated value of the non-supply of goods and services under import contracts and the estimated value of fictitious transactions in securities and accounts of residents abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) Changes in reserve assets that arise as a result of transactions with reserve assets, i.e., excluding monetisation of gold and valuation changes. This definition differs from that employed in the calculation of total official reserves. See "-Official Reserves."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Bank of Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Current Account\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Since 2000, Russia has registered a current account surplus. In 2006, Russia\par recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$94.7 billion (approximately 9.6% of\par GDP), an 11.9% increase compared to 2005. The growth in the current account\par surplus was primarily caused by a higher trade surplus, which was, in turn,\par caused largely by a 24.5% increase in the value of exported goods in 2006\par compared to 2005. The increase in exports, including oil and natural gas, was\par primarily driven by price, not volume. For example, in 2006 compared to 2005,\par the volume of oil exports decreased by 1.6%, whereas the value of oil exports\par increased by 22.6%. Similarly, in 2006 compared to 2005, the volume of natural\par gas exports decreased by 3.1%, while the value of gas exports increased by\par 38.3%. In 2006 compared to 2005, the average export price for Urals oil and for\par natural gas grew by 24.6% and 42.7%, respectively. Growth in the average export\par prices of certain other key commodities, including copper (76.3%), nickel\par (78.9%) and aluminium (26.8%), underpinned export growth over the same period.\par The value of imported goods increased by 31% in 2006 compared to 2005, largely\par as a result of the real appreciation of the rouble against the euro and dollar\par and growth in the overall economy. Despite the growth in the value of Russia's\par imported goods, Russia's trade surplus for goods was U.S.$139.3 billion, a\par 17.7% increase compared to 2005. The deficit in investment income increased by\par 42.1% from U.S.$17.8 billion in 2005 to U.S.$25.3 billion in 2006. The negative\par balance in employee compensation increased by 3.8 times from U.S.$1.1 billion\par to U.S.$4.2 billion.\par \par In 2007, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$77.8 billion\par (approximately 6% of GDP), a 17.8% decrease compared to 2006. This decrease was\par primarily the result of a lower trade surplus, combined with a weakening\par balance of trade in services and an increase in outflows of current transfers.\par In 2007, imports of goods increased by 36% compared to 2006. This growth was\par caused by the real appreciation of the rouble (by 12.8% against the dollar and\par 4.2% against the euro) and by the expansion of domestic demand, as reflected by\par the 8.5% real GDP growth rate. The rise in the value of imported goods was\par partially offset by a 17% increase in the value of exported goods, which was\par primarily price- and not volume-driven. For example, Russia recorded growth in\par the average export price for Urals oil (14.1%), iron ore and concentrates\par (29.8%), coal (15.2%), nickel (40.8%) and aluminum (16.3%) in 2007 compared to\par 2006. At the same time, as measured in volumes, there was a deceleration in the\par growth of oil and iron ore exports and a contraction in exports of natural gas\par and ferrous metals. The resulting trade balance for goods was\par U.S.$130.9 billion in 2007 compared to U.S.$139.3 billion in 2006. Also\par contributing to the decline in the current account surplus was an increase in\par both the negative balance of services (from U.S.$13.6 billion in 2006 to\par U.S.$18.9 billion in 2007), which was attributable mainly to a rise in imports,\par and the net outflow of current transfers (from U.S.$1.5 billion in 2006 to\par U.S.$3.5 billion in 2007). The investment income deficit decreased by 7.5%\par (from U.S.$25.3 billion in 2006 to U.S.$23.4 billion in 2007); however, it was\par offset by a 73.8% rise in the net outflow of employee compensation (from\par U.S.$4.2 billion in 2006 to U.S.$7.3 billion in 2007).\par \par In 2008, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$103.5 billion\par (approximately 6.3% of GDP), a 33% increase compared to 2007. This increase was\par caused predominantly by a higher trade surplus, due, in turn, to exports\par increasing at a faster rate than imports. The value of exported goods increased\par by 33.1% in 2008 compared to 2007, primarily as a result of an increase in\par export prices, in particular, the price of oil and petroleum products. For\par example, in 2008 compared to 2007, the volume of crude oil exports dropped by\par 5.8%, whereas the value of crude oil exports increased by 32.6%. The average\par export price for Urals oil grew by 41.2% in 2008 compared to 2007. Similarly,\par over the same period, increases in the value of natural gas exports (51.3%)\par outpaced increases in the volume of gas exports (1.8%). An increase in the\par average export price of coal (45.5%), iron ore and concentrates (74.9%),\par petroleum products (45.6%) and mixed fertilisers (258.2%) underpinned export\par growth in 2008 compared to 2007. Concurrently, the value of imported goods\par increased by 30.6%, primarily as a result of strong real appreciation of the\par rouble (13.4% against the dollar and 6.4% against the euro) and rising GDP in\par the first three quarters of 2008. Despite the growth in the value of Russia's\par imported goods, the trade surplus for goods increased by 37.3% compared to 2007\par to U.S.$179.7 billion. The increase in the trade surplus was partially offset\par by a rise in the services deficit and the investment income deficit, as well as\par in the net outflows of employee compensation. The negative balance of services\par increased by U.S.$5.4 billion in 2008 compared to 2007, amounting to\par U.S.$24.3 billion. This increase was due to imports of services rising at a\par faster rate than exports. The sharp increase in the investment income deficit\par (U.S.$23.4 billion in 2007 compared to U.S.$34.8 billion in 2008) was caused\par largely by a sharp rise in investment income payable. Net outflows in employee\par compensation increased by 97.3% to U.S.$14.4 billion in 2008, compared to\par U.S.$7.3 billion in 2007.\par \par In 2009, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$48.6 billion\par (approximately 4% of GDP), a 53% decrease compared to 2008. This sharp decline\par in the current account balance was primarily attributable to a 37.9% drop in\par the trade balance for goods from U.S.$179.7 billion in 2008 to\par U.S.$111.6 billion in 2009. In 2009, despite both nominal and real depreciation\par of the rouble, the value of exported goods fell significantly to\par U.S.$303.4 billion from U.S.$471.6 billion in 2008. This 35.7% decline in\par exports was primarily caused by a drop in both the price and volume of\par commodity exports as well as by the overall contraction of the economy due to\par the global financial crisis. The average export price for Urals oil, for\par example, declined by 38.8% in 2009 compared to 2008. Although the current\par account balance deteriorated during this period, the value of imported goods\par fell by 34.3% from U.S.$291.9 billion in 2008 to U.S.$191.8 billion in 2009,\par and the value of imported services fell by 18.7% from U.S.$75.5 billion in 2008\par to U.S.$61.4 billion in 2009 due to contracting domestic demand associated with\par the global financial crisis. In 2009, Russia also experienced a 38.2% decrease\par in net employee compensation outflows from U.S.$14.4 billion in 2008 to\par U.S.$8.9 billion in 2009 and an 9.8% rise in net investment income from a\par deficit of U.S.$34.8 billion in 2008 to a deficit of U.S.$31.4 billion in 2009.\par \par In 2010, Russia recorded a current account surplus of U.S.$70.3 billion\par (approximately 4.7% of GDP), which was 44.7% higher than the current account\par surplus in 2009. This increase in the current account balance was primarily\par attributable to an increase in the trade surplus for goods from\par U.S.$111.6 billion in 2009 to U.S.$151.7 billion in 2010, which, in turn, was\par caused by export prices and volumes increasing at a faster rate than import\par prices and volumes. For example, the average export price for Urals oil\par increased by 28.9% in 2010 compared to 2009. The export prices of Russian\par aluminum, copper and nickel were also higher in 2010 compared to 2009. Although\par the current account balance improved in 2010 compared to 2009, the value of\par imported goods and services increased by 27.6% from U.S.$253.2 billion in 2009\par to U.S.$323.1 billion in 2010. This increase was due primarily to an expansion\par in import volumes, which, in turn, was driven by the improvement in domestic\par demand in 2010 compared to 2009, as well as to a rise in import prices. In\par 2010, Russia also experienced a 27.7% increase in the net investment income\par deficit (from U.S.$31.4 billion in 2009 to U.S.$40.1 billion in 2010), which\par was due to an increase in investment income payable and partially offset by a\par rise in investment income received, and a 24.1% increase in the net current\par transfers deficit (from U.S.$2.9 billion in 2009 to U.S.$3.6 billion in 2010).\par \par In the first nine months of 2011, Russia recorded a current account surplus of\par U.S.$71.5 billion (approximately 5.6% of GDP), which was 24.1% higher than the\par current account surplus in the first nine months of 2010. This increase in the\par current account balance was primarily attributable to an increase in the trade\par surplus for goods from U.S.$115.3 billion in the first nine months of 2010 to\par U.S.$144.8 billion in the first nine months of 2011, which, in turn, was caused\par by export prices and volumes increasing at a faster rate than import prices and\par volumes. For example, the average world prices for Urals oil increased by 44.7%\par in the first nine months of 2011 compared to the analogous period in 2010.\par Despite general deceleration of the growth of world prices for aluminium,\par copper and nickel, the export prices of Russian aluminum, copper and nickel\par were also higher in the first nine months of 2011 compared to the first nine\par months of 2010. Although the current account balance improved in the first nine\par months of 2011 relative to the first nine months of 2010, the value of imported\par goods and services increased by 32.5% from U.S.$225.8 billion in the first nine\par months of 2010 to U.S.$299.2 billion in the first nine months of 2011. This\par increase was due primarily to an expansion in import volumes, which, in turn,\par was driven by continued improvement in domestic demand, and to a rise in import\par prices in the first nine months of 2011 compared to the analogous period in\par 2010. The trade balance for goods and services was partially offset by a 33.3%\par rise in investment income outflows from U.S.$28.5 billion in the first nine\par months of 2010 to U.S.$38 billion in the first nine months of 2011.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital and Financial Account\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2006, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$104.2 billion, a 35.9% increase compared to 2005. This increase largely\par reflected a growth in official foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and a reversal of the\par other investments balance from surplus to deficit. The rise in foreign reserves\par (U.S.$107.5 billion in 2006 compared to U.S.$61.5 billion in 2005) was mainly\par due to the Bank of Russia's policy of preventing excessive real appreciation of\par the rouble as well as to the Government's expansion of the Stabilisation Fund\par (the Fund's proceeds at the end of 2006 increased by approximately 1.2 trillion\par roubles compared to year-end 2005). See "Public Finance-Deficit Financing" for\par more information regarding the Stabilisation Fund. The other investments\par balance worsened from a U.S.$9 billion surplus in 2005 to a U.S.$19.1 billion\par deficit in 2006. The deficit was primarily driven by the following factors: (i)\par  an increase in Russian capital outflows in the form of loans extended to\par foreign residents; (ii) a decrease of arrears of Russian companies owed to\par nonresidents; (iii) an increased interest of Russian residents in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  demand and time deposits as a form of short-term savings; (iv) a\par reduction compared to 2005 in the value of foreign loans to Russian businesses;\par (v) Russia's prepayment of approximately U.S.$21.6 billion in debt to the Paris\par Club; and (vi) an exchange of Russian Federation Eurobonds for a portion of the\par uninsured Soviet-era trade indebtedness for which Russia has agreed to be\par responsible ("FTO Debt"), which led to an approximate U.S.$1.4 billion decline\par in foreign liabilities. Net foreign direct investment improved from\par U.S.$0.1 billion in 2005 to U.S.$6.6 billion in 2006 because the volume of\par foreign investment in Russia exceeded the volume of Russian investment abroad.\par The portfolio investment deficit of U.S.$11.4 billion in 2005 improved to a net\par surplus of U.S.$15.7 billion in 2006 due to increased purchases of Russian\par corporate securities by non-residents and at the same time a reduction in\par Russian investments in foreign securities, as well as the exchange of newly\par issued Russian Federation Eurobonds for a portion of Russia's FTO Debt. As a\par result of net inflows of foreign investment to both the banking and corporate\par sectors, net capital inflows to the private sector equalled U.S.$41.4 billion\par in 2006, a significant increase from the U.S.$0.1 billion of net outflows in\par 2005.\par \par In 2007, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$64.4 billion, a 38.2% decrease compared with 2006. This improvement was\par mainly the result of increased foreign lending to Russian businesses. Loans\par received by Russian residents amounted to U.S.$127.3 billion in 2007 compared\par to U.S.$18.3 billion in 2006. The foreign direct investment balance also\par contributed to the decrease in the capital and financial account deficit,\par having increased by U.S.$2.6 billion in 2007 compared to 2006. This 39%\par increase was the result of foreign investments in Russia exceeding Russian\par investments abroad. The net portfolio investment surplus declined from\par U.S.$15.7 billion in 2006 to U.S.$5.6 billion in 2007. This decline was due to\par a sharp increase of Russian investments in foreign securities from a surplus of\par U.S.$6.2 billion in 2006 to a deficit of U.S.$10 billion in 2007 and was\par partially offset by a 63.2% increase in foreign holdings of Russian securities.\par The overall improvement in the financial account was partially offset by the\par continued increase in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, as reflected in part by the\par approximate 1.2 trillion rouble increase in the Stabilisation Fund, and a sharp\par rise in net capital transfers from a surplus of U.S.$0.2 billion in 2006 to a\par deficit of U.S.$10.2 billion, which was caused by debt relief granted by Russia\par to certain of its debtors. See "Public Debt and Related Matters-External\par Financial Assets." As a result of net inflows to both the banking and corporate\par sectors, net capital inflows to the private sector equalled U.S.$81.7 billion\par in 2007, a 97% increase compared to 2006.\par \par In 2008, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$92.3 billion, a 43.3% increase compared to 2007. This increase reflected a\par reversal from surplus to deficit in both the portfolio investment balance and\par the other investments balance. The portfolio investment balance reversed from a\par U.S.$5.6 billion surplus in 2007 to a U.S.$35.4 billion deficit in 2008 as\par Russian residents repaid or bought back their foreign debt. The other\par investments balance moved from a U.S.$79.7 billion surplus in 2007 to a\par U.S.$114.3 billion deficit in 2008, as the global financial crisis prompted\par investors to reduce their exposure to Russian assets. Russian investors\par increased their holdings of hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  demand and time\par deposits, leading to combined outflows of U.S.$69.3 billion in 2008 as compared\par with combined inflows of U.S.$3 billion in 2007. Similarly, foreign loans to\par Russian businesses declined from U.S.$127.3 billion in 2007 to\par U.S.$63.5 billion in 2008. At the same time, Russian residents increased the\par value of loans extended to foreign residents, in part, to foreign subsidiaries\par of Russian companies. As a result, net private sector capital outflows\par (including outflows from both the banking and corporate sectors) in 2008\par reached U.S.$133.9 billion. A U.S.$38.9 billion decrease in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves in 2008, compared to an increase of U.S.$148.9 billion in 2007, and an\par increase in the direct investment balance from U.S.$9.2 billion in 2007 to\par U.S.$19.4 billion in 2008 partially offset the deterioration in the portfolio\par investment balance and other investments balance.\par \par In 2009, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$46.9 billion, a 49.2% decrease compared to 2008. This improvement was\par mainly attributable to a reduction in the portfolio investment balance deficit\par and the other investments deficit. The portfolio investment balance improved\par from a deficit of U.S.$35.4 billion in 2008 to a deficit of U.S.$2.2 billion in\par 2009 primarily because of an increase of foreign purchases of Russian\par securities as the rouble stabilised and the overall Russian economy began to\par recover in the second quarter of 2009. The deficit in other investments\par improved significantly, from a deficit of U.S.$114.3 billion in 2008 to a\par deficit of U.S.$19 billion in 2009, despite a sharp decline in foreign loans to\par Russian residents. The other investments deficit fell for several reasons.\par Russian investments in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash, demand and time deposits\par decreased, as did the value of Russian loans to foreign residents. Foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash holdings reversed from a U.S.$29.8 billion deficit in 2008 to a\par U.S.$6.3 billion surplus in 2009, and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits decreased from\par a deficit of U.S.$39.5 billion in 2008 to a surplus of U.S.$6.4 billion in\par 2009. The volume of Russian loans to foreign residents declined from a deficit\par of U.S.$54.3 billion in 2008 to a surplus of U.S.$7.4 billion in 2009.\par Concurrently, an increase in arrears was recorded, reflecting debt relief\par granted by Russia to certain of its debtors. The drop in the other investments\par deficit was also supported by a deceleration in the non-repatriation of export\par proceeds and import advances not repaid in time (from U.S.$39.3 billion in 2008\par to U.S.$27.1 billion in 2009). In 2009, despite net inflows in the second and\par fourth quarters, Russia recorded overall net private sector capital outflows of\par U.S.$56.1 billion, which were mainly driven by the repayment of loans to\par Russian borrowers, mainly in the banking sector. The growth in foreign reserves\par in 2009 equalled U.S.$3.4 billion. The improvement in the portfolio investment\par deficit and other investment deficit as well as the expansion of foreign\par reserves was partially offset by a reversal of the net direct investment\par balance, from a net inflow of U.S.$19.4 billion in 2008 to a net outflow of\par U.S.$7.2 billion in 2009. This reversal was primarily caused by a sharp decline\par in foreign direct investment in Russia. The improvement in the financial\par account deficit was partially offset by net outflows of capital transfers\par amounting to U.S.$11.6 billion, which was the result of debt relief granted by\par Russia to certain of its debtors.\par \par In 2010, Russia recorded a capital and financial account deficit of\par U.S.$62.2 billion, a 32.6% increase compared to 2009. This increase reflected\par primarily an acceleration in the growth of foreign reserves from\par U.S.$3.4 billion in 2009 to U.S.$36.8 billion in 2010, which, in turn, was due\par mainly to a significant improvement in the current account surplus from\par U.S.$48.6 billion in 2009 to U.S.$70.3 billion in 2010 and to a reduction in\par capital flight, i.e., the aggregate change in the other investments balance,\par portfolio investment balance, financial derivatives balance and direct\par investment balance. In 2010, the other investments deficit decreased to\par U.S.$12.8 billion from U.S.$19 billion in 2009, the portfolio investment\par deficit decreased to U.S.$1.7 billion from U.S.$2.2 billion in 2009 and the\par financial derivatives deficit decreased to U.S.$1.8 billion from\par U.S.$3.2 billion in 2009. The decrease in the other investments deficit, in\par turn, was primarily attributable to an increase in foreign investment in\par Russian demand and time deposits (from an outflow of U.S.$2.3 billion in 2009\par to an inflow of U.S.$10.2 billion in 2010), a reduction in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par holdings (from a decrease of U.S.$6.3 billion in 2009 to a decrease of\par U.S.$15.3 billion in 2010), a decline in investments in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  demand\par and time deposits (from a withdrawal of U.S.$6.4 billion in 2009 to a\par withdrawal of U.S.$9.5 billion in 2010) and a decrease in payments made by\par Russian entities on loans received from foreign investors (from an outflow of\par U.S.$32 billion in 2009 to an outflow of U.S.$0.8 billion in 2010). Partially\par offsetting the decrease in the other investment deficit was an increase in the\par volume of Russian loans to foreign residents (from a surplus of\par U.S.$7.4 billion in 2009 to a deficit of U.S.$16.3 billion in 2010). The\par increase in the capital and financial account deficit was partially offset by\par an improvement in the country's capital account from a deficit of\par U.S.$11.9 billion in 2009 to a surplus of U.S.$0.1 billion in 2010. In 2010,\par Russia had net private sector outflows of U.S.$33.6 billion.\par \par In the first nine months of 2011, Russia recorded a capital and financial\par account deficit of U.S.$66 billion, a 21.5% increase compared to the first nine\par months of 2010. This increase reflected primarily a reversal from surplus to\par deficit in the portfolio investment balance and an increase in the other\par investments deficit. The portfolio investment balance moved from a\par U.S.$9.3 billion surplus in the first nine months of 2010 to a\par U.S.$11.4 billion deficit in the first nine months of 2011 mainly due to the\par repayment of foreign debt by Russian corporates and banks. The deficit in the\par other investments balance increased from U.S.$9.8 billion in the first nine\par months of 2010 to U.S.$25.4 billion in the first nine months of 2011 mainly\par because Russian investors increased their holdings of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  demand\par and time deposits and increased the value of loans extended to foreign\par residents. The growth of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves decelerated from an increase\par of U.S.$45.4 billion in the first nine months of 2010 to an increase of\par U.S.$21.2 billion in the first nine months of 2011. At the same time, net\par private sector capital outflows reached U.S.$46.4 billion in the first nine\par months of 2011, compared to U.S.$14.3 billion in the first nine months of 2010.\par \par Exchange rate fluctuations and periods of economic uncertainty have often\par caused Russian banks, enterprises and households to convert their current\par holdings and savings into foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (particularly dollar-denominated)\par assets. Though the impact of these factors has declined in recent years,\par capital flight has historically been significant in Russia, caused largely by\par illegal outflows through non-repatriation of export proceeds and unrefunded\par import advances. Non-repatriation of export proceeds and unrefunded import\par advances fluctuated between 2006 and 2010 (U.S.$19.9 billion in 2006,\par U.S.$34.5 billion in 2007, U.S.$39.3 billion in 2008, U.S.$27.1 billion in 2009\par and U.S.$30.6 billion in 2010). In the first nine months of 2011, they equalled\par U.S.$21.7 billion, compared to U.S.$22 billion in the first nine months of\par 2010. Russia's balance of payments accounts include a high level of unaccounted\par transactions, which likewise have historically fluctuated, including inflows of\par U.S.$9.5 billion in 2006 and outflows of U.S.$13.3 billion, U.S.$11.3 billion,\par U.S.$1.7 billion and U.S.$8 billion in 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively.\par Outflows due to unaccounted transactions in the first nine months of 2010 and\par 2011 equalled U.S.$3.2 billion and U.S.$5.4 billion, respectively.\par \par The nominal depreciation of the dollar during 2006 and 2007 encouraged Russian\par individuals and small businesses to convert dollar cash into roubles and\par resulted in a U.S.$9.5 billion and U.S.$15.5 billion decline in the stock of\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cash in 2006 and 2007, respectively. The sharp depreciation of\par the rouble in 2008 (19.7% against the dollar and 15.3% against the euro, in\par nominal terms) as well as instability in global markets encouraged Russian\par residents to convert rouble holdings into foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and resulted in a\par U.S.$29.8 billion increase in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings. In 2009, the rouble\par continued to depreciate (by 2.9% against the dollar and 4.7% against the euro,\par in nominal terms), but at a slower rate than in the previous year. Deceleration\par in the nominal depreciation of the rouble, together with the relative\par improvement of the economy in the second half of 2009 compared to the first\par half of 2009, led to a U.S.$6.3 billion decline in the stock of foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings among Russian households and businesses in 2009. In 2010, the\par stock of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings declined by U.S.$15.3 billion. This sharp\par decline in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings was, in part, due to the relatively\par stronger value of the rouble (the average rouble/dollar exchange rate\par appreciated from 31.93 roubles/dollar in 2009 to 30.34 roubles/dollar in 2010)\par and to relatively greater stability on the global markets in 2010 compared to\par 2009. The decline in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings equalled U.S.$3.5 billion in the\par first nine months of 2011, compared to U.S.$14.2 billion in the first nine\par months of 2010. This slowdown in the decline of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings took\par place despite rouble depreciation in the third quarter of 2011 (by 15.2%\par against the dollar and 7.4% against the euro).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign Trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign Trade Regime\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Following the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Russia introduced import tariffs\par for a broad range of goods. In an effort to bring its external trade regime\par more closely in line with WTO standards, Russia has introduced a maximum import\par tariff rate and has worked to unify rates within commodity groups. Currently,\par the maximum rate of import tariffs is 30%, with the exception of certain\par commodity groups, which are levied at various rates, reaching 100% for spirits.\par In January 2009, Russia substantially raised its import tariffs on used cars in\par an effort to support the country's domestic automotive industry. The tariffs,\par which are calculated in accordance with a formula that is based on the age and\par engine type of the vehicle, have remained substantially the same since this\par increase. In January 2010, Russia imposed a ban on the import of poultry\par containing chlorine levels in excess of a certain threshold. This new\par regulation led to significant reductions in poultry imports from the United\par States, one of Russia's primary suppliers. The ban on most U.S. poultry imports\par was rescinded in August 2010. In December 2011, Russia concluded all accession\par agreements required for membership in the WTO. Formal membership in the WTO is\par subject to ratification by the State Duma. See "Russian\par Federation-International Relations-Russia's Position in the International\par Community."\par \par Exports are an important source of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earnings for Russia.\par Currently, Russia has various export restrictions in place, although these\par restrictions are subject to elimination in accordance with the requirements of\par Russia's admission to the WTO. The Government has also introduced duties on the\par export of oil and natural gas. Duties on oil exports are based on world oil\par prices, and the duty on natural gas is 30%. See "Public Finance-Russian Tax\par System" for a discussion of these customs duties. Effective 1 July 2010, a new\par Customs Code went into effect, which seeks to facilitate customs clearance\par procedures. See "The Russian Economy-Civil and Commercial Law" for a discussion\par of the Customs Code.\par \par Composition of Trade\par \par The following table illustrates the composition of Russia's exports and imports\par on a customs basis (excluding unregistered trade adjustments) for the periods\par indicated:\par \par                           Structure of Trade(1)(2)(3)                          \par \par                         (excluding unregistered trade)                         \par \par                                        For the                                                                \par                                       year ended 