cs and includes trade with Belarus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) For those transactions denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than the dollar, conversions into dollars are made at the exchange rate prevailing on the date of the relevant transaction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (4) From 1 July 2010, when the Customs Code of the Customs Union among Russia,\par Belarus and Kazahkstan took effect, all trade between Russia and Kazahkstan is\par included in the row "other" and no longer itemised by product or service type.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) Includes oil, gas and coal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Customs Service.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exports of mineral products (including oil, gas and coal), metals and precious stones and metals account for the vast majority of Russia's total exports, representing 80.8% of exports in 2007, 83% in 2008, 80.2% in 2009, 81.1% in 2010 and 81.5% in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2008, the share of mineral products, metals and precious stones and metals\par as a percentage of total exports rose to 83% from 80.8% in 2007. This increase\par was due to a sharp increase in oil prices (in 2008, compared to 2007, the value\par of oil and gas exports increased by 32.6% and 54.1%, respectively, whereas the\par volume of oil exports decreased by 6% and the volume of gas exports increased\par by 1.8%), which, in turn, led to an increase in the share of mineral products\par as a share of total exports from 64.9% in 2007 to 69.8% in 2008. Partially\par offsetting this increase was an approximate 17% decrease in the contribution of\par metals and precious stones and metals to overall exports in 2008 compared to\par 2007. In 2009, the overall share of mineral products, metals and precious\par stones and metals as a percentage of total exports declined to 80.2% from 83%\par in 2008. This decrease was primarily due to the global financial crisis, which\par contributed to a contraction both in the volume and value of mineral products\par and metals. In 2010 and 2011, the overall share of mineral products, metals and\par precious stones and metals as a percentage of total exports increased to 81.2%\par and 81.5%, respectively, compared to 80.2% in 2009 due in large part to higher\par prices for oil and oil products.\par \par Other significant exports include machinery, equipment and transport (5.6%,\par 4.9%, 5.9%, 5.4% and 4.5% of total exports in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011,\par respectively) and chemical products and rubber (5.9%, 6.4%, 6.2%, 6.2% and 6%\par of total exports in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively).\par \par Imports of machinery, equipment and transport accounted for 50.9% of total\par imports in 2007, 52.7% in 2008, 43.4% in 2009, 44.4% in 2010 and 48% in 2011.\par Russia also imports significant amounts of foodstuffs and agricultural\par products, chemical products and rubber. In the aggregate, foodstuffs and\par agricultural products accounted for 13.8% of imports in 2007, 13.2% in 2008,\par 17.9% in 2009, 15.9% in 2010 and 13.9% in 2011. Chemical products and rubber\par constituted 13.8%, 13.2%, 16.7%, 16.1% and 14.9% of total imports in 2007,\par 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Direction of Trade\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The following tables illustrate the geographic distribution of Russia's trade\par on a customs basis (excluding unregistered trade adjustments) for the periods\par indicated:\par \par                                Exports(1)(2)(3)                                \par \par                         (excluding unregistered trade)                         \par \par                                              For the                   \par                                            year ended                   \par                                            31 December                  \par            