                                                                      \par                                                                                           \par Ukraine          13,330   44.3  16,254   21.9   9,129 (43.8)  14,047   53.9  20,121   43.2\par                                                                                           \par Belarus           8,879   29.7  10,552   18.8   6,718 (36.3)   9,954   48.2  13,685   37.5\par                                                                                           \par Kazakhstan        4,623   20.4   6,379   38.0   3,693 (42.1)   4,449   20.5   6,859   54.2\par                                                                                           \par Uzbekistan        1,471   13.9   1,300 (11.6)     846 (34.9)   1,557   84.0   1,860   19.5\par                                                                                           \par Other CIS         1,568   35.3   2,121   35.3   1,432 (27.7)   7,185  401.8  10,059   40.0\par                                                                                           \par Total           199,754   45.0 267,101   33.7 167,348 (37.3) 228,912   36.8 305,313   33.4\par imports                                                                                   \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by the Customs Service. Figures in this table are current as of 25 February 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Figures differ from the presentation in "-Balance of Payments" due to classification, coverage and other adjustments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) For those transactions denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than the dollar, conversions into dollars are made at the exchange rate prevailing on the date of the relevant transaction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes Bulgaria, Romania, Vietnam, North Korea, Mongolia and Cuba, in addition to China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: Customs Service; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Between 2007 and 2011, Russia's exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries grew by approximately\par 64.4% compared to 71.3% growth in total Russian exports. As a percentage of\par total exports, exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries remained stable, accounting for 64-67%\par of Russia's total exports (as measured by value) in each year from 2007 through\par 2011. In 2009, Russian exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries declined by 38% compared to\par 2008 as a result of the contraction in worldwide demand resulting from the\par global financial crisis. Overall total exports in 2009 fell by 35.5%. Due to\par the economic recovery following the global financial crisis, Russia's exports\par to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries grew by 35.5% in 2010 and 25.6% in 2011, compared to overall\par growth in Russian exports of 31.6% in 2010 and 30% in 2011. Exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par countries accounted for 65% of total exports in 2010.\par \par In 2007, Russian exports to CIS countries grew at a faster rate than exports to\par non-CIS countries (an increase of 24.5% to CIS countries compared to an\par increase of 15.6% to non-CIS countries.) This rise in exports was primarily due\par to growth of overall economic activity in the CIS countries. At a growth rate\par of 32.3%, exports to CIS countries grew in 2008 in parallel with exports to\par non-CIS countries. In 2009, Russia's exports to CIS countries fell by 32.8%,\par which was slightly less than the rate at which total Russian exports declined\par (35.5%). In 2010 and 2011, Russia's exports to CIS countries increased by 27.3%\par and 31.3%, respectively, which was primarily due to an improvement in the\par economy following the global financial crisis. From 2007 through 2011, Russian\par exports to CIS countries recorded an overall growth rate of 77.6%. Exports to\par CIS countries accounted for 15% and 15.2% of Russia's total exports in 2010 and\par 2011, respectively.\par \par Growth in exports to transition economies, i.e., Bulgaria, Romania, Vietnam,\par China, North Korea, Mongolia and Cuba, generally followed trends in Russia's\par total exports, accounting for approximately 7-8% of total exports between 2007\par and 2011. Exports to transition economies grew by 5.5% in 2007 and 31.5% in\par 2008, before falling by 30.7% in 2009 due to the global financial crisis. Due\par to the subsequent economic recovery, exports to transition economies grew by\par 27.2% in 2010 and, due to a sharp rise in trade with China, by 54% in 2011.\par \par Supported by a high rate of GDP growth, Russia's imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries\par grew by 43.4% in 2007 and 35.3% in 2008, in line with overall import growth\par during the same period. Russian imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries equalled 61.9% and\par 62.7% of total Russian imports (as measured by value) in 2007 and 2008,\par respectively. In 2009, because of the contraction in domestic demand and\par consumer income resulting from the global financial crisis, Russian imports\par from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries dropped by 39.6%, while overall imports declined by 37.3%\par compared to 2008. Due to the economic recovery following the global financial\par crisis, Russia's imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries grew by 31.6% in 2010 and 35.9% in\par 2011, compared to overall growth in Russian imports of 36.8% in 2010 and 33.4%\par in 2011. Imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries accounted for 58.1% and 59.2% of total\par imports in 2010 and 2011, respectively.\par \par Between 2007 and 2011, the share of total imports from transition economies\par followed an upward trend, primarily due to a rise in imports from China. In\par 2007, imports from transition economies accounted for 13.1% of total imports,\par compared with 14%, 14.9%, 18.4% and 17.2% in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011,\par respectively. In 2009, imports from transition economies declined by 33.5%\par compared to 2008 due to weaker domestic demand, resulting from the global\par financial crisis. Due to the subsequent economic recovery-and significantly\par driven by a rise in imports from China-imports from transition economies grew\par by 68.9% in 2010 and 24.8% in 2011.\par \par Russia's overall economic growth exhibited over the past decade led to a\par decline in the share of imports from CIS countries, as imports from other\par countries became more attractive to Russian consumers. In 2007, Russian imports\par from CIS countries accounted for 15% of total Russian imports, compared with\par 13.7%, 13%, 13.9% and 14.5% in 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. Imports\par from CIS countries declined by 40.4% in 2009 compared to 2008 due to the\par effects of the global financial crisis. As a result of the subsequent economic\par recovery, imports from CIS countries increased by 45.4% in 2010 and 39.6% in\par 2011.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the non-CIS countries, Germany, the Netherlands, China, Italy, Turkey, United States, Poland and Japan are Russia's leading trading partners, as measured by value. Belarus, Ukraine and increasingly Kazakhstan are Russia's leading trading partners as measured by value within the CIS.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's largest export markets in 2009, 2010 and 2011 were the Netherlands\par (12.1%, 13.6% and 12.1%, respectively, of Russian exports), Italy (8.3%, 6.9%\par and 6.3%, respectively), Germany (6.2%, 6.3% and 6.6%, respectively), Turkey\par (5.4%, 5.1% and 4.9%, respectively), Belarus (5.4%, 4.6% and 4.8%,\par respectively), China (5.5%, 5.1% and 6.8%, respectively) and Ukraine (4.6%,\par 5.8% and 5.9%, respectively). In 2009, 2010 and 2011, the most important\par exporters to Russia were China (13.6%, 17% and 15.8%, respectively, of Russian\par imports), Germany (12.7%, 11.7% and 12.3%, respectively), Japan (4.3%, 4.5% and\par 4.9%, respectively), Ukraine (5.5%, 6.1% and 6.6%, respectively), the United\par States (5.5%, 4.8% and 4.8%, respectively), Italy (4.7%, 4.4% and 6.6%,\par respectively), Belarus (4%, 4.3% and 4.5%, respectively), France (5%, 4.4% and\par 4.3%, respectively) and the Republic of Korea (2.9%, 3.2% and 3.8%,\par respectively). In 2009, 2010 and 2011, Ukraine and Belarus together accounted\par for 67.6%, 71.5% and 72.8%, respectively, of Russia's foreign trade with CIS\par countries.\par \par Official Reserves\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information with respect to the official reserves of the Russian Federation as of the indicated dates:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                              Official Reserves(1)                              \par \par                      As at                                                        \par                   31 December                                                     \par                                                                                   \par                       2006      2007    2008    2009    2010    2011              \par                                                                                   \par                    (millions                                                      \par                   of dollars,                                                     \par                    except as                                                      \par                   indicated)                                                      \par                                                                                   \par Official reserves      295,567 466,750 411,748 416,653 443,591 453,952            \par (excluding gold)                                            