prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Bank of Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As at 31 December 2006, total official reserves (including gold) stood at\par approximately U.S.$303.7 billion, which represents 17.4 months of import\par coverage and an increase of approximately U.S.$121.5 billion compared to\par year-end 2005. This improvement was due primarily to an increase in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves (backed by high commodity prices and the country's large\par trade surplus). As at 31 December 2006, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amounted to\par approximately U.S.$295.3 billion, and gold reserves stood at approximately\par U.S.$8.2 billion. The growth in reserves was also supported by net private\par sector foreign capital inflows of U.S.$41.8 billion, a significant expansion\par over net inflows in 2005.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The growth of official reserves continued in 2007 due to favourable world commodity prices and high inflows of foreign capital, including net private capital inflows of U.S.$83.1 billion. At 31 December 2007, total official reserves (including gold) stood at approximately U.S.$478.8 billion, representing approximately 20 months of import coverage. As of the same date, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amounted to approximately U.S.$466.4 billion, and gold reserves stood at approximately U.S.$12 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As of 31 December 2008, total official reserves (including gold) were\par U.S.$426.3 billion, a decrease of approximately U.S.$52.5 billion compared with\par year-end 2007. These reserves represented approximately 13.9 months of import\par coverage. As of the same date, gold reserves equalled approximately\par U.S.$14.5 billion. The decrease in total reserves was due to an 11.9% drop in\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to U.S.$410.7 billion. The decline in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, in turn, was primarily attributable to the onset of the\par global financial crisis in the second half of 2008 and its negative impact on\par world commodity prices and aggregate demand. As a result of the financial\par crisis, Russia experienced a significant outflow of private capital, amounting\par to U.S.$132.8 billion.\par \par As of 31 December 2009, total official reserves (including gold) stood at\par approximately U.S.$439.5 billion, an increase of approximately\par U.S.$13.2 billion compared to year-end 2008. These reserves represented nearly\par 21 months of import coverage. The increase in reserves was primarily\par attributable to a significant rise in Russia's balance of special drawing\par rights, i.e., international reserve assets created by the IMF, from\par U.S.$1 million in 2008 to U.S.$8.9 billion in 2009 and in the country's gold\par reserves from approximately U.S.$14.5 billion in 2008 to U.S.$22.8 billion in\par 2009. The increase in special drawing rights was due to an improvement in\par Russia's reserve position with the IMF. The increase in gold reserves was the\par result of Russian purchases of gold as a means to diversify and protect its\par reserves during the crisis. The overall rise in total reserves was partially\par offset by a reduction in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from U.S.$410.7 billion in\par 2008 to U.S.$405.8 billion in 2009. This decrease was primarily due to the\par continued impact of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy,\par particularly in the first quarter of 2009 when foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves\par dropped by U.S.$30.5 billion and net private capital outflows amounted to\par U.S.$35 billion.\par \par As of 31 December 2010, total official reserves (including gold) equalled\par U.S.$479.4 billion, an increase of approximately U.S.$13.9 billion compared to\par year-end 2009. Reserves as of 31 December 2010 represented approximately\par 24 months of import coverage. The increase in reserves was primarily\par attributable to a 6.7% rise in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from\par U.S.$405.8 billion at year-end 2009 to U.S.$432.9 billion at 31 December 2010\par and a 57% rise in official gold reserves from U.S.$22.8 billion at year-end\par 2009 to U.S.$35.8 billion at 31 December 2010. The increase in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves was, in part, due to higher oil prices, and the increase in gold\par reserves reflected the Government's continuing policy of diversifying its\par reserves portfolio.\par \par As of 31 December 2011, total official reserves (including gold) equalled\par U.S.$499 billion, an increase of approximately U.S.$19.2 billion compared to\par year-end 2010. The increase in reserves was primarily attributable to a 1.9%\par rise in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from U.S.$432.9 billion at 31 December 2010\par to U.S.$441.2 billion at 31 December 2011, a 24.9% rise in the value of gold\par reserves from U.S.$35.8 billion at 31 December 2010 to U.S.$44.7 billion at\par 31 December 2011 and a 114.5% rise in Russia's reserve position with the IMF\par from U.S.$1.9 billion at 31 December 2010 to U.S.$4.1 billion at 31 December\par 2011. Reserves as of 31 December 2011 provided approximately 18.5 months of\par import coverage.\par \par As of 31 December 2011, Russia's total external debt (public and private)\par equalled approximately U.S.$538.9 billion, or 29% of GDP, with Russia's\par official reserves providing approximately 93% coverage. As of 31 December 2011,\par Russia's reserves were the third highest among the G8 countries and the fourth\par highest in the world.\par \par The Government strictly regulates Russia's output, sale and export of precious\par stones and metals, including gold, platinum and diamonds. As part of this\par regulatory regime, the Government licenses and has a right of first refusal to\par purchase Russia's precious metals output. The Government may add to its\par reserves any purchased output or any proceeds from the sale and export of\par precious metals.\par \par Both the Bank of Russia and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  currently have official\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and gold reserves. Reserves of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are\par allocated to either the National Wealth Fund or Reserve Fund. See "Public\par Finance-Deficit Financing" for more information on the National Wealth Fund and\par Reserve Fund. The percentage of Russia's total official reserves that are\par controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  varies year to year and depends on the\par reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  undertaken by the Bank of Russia and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par during the year. In 2007, for example, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held\par approximately one-third of the country's total official reserves, whereas, in\par 2008, it held nearly one-half. This change was generally due to the Bank of\par Russia's decision to defend the rouble at the end of 2008 and to the decision\par to accumulate reserves in the Funds controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At\par year-end 2009, the share of foreign reserves held by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par declined to approximately one-third due to the depletion of the Reserve Fund,\par the proceeds of which were used to cover the budget deficit, and to the\par accumulation of reserves at the Bank of Russia. The Bank of Russia was able to\par increase its reserves because there was less need to support the rouble in 2009\par than in 2008. As of 31 December 2010 and 2011, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held\par approximately 21% and 19%, respectively, of the country's total official\par reserves, with the remainder held by the Bank of Russia.\par \par                                 PUBLIC FINANCE                                 \par \par The information presented herein with respect to the federal budget has been\par prepared substantially in accordance with the guidelines and definitions set\par forth in the IMF's publication "Government Finance Statistics" (GFS-1986).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia maintained a strong fiscal position through 2008, running surpluses of\par 6% and 4.9% of GDP in 2007 and 2008, respectively. In 2009, Russia recorded a\par budget deficit of 6.3% of GDP. The federal budget has followed a similar trend,\par producing primary surpluses of 5.8% and 4.5% of GDP in 2007 and 2008,\par respectively, and a primary deficit of 5.5% in 2009. Russia's positive fiscal\par position through 2008 was attributable largely to favourable conditions in\par world energy markets, resulting in higher revenues from related production,\par export duties, excise and other taxes as well as higher profit tax revenues,\par combined with improvements in the federal budgetary process, allowing for\par greater control over expenditure, and more efficient tax policy. The reduction\par in the budget surplus in 2007 was caused primarily by an increase in spending\par on the national economy and state administration as well as growth in the\par volumes of federal transfers to sub-federal budgets. The surplus continued to\par decrease in 2008 primarily because of a contraction in tax revenues (as a share\par of GDP), particularly domestic VAT proceeds, and a slight rise in expenditures\par mainly in the form of transfers to state extra-budgetary funds. The federal\par budget deficit in 2009 was mainly due to the effects of the global financial\par crisis, which reduced revenues (particularly as a result of the decline in\par worldwide demand for Russia's commodity exports) and caused expenditures to\par rise as a consequence of the Government's fiscal stimulus package. In 2010,\par Russia ran an enlarged budget deficit of 3.5% of GDP and a federal budget\par primary deficit of 3.6% of GDP. As the economy continued to recover in 2011,\par Russia recorded an enlarged budget surplus of 1.6% of GDP and a federal budget\par primary surplus of 1.3% of GDP, the first time such surpluses were recorded\par since 2008.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth certain summary information regarding Russia's public finances for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                               Enlarged Budget(1)                               \par \par                     For the                                                       \par                   year ended                                                      \par                   31 December                                                     \par                                                                                   \par                       2007        2008       2009        2010        2011         \par                                                                                   \par                    (millions                                                      \par                   of roubles)                                                     \par                                                                        