  10.7    11.3     11.5               \par                                                                               \par Surplus (deficit)            0.5    0.9    0.5     0.7      0.8               \par                                                                               \par Memo:                                                                         \par                                                                               \par Nominal GDP               33,248 41,277 38,809  45,166   54,369               \par (billions of                                                                  \par roubles)                                                                      \par \par \par Notes:\par \par (1) Calculated on the basis of cash or other items of value actually collected\par or spent. Certain data presented in this table differ from data made public\par previously due to regular revisions made by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Figures in\par this table are current as of 15 February 2012.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Consolidated budget revenues less non-interest expenditure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Federal budget revenues less non-interest expenditure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes financial aid to regions and other extra-budgetary transfers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (5) Beginning in 2011, transfers to state extra-budgetary funds are no longer\par indicated as a separate line item and instead are included in the expenditure\par line item "social and cultural sphere." Transfers to state extra-budgetary\par funds amounted to 2,547.1 billion roubles in 2011, including 2,380.1 billion\par roubles to the Pension Fund, 145.9 billion roubles to the Social Insurance Fund\par and 21.1 billion roubles to the Federal Medical Insurance Fund.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Budgetary Process\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is centrally involved in each stage of the federal\par budgetary process, including establishing fiscal policy objectives, preparation\par of the budget, adoption of the budget by the Federal Assembly, executing the\par budget (including later amendments) and auditing the budget's execution. The\par Audit Chamber also monitors and audits the execution of the budget.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Budget Preparation and Adoption\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Under the Budget Code, the draft budget is prepared by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and presented, first, to the Government, and then, not later than 1 October of\par the current financial year, to the State Duma, where it proceeds through three\par readings. In the first reading, the budget's overall macroeconomic framework\par and the budgeted revenue, deficit (surplus) and expenditure are established.\par The State Duma cannot increase revenues or the deficit ceiling without the\par approval of the Government. The second reading is devoted to allocating total\par authorised expenditure (other than reserve expenses) to specific purposes\par according to functional classifications within limits defined after the first\par reading as well as allocating the expenditure among government institutions,\par lending and borrowing programmes and other items. Budgeted reserve expenses for\par the first budget year amount to no less than 2.5% of total budgeted expenses\par for that year and, for the second budget year, to no less than 5% of total\par budgeted expenses for that year. In the third reading, the budget as a whole is\par adopted, and no further amendments are permitted. After the State Duma's third\par reading, the Federation Council votes on the bill. Once passed by the\par Federation Council, the bill is sent to the President for consideration. The\par draft budget becomes law following its signature by the President and\par publication in the authorised print media. The federal law becomes effective on\par 1 January of the relevant year. During the course of the fiscal year, the\par Government may submit to the State Duma amendments to the budget law for\par consideration in accordance with an expedited legislative procedure. Pursuant\par to this procedure, the State Duma is required to consider amendments to the\par budget in three readings, all of which must take place within 25 days after the\par amendments have been submitted. Such amendments must then be reviewed by the\par Federation Council within 14 days after their receipt from the State Duma.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with amendments introduced in 2007, beginning in 2008, the budget includes figures on a three-year, rather than annual, basis. Therefore, the 2012 Budget Law includes figures for 2012 as well as forecasted figures for 2013 and 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Budget Execution\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is responsible for the execution of the budget law.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The budget law sets overall nominal levels for revenue, expenditure and deficit\par (surplus), as well as caps for domestic and foreign borrowing and other sources\par of deficit financing. Subject to the five percent threshold discussed below,\par approval of the State Duma is required if the budget plan exceeds the nominal\par level of expenditure or borrowing. In each year since 2005, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has executed the budget using only revenue actually collected and\par without violating the deficit ceiling established in the budget law.\par \par The expenditure levels established in the budget law are annual spending limits\par with a breakdown for each line item. Upon adoption of the budget law, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  promulgates annual allocation targets for each line item.\par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may also establish binding quarterly or monthly\par allocation targets for each line item in order to enhance \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  control\par over budgetary performance. The Budget Code sets forth an exhaustive list of\par circumstances when the spending limits for a particular line item can be\par modified without legislative amendment. For example, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par may increase expenditure for a certain line item by no more than five percent\par if the amount initially allocated proves to be insufficient. Increases of over\par five percent and other modifications to spending limits beyond those stipulated\par in the Budget Code can be made only after corresponding amendments are made to\par the budget law.\par \par The budget law adopted in 2007 provided for a fiscal surplus of 1,501.8 billion\par roubles, or 4.8% of GDP, and a primary surplus of 1,658.7 billion roubles, or\par 5.3% of GDP. Supported by higher than projected oil prices, federal budget\par revenues in 2007 amounted to 23.4% of GDP, as compared to the budgeted estimate\par of 22.3% of GDP, while total expenditure of 18% of GDP was slightly higher in\par relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 17.5% of GDP. The year ended with\par an actual surplus of 5.4% of GDP, higher than what was originally budgeted.\par Federal budget revenues transferred to the Stabilisation Fund amounted to\par 1,602.8 billion roubles, or 4.8% of GDP, in 2007. In 2007, the budgeted oil\par price assumption was U.S.$61 per barrel, and the breakeven oil price, i.e., the\par price of oil required to balance the federal budget, was U.S.$29 per barrel.\par \par The 2008 budget law provided for a fiscal surplus of 1,435.7 billion roubles or\par 3.4% of GDP, and a primary surplus of 1,614.7 billion roubles, or 3.8% of GDP.\par Federal budget revenues in 2008 amounted to 22.5% of GDP, as compared to the\par budgeted estimate of 21.2% of GDP, while total expenditure of 18.3% of GDP was\par slightly higher in relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 17.8% of GDP.\par The year ended with an actual surplus of 4.1% of GDP, higher than what was\par originally budgeted. In 2008, the Stabilisation Fund was replaced by the\par Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund, and its outstanding balance was\par transferred to the two new funds. See "-Deficit Financing" for more information\par on the Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund. Federal budget revenues\par transferred to the Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund in 2008 (excluding\par transfers from the Stabilisation Fund) equalled 420.6 billion roubles (1% of\par GDP) and 1,590.2 billion roubles (3.9% of GDP), respectively. At the end of\par 2008, the balance of the Reserve Fund was 4,027.6 billion roubles, or 9.8% of\par GDP, and the balance of the National Wealth Fund was 2,584.5 billion roubles,\par or 6.3% of GDP. In 2008, the budgeted oil price assumption was U.S.$53 per\par barrel, and the breakeven oil price was U.S.$57 per barrel.\par \par The 2009 budget law envisaged a fiscal deficit of 2,978.4 billion roubles, or\par 7.4% of GDP, and a primary deficit of 2,749.3 billion roubles, or 6.8% of GDP.\par Federal budget revenues in 2009 amounted to 18.9% of GDP, as compared to the\par budgeted estimate of 16.6% of GDP, while total expenditure of 24.9% of GDP was\par slightly higher in relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 24% of GDP. The\par year ended with an actual federal deficit of 6% of GDP, lower than what was\par originally budgeted. Federal budget revenues transferred to the Reserve Fund in\par 2009 equalled 489 billion roubles (1.3% of GDP). No federal budget revenues\par were transferred to the National Wealth Fund in 2009. Proceeds from the Reserve\par Fund were used to finance the budget deficit in 2009, which led to a 54.5%\par decline in the Reserve Fund balance during 2009 to 1,831 billion roubles (4.7%\par of GDP). In 2009, the budgeted oil price assumption was U.S.$61 per barrel, and\par the breakeven oil price was U.S.$97 per barrel.\par \par The 2010 budget law was amended twice during 2010. The original 2010 budget law\par (the "Original 2010 Budget Law") envisaged a fiscal deficit of 2,937 billion\par roubles, or 6.8% of GDP, and a primary deficit of 2,633 billion roubles, or\par 6.1% of GDP. The 2010 budget law, as amended in November 2010 (the "Amended\par 2010 Budget Law"), envisaged a fiscal deficit of 2,381 billion roubles, or 5.3%\par of GDP, and a primary deficit of 2,161 billion roubles, or 4.8% of GDP. Federal\par budget revenues in 2010 amounted to 18.4% of GDP, as compared to the budgeted\par estimate of 16.1% of GDP according to the Original 2010 Budget Law and 17.4%\par according to the Amended 2010 Budget Law. Total expenditure in 2010 was 22.5%\par of GDP, lower in relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 22.9% of GDP\par according to the Original 2010 Budget Law and 22.7% of GDP according to the\par Amended 2010 Budget Law. The year ended with an actual federal deficit of 4% of\par GDP, lower than what was estimated in either the Original 2010 Budget Law or\par the Amended 2010 Budget Law. No federal budget revenues were transferred to\par either the Reserve Fund or the National Wealth Fund in 2010. Proceeds from the\par Reserve Fund were used to finance the budget deficit, while proceeds from the\par National Wealth Fund were allocated to the co-financing of pensions. In 2010,\par the budgeted oil price assumption was U.S.$58 per barrel, and the breakeven oil\par price was U.S.$106 per barrel.\par \par The 2011 budget law was amended three times in 2011. The original 2011 budget\par law (the "Original 2011 Budget Law") envisaged a fiscal deficit of 1,814\par billion roubles, or 3.6% of GDP, and a primary deficit of 1,424 billion\par roubles, or 2.8% of GDP. The 2011 budget law, as amended for the final time in\par November 2011 (the "Amended 2011 Budget Law") envisaged no fiscal deficit and a\par primary surplus of 267 billion roubles, or 0.5% of GDP. Federal budget revenues\par in 2011 amounted to 20.9% of GDP, lower in relative terms than the budgeted\par estimate of 17.6% of GDP according to the Original 2011 Budget Law and 20.9% of\par GDP according to the Amended 2011 Budget Law. Total expenditure in 2011 was\par 20.1% of GDP, lower in relative terms than the budgeted estimate of 21.2% of\par GDP under the Original 2011 Budget Law and 20.9% of GDP under the Amended 2011\par Budget Law. The year ended with an actual federal surplus of 0.8% of GDP. The\par outperformance of the fiscal balance in 2011 relative to projections was in\par large part due to higher than expected oil and gas prices. As in 2010, no\par federal budget revenues were transferred to either the Reserve Fund or the\par National Wealth Fund in 2011. No proceeds from the Reserve Fund were used in\par 2011, while proceeds from the National Wealth Fund were allocated to the\par co-financing of pensions. In 2011, the budgeted oil price assumption was\par U.S.$75 per barrel, and the breakeven oil price was U.S.$108 per barrel.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2012 Federal Budget Law(1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                    2012            2013                  2014            \par                                                                                  \par                  (billions (% of (billions (% of (billions (% of                 \par                     of     GDP)     of     GDP)     of     GDP)                  \par                  roubles,        roubles,        roubles,                        \par        