xport duties                5.5       6.9       5.3       5.6        6.8         \par                                                                                   \par Other non-tax                2.5       1.3       2.4       2.3        1.7         \par revenue                                                                           \par                                                                                   \par                     (% of total                                                   \par                     revenue net                                                   \par                       of UST)                                                     \par                                                                                   \par Tax revenue (excl.          57.3      53.9      49.6      53.0       52.7         \par UST)                                                                              \par                                                                                   \par Profit taxes                 8.7       8.7       2.9       3.1        3.0         \par                                                                                   \par VAT                         30.7      24.3      30.0      30.1       28.6         \par                                                                                   \par Domestic VAT                18.8      11.4      17.2      16.0       15.4         \par                                                                                   \par Import VAT                  11.8      12.9      12.8      14.1       13.2         \par                                                                                   \par Excise taxes                 1.8       1.8       1.5       1.7        2.4         \par                                                                                   \par Natural resource            15.7      18.7      14.7      17.0       18.0         \par taxes                                                                             \par                                                                                   \par Other tax revenue            0.5       0.4       0.5       1.2        0.6         \par                                                                                   \par Non-tax revenue             42.7      46.1      50.4      47.0       47.1         \par                                                                                   \par Customs duties              31.5      39.7      36.8      34.4       38.7         \par                                                                                   \par Import duties                6.6       7.1       6.8       7.1        6.1         \par                                                                                   \par Export duties               24.9      32.6      29.9      30.2       32.6         \par                                                                                   \par Other non-tax               11.2       6.4      13.6      12.6        8.3         \par revenue                                                                           \par                                                                                   \par GDP (billions of          33,248    41,277    38,809    45,166     54,369         \par roubles)                                                                          \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Figures in this table are current as of 15 February 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) The UST was repealed as of 1 January 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Translated into international standards from the budget reports of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Sources of Federal Revenue\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main sources of Russia's federal budget revenue are taxes and customs duties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The main elements of Russia's tax system are VAT, natural resource extraction\par taxes, a corporate profits tax, a system of excise taxes and a personal income\par tax. Sub-federal governments may impose only those taxes that are contemplated\par by the Tax Code of the Russian Federation (the "Tax Code").\par \par Through 2009, the federal budget included a portion of the UST, the revenue\par from which was transferred to the Pension Fund after its collection by the\par Government. The UST's contribution to the federal budget equalled 5.2%, 5.5%\par and 6.9% of total federal budget revenue in 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively.\par These amounts were budgeted as part of both the federal budget's revenues and\par expenditures, and therefore did not affect the budget balance. As of 1 January\par 2010, the UST was repealed and replaced with a system of direct payments to\par various extra-budgetary funds. See "-State Extra-Budgetary Funds." The UST is\par excluded from the following discussion and, unless otherwise noted, figures in\par this section are exclusive of UST. See "-Russian Tax System."\par \par VAT is the largest source of tax-generated federal budget revenue, accounting\par for 30.7%, 24.3%, 30%, 30.1% and 28.6% of total cash revenue in 2007, 2008,\par 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. All VAT payments for domestically-produced\par and imported goods and services are transferred to the federal budget. The\par generally applicable rate of VAT is 18%, though certain items are charged at a\par reduced rate of 10% and even 0%, and certain limited items are exempt from VAT.\par \par The natural resource extraction taxes are an important, but fluctuating, source\par of revenue, due to the volatility of global commodities prices for exported\par goods, particularly oil and gas. The extraction taxes are allocated between the\par federal budget and the sub-federal budgets. For example, 100% of the revenues\par from the diamond extraction tax are allocated to the regional budgets, whereas\par 100% of the revenues from the natural gas extraction tax and, beginning\par 1 January 2010, 100% of the revenues from the oil extraction tax are\par transferred to the federal budget. Prior to 2010, tax revenues from the oil\par extraction tax were apportioned between the federal Government and the regional\par governments on a 95%-5% basis. See "-Federal-Sub-Federal Fiscal Relations."\par Certain taxes on resource extraction are adjusted in accordance with the\par international prices of commodities. In the case of crude oil, it is calculated\par at a specific rate in roubles per tonne depending on the current international\par oil price. For natural gas, the tax is set at a specified rate in roubles per\par thousand cubic metres of gas produced, subject to certain reductions.\par See "-Russian Tax System" below. The natural resource extraction taxes\par accounted for 15.7%, 18.7%, 14.7%, 17% and 18% of total federal budget revenue\par in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. The prominence of the natural\par resource extraction taxes, particularly from 2006 to 2008 and again in 2010 and\par 2011, was a result of the favourable global prices for oil and gas that\par prevailed at that time.\par \par The corporate profit tax base rate was 24% between 2006 and 2008. The rate was\par lowered to 20% effective 1 January 2009. The federal portion of the profit tax\par was 6.5% from 2006 through 2008, with the sub-federal portion equalling 17.5%.\par From 1 January 2009, the federal and sub-federal allocation was set at 2% and\par 18%, respectively, with regional authorities entitled to lower their 18% share\par to 13.5%. Corporate profit tax accounted for 8.7%, 8.7%, 2.9%, 3.1% and 3% of\par total cash revenue in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. The share\par of federal budget revenues attributable to the profit tax has diminished since\par 2009, due to the decline in the portion of profit tax allocated to the federal\par budget and, in 2009, the decline in Russian corporate earnings resulting from\par the global financial crisis.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's tax system also includes excise duties and a number of other taxes, though they are relatively insignificant to the federal budget. These taxes combined constituted approximately 2.3%, 2.2%, 2%, 2.9% and 3% of federal budget revenues in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Personal income tax is currently assessed at a flat rate of 13%. All personal income tax revenue is allocated to the sub-federal and local governments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Customs duties on exports and imports make a sizable contribution to the\par federal budget. Export duties accounted for 24.9% of federal revenues in 2007,\par before rising in 2008 to 32.6% of federal budget revenues, on the back of\par record high oil prices and an increase in the volume of natural gas exports. In\par 2009, export duties declined to 29.9%, due to the decline in oil prices and to\par the relatively greater contribution made by other taxes to the federal budget.\par Export duties as a share of federal budget revenues increased to 30.2% in 2010\par due, in part, to a 28.9% increase in the average price for Urals oil in 2010\par relative to 2009, and to 38.7% in 2011 due mainly to a 39.8% increase in the\par average Urals oil price in 2011.\par \par Import duties are also an important source of federal budget receipts and\par amounted to 6.6%, 7.1%, 6.8%, 7.1% and 6.1% of federal revenues in 2007, 2008,\par 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. The rise in import duties in 2008 relative\par to 2007 was primarily attributable to the increase in import receipts, which,\par in turn, was caused, in part, by an increase in the volume of imports and more\par generally by rising consumer demand and appreciation of the rouble. The 4.2%\par decline recorded in 2009, compared to 2008, corresponded with an overall\par decline in imports, which was due to weakening domestic demand resulting from\par the global financial crisis.\par \par Federal budget revenues are further enhanced by other non-tax revenue, which\par includes revenues from foreign trade activities (excluding customs duties),\par proceeds from the granting of licences for the use of natural resources,\par revenues from the use of state property, including Bank of Russia profit\par transfers, payments for government services and various other administrative\par payments. Other non-tax revenues constituted 11.2%, 6.4%, 13.6%, 12.6% and 8.3%\par of federal budget revenues in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively.\par The large decrease in other non-tax revenues in 2008 was primarily due to a\par decline in revenues from the use of state property, foreign trade activities\par (excluding customs duties), administrative payments and transfers from regional\par budgets to the federal budget. The significant increase recorded in 2009 was\par mainly the result of an increase in revenues from foreign trade activities\par (excluding customs duties) and revenues from the use of state property. Other\par non-tax revenues as a share of federal budget revenues decreased to 12.6% in\par 2010 due to the relatively greater contribution of other taxes to the federal\par budget and to a decrease in revenues from foreign trade activities (excluding\par customs duties) and administrative payments. In 2011, other non-tax revenues\par equalled 8.3% of overall federal budget revenues.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue Performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In each of the years from 2007 through 2011, federal budget revenue (as a share of GDP) has been higher than originally budgeted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2007, federal budget revenues as a share of GDP remained at 22.2% (or 23.4%\par of GDP, including UST, which was 4.9% higher than the budgeted amount).\par Compared with the preceding year, federal tax revenues grew by 28.9% in\par absolute terms, by 3.7% as a share of GDP and by 4.2% as a percentage of total\par revenue. This increase was driven mostly by a 49.7% increase in absolute terms\par in VAT proceeds, which, in turn, was caused by changes in the way domestic VAT\par is calculated. In 2007, non-tax revenues (including customs duties) also\par recorded an increase in absolute terms (17.3%); however, as a share of GDP and\par total revenues, they declined by 5% and 5.1%, respectively. The absolute\par increase in non-tax revenues was primarily driven by an increase in proceeds\par from import duties and by large growth in other non-tax revenues. The growth in\par import duty receipts was, in part, due to an increase in the volume of imports\par and, more generally, to growing domestic demand for foreign goods and to rouble\par appreciation. The increase in other non-tax revenues was attributable mainly to\par growth in revenues from the use of state property, from administrative payments\par and from transfers from regional budgets to the federal budget.\par \par In 2008, federal budget revenues amounted to 21.2% of GDP (or 22.5% of GDP,\par including UST, which was 6.1% higher than the originally budgeted target).\par Federal tax revenues in 2008 grew by 12.9% in absolute terms, although as a\par share of GDP and total revenues, they fell by 9.4% and 5.9%, respectively. The\par absolute increase in federal tax revenues was due primarily to a 41.5% growth\par in absolute terms in proceeds from natural resource taxes and to a 25.1% rise\par in UST allocated to the Pension Fund. The substantial growth in the natural\par resource taxes was, in turn, caused primarily by rising international oil\par prices. In 2008, non-tax revenues recorded a significant increase of 28.5% in\par absolute terms, together with increases of 2.1% and 8% as a share of GDP and\par total revenues, respectively. Growth in non-tax revenues was driven by a 55.8%\par increase in export duties and a 28.2% rise in import duties in absolute terms.\par Export duties constituted 5.5% of GDP and 6.9% of GDP in 2007 and 2008,\par respectively, whereas the share of GDP attributable to import duties remained\par constant at 1.5% in both years. Export duties grew in 2008 primarily because of\par higher international oil prices, whereas import duties increased, in part, due\par to an increase in the volume of imports and, more generally, to growing\par domestic demand for foreign goods and to rouble appreciation.\par \par In 2009, federal budget revenues equalled 17.6% of GDP (or 18.9% of GDP,\par including UST, which was 13.9% higher than the original budget target). Federal\par tax revenues in 2009 compared to 2008 declined by 25.5% in absolute terms,\par 20.6% as a share of GDP and 8% as a share of total revenues. The principal\par reasons for this decline were a 74.3% drop in absolute terms in profit tax due,\par in part, to a reduction of the profit tax rate from 24% to 20% in 2009 and a\par 38.5% contraction in absolute terms in proceeds from natural resource taxes. In\par 2009, in absolute terms and as a share of GDP, non-tax revenues also declined\par by 14.9% and 9.2%, respectively. However, as a share of total revenues, non-tax\par revenues grew by 9.3%. The nominal decrease in non-tax revenues was caused by a\par 28.6% decline in export duties and a 25.3% decline in import duties, both of\par which were a function of the weakening economy. Export duties contracted as\par worldwide demand for Russian commodity exports, especially oil, fell, and\par import duties declined in line with reduced domestic demand for foreign goods\par and services. A 66.9% nominal rise in other non-tax revenues (mainly the result\par of an increase in revenues from foreign trade activities (excluding customs\par duties) and revenues from the use of state property) partially offset the\par overall decline in non-tax revenues.\par \par In 2010, federal budget revenues equalled 18.4% of GDP, which was 14.3% higher\par than the original budget target. In absolute terms, federal tax revenues\par increased by 13% in 2010 compared to 2009. Although federal tax revenues, as a\par share of GDP, declined slightly from 10% in 2009 to 9.7% in 2010, as a share of\par total revenues, they increased from 49.6% in 2009 to 53% in 2010. The absolute\par increase in federal tax revenues was primarily due to a 21.9% increase in VAT\par proceeds and a 30.5% increase in profit tax proceeds, both mainly driven by GDP\par growth, and a 40% increase in proceeds from natural resource taxes, mainly\par attributable to higher oil prices in 2010 compared to 2009. In 2010 compared to\par 2009, non-tax revenues increased in absolute terms by 13.4%. However, as a\par share of GDP and as a share of total revenues, non-tax revenues decreased by\par 3.4% and 6.7%, respectively. The nominal increase in non-tax revenues was\par primarily driven by a 13.7% rise in customs revenues, due, in turn, to a 22.8%\par increase in export duties, and a 12.6% rise in other non-tax revenues. The\par increase in export duties was primarily due to an increase in the prices for\par Russian commodity exports including oil.\par \par In 2011, federal budget revenues equalled 20.9% of GDP, which was 18.8% higher\par than the original budget target. In absolute terms, federal tax revenues\par increased by 36.9% in 2011 compared to 2010. This increase, in turn, was due to\par nominal increases in revenues from profit tax (34.3%), VAT (30.1%), excise\par taxes (93.4%) and natural resource taxes (45.3%). In absolute terms, non-tax\par revenues rose by 37.2% in 2011 compared to 2010. This increase was due to an\par increase in revenues from customs duties. Revenues from customs duties rose by\par 54.3% in nominal terms, due mainly to an increase in export duties.\par \par The Government adopted a resolution in 2000 that allowed enterprises to\par reschedule their tax arrears in exchange for the full and orderly payment of\par current tax payments. In 2007 and 2008, tax arrears amounted to 3% of GDP and\par 2.3% of GDP, respectively. Tax arrears as a share of GDP increased to 3% in\par 2009, in part due to the global financial crisis, before falling again to 2.4%\par in 2010 and 1.8% in 2011.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government's privatisation proceeds, which are treated as revenue, accounted for 0.1% of GDP in 2007, 0.02% of GDP in 2008 and 0.01% of GDP in each of 2009 and 2010. Privatisation proceeds equalled 0.2% of GDP in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Budget Expenditure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information regarding federal budget expenditure for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                          Federal Budget Expenditure(1)                         \par \par                   For the                                                        \par                 year ended                                                       \par                 31 December                                                      \par                                                                                  \par                     2007       2008      2009         2010           2011        \par                                                                                  \par                  (millions                                                       \par                 of roubles)                                                      \par                                                                                  \par Total              5,986,562 7,570,879 9,636,823     10,115,627     10,935,223   \par expenditure                                                                      \par                                                                                  \par Total              5,581,582 7,064,111 9,127,048     10,115,627     10,935,223   \par 