                                                                          \par Law and order(4)              11.9   11.8   11.0    10.7    11.3               \par                                                                                \par Defence(5)                    14.9   14.7   13.0    12.6    13.8               \par                                                                                \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 National economy(6)           17.7   16.3   19.8    14.4    13.5           \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2    \par                                                                                \par Of which                                                                       \par                                                                                \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Housing expenditures           5.3    1.8    1.7     2.3     2.1           \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2    \par (7)                                                                            \par                                                                                \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Social and cultural           13.9   14.4   13.2    12.6    12.8           \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2    \par sphere(8)                                                                      \par                                                                                \par Environmental                  0.1    0.1    0.1     0.1     0.2               \par protection(9)                                                                  \par                                                                                \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial aid to              15.3   15.5   16.2    13.6    13.4               \par regions and other                                                          \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2    \par extra-budgetary                                                                \par transfers                                                                      \par                                                                                \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Transfers to state            18.7   22.4   23.2    27.3    23.3               \par extra-budgetary funds                                                      \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2    \par (10)                                                                           \par                                                                                \par GDP (billions of            33,248 41,277 38,809  45,166  54,369               \par roubles)                                                                       \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Figures in this table are current as of 15 February 2012. Beginning in\par 2011, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allocates interbudgetary transfers to each of the\par line items below "non-interest expenditure net of UST." However, for comparison\par purposes, the numbers in this table for each of the line items below\par "non-interest expenditure net of UST" exclude interbudgetary transfers.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) The UST was repealed as of 1 January 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Including interbudgetary transfers, in 2011, expenditures on state administration would equal 787,351 million roubles (1.4% of GDP and 7.2% of total expenditures net of UST).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Including interbudgetary transfers, in 2011, expenditures on law and order would equal 1,259,822 million roubles (2.3% of GDP and 11.5% of total expenditures net of UST).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (5) Including interbudgetary transfers, in 2011, expenditures on defence would\par equal 1,515,960 million roubles (2.8% of GDP and 13.9% of total expenditures\par net of UST).\par \par (6) Including interbudgetary transfers, in 2011, expenditures on the national\par economy would equal 2,069,963 million roubles (3.8% of GDP and 18.9% of total\par expenditures net of UST).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (7) Including interbudgetary transfers, in 2011, expenditures on housing expenditures would equal 279,799 million roubles (0.5% of GDP and 2.6% of total expenditures net of UST).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (8) Including interbudgetary transfers (except for transfers to state extra-budgetary funds), in 2011, expenditures on the social and cultural sphere would equal 1,823,689 million roubles (3.4% of GDP and 16.7% of total expenditures net of UST).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (9) Including interbudgetary transfers, in 2011, expenditures on environmental\par protection would equal 17,561 million roubles (0.0% of GDP and 0.2% of total\par expenditures net of UST).\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (10) Of the 2,547 billion roubles in transfers to state extra- budgetary funds in 2011, 2,380.1 billion roubles were allocated to the Pension Fund, 145.9 billion roubles were allocated to the Social Insurance Fund and 21.1 billion roubles were allocated to the Federal Medical Insurance Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Total federal budget expenditure amounted to 16.8% of GDP in 2007 primarily as\par a result of absolute spending growth in each of the principal non-interest\par expenditure categories, in particular, national economy (148.3%), state\par administration (86.6%), financial aid to regions and other extra-budgetary\par transfers (46.5%), social and cultural sphere (25.9%), defence (22%) and law\par and order (21.2%).\par \par In 2008, total federal budget expenditure amounted to 17.1% of GDP, an increase\par in absolute terms of 26.6% compared to 2007, primarily due to spending\par increases in each of the principal non-interest expenditure categories, in\par particular, the national economy, social and cultural sphere, financial aid to\par regions and other extra-budgetary transfers and transfers to state\par extra-budgetary funds.\par \par Total federal budget expenditure increased significantly in 2009, by 37.4% as a\par share of GDP to 23.5% of GDP, and by 29.2% in absolute terms. The principal\par reasons for this growth were increases in spending on the national economy (72%\par growth as a share of GDP), financial aid to regions and other extra-budgetary\par transfers (40.7% growth as a share of GDP) and transfers to state\par extra-budgetary funds (42.1% growth as a share of GDP). Fiscal spending as a\par percentage of GDP increased substantially in 2009 due to the implementation of\par the Government's anti-crisis programme.\par \par In 2010, total federal budget expenditure decreased by 4.7% as a share of GDP,\par despite increasing by 10.8% in absolute terms. The principal reason for this\par nominal growth was an increase in transfers to state extra-budgetary funds from\par 5.4% of GDP in 2009 to 6.1% of GDP in 2010. Expenditures on state\par administration, law and order, defence, the national economy, the social and\par cultural sphere and financial aid to the regions and other extra-budgetary\par transfers decreased as a share of GDP in 2010. Spending on the national economy\par experienced a particularly large decrease, falling by 19.1% in absolute terms\par and by 37.2% as a share of GDP.\par \par In 2011, total federal budget expenditure decreased by 10.3% as a share of GDP,\par despite increasing by 8.1% in absolute terms. The principal reason for this\par nominal growth was a 13.9% increase in nominal terms in state administration\par and law and order expenditures (excluding interbudgetary transfers), an 18.6%\par increase in nominal terms in defence expenditures (excluding interbudgetary\par transfers) and a 10.9% increase in nominal terms in social and cultural\par expenditures.\par \par Debt service (which includes only payments in respect of discount and interest\par on Government debt) as a share of GDP remained roughly constant during the\par period under review (0.4%, 0.4%, 0.5%, 0.4% and 0.5% in 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010\par and 2011, respectively). As a share of total expenditure, debt service\par contracted from 2.6% in 2007 to 1.9% in each of 2009 and 2010 and to 2.4% in\par 2011. The decline in debt service through 2010 was attributable in part to the\par Government's conservative debt policies during this period and conservative\par fiscal policies in 2007 and 2008.\par \par Between 2007 and 2011, the share of non-interest spending in federal\par expenditures increased by 96.2% in absolute terms and by 19.5% as a share of\par GDP. Spending in each of the principal non-interest expenditure categories\par contributed to this growth. In particular, nominal spending (excluding\par interbudgetary transfers) on law and order, defence and the national economy\par grew by 85.4%, 82% and 50%, respectively, between 2007 and 2011. Nominal\par spending on social and cultural programmes also grew during this period.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deficit Financing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2007 and 2008, the enlarged budget (comprising the federal, consolidated\par sub-federal and local budgets and state extra-budgetary funds) registered a\par surplus. The enlarged budget's surplus was 6% of GDP in 2007 and 4.9% of GDP in\par 2008. In 2009 and 2010, Russia recorded enlarged budget deficits of 6.3% of GDP\par and 3.5% of GDP, respectively. In 2011, Russia's enlarged budget returned to\par surplus, recording a surplus of 1.6% of GDP.\par \par The federal budget registered annual surpluses of 5.4% of GDP in 2007 and 4.1%\par of GDP in 2008 and an annual deficit of 6% of GDP in 2009 and 4% of GDP in\par 2010. In 2011, the federal budget recorded a surplus of 0.8%. The federal\par budget recorded primary surpluses of 5.8% in 2007 and 4.5% in 2008, primary\par deficits of 5.5% of GDP in 2009 and 3.6% of GDP in 2010 and a primary surplus\par of 1.3% in 2011. The surplus decreased in 2008 because of a contraction in tax\par revenues as a share of GDP, particularly domestic VAT proceeds, and a slight\par rise in expenditures mainly in the form of transfers to state extra-budgetary\par funds. In 2009, the federal budget went into deficit due to a decline in tax\par and non-tax revenues, in particular, revenues collected from profit tax, the\par natural resource taxes and export duties, and to an increase in government\par spending, including spending on the national economy, defence, law and order\par and social programmes and financial aid to regions and other extra-budgetary\par transfers and state extra-budgetary funds. The decline in revenues was mainly\par due to the negative effects of the global financial crisis, including lower\par worldwide demand for Russia's commodities exports. The increase in\par expenditures, in particular, spending on the national economy and transfers to\par regional budgets, was, in part, the result of the Government's fiscal stimulus\par package designed to mitigate the effects of the global financial crisis on the\par Russian economy. Despite a 10.2% nominal rise in expenditures in 2010 compared\par to 2009 (see "-Federal Budget Expenditure," above), the federal budget deficit\par improved to 4% of GDP in 2010 from 6% of GDP in 2009 due to nominal increases\par in both tax and non-tax revenues. See "-Federal Budget Revenue-Revenue\par Performance." The federal budget recorded a surplus in 2011 due to both an\par increase in tax and non-tax revenues. As a share of GDP, tax revenues increased\par from 9.7% in 2010 to 11% in 2011, which, in turn, was due to an increase in\par revenues from VAT, excise taxes and natural resource taxes as a share of GDP.\par As a share of GDP, non-tax revenues increased from 8.6% of GDP in 2010 to 9.9%\par of GDP in 2011, due mainly to growth in revenues from export duties. Total\par expenditures, as a share of GDP, dropped from 22.4% in 2010 to 20.1% in 2011,\par despite increasing in absolute terms by 8.1%. This nominal increase was due to\par a 34.7% rise in debt servicing expenditures and a 7.6% rise in non-interest\par expenditures, particularly in expenditures on state administration, law and\par order, defence and in the social and cultural sphere.\par \par The Government established the Stabilisation Fund in 2004, initially\par contributing to it approximately 106 billion roubles from the financial\par reserve. The Stabilisation Fund, the rules for which were set out in the Budget\par Code, was created to reduce the effects of oil price fluctuations on the\par Russian budgetary process. Revenue derived from export duties on crude oil and\par the oil extraction tax, attributable to oil prices in excess of the budget's\par oil price assumption (U.S.$20 per barrel in 2004 and 2005 and U.S.$27 per\par barrel in 2006 and 2007), were contributed to the Stabilisation Fund. The base\par amount of the Stabilisation Fund was fixed at 500 billion roubles. If oil\par prices fell below the budget's assumed price and, as a consequence, the\par Stabilisation Fund's assets fell below the base amount of 500 billion roubles,\par the Stabilisation Fund could only be used for deficit financing.\par \par The federal surplus accounted for 5.4% of GDP for 2007. Net foreign financing\par in 2007 equalled negative 0.5% of GDP, including the redemption of foreign debt\par amounting to 0.6% of GDP. Net domestic borrowing amounted to 0.5% of GDP. In\par 2007, an additional 1,602.8 billion roubles of excess revenue was transferred\par to the Stabilisation Fund, raising its balance at the end of the year to\par 3,849.1 billion roubles.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2008, the federal surplus totalled 4.1% of GDP. Net foreign financing equalled negative 0.3% of GDP, including the redemption of foreign debt equalling 0.4% of GDP and gross foreign borrowing of 0.1% of GDP. Net domestic borrowing was 0.3% of GDP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2008, the Stabilisation Fund was replaced by the Reserve Fund and National Wealth Fund, and its remaining balance was transferred to the two new funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Reserve Fund was created to ensure the availability of sufficient budgetary\par funding in the event of a shortfall in the federal budget due to a decline in\par oil and gas revenues and to alleviate inflationary pressures resulting from the\par export of natural resources. The Reserve Fund consists of proceeds generated\par from export customs duties on oil, oil products and gas as well as from taxes\par levied on the extraction of hydrocarbons. Proceeds in the Reserve Fund must not\par exceed 10% of Russia's GDP forecast for the particular year. At year-end 2008,\par the Reserve Fund had a balance of 4,027.6 billion roubles.\par \par The National Wealth Fund was created to support Russia's pension system.\par Pursuant to a January 2008 Government resolution, as amended, proceeds from the\par National Wealth Fund may be invested, subject to certain conditions, in a range\par of assets, including foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rouble-denominated assets, debt and\par equity securities of both Russian and foreign entities and deposits with banks,\par such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . No more than 655 billion roubles may be deposited in\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and no more than 40% of the National Wealth Fund's overall\par proceeds may be invested in rouble assets. At year-end 2008, the National\par Wealth Fund had a balance of 2,584.5 billion roubles.\par \par In 2009, the federal budget recorded a deficit of 6% of GDP. Net foreign\par financing in 2009 amounted to negative 0.3% of GDP and included the redemption\par of foreign debt of 0.4% of GDP and gross foreign borrowing of 0.08% of GDP. Net\par domestic borrowing was 1% of GDP. In 2009, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  began to use\par proceeds from the Reserve Fund to finance the federal budget deficit, spending\par 2,964.8 billion roubles by year-end 2009 for this purpose. In 2009, the\par Ministry also began to invest Reserve Fund money in IMF instruments, including\par special drawing rights. As a result of these transactions, at the end of 2009,\par Russia had acquired U.S.$8.9 billion of special drawing rights. See "Balance of\par Payments and Foreign Trade-Official Reserves." As of 1 January 2010, the\par balance of proceeds in the Reserve Fund was 1,831 billion roubles. As of\par 1 January 2010, the balance of the National Wealth Fund, including the amount\par on deposit with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , equalled approximately 2,769 billion roubles.\par \par In 2010, the federal budget recorded a deficit of 4% of GDP. Net foreign\par financing equalled 0.3% of GDP, including the redemption of foreign debt\par equalling 0.2% of GDP and gross foreign borrowing of 0.5% of GDP. Net domestic\par borrowing was 1.4% of GDP. In 2010, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to use\par proceeds from the Reserve Fund to finance the federal budget deficit, spending\par approximately 3,830.6 billion roubles for this purpose. As of 1 January 2011,\par the balance of proceeds in the Reserve Fund was 775.2 billion roubles. During\par 2010, the balance of the National Wealth Fund decreased by 2.5 billion roubles\par to 2,695.5 billion roubles. The reduction in proceeds of the National Wealth\par Fund during this period was primarily attributable to the co-financing of\par pensions.\par \par In 2011, the federal budget recorded a surplus of 0.8% of GDP. Redemption of\par foreign debt equalled 0.2% of GDP and gross foreign borrowing equalled 0.1% of\par GDP. Net domestic borrowing was 0.6% of GDP. In 2011, no money was spent from\par the Reserve Fund and 3.4 billion roubles were spent from the National Wealth\par Fund. The balance of the Reserve Fund and the National Wealth Fund, at\par 1 January 2012, was 811.5 billion roubles and 2,794.4 billion roubles,\par respectively. As of 1 February 2012, the balance of the Reserve Fund increased\par to 1,863 billion roubles. According to the 2012 Budget Law, the balance of the\par Reserve Fund is expected to amount to 2,379 billion roubles at year-end 2012,\par 2,971 billion roubles at year-end 2013 and 4,118 billion roubles at year-end\par 2014, and the balance of the National Wealth Fund is expected to be\par 2,662 billion roubles at year-end 2012, 2,671 billion roubles at year-end 2013\par and 2,801 billion roubles at year-end 2014.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal-Sub-Federal Fiscal Relations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The three sources of funding for the Federation subjects are tax revenues (both federal and regional), non-tax revenues and transfers from the federal budget.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sub-federal and local governments are directly assigned a specified portion of\par the revenue from certain federal taxes. From 2009, sub-federal and local\par governments have been allocated proceeds from the following federal taxes: 100%\par of personal income tax, 18% of the profit tax (see discussion below), 100% of\par excise taxes on alcohol production and beer, 50% of excise taxes on ethyl\par alcohol from foodstuffs and on certain other products containing alcohol, 100%\par of widespread mineral deposits and diamond extraction taxes, (in 2009 only) 5%\par of the hydrocarbon tax (excluding the natural gas extraction tax) and 60% of\par certain other mineral extraction taxes. Taxes imposed directly by the\par sub-federal subjects since 2009 include the property tax on organisations, the\par transport tax and the gambling business tax. Taxes imposed directly by local\par governments include the land tax and the property tax on individuals. The share\par of domestic VAT transferable to the regions is zero.\par \par In 2007 and 2008, Russia imposed a profit tax at the unified rate of 24%. Of\par the 24% profit tax, 6.5% was allocated to the federal budget and 17.5% was\par allocated to the regional budgets. The unified profit tax rate was lowered to\par 20% effective 1 January 2009, of which 2% is allocated to the federal budget\par and 18% to the regional budgets, which regional authorities, at their\par discretion, may lower to 13.5%. 100% of the revenues from the natural gas\par extraction tax and, effective 1 January 2010, 100% of the revenues from the\par hydrocarbon extraction tax are transferred to the federal budget. Between 2007\par and 2009, 95% of the hydrocarbon extraction tax was transferred to the federal\par budget, with the remaining 5% allocated to regional budgets. The federal\par authorities may change the distribution of taxes in a given year only through\par legislative amendment.\par \par In 2009, revenues of the consolidated sub-federal budgets stood at 5.9 trillion\par roubles, an approximate 4.4% decrease compared to 2008. In 2008 and 2009, tax\par and non-tax revenues comprised 82.3% and 75%, respectively, of the overall\par revenues of the consolidated sub-federal budgets. In 2010, revenues of the\par consolidated sub-federal budgets stood at 6.5 trillion roubles, an approximate\par 10.3% increase compared to 2009. Federal financial aid accounted for 21.1% of\par the overall revenues of the consolidated sub-federal budgets in 2010, compared\par to 25% in 2009. In 2011, revenues of the consolidated sub-federal budgets stood\par at 7.6 trillion roubles, an approximate 16.9% increase compared to 2010.\par Federal financial aid accounted for 8.5% of overall revenues of the\par consolidated sub-federal budgets in 2011, a significant decrease from 2010 when\par federal financial aid comprised 21.1% of overall consolidated sub-federal\par budget revenues.\par \par Due to significant differences in the level of social-economic development\par among the Federation subjects, the Government has established various regional\par support funds, i.e., expenditure line items in the federal budget, through\par which it allocates by means of subsidies and transfers a portion of federal tax\par revenues to those regions most in need of assistance.\par \par Since its establishment in 1994, the Fund for Regional Support has been a\par primary source of federal financial support to regional governments. Through\par this fund, 260.4 billion roubles, 328.6 billion roubles, 374 billion roubles\par and 397 billion roubles in 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively, were\par transferred to the regions. In 2011, federal subsidies channelled through this\par fund amounted also to 397 billion roubles. As a result of these subsidies, the\par gap in financial means between the wealthiest Federation subjects and the\par poorest has narrowed.\par \par The Compensation Fund was established in 2001, initially to provide financial\par support to regional budgets to help finance obligations, including under\par various social laws, such as the Law On Social Protection of the Handicapped\par and the Law On Childhood Benefits. In 2008, the purpose of the Compensation\par Fund was broadened to include the financing of any regional expenses incurred\par in connection with the performance of obligations delegated to the Federation\par subjects by the federal Government. In 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, federal\par subsidies channelled through the Compensation Fund amounted to 141.2 billion\par roubles, 153.2 billion roubles, 285 billion roubles and 379 billion roubles,\par respectively. In 2011, federal subsidies transferred through the Compensation\par Fund amounted to 338.3 billion roubles.\par \par The Fund for Co-Financing Social Expenditure was established in 2002 (and\par renamed the Fund for Co-Financing Expenditure in 2008) to co-finance\par expenditures on health care, culture, education and salaries of public sector\par employees, as well as subsidies for housing and public utilities. Federal\par subsidies provided through this fund amounted to 356.8 billion roubles,\par 435.9 billion roubles, 531 billion roubles and 414.5 billion roubles in 2007,\par 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively. In 2011, federal subsidies provided through\par this fund equalled 514.7 billion roubles.\par \par Consolidated sub-federal budget expenditures (including expenditures financed\par by federal transfers and federal budget loans) were 14.4% of GDP in 2007, 15.1%\par of GDP in 2008, 16.1% of GDP in 2009. 14.7% of GDP in 2010 and 14.1% of GDP in\par 2011. Social expenditures by regional and local authorities are substantial\par (approximately 7.8% of GDP in 2007 and 7.4%, 8.4%, 8.1% and 8.3% in 2008, 2009,\par 2010 and 2011, respectively). Between 2007 and 2011, sub-federal governments\par financed approximately 76-79% of education expenditure. From 2007 through 2009,\par sub-federal government expenditure accounted for a decreasing proportion of\par health care expenditure (79.4%, 73.8% and 68.3% of total health care\par expenditure in 2007, 2008 and 2009, respectively). In 2010 and 2011,\par consolidated sub-federal government expenditure accounted for 69.6% and 70.5%\par of health care expenditure, respectively. Sub-federal spending on the national\par economy, a portion of which is allocated to housing programmes, is also\par substantial, amounting to 5.1%, 5.4%, 5.1%, 4.3% and 4.2% of GDP in 2007, 2008,\par 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively.\par \par Significant progress has been made in Russia in the area of federal/sub-federal\par fiscal relations. All extraordinary fiscal arrangements between the regions and\par the federal Government have been eliminated. The transfer of federal funds to\par the regions has become more transparent and efficient through the creation of\par new funds, the reconfiguration of existing funds and the introduction of new\par methods to calculate transfer volumes. In 2003, the Law on General Principles\par of Regional Government Organisation was amended, and a new law on local\par self-governance was adopted. These two laws established a clear division of\par spending powers among the various levels of the budgetary system. They also\par introduced a mechanism for transferring authority from one budgetary level to\par another, intended to prevent non-financed mandates and to impose temporary\par restrictions on the budgetary powers of insolvent regional and local\par authorities. Amendments to the Budget Code and Tax Code that came into effect\par in 2005 provide a framework for the implementation of the division of fiscal\par powers.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State Extra-Budgetary Funds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par At present, the largest state extra-budgetary funds are the Pension Fund\par (expenditures of 1,786.5 billion roubles, 2,357.8 billion roubles,\par 3,008.7 billion roubles and 4,249.2 billion roubles in 2007, 2008, 2009 and\par 2010, respectively), the Social Insurance Fund of the Russian Federation (the\par "Social Insurance Fund") (expenditures of 303.1 billion roubles, 379.4 billion\par roubles, 448.5 billion roubles and 491.2 billion roubles in 2007, 2008, 2009\par and 2010, respectively) and the Federal Medical Insurance Fund (expenditures of\par 158.2 billion roubles, 168.7 billion roubles, 130 billion roubles and\par 109.1 billion roubles in 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively). According to\par preliminary data, in 2011, expenditures from the Pension Fund, Social Insurance\par Fund and Federal Medical Insurance Fund equalled 4,822.4 billion roubles,\par 456.9 billion roubles and 336 billion roubles, respectively. The substantial\par increase in the Federal Medical Insurance Fund in 2011 was mainly due to\par expenditure as part of the 2011 healthcare modernisation programme. The large\par rise in Pension Fund expenditure in 2009 and 2010 was due to a Government\par decision to increase the size of pensions to subsistence levels. Between 25%\par and 30% of the Russian population receives pensions.\par \par In 2001, nearly all social contributions were consolidated into the UST, a\par single 35.6% payroll tax, paid by employers. In 2005, the UST was reformed, and\par a regressive scale of payroll for higher wages was introduced. The top rate was\par reduced to 26%, with 20% transferred to the federal budget, 3.1% transferred to\par federal and regional medical insurance funds and 2.9% transferred to the Social\par Insurance Fund. Before the UST was abolished, the employer was entitled to\par offset a portion of its 20% UST transfer to the federal budget against its\par mandatory contributions to the Pension Fund. Since 2005, the Pension Fund also\par receives transfers from the federal budget to finance social benefits and,\par until the repeal of the UST at the end of 2009, to cover the difference between\par UST proceeds and pension commitments. Regional medical insurance funds receive\par transfers from regional budgets and the Federal Medical Insurance Fund. See\par "The Russian Economy-Pension Reform" above and "-Russian Tax System" below.\par \par Effective 1 January 2010, the UST was repealed and replaced with a system of\par direct payments to state extra-budgetary funds. In 2010, the employer made\par direct payments equal to 26% of the first 415,000 roubles of each employee's\par annual salary, which amount was increased in 2011 to 34% of the first 463,000\par roubles of each employee's annual salary. Contributions to these social funds\par do not affect the basis for determining the employer's profit tax.\par \par The current state extra-budgetary funds have generally remained balanced since\par their creation in 1992, reflecting their stable revenue base. State\par extra-budgetary funds recorded an overall surplus of 0.5% of GDP in 2007, 0.9%\par of GDP in 2008, 0.5% of GDP in 2009, 0.7% of GDP in 2010 and 0.8% in 2011. The\par Pension Fund ran overall surpluses of 0.5% of GDP in 2007, 0.9% of GDP in 2008,\par 0.6% of GDP in 2009, 0.8% of GDP in 2010 and 0.6% of GDP in 2011. However,\par these surpluses in the Pension Fund take into account a growing proportion of\par contributions to the Pension Fund from the federal budget. For example\par contributions from the federal budget to the Pension Fund amounted to\par 197.1 billion roubles (0.5% of GDP) in 2008, 399.3 billion roubles (1% of GDP)\par in 2009, 1,316.3 billion roubles (2.9% of GDP) in 2010 and 924.4 billion\par roubles (1.7% of GDP) in 2011. In 2012, contributions to the Pension Fund from\par the federal budget are anticipated to amount to 1,075.1 billion roubles (1.9%\par of GDP). In 2009, 2010 and 2011, the federal budget also allocated 70.7 billion\par roubles (0.2% of GDP), 45.1 billion roubles (0.1% of GDP) and 65.4 billion\par roubles (0.1% of GDP), respectively, to cover the deficit in the Social\par Insurance Fund.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2012, the Pension Fund's budget law envisages revenue of 5,696.9 billion roubles, or 9.7% of GDP, and expenditure of 5,407.3 billion roubles, or 9.2% of GDP. Pension Fund expenditures are expected to increase in the future as a result of demographic and other factors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian Tax System\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Beginning in 1999, Russia initiated a tax reform programme, which has reduced\par the total number of taxes, improved the stability, transparency and fairness of\par the system through the elimination of certain tax exemptions, reduced the\par overall tax burden and improved tax collection. As a result of these reforms,\par the following taxation measures have been put into place.\par \par Russia imposes a flat personal income tax rate (13%), which replaced the\par previous progressive scale of between 12% and 30%. Certain types of income,\par such as interest income from bank deposits in excess of certain limits,\par however, remain subject to tax at 35%. The tax rate for income in the form of\par dividends is 9%, and the personal income tax rate for non-residents is now 30%,\par except for income in the form of dividends, which is taxed at 15%, and income\par from certain types of labour activities, which is taxed at 13%. Certain types\par of income earned by an individual from a donation or inheritance is subject to\par the 13% personal income tax instead of the previous donation and inheritance\par taxes.\par \par Prior to 2010, Russia also imposed the UST, which, through 2009, was assessed\par pursuant to the following regressive scale: a rate of 26% on annual wages up to\par 280,000 roubles; a rate of 10% on the portion of annual wages between 280,001\par roubles and 600,000 roubles; and a rate of 2% for the portion of annual wages\par in excess of 600,000 roubles. The UST was abolished as of 1 January 2010. See\par "-Federal Budget Revenue-Sources of Federal Revenue" and "-State\par Extra-Budgetary Funds" above.\par \par Prior to 2009, Russia imposed a profit tax at a unified rate of 24%. In 2007\par and 2008, of the 24% profit tax, 6.5% was allocated to the federal budget and\par 17.5% was allocated to the regional budgets. The unified profit tax rate was\par lowered to 20% effective 1 January 2009, of which 2% is allocated to the\par federal budget and 18% to the regional budgets, which regional authorities, at\par their discretion, may lower to 13.5%. During recent years, the number of\par permitted profit-related tax deductions was expanded, for example, the ability\par to deduct research and development expenses was introduced. In addition, a\par deductible amortisation premium of 10% or 30% on new capital assets (depending\par on the asset) was introduced. Profit tax is calculated using an accrual, as\par opposed to cash, method.\par \par Russia imposes a modified royalty tax on natural resource extraction, which\par replaced three taxes on natural resources, including the royalty and excise\par taxes on crude oil and mineral restoration payments. The natural resource\par extraction tax on oil is calculated according to a formula that takes\par international oil prices and the rouble exchange rate into account. Since 1\par January 2012, the natural resource extraction tax rate for oil has equalled 446\par roubles per tonne. The natural resource extraction tax charged on natural gas\par is currently 509 roubles per thousand cubic metres, although for certain\par taxpayers this amount may be reduced.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Small businesses pay a single tax under a simplified tax system, pursuant to which such firms elect to pay either 6% of revenues or 15% of profits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2004 and 2005, sales tax and a number of local taxes and fees were either\par reduced or eliminated. The general VAT rate was reduced from 20% to 18% (though\par some goods retained their preferential 0% and 10% rates), and the 0.8% tax on\par securities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was abolished. A stamp duty applicable to the issuance of\par securities was introduced and is assessed at a rate of 0.2% of the aggregate\par amount of the securities being issued up to 200,000 roubles. Excise duties were\par indexed or, in the case of natural gas, abolished.\par \par Beginning in 2006, general VAT is calculated using an accrual method, as\par opposed to the previous choice between cash or accrual methods. Additionally,\par input VAT may now be offset or refunded as soon as an invoice is received,\par instead of after payment has been made for the purchased asset. As of 1 January\par 2007, VAT refunds on transactions levied at a 0% rate may be claimed through a\par VAT declaration, eliminating the need for a separate VAT return.\par \par A property tax is imposed on legal entities and organisations at a maximum rate\par of 2.2% of the tax base. Property tax revenue is allocated to sub-federal\par budgets, and regional authorities may set lower rates in their jurisdictions. A\par preferred tax regime for agricultural producers is also in place, under which a\par unified agricultural tax (including a preferred regime for payments to social\par funds) has replaced corporate income tax, VAT and property tax. Regional land\par taxes are determined region-by-region and are calculated on a cadastral-value\par base, with a maximum general rate of 1.5%.\par \par Export duties on oil are determined on a monthly basis by the Government based\par on the average market price of Urals oil and are currently assessed in the\par following manner. For average market oil prices of up to U.S.$109.50 per tonne,\par export duty is 0%; for average market prices between U.S.$109.50 and U.S.$146\par per tonne, the export duty is assessed at up to 35% of the difference between\par the average market price and U.S.$109.50; for average market prices between\par U.S.$146 and U.S.$182.50 per tonne, the export duty is assessed at up to 45% of\par the difference between the average market price and U.S.$146 plus U.S.$12.78;\par and when the average market price exceeds U.S.$182.50 per tonne, the export\par duty is assessed at up to 65% of the difference between the market price and\par U.S.$182.50 plus U.S.$29.20. For March 2012, the export duty on oil is\par U.S.$411.20 per tonne. The export duty on natural gas is 30% of its customs\par value.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiscal Reforms\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2005, the Government adopted reforms for its social benefit programme and\par reforms resulting in the redistribution of tax revenue and obligations between\par budgets of different levels.\par \par Social benefit reform is focused on eliminating unfunded benefit entitlements\par and replacing social privileges with targeted social benefits, generally in the\par form of monthly cash payments. See "The Russian Economy-Social Benefits and\par Expenditure." These changes are intended to improve the efficiency of the\par social protection programmes and to balance the responsible government's social\par obligations and financial resources.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Certain obligations have also been redistributed between governments. Civil defence and internal security outlays, military commissariats and care for the disabled have been transferred to the federal budget. Expenditures for the maintenance of kindergartens, vocational-technical schools and general-education schools and certain other social expenses have been transferred to regional budgets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since 2005, federal and all regional and local budgets have been prepared in\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 accordance with international standards of budget accounting. These new classifications relate to budget revenues, expenditures and sources of deficit financing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Government adopted a plan for the reform of the 2004-2006 budgetary\par process, which was intended to focus on results-oriented budgeting, in an\par attempt to shift the focus from managing financial resources to managing\par results. The reform measures included increased autonomy and responsibility for\par the departments, agencies and individuals managing expenditures and explicit\par medium-term benchmarks. As a part of these reforms, the Government envisaged\par the shift to multi-year budgeting and developed rules regulating deviations\par from the volume and structure of budget appropriations. Government liabilities\par were also subdivided into separate recurring and non-recurring amounts. These\par reforms continued to be implemented in 2007 and 2008.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In June 2010, the Government approved a programme through 2012 that is designed to enhance the effectiveness of budgetary expenditures. In particular, the programme envisages the development of measures for ensuring the long-term stability of the country's budgetary system and for continuing the shift towards results-oriented budgeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          MONETARY AND FINANCIAL SYSTEM                         \par \par The Bank of Russia\par \par The Bank of Russia is \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , established by the Constitution\par and federal law as an independent entity with specific powers and\par responsibilities. Pursuant to the Law on the Bank of Russia, as amended (the\par "Law on the Bank of Russia"), the Bank of Russia is charged with responsibility\par for protecting and ensuring the stability of the rouble, developing and\par strengthening Russia's banking system and ensuring the efficient and\par uninterrupted functioning of the settlement system. The Law on the Bank of\par Russia prohibits any government authority from interfering with activities\par undertaken by the Bank of Russia in furtherance of its constitutionally and\par legislatively determined responsibilities, permits the Bank of Russia to issue\par resolutions within its area of competence and subjects all federal laws and\par federal executive actions that would affect the activities of the Bank of\par Russia to review by the Bank of Russia. The Bank of Russia is accountable to\par the State Duma in that it is required to present an annual report of its\par activities to the State Duma and to solicit the State Duma's views on the Bank\par of Russia's monetary policy.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Russia has monopoly authority over the printing of money and is also responsible for acting as lender of last resort to Russian credit institutions, holding a substantial part of Russia's official reserves, registering credit organisations (together with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Tax Service\f28 \uc2 \f2 ), issuing banking licences and overseeing the bankruptcy and liquidation proceedings of credit institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bank of Russia's governor, who is also the chair of the Bank of Russia's\par 12-member board of directors, is nominated by the President and confirmed by\par the State Duma. The governor is appointed for a four-year term, and under the\par Law on the Bank of Russia may not be dismissed except pursuant to grounds\par provided in the Law on the Bank of Russia, including violation of a federal law\par relating to the activities of the Bank of Russia or the commission of a crime.\par Sergei Ignatiev, who began his term on 20 March 2002, is the current governor\par of the Bank of Russia. Members of the board of directors of the Bank of Russia\par are nominated by the Bank of Russia's governor and confirmed by the State Duma.\par Board members serve four-year terms, may be dismissed only with the governor's\par approval and may not be officers of the Government or members of the Federal\par Assembly.\par \par The Bank of Russia registers (together with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Tax Service\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) and\par licenses all credit institutions, regulates their banking activities and\par establishes accounting and record-keeping standards. The Bank of Russia also\par sets standards for banks with respect to capital adequacy, loan loss provisions\par and exposure to individual creditors and shareholders. The Bank of Russia\par monitors compliance by commercial banks with its regulations through a\par reporting system, periodic inspections and requests for information, and\par enforces its regulations through a variety of sanctions, including the power to\par issue warnings, impose temporary restrictions on a bank's activities, appoint a\par temporary administrator, impose fines and revoke banking licences.\par \par The Bank of Russia also supervises acquisitions in the banking sector. Any\par acquisition of more than 1% of the charter capital of a credit institution\par requires notice to the Bank of Russia, and any acquisition of more than 20%\par requires prior Bank of Russia approval. Prior Bank of Russia consent is also\par required for any direct or indirect acquisition of shares in a credit\par institution crossing certain ownership thresholds (including, for example, 25%,\par 50% and 75% thresholds) and for any purchase of a 100% stake in such credit\par institution.\par \par Amendments to the Law on the Bank of Russia enacted in 2002 expanded the Bank\par of Russia's functions and adjusted the composition of the National Banking\par Committee, the government-controlled supervisory board of the Bank of Russia.\par The Committee consists of representatives of the Government, the Federation\par Council, the State Duma, the Presidential Administration and the Bank of\par Russia's governor. The Bank of Russia's accounting rules and staff salaries are\par subject to the Committee's approval. According to the Law on the Bank of\par Russia, as of 1 January 2012, the statutory capital of the Bank of Russia was\par three billion roubles.\par \par Monetary Policy\par \par Money Supply\par \par The following table sets forth information concerning Russia's money supply as\par of the dates indicated:\par \par                               Monetary Survey(1)                               \par \par                     As of                                                          \par                   1 January                                                        \par                                                                                    \par                      2008        2009        2010        2011        2012          \par                                                                                    \par                   (millions                                                        \par                       of                                                           \par                    roubles,                                                        \par                     except                                                         \par                     ratio)                                                  