                                                \par                                                                                    \par Other               2,065,195   2,968,660   3,600,220   3,893,533   5,013,125      \par nonfinancial                                                                       \par organisations                                                                      \par                                                                                    \par Households          4,289,921   5,053,098   6,408,324   8,266,026   9,790,833      \par                                                                                    \par Deposits not           40,518     307,041     336,399     289,021     533,344      \par included in broad                                                                  \par money                                                                              \par                                                                                    \par Securities not        701,375     657,165     617,860     643,084     792,411      \par included in broad                                                                  \par money                                                                              \par                                                                                    \par Shares and other    3,201,948   5,151,066   6,181,436   6,968,692   7,593,488      \par equity                                                                             \par                                                                                    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other items (net) (315,202) (123,049) 915,016 817,465 1,729,554\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2       \par                                                                                    \par Other liabilities   1,306,187   2,197,940   3,616,755   3,767,406   5,054,781      \par                                                                                    \par Other assets        1,438,613   2,079,655   2,351,613   2,591,521   2,915,715      \par                                                                                    \par Memo:                                                                              \par                                                                                    \par Monetary base       5,513,332   5,578,717   6,467,318   8,190,329   8,644,100      \par (broad                                                                             \par definition)(4)                                                                     \par                                                                                    \par Money supply (M2)  12,868,978  12,975,865  15,267,572  20,011,894  24,543,400      \par (national                                                                          \par definition)(5)                                                                     \par                                                                                    \par Velocity of M2(6)        3.16        3.11        3.05        2.67        2.64      \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Certain data presented in this table differ from previously published data due to regular revisions by the Bank of Russia. Data are presented in accordance with the IMF's "Monetary and Financial Statistics Manual" (IMF, 2000). Data in this table are current as of 9 February 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Comprises cash issued by the Bank of Russia into circulation less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings of the Bank of Russia and credit institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Comprises domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  current and other demand deposits within the banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Comprises cash outside the Bank of Russia, correspondent account balances, deposit account balances and required reserves of credit institutions maintained at the Bank of Russia and Bank of Russia bonds held by banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (5) M2 is defined as total cash in circulation (outside banks) and balances in the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on accounts of resident non-financial organisations, financial organisations (excluding credit organisations) and individuals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (6) Average velocity based on monthly series of M2 and nominal GDP for the period\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Bank of Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Russia's monetary policy is focused on creating conditions for\par lower inflation and on maintaining exchange-rate stability. In response to the\par global financial crisis, beginning in the second half of 2008, the Bank of\par Russia implemented a set of additional measures designed to maintain financial\par sector stability, and, in particular, expanded the volume of liquidity\par available to the banking and corporate sectors. As the Russian economy\par continues to recover from the crisis, the Bank of Russia intends to gradually\par reduce its use of anti-crisis measures, while at the same time maintaining a\par stimulative, growth-oriented monetary policy with relatively low interest\par rates. Going forward, the Bank of Russia intends to switch to an\par inflation-targeting and free floating exchange rate regime and at the same time\par continue the transition from an exchange rate based policy to an interest rate\par based policy.\par \par In 2006 and 2007, large purchases of dollars by the Bank of Russia resulted in\par the rapid growth of Russia's international reserves and monetary aggregates. M2\par increased by 48.7% in 2006 compared to 2005 and by 43.5% in 2007 compared to\par 2006. In 2006, positive trends in foreign trade continued and Russia\par experienced additional growth in its current account surplus. Although the\par current account surplus and trade balance deteriorated in 2007, each remained\par higher than its 2004 level (the current account surplus and trade balance in\par 2007 exceeded their 2004 levels by approximately 31% and 53%, respectively).\par Concurrently, positive changes in the Russian economy, an improved investment\par climate, sustained economic growth and political stability led to increases in\par foreign investment. The general decline of the dollar against other major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also contributed to this exchange inflow, as did the high prices for\par commodity exports, particularly oil, and the low interest rates available in\par the international capital markets, which facilitated high levels of foreign\par borrowing by the Russian corporate sector. Since 2004, Russia has been assigned\par an investment grade rating from all three major credit rating agencies (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par upgraded Russia's rating in 2003, and Fitch and Standard & Poors followed suit\par in 2004), which supported international investment in Russia and helped\par underpin a sustained appreciation of the rouble through the first three\par quarters of 2008.\par \par The large inflow of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2006 and 2007 caused a persistent\par excess of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  supply over demand. To prevent excessive rouble\par appreciation, the Bank of Russia purchased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in large amounts.\par Total official reserves increased by U.S.$121.49 billion in 2006 and\par U.S.$175 billion in 2007. The Bank of Russia was supported in this effort by\par the Stabilisation Fund, which absorbed a substantial amount of Russia's export\par revenues and thereby allowed the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sterilise a significant\par volume of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during this period.\par \par During the first half of 2008, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows continued due to high\par energy prices, and the Bank of Russia continued to undertake sterilisation\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow appreciation of the rouble. As a result, official reserves\par increased by approximately U.S.$90.2 billion in the first half of 2008. During\par the same period, the Bank of Russia tightened its monetary policy in order to\par control inflation. Between February and July 2008, the refinancing rate was\par raised in four separate increments from 10% to 11% per annum.\par \par Beginning in the third quarter of 2008, the global financial crisis had a\par substantial negative effect on the Russian financial and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. The\par prices of Russia's key exports, including oil, declined, and investors began to\par withdraw funds from rouble-denominated assets in line with an acceleration of\par capital outflows from emerging markets in general. Consequently, foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows decreased considerably in the second half of 2008, and demand\par for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outpaced supply. The amount of hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  generated\par through direct repo deals in 2008, most of which was provided in the fourth\par quarter of the year, totalled 21.51 trillion roubles, nearly triple the amount\par disbursed in 2007. In the fourth quarter of 2008, the Bank of Russia also\par adopted measures to stabilise the banking sector. To address the shortage of\par rouble liquidity and prevent corporate bankruptcies, the Bank of Russia\par initiated a plan whereby banks, depending on their credit rating, were entitled\par to receive unsecured loans worth up to 150% of their equity capital. By the end\par of 2008, more than 140 banks qualified for this programme, and, by February\par 2009, debt on unsecured loans rose to 1.92 trillion roubles. See\par "-Banking-Anti-Crisis Measures" for a more complete discussion of the policies\par undertaken to support the banking sector. As a consequence of its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's total official reserves decreased by\par approximately U.S.$142.7 billion in the second half of 2008. During the latter\par part of 2008, the Bank of Russia raised its refinancing rate on three occasions\par (from 10.75% to 11% on 14 July, 12% on 12 November and 13% on 1 December). Due,\par in part, to a relatively tight monetary policy as well as to substantial\par capital outflows, the overall growth of M2 in 2008 slowed considerably,\par increasing by 0.8% compared to the preceding year.\par \par The global financial crisis continued to negatively impact the Russian economy\par in 2009. In early 2009, in an effort to constrain inflationary pressures, the\par Bank of Russia raised rates on its open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; however, it left its\par refinancing rate unchanged. Concurrently, the demand for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par continued to outpace supply (net sales of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to households and\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits increased substantially in January) and private\par capital outflows continued to rise, both of which, in turn, contributed to an\par outflow of international reserves and a contraction in M2 in January. The\par reduction in M2 exacerbated the shortage of credit in the domestic banking\par sector and caused short-term rates on interbank loans to rise, which prompted\par the Bank of Russia to use its refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to provide more liquidity\par to credit institutions. In January, the Bank of Russia provided an average of\par approximately 820 billion roubles in credit per day to the banking sector.\par \par Beginning in February 2009, the Russian markets exhibited certain signs of\par recovery. By the second quarter, the Government's anti-crisis measures began to\par take effect, inflationary pressures partially subsided, the exchange rate began\par to stabilise and the price of oil experienced a slight recovery. As conditions\par on the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market improved, the banking sector's demand\par for additional liquidity began to fall (the average daily volume of liquidity\par injection \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  carried out by the Bank of Russia declined through the\par remainder of 2009 from its January peak and the volume of unsecured Bank of\par Russia loans to banks began to contract from its peak in February).\par \par As a result of the relatively more favourable economic environment, the Bank of\par Russia was able to ease the tight monetary policy it had kept in place since\par the second half of 2008. In particular, the Bank of Russia implemented a series\par of interest rate reductions, which were, in part, designed to reduce the high\par cost of credit and alleviate the liquidity shortage. During the last three\par quarters of 2009, the Bank of Russia lowered its refinancing rate from 13% to\par 8.75% and reduced its minimum rates on direct one-day REPO transactions from\par 10% to 6%, causing rates on short-term interbank credit to fall as well. The\par interest rate reductions were limited in their ability to expand the credit\par base, however, by high borrower risk estimates (in part due to growth in\par overdue loans to non-financial organisations and households), general\par uncertainty over the economy and the need for banks to restructure their\par balance sheets and thus keep assets as liquid as possible. In all of 2009, M2\par increased by 17.7% despite its initial contraction in January. The increase was\par largely due to the Bank of Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market and the expansion of net credit to the federal Government, which was\par prompted, in turn, by the decision to use proceeds from the Reserve Fund to\par cover the budget deficit. The Bank of Russia's total official reserves\par increased overall in 2009 by U.S.$13.2 billion, supported by the strengthening\par of the rouble and the resumption of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows in the final three\par quarters of the year.\par \par In the first half of 2010, the Bank of Russia continued to ease its monetary\par policy in an effort to stimulate credit activity and internal demand. In\par particular, it reduced its refinancing rate four times. From 1 June 2010\par through the end of the year, the refinancing rate equalled 7.75%. The Bank of\par Russia also lowered the upper and lower limits of its interest rate band during\par the first half of 2010. It lowered its overnight credit rate, the upper limit,\par on four occasions, from 8.75% to 7.75%, and its "tom-next" deposit rate and\par standard overnight deposit rate, the lower limit, on three occasions, from 3.5%\par to 2.5%. Cuts in the Bank of Russia's interest rate band, in turn, led to a\par decline in interbank rates. During the second half of 2010, the Bank of Russia\par maintained its accommodative monetary policy. It left its key interest rates\par unchanged, except that it increased the stand-by deposit rate from 2.5% to\par 2.75% effective 27 December 2010 in order to reduce interest rate volatility\par and absorb surplus liquidity. M2 increased by 31.1% in 2010. The increase in M2\par was the result of several factors: The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the partial financing of the federal budget from the\par Reserve Fund and, particularly in the second half of 2010, the expansion of\par bank lending to the real economy. Total official reserves increased by\par U.S.$39.9 billion in 2010.\par \par To manage inflation risks, the Bank of Russia gradually tightened its monetary\par policy in the first half of 2011. In particular, the refinancing rate was\par increased to 8% in the first quarter of 2011 and then again to 8.25% in the\par second quarter of the year. At the same time, the overnight credit rate was\par increased twice (to 8% in February 2011 and then again to 8.25% in May), and\par the overnight deposit rate was increased four times (to 2.75% at the beginning\par of 2011, and then to 3% in February, 3.25% in the beginning of May and 3.5% at\par the end of May). During the summer months, the Bank of Russia kept its key\par monetary policy parameters unchanged due, in part, to slower inflation and\par continued uncertainty about the economic recovery. In September 2011, due in\par part to a contraction in banking sector liquidity, the Bank of Russia increased\par interest rates on certain of its liquidity absorption \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and lowered\par interest rates on liquidity provision \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , thereby narrowing its\par interest rate band. For example, the overnight deposit rate was increased to\par 3.75%, while rates on Lombard loans were lowered from 6.75% to 6.5%. In 2011,\par M2 increased by 22.6%. This slowdown in the rate of growth (compared to 31.1%\par in 2010) was reflected in a decrease in the rate of growth of household rouble\par deposits (22.4% in 2011, compared to 43.6% in 2010). Nevertheless, the growth\par in M2 was mainly due to an expansion of bank lending to the real economy, as in\par 2010. In December 2011, the Bank of Russia narrowed the interest band further,\par lowering the overnight credit rate to 8% and the rates on Lombard loans to\par 6.25%, while increasing the standard overnight deposit rate to 4%. In late\par December 2011, the refinancing rate was lowered to its current rate of 8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 9% and 11.9%, respectively, consumer price inflation remained relatively\par high in 2006 and 2007. Producer price inflation also remained high during this\par period, amounting to 10.4% in 2006 and 25.1% in 2007. The gradual adjustment of\par prices for communal services, electricity and transport continued to be one of\par the leading drivers of overall inflation during this period. High oil prices\par also continued to exert additional upward pressure on prices.\par \par Despite a deceleration in the growth of M2 in 2008, consumer price inflation\par during this period increased to 13.3%, its highest annual level since 2002.\par This increase was primarily attributable to the global financial crisis. As the\par global crisis began to impact the Russian economy in the third quarter of 2008,\par confidence in the banking sector deteriorated. This, in turn, caused domestic\par savings to decrease and consumption to increase, thereby fuelling inflationary\par pressures. At the same time, the depreciation of the rouble, and, in\par particular, the November 2008 devaluation (see "-Exchange Rates" below), caused\par further upward pressure on consumer price levels, especially given the strong\par dependence of the country's consumer market on imports of finished products and\par raw materials. During 2008, industrial prices exhibited 7% deflation primarily\par because of a contraction in prices in the mining and quarrying and coke and\par refined petroleum manufacturing sectors.\par \par In 2009, the increase in M2 was accompanied by a decline in consumer price\par inflation to 8.8%. The inflation rate decreased in 2009 primarily because of\par the Bank of Russia's tight monetary policy in the beginning of the year, the\par overall slowdown in economic growth, including lower consumer income and\par depressed demand, and the rouble appreciation that began at the end of the\par first quarter. The decline in consumer price inflation occurred despite the\par gradual easing of interest rates in the second, third and fourth quarters and\par the devaluation of the rouble in the beginning of the year. During 2009,\par industrial prices rose by 13.9% mainly due a surge in prices in the mining and\par quarrying and coke and refined petroleum manufacturing sectors, as well as in\par electricity, gas and water production and supply.\par \par Consumer price inflation in 2010 remained at 8.8%, the same level as in 2009.\par Consumer prices decelerated to 6.2% in the first nine months of 2010 compared\par to 8.1% in the first nine months of 2009. However, consumer prices increased\par considerably from August 2010 on the back of accelerating food prices, which\par were due, in part, to the impact of adverse weather conditions on the\par agricultural sector in the second half of 2010. Overall, food prices rose by\par 12.9% in 2010 compared to 6.1% in 2009, while the growth in prices for non-food\par goods declined from 9.7% in 2009 to 5% in 2010 and the growth in prices for\par paid services to households, which include housing, transport and utility\par prices, decelerated from 11.6% in 2009 to 8.1% in 2010. Producer price\par inflation rose by 16.7% in 2010, compared to 13.9% in 2009. The acceleration in\par producer price growth was due primarily to a rise in the growth of prices in\par manufacturing industries (from 5.9% in 2009 to 16.9% in 2010), and, in\par particular, to significant price growth in the coke industry (from 7.3% in 2009\par to 54.9% in 2010), metals (from 4.9% in 2009 to 24.1% in 2010) and chemicals\par (from deflation of 0.5% in 2009 to growth of 30.6% in 2010). Producer price\par growth in 2010 was also supported by the rise in producer prices of foodstuffs\par (from 7.5% in 2009 to 14.3% in 2010), textiles (7.7% in 2009 to 13.2% in 2010)\par and building materials (from 0.1% in 2009 to 9.1% in 2010). The acceleration of\par producer price inflation in 2010 compared to 2009 was partially offset by\par declines in both the growth of metals prices (from 61% in 2009 to 16.1% in\par 2010) and the growth of prices associated with the production and distribution\par of electricity, gas and water (from 18.3% in 2009 to 13.8% in 2010).\par \par In 2011, both consumer price inflation and producer price inflation decreased,\par accompanied by a deceleration in the rate of growth in the M2 monetary\par aggregate. Consumer price inflation equaled 6.1% in 2011. Overall, food prices\par rose by 3.9% compared to 12.9% in 2010, while the decrease in consumer price\par inflation was due, primarily, to a 24.7% reduction in fruit and vegetable\par prices compared to 2010. Prices for other food goods, excluding fruit and\par vegetables, rose by 7.4% in 2011 (compared to 9.4% in 2010). Growth in prices\par for non-food goods and for paid services to households accelerated from 5.0% in\par 2010 to 6.7% in 2011 and from 81% in 2010 to 8.7% in 2011, respectively.\par Producer price inflation equaled 12.0% in 2011, compared to 16.7% in 2010. This\par decrease in the inflation rate was primarily due to a deceleration in the\par growth of prices in manufacturing industries (from 8.6% in 2010 to 8.3% in\par 2011) and a deceleration in the growth of prices associated with the production\par and distribution of electricity, gas and water (from 13.8% in 2010 to 5.1% in\par 2011). The decrease in producer prices of foodstuffs (from 14.3% in 2010 to\par 12.5% in 2011) also supported the deceleration of producer price inflation in\par 2011.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table illustrates the movement in consumer and industrial producer prices for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Inflation: Consumer and Producer Prices                    \par \par                                             Consumer    Industrial            \par                                               Price      Producer             \par                                             Inflation     Price               \par                                                        Inflation(1)           \par                                                                               \par                                            (% change,                         \par                                              end of                           \par                                              period)                          \par                                                                               \par 2006                                               9.0         10.4           \par                                                                               \par 2007                                              11.9         25.1           \par                                                                               \par 2008                                              13.3        (7.0)           \par                                                                               \par 2009                                               8.8         13.9           \par                                                                               \par 2010                                               8.8         16.7           \par                                                                               \par 2011                                               6.1         12.0           \par \par \par Note:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) The CPI basket is weighted as follows: 37%-food; 37%-non-food goods; and 26%-services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instruments of Monetary Policy and Interest Rates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The primary instruments of monetary policy employed by the Bank of Russia are open-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (including the purchase and sale of government securities in the secondary market), bank reserve requirements and standing facilities. The Bank of Russia has no single key policy rate, but rather an interest rate target range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As the exchange rate becomes more flexible, the Bank of Russia expects to have\par greater control over market interest rates. The Bank of Russia influences\par short-term interest rates through its interest rate band, the upper limit being\par the overnight credit rate, i.e., the refinancing rate, and the lower limit the\par standby deposit rate. In 2011-2012, the key interest rates of the Bank of\par Russia have been, and are expected to continue to be, driven by liquidity\par movements within the banking sector. As a result of the banking sector\par returning to its status as a net debtor of the Bank of Russia in September\par 2011, the key short-term money market interest rate has been the minimum\par auction rate for refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par From 2005 through 2007, the refinancing rate exhibited a downward trend, moving\par in graduated intervals from 13% in January 2005 to 10% in June 2007. During the\par same period, the stand-by deposit rate increased in graduated intervals from\par 0.5% in March 2006 to 2.75% in August 2007. The Bank of Russia repo rate did\par not exhibit sharp changes during this period, adjusting between 6% and 7.17%\par from 2005 through 2007. The interbank market overnight rate adjusted regularly\par between 2005 and 2007, without exceeding the refinancing rate and only briefly\par and slightly falling below the stand-by deposit rate on several occasions in\par 2006 and 2007. In the first half of 2008, both the refinancing rate and the\par stand-by deposit rate increased on several occasions. During the same period,\par the Bank of Russia repo rate continued to adjust within the 6-6.8% band and the\par overnight interbank rate generally remained within the corridor set by the\par refinancing rate and stand-by deposit rate, except for brief periods when it\par dipped slightly below the deposit rate floor. Adjustments in the refinancing\par rate and the rates on deposit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were active tools in the Bank of\par Russia's response to the global financial crisis. The refinancing rate was\par increased three times in the second half of 2008 (to 11% on 14 July, 12% on\par 12 November and 13% on 1 December). In April 2009, the Bank of Russia began to\par lower the refinancing rate, which reached its pre-crisis level of 11% in July\par and was further reduced to 8.75% in late December. During the global financial\par crisis and through 2009, the Bank of Russia repo rate adjusted in line with the\par movements in the refinancing rate and stand-by deposit rate. However, during\par the peak of the crisis at the end of 2008 and beginning of 2009, the interbank\par overnight rate exceeded the ceiling set by the refinancing rate, at times quite\par sharply. For example, in January 2009, the interbank overnight rate exceeded\par 27%. Similarly, rates on Bank of Russia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for regulating liquidity\par were raised in the second half of 2008 and first quarter of 2009 and were\par subsequently lowered after inflationary pressures had eased. Each of the\par refinancing, stand-by deposit, repo and interbank overnight rates exhibited a\par downward trend during the first half of 2010, before stabilising during the\par second half of the year. From 1 June 2010 to 27 February 2011, the refinancing\par rate equalled 7.75%. The stand-by deposit rate equalled 2.5% from 1 June 2010\par to 26 December 2010 and 2.75% from 27 December 2010 to 27 February 2011. In\par 2010, the repo rate moved in line with the refinancing rate, and the interbank\par overnight rate generally remained below the repo rate and above the stand-by\par deposit rate, except for a brief period in the beginning of January 2010, when\par it briefly fell below the deposit rate. In 2011, the refinancing rate was\par increased to 8% in the first quarter and then to 8.25% in the second quarter,\par before being reduced to its current rate of 8% in late December 2011. See\par "-Money Supply" for further discussion of interest rate movements in 2011.\par \par The Bank of Russia refinances credit organisations for periods ranging from one\par day to six months through auctions (direct repo transactions, Lombard auctions\par and, during the recent economic crisis, unsecured credits) and standing\par facilities (overnight loans, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, direct repo transactions, Lombard\par loans with fixed terms and collateralised non-market instruments). The Bank of\par Russia regulates banking liquidity through deposit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (via both\par standing facilities and auctions), reserve requirements and the purchase and\par sale of Bank of Russia bonds and in the event of a liquidity deficit in the\par banking sector, through refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Though less frequently used as\par a tool to regulate liquidity, the Bank of Russia also purchases and sells\par Russian Government bonds without the obligation to repurchase or resell. In\par April 2010, the Bank of Russia decided to no longer carry out six- and 12-month\par REPO auctions and auctions of 12-month Lombard loans as well as to no longer\par extend collateralised credit facilities for periods of 181 to 365 calendar days\par to credit organisations. In September 2010, the Bank of Russia decided that\par from 1 October 2010 auctions of 6-month Lombard loans would no longer be held.\par In August 2011, the Bank of Russia approved a new standing facility: the\par provision of loans secured by gold. The initial interest rate for facilities up\par to 90 days was set at 7% (subsequently lowered to 6.75%), and the initial\par interest rate for facilities between 91 and 180 days was set at 7.25%. In\par November 2011, the Bank of Russia discontinued its policy of extending 91 to\par 180-day credits secured by non-market assets as this instrument was more often\par used as an anti-crisis measure.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exchange Rates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with Russia's unified monetary policy, the Bank of Russia has adopted a managed floating exchange rate regime, which is designed to limit excessive rouble volatility without imposing quantitative targets or fixed limits on the exchange rate. Currently, the rouble is moving in line with other major commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the other BRIC countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information with respect to the rouble/dollar and rouble/euro exchange rates for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par               Official                                                      \par               Exchange                                                      \par                Rates                                                        \par                                                                             \par               Rouble/                      Rouble/Euro                      \par                Dollar                                                       \par                                                                             \par               Average    End of    Average   End of                         \par                 (1)     Period(2)    (1)    Period(2)                       \par                                                                             \par 2006             27.18       26.33   34.11       34.70                      \par                                                                             \par 2007             25.57       24.55   35.01       35.93                      \par                                                                             \par 2008             24.98       29.38   36.41       41.44                      \par                                                                             \par 2009             31.93       30.24   44.13       43.39                      \par                                                                             \par 2010             30.34       30.48   40.17       40.33                      \par                                                                             \par 2011             29.38       32.20   41.03       41.41                      \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) The average rates are calculated as the average of the exchange rates on the last business day of each month for the period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) The period end rates are quoted for the last business day of the relevant period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Source: Bank of Russia.\par \par In an effort to neutralise sharp exchange rate fluctuations in the domestic\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the Bank of Russia adopted a managed floating exchange\par rate regime in September 1998, which it continues to follow. Until 2005, the\par Bank of Russia focused primarily on maintaining a stable dollar/rouble exchange\par rate. Beginning 1 February 2005, the Bank of Russia adopted a bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par basket, consisting of the dollar and euro, as an operating benchmark and began\par using movements in the rouble value of the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as a reference\par point for its exchange rate policy. The basket initially consisted of \u8364\'20\'AC0.1 and\par U.S.$0.9. Over time, the weighting of the euro has gradually increased, and the\par basket is currently set at \u8364\'20\'AC0.45 and U.S.$0.55. In February 2009, the Bank of\par Russia introduced a floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band of permissible movements in the\par rouble against the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket. The borders of this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band are\par designed to adjust automatically depending on the volume of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band effectively replaced the Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fixed band and has become the Bank of Russia's principal means to\par manage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations. The fixed band since its inception\par underwent a gradual widening and, with a corridor of 26 roubles and 41 roubles,\par was at its widest in January 2009 and officially cancelled in October 2010. The\par dollar retains its leading position and continues to be the preferred \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par for exports and domestic savings, although the euro has become an increasingly\par common savings instrument among Russian residents, particularly in 2006 and\par 2007 when the dollar weakened against both the rouble and the euro. Since 2005,\par exchange rate flexibility has increased substantially, and, starting from 2009,\par the scale of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic exchange market has\par begun to decrease.\par \par In 2006 and 2007, Russia experienced large inflows of oil-related foreign\par capital. Growing international confidence in the Russian economy and the\par profitability of the oil sector supported the corporate sector's intensive\par borrowing and accumulation of large liabilities denominated in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as well as real appreciation of the rouble. In 2006, the rouble\par appreciated against the dollar by 10.7% in real terms and against the euro by\par 11.5% in real terms. The real effective exchange rate, i.e., the trade-weighted\par exchange rate, of the rouble appreciated by 9.4% in 2006. In 2006, net foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases on an average monthly basis totalled approximately\par U.S.$9.8 billion. The rouble continued to appreciate in real terms in 2007. In\par 2007, it appreciated by 12.8% against the dollar and 4.2% against the euro, and\par the real effective exchange rate of the rouble appreciated by 4.2%. Net foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases on an average monthly basis totalled approximately\par U.S.$11.9 billion in 2007, which included U.S.$2 billion of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par sales in August 2007.\par \par During the first half of 2008, the rouble continued to rise as world oil and\par gas prices remained high and the country continued to experience net inflows of\par private capital. In order to manage the rouble appreciation, the Bank of Russia\par purchased foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as it historically has done when confronted with\par high oil and gas prices, and adjusted the corridor in which the rouble was\par allowed to move against the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket. In the first half of 2008, the\par rouble appreciated against the dollar by 19% in real terms and against the euro\par by 4.5% in real terms. The real effective exchange rate of the rouble\par appreciated by 4.5% in the first half of 2008. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on an\par average monthly basis totalled approximately U.S.$12.1 billion during the first\par six months of 2008. The bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as at 30 June 2008 equalled\par 29.51 roubles.\par \par In the second half of 2008, the global financial crisis, and, in particular,\par the drop in world oil prices and the deteriorating commodities and financial\par markets, prompted a sell-off of rouble-denominated assets. As a result, in the\par second half of 2008, the rouble depreciated in real terms by 13.4% against the\par dollar and 6.4% against the euro. The real effective exchange rate of the\par rouble depreciated by 5.1% in the second half of 2008. Concurrently, the high\par level of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  indebtedness of both the financial and non-financial\par sectors exacerbated the impact of the global crisis on the rouble. A large\par share of this debt came due in the fourth quarter of 2008, when equity\par markets-and thus the value of collateral offered by corporate debtors-were\par deteriorating. A large number of creditors began to demand additional\par collateral or early repayment, which had the effect of significantly increasing\par the demand for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In order to meet this increased demand as well\par as manage the downward pressure on the rouble, the Bank of Russia sold foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic market during the second half of 2008, resulting in\par net sales of dollars and euros of U.S.$69.2 billion in 2008, compared to net\par purchases of U.S.$142.3 billion in 2007. In July and August 2008, the Bank of\par Russia sold U.S.$26.5 billion and U.S.$0.9 billion, respectively, of roubles on\par the domestic market. In September, October, November and December 2008, the\par Bank of Russia sold U.S.$17.9 billion, U.S.$43.1 billion, U.S.$33.8 billion and\par U.S.$74.2 billion, respectively, of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic market.\par Net foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales on an average monthly basis were approximately\par U.S.$5.8 billion in 2008. In November 2008, the Bank of Russia announced it\par would gradually widen the corridor in which the rouble would be allowed to\par trade against the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket. In all of 2008, the rouble depreciated in\par nominal terms by 19.7% against the dollar and by 15.3% against the euro.\par Nevertheless, in 2008, the rouble appreciated in real terms against both the\par dollar (by 13.4%) and the euro (by 6.4%). The bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket equalled 34.81\par roubles as at 31 December 2008.\par \par The exchange rate policy in 2009 was designed primarily to mitigate the impact\par of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy. In the first days of\par January 2009, the Bank of Russia accelerated its controlled devaluation of the\par rouble. Expectations that the rouble would continue to depreciate put\par additional downward pressure on the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , prompting further sales\par of rouble assets. At the same time, demand for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic\par money markets continued to outpace supply (net sales of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par Bank of Russia reached U.S.$39.6 billion in January 2009). As a consequence,\par interest rates on rouble assets increased sharply, causing the rouble interbank\par loan market to tighten considerably. In mid-January, in an effort to slow\par depreciation of the rouble, the Bank of Russia limited its disbursement of\par unsecured loans. This measure had the effect of increasing the demand for\par roubles, thereby strengthening the rouble against the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket, as\par liquidity tightened and tax payments came due. In a further effort to stabilise\par the rouble, the Bank of Russia on 23 January 2009 announced that it had\par completed its gradual adjustment of the exchange rate, setting the upper and\par lower limits of the exchange rate corridor at 41 roubles and 26 roubles,\par respectively, against the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket. In January 2009, the Bank of\par Russia sold U.S.$39.6 billion of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market in an effort to defend the rouble. Beginning in February 2009, the Bank\par of Russia began to use the floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band discussed above. At its\par inception, the width of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band was set at two roubles.\par \par From February through June 2009, Russia experienced a rise in foreign capital\par inflows. This positive development was caused by the relative stabilisation of\par the global financial markets (due to large-scale anti-crisis measures adopted\par in developed and emerging economies), the increase in the price of oil (Urals\par oil reached U.S.$60 a barrel by June) and the prevalence of high domestic\par interest rates relative to foreign rates. Despite these developments in the\par market, during the first half of 2009, the rouble depreciated against the\par dollar by 17.6% in real terms and 5.7% in nominal terms and against the euro by\par 7% in real terms and 5.7% in nominal terms. From February through June 2009,\par the Bank of Russia was a net purchaser of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with the volume of\par purchases of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic market reaching\par U.S.$34.2 billion. After a two-month period of exchange rate stability, during\par which the Bank of Russia sold U.S.$5.3 billion in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , capital\par inflows resumed in September 2009 mainly because of a further rise in the price\par of oil and the continued disparity between domestic and foreign interest rates.\par These factors caused the rouble to strengthen in the fourth quarter of 2009.\par From September through December 2009, the Bank of Russia was a net purchaser of\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , selling U.S.$27.9 billion of roubles on the domestic market.\par In 2009, net foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases on an average monthly basis totalled\par approximately U.S.$1.4 billion. In 2009, the rouble depreciated in nominal\par terms by 2.9% against the dollar and 4.7% against the euro. In real terms, the\par rouble depreciated by 12.2% against the dollar and 8.3% against the euro in\par 2009. The real effective exchange rate of the rouble depreciated by 5.6% in\par 2009. During 2009, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band adjusted more than twenty times,\par although it remained a two-rouble wide trading band until July 2009. During the\par first months of 2009, the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket generally traded in the upper part\par of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band; however, from March 2009 through June 2009, the rouble\par appreciated, and the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket moved to the lower boundary of the\par band. In July 2009, the Bank of Russia widened the trading band to three\par roubles, and, during the second half of 2009, the value of the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par basket fluctuated within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band. The bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as at\par 31 December 2009 equalled 36.16 roubles.\par \par In the first quarter of 2010, Russia experienced net private sector capital\par outflows of U.S.$15 billion, compared to inflows of U.S.$8.8 billion in the\par fourth quarter of 2009, which put downward pressure on the rouble. Net private\par sector capital outflows were, in part, caused by concerns regarding the\par stability of the European debt markets and by the reduction in domestic\par interest rates. See "-Money Supply," above, for a discussion of the interest\par rate cuts in the first half of 2010. Lower domestic interest rates, in turn,\par led to the narrowing of the interest rate differential between relatively\par higher domestic rates and lower foreign rates. At the same time, the prices for\par many of Russia's dollar-denominated exports rose, generating upward pressure on\par the rouble. For example, in the first quarter of 2010 relative to the first\par quarter of 2009, the price of Urals oil increased by 73.8%, world coal prices\par grew by 32.3% and aluminum and nickel prices increased by 58.5% and 89.2%,\par respectively. During the first three months of 2010, in nominal terms, the\par rouble depreciated by 1.5% against the dollar and appreciated by 5.2% against\par the euro. In real terms, the rouble appreciated by 2.3% against the dollar and\par by 7.7% against the euro. The real effective exchange rate of the rouble\par appreciated by 4% in the first quarter of 2010. The bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as at\par 31 March 2010 equalled 34.02 roubles.\par \par In the second quarter of 2010, Russia experienced net private sector capital\par inflows of U.S.$3.5 billion, compared to outflows of U.S.$15 billion in the\par first quarter of 2010, putting upward pressure on the rouble. Notwithstanding\par these inflows, domestic interest rates continued to fall. See "-Money Supply,"\par above. During the same period, the prices for certain Russian exports, such as\par natural gas, contracted, whereas the prices for other key exports, such as\par Urals oil and nickel, increased. For example, the price of Urals oil fluctuated\par during the second quarter of 2010, averaging U.S.$76.60 per barrel, a 2%\par increase relative to the first quarter of 2010. During the second quarter of\par 2010, in nominal terms, the rouble depreciated by 1.1% against the dollar and\par appreciated by 7.3% against the euro. In real terms, the rouble depreciated by\par 0.1% against the dollar and appreciated by 8% against the euro. The real\par effective exchange rate of the rouble appreciated by 4.4% in the second quarter\par of 2010. The bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as at 30 June 2010 equalled 34.34 roubles. In\par both the first and second quarters of 2010, changes in the rouble/dollar real\par exchange rate were mainly due to the differential between domestic and U.S.\par inflation, whereas the real appreciation of the rouble against the euro was\par primarily driven by the strengthening of the nominal rouble/euro rate.\par \par In the third quarter of 2010, Russia experienced estimated net private sector\par capital outflows of U.S.$2.8 billion, compared to inflows of U.S.$3.5 billion\par in the second quarter of 2010, putting downward pressure on the rouble. Though\par the Bank of Russia left its interest rates unchanged during the third quarter\par (see "-Money Supply"), key financial market interest rates, including rates on\par interbank loans and long-term loans to households, continued to decline. During\par the third quarter of 2010, in nominal terms, the rouble depreciated by 1.3%\par against the dollar and by 2.3% against the euro, in part due to stronger demand\par for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  driven by corporate foreign debt payments. In real terms,\par the rouble appreciated by 0.1% against the dollar and depreciated by 0.9%\par against the euro. The real effective exchange rate of the rouble depreciated by\par 1.3% in the third quarter of 2010. Both Russian inflation and changes in\par nominal exchange rates played significant roles in the real rouble/dollar and\par rouble/euro exchange rate dynamics in the third quarter of 2010. The\par bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as of 1 October 2010 equalled 35.43 roubles.\par \par In the fourth quarter of 2010, Russia experienced net private sector capital\par outflows of U.S.$19.3 billion, which, together with foreign debt payments by\par Russian companies, put downward pressure on the rouble. The rise in energy\par prices during the fourth quarter, however, partially offset the downward\par pressure on the rouble. During the fourth quarter of 2010, the rouble\par depreciated by 0.3% against the dollar and 5.5% against the euro in nominal\par terms. In real terms, the rouble appreciated by 1.5% against the dollar and\par depreciated by 3.9% against the euro. The real effective rate of the rouble\par depreciated by 3.1%. The differential between Russian and U.S. inflation was\par the primary driver behind the appreciation of the real rouble/dollar exchange\par rate in the fourth quarter of 2010, whereas it was the rouble's nominal\par depreciation against the euro which was the main contributor to the\par depreciation of the real rouble/euro exchange rate.\par \par Russia's overall net private sector capital outflows in 2010 totalled\par U.S.$33.6 billion. In 2010, the rouble depreciated by 0.8% against the dollar\par and appreciated by 7.1% against the euro in nominal terms. In real terms, the\par rouble appreciated by 4% against the dollar and by 14.5% against the euro. The\par real effective exchange rate of the rouble appreciated by 6.9% in 2010.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2010, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  carried out targeted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to limit rouble volatility and to correct imbalances in the supply of and demand for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Net foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases on an average monthly basis totalled approximately U.S.$2.8 billion in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During 2010, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band continued to adjust regularly. During the\par first eight months of 2010, the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket traded in the lower part of\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band, before moving to the upper part of the band in September\par 2010. In October 2010, in line with its implementation of a more flexible\par exchange rate policy, the Bank of Russia expanded the three-rouble wide band by\par 50 kopeks in both directions. As of 1 January 2011, the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket was\par valued at 34.91 roubles, which was in the central part of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band.\par \par Russia's overall net private sector capital outflows in 2011 totalled U.S.$84.2\par billion. In 2011, the rouble appreciated by 3.4% against the dollar and\par depreciated by 1.5% against the euro in nominal terms. In real terms, the\par rouble appreciated by 8.8% against the dollar and by 4.1% against the euro. The\par real effective exchange rate of the rouble appreciated by 4.7% in 2011. During\par the first ten months of 2011, the value of the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket decreased, to\par a great extent due to movements in the dollar rouble exchange rate. From\par November 2011, the value of the bi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket stabilised and reached 36.46\par roubles as of 1 January 2012. In recent months, the scale of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the domestic exchange market has decreased.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary Policy for 2012 through 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In October 2011, the Bank of Russia published its monetary policy guidelines for 2012 through 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bank of Russia's principal monetary policy objective for 2012 through 2014\par is to complete the transition to an inflation-targeting regime and to continue\par its policy of reducing inflation. At the same time, according to its monetary\par policy guidelines, the Bank of Russia intends to continue implementing its\par managed floating exchange rate regime, eliminating exchange rate targets and\par limiting its direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , mainly on the dollar/rouble market,\par to those circumstances where it is necessary to soften excessive rouble\par volatility. The Bank of Russia intends to continue using its floating\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band, which, it believes, will allow greater exchange rate\par flexibility, thereby improving control over market interest rates, while\par limiting exchange rate volatility.\par \par The Bank of Russia also plans to increase the transparency of its monetary\par policy by conducting regular interviews and press conferences and issuing press\par releases and other reports (often in English) informing the public of its most\par important policy decisions and their consequences.\par \par As part of its monetary policy guidelines for 2012 through 2014, the Bank of\par Russia has adopted three scenarios, each based on a different oil price\par assumption. The following discussion is based on what the Bank of Russia has\par deemed the "second" scenario, which assumes an oil price higher than in the\par first scenario, but lower than in the third scenario. For 2012, the average\par price per barrel of Urals is assumed to be U.S.$100. Based on this assumption,\par the Bank of Russia expects current account inflows of U.S.$36.5 billion,\par capital and financial account (excluding reserve assets) outflows of\par U.S.$10.5 billion, a decrease in reserve assets of U.S.$26 billion and a\par year-on-year increase in M2 of 16-18%. In 2012, the Bank of Russia forecasts\par year-on-year GDP growth of 3.7% and is targeting consumer price inflation at\par 5-6%. For 2013, the average price per barrel of Urals is assumed to be U.S.$97.\par Based on this assumption, the Bank of Russia expects current account inflows of\par U.S.$5.9 billion, capital and financial account (excluding reserve assets)\par inflows of U.S.$18 billion, a decrease in reserve assets of U.S.$23.8 billion\par and a year-on-year increase in M2 of 16-19%. In 2013, the Bank of Russia\par forecasts year-on-year GDP growth of 3.5-4.8% and is targeting consumer price\par inflation at 4.5-5.5%. For 2014, the average price per barrel of Urals is\par assumed to be U.S.$101. Based on this assumption, the Bank of Russia expects\par current account outflows of U.S.$6 billion, capital and financial account\par (excluding reserve assets) inflows of U.S.$33 billion, a decrease in reserve\par assets of U.S.$26.9 billion and a year-on-year increase in M2 of 17-20%. In\par 2014, the Bank of Russia forecasts year-on-year GDP growth of 3.5-4.8% and is\par targeting consumer price inflation at 4-5%. There can be no assurance that any\par of the Bank of Russia's projections on the future development of the Russian\par economy will prove to be accurate. These projections are based on assumptions\par as to the oil price and other important matters, which may not prove to be\par accurate and may significantly effect the Bank of Russia's projected outcomes.\par See "Forward-Looking Statements."\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Structure of the Banking Industry\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All credit institutions are required to be licensed by the Bank of Russia. A\par general banking licence is the most comprehensive licence available and allows\par a credit institution to engage in various banking \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Under Russian\par law, commercial banks holding general licences are allowed to open branches\par (upon prior Bank of Russia consent) and representative offices in foreign\par countries (with post notification of the Bank of Russia). Banks without a\par general licence require a separate licence to conduct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par The total number of credit institutions licensed to conduct banking \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in Russia decreased from 1,012 at year-end 2010 to 978 at year-end 2011, due\par mainly to a decrease in the number of credit institutions with fewer than\par 150 million roubles in share capital. Of the 978 credit institutions with\par banking licences at year-end 2011, 922 were banks and 56 were non-bank credit\par institutions, 273 held general licences and 661 held \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  licences.\par As of 1 January 2012, credit institutions licensed to conduct banking\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  included 77 wholly foreign-owned organisations and 36 banks with\par majority foreign ownership. Licensed foreign banks are subject to the general\par banking laws and regulations and are entitled to participate in the same range\par of banking activities as their Russian counterparts. As of 1 January 2012,\par there were 2,807 branches of operating credit institutions in Russia, of which\par 155 belonged to 100% foreign-owned banks in Russia and 524 belonged to\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which is the successor of the former state savings bank in the Soviet\par Union and in which the Bank of Russia has greater than a 50% stake.\par \par Between 2008 and 2011, total assets within the Russian banking system expanded\par by approximately 2.1 times. The five largest Russian banks held 42.3% and 49.6%\par of these assets as of 1 January 2008 and 30 November 2011, respectively. As of\par 1 January 2012, the five largest credit institutions in terms of assets\par accounted for approximately 50% of total banking assets. As of 1 January 2012,\par client accounts constituted approximately 62.7% of the liabilities of the\par banking sector, whereas loans, including loans to banks and overdue debt,\par constituted 69% of the sector's assets. As of 1 January 2012, revenues from\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  constituted approximately 65.4% of the revenue\par structure of the country's operating credit institutions.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From 2006 until the fourth quarter of 2008, the banking sector was a net creditor of the Bank of Russia. As of 1 January 2006, 2007 and 2008, net credits of the banking sector to the Bank of Russia equalled 552.1 billion roubles, 810 billion roubles, and 1,124 billion roubles, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The global financial crisis had a severe negative impact on credit\par institutions, resulting in the banking sector becoming a net debtor of the Bank\par of Russia. As of 1 January 2009, net credits of the Bank of Russia to the\par banking sector equalled 2,514.9 billion roubles. At the same time, the crisis\par caused overall banking sector profits to drop by approximately 98% in the first\par half of 2009, compared to the first half of 2008. The quality of loans issued\par by Russian banks also deteriorated as a result of the crisis, with the\par percentage share of total credit under loans classified as problem loans or\par worse equalling 3.8% as of 1 January 2009 and 9.5% as of 1 January 2010,\par compared with 2.5% as of 1 January 2008. Overdue claims of the banking sector\par on loans, deposits and other placements increased from 184.1 billion roubles as\par of 1 January 2008 to 1,043.4 billion roubles as of 1 December 2009. This caused\par credit institutions to make substantial loss provisions, which increased from\par 3.6% of total loans as of 1 January 2008 to 9.1% of total loans as of 1 January\par 2010.\par \par The banking sector experienced a recovery in 2010 and 2011. By the beginning of\par 2010, the banking sector returned to its pre-crisis position as a net creditor\par of the Bank of Russia. As of 1 January 2010, net credits of the banking sector\par to the Bank of Russia equalled 53.2 billion roubles, which increased further to\par 1.64 trillion roubles as of 1 January 2011. As of 1 January 2012, the banking\par sector became a net debtor of the Bank of Russia in the amount of 0.1 trillion\par roubles as a result of an increase in commercial bank borrowings from the Bank\par of Russia. In addition, overall banking sector profits increased from\par 205.1 billion roubles in 2009 to 573.4 billion roubles in 2010 and\par 848.2 billion roubles in 2011. The quality of loans issued by Russian banks\par improved slightly in 2010 and 2011, with the percentage share of total credit\par under loans classified as problem loans or worse equalling 8.2% of total loans\par as of 1 January 2011 and 6.6% as of 1 January 2012, compared to 9.5% as of\par 1 January 2010. Despite this improvement, overdue claims of the banking sector\par on loans, deposits and other placements increased from 1,014.7 billion roubles\par as of 1 January 2010 to 1,035.9 billion roubles as of 1 January 2011 and\par 1,133 billion roubles as of 1 January 2012. Loss provisions of credit\par institutions increased to 8.5% of total loans as of 1 January 2011, although\par they decreased to 6.9% as of 1 January 2012.\par \par In light of the 8% minimum capital requirement set forth under Basel II, the\par Russian banking sector is adequately capitalised. The banking sector's capital\par adequacy ratio was 14.9% as of 1 January 2007. The capital adequacy ratio began\par to rise in the first half of 2007, before falling again in the second half of\par the year. Nevertheless, at 1 January 2008, it equalled 15.5%, slightly higher\par than the ratio at the beginning of the prior year. During the first nine months\par of 2008, the capital adequacy ratio declined, before rising in the last quarter\par of the year. At 1 January 2009, the capital adequacy ratio equalled 16.8%.\par After declining in January 2009, the capital adequacy ratio demonstrated an\par upward trend through the remainder of the year, resulting in a ratio of 20.9%\par as at 1 January 2010. As of 1 January 2011 and 2012, the capital adequancy\par ratio equalled 18.1% and 14.7%, respectively. Total capital in the Russian\par banking sector has increased in recent years: from 1,692.7 billion roubles as\par of 1 January 2007 to 5,242.1 billion roubles as of 1 January 2012. In November\par 2010, at a summit of G20 member-countries Russia committed to implement by\par year-end 2018 the liquidity and capital adequacy requirements set forth under\par Basel III.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table contains certain key indicators of the overall banking sector's financial soundness as of the dates indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                  As of                                        \par                                1 January                                      \par                                                                               \par                                      2007 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012            \par                                                                               \par                                                   (%)                                         \par                                                                               \par Capital Adequacy Risk                                                         \par                                                                               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ratio of own funds (capital)         14.9 15.5 16.8 20.9 18.1 14.7            \par to risk-weighted assets(1)                                                 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2   \par                                                                               \par Credit Risk                                                                   \par                                                                               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Share of problem loans and bad        2.4  2.5  3.8  9.5  8.2  6.6         \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2   \par loans in total loans(2)                                                       \par                                                                               \par Loan loss provisions as a             4.1  3.6  4.5  9.1  8.5  6.9            \par share of total loans                                                          \par                                                                               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ratio of insider loans to own         4.0  3.4  2.2  1.5  1.8  1.4         \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2   \par funds(3)                                                                      \par                                                                               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ratio of claims on insiders to        1.1  1.1  0.9  0.7  0.6  0.7         \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2   \par own funds                                                                     \par                                                                               \par Liquidity Risk                                                                \par                                                                               \par Ratio of liquid assets to            26.8 24.8 25.9 28.0 26.8 23.9            \par total assets(4)                                                               \par                                                                               \par Market Risk(5)                       45.1 38.7 23.2 49.6 48.6 49.7            \par                                                                               \par Of which                                                                      \par                                                                               \par Interest rate risk(6)                19.3 24.3 16.4 37.5 36.7 33.8            \par                                                                               \par Equity position risk(7)              20.4 10.8  3.4  8.7  8.6 12.9            \par                                                                               \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk(8)              5.3  3.6  3.4  3.5  3.2  3.0            \par                                                                               \par Return on Assets(9)                   3.3  3.0  1.8  0.7  1.9  2.4            \par                                                                               \par Return on Equity(10)                 26.3 22.7 13.3  4.9 12.5 17.6            \par \par \par Notes:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Risk-weighted assets are calculated by totalling the credit risks on a\par credit institution's assets. A credit institution's assets are divided into\par five groups on the basis of risk, and risk coefficients are set for each group.\par Since 1 July 2010, the risk coefficients are set in accordance with the\par Simplified Standardised Approach to credit risk within the framework of Basel\par II.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) A problem loan is defined as a loan, 51-100% of which is written off by the creditor. A bad loan is a loan that is 100% written off by the creditor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) Includes loans, bank guarantees and sureties granted by credit institutions to its owners (shareholders).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Liquid assets are those financial assets that credit institutions can receive, claim or sell within 30 calendar days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par (5) Market risk is defined as the risk of financial loss resulting from changes\par in (i) current (fair) value of financial instruments, (ii) exchange rates and\par (iii) the price of precious metals. Financial instruments include (i) equity\par and debt instruments acquired by credit institutions and held for trading or\par otherwise available for sale, (ii) financial instruments denominated in foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or in roubles if their value is tied to movements in exchange rates\par and (iii) derivative instruments, such as interest rate and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par swaps.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (6) The market risk on financial instruments sensitive to changes in interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (7) The market risk on financial instruments sensitive to changes in the current (fair) value of equities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (8) The market risk on positions in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and precious metals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (9) Calculated as the ratio of financial results (before tax) of credit institutions over the reporting period to the average amount of assets of credit institutions over the reporting period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (10) Calculated as the ratio of financial results (before tax) of credit institutions over the reporting period to the average amount of own funds (capital) of credit institutions over the reporting period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Bank of Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Pursuant to the federal deposit insurance law, subject to certain exceptions,\par banks are required to make quarterly contributions of up to 0.15% of their\par deposits (calculated as the average of a respective bank's daily balances\par during the relevant quarter) to a federal deposit insurance fund, which is\par managed and controlled by the Deposit Insurance Agency. At the discretion of\par the Deposit Insurance Agency, this rate may be increased to 0.3% in the event\par the deposit insurance fund requires additional funding. Currently, banks are\par required to make quarterly contributions of 0.1% of their deposits to the\par federal deposit insurance fund. The fund insures the full repayment of deposits\par up to 700,000 roubles per depositor per bank. Until 2007, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's\par contributions to the fund were kept on a separate account. The state guaranteed\par household deposits at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2   until 1 January 2007.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banking Supervision and Regulation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Russian banks are subject to prudential regulations intended to move the\par Russian banking industry toward compliance with the capital adequacy standards\par established by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Basel Committee on Banking Supervision\f28 \uc2 \f2 . These regulations\par impose requirements with respect to minimum capital, capital adequacy, exposure\par to individual borrowers and shareholders, deposit taking and equity investments\par in other companies.\par \par The Bank of Russia operates an early warning system under which it analyses the\par most significant prudential ratios for all banks on a monthly basis. Banks that\par fail to comply with their reporting obligations can be forced to close their\par correspondent accounts with other banks and make all settlements exclusively\par through the Bank of Russia. The ratios reviewed by the Bank of Russia include\par capital adequacy ratios, credit risk ratios, liquidity risk ratios and market\par risk ratios (see table above).\par \par The Bank of Russia imposes a reserve requirement on all banks. The reserve\par ratio is currently 5.5% for liabilities to non-resident legal entities,\par denominated in either roubles or foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and 4% for all other\par financial obligations in any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Credit institutions are required to meet\par their reserve requirements on average during each calendar month or reserve\par maintenance period. This provision provides for flexible liquidity management\par and decreases the day-to-day dependence on interbank funds.\par \par The Law on Insolvency (Bankruptcy) of Credit Institutions provides the\par legislative framework for the restructuring of banks. This law sets out the\par standards to be used by the Bank of Russia in appointing temporary bank\par administrators, sets forth a liquidation procedure for banks and gives the Bank\par of Russia the power to license the receivers of bankrupt banks and to propose\par receivers to arbitration courts for their approval.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anti-Crisis Measures\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In response to the global financial crisis, Russia undertook a series of\par measures designed to protect the stability of the banking sector. These\par measures focused mainly on the following priority areas: bolstering financial\par sector liquidity; supporting the interbank market; facilitating bank loans to\par the real economy; and ensuring the recovery and survival of banks that are\par essential to the stability of the overall sector. Since October 2008, the\par following measures have been implemented:\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 In October 2008, the Federal Assembly passed the Financial System Support\par Law, which authorised the Government to supply up to 910 billion roubles in\par long-term subordinated loans (with a maturity of up to 31 December 2019) to\par state-owned and private banks. Pursuant to this law, the Government provided\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   with 410 billion roubles, using proceeds from the National\par Wealth Fund, and the Bank of Russia provided \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2   with 500 billion roubles.\par Of the 410 billion roubles lent to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , 200 billion roubles were\par disbursed to VTB, 25 billion roubles to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian Agricultural Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the\par remainder to various private banks.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 Pursuant to the Financial System Support Law, through 31 December 2009,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   was given the authority to grant foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans (of up\par to U.S.$50 billion in aggregate) to Russian companies, including financial\par institutions, for the refinancing of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans obtained prior to\par 25 September 2008. Approximately U.S.$11.6 billion were disbursed under this\par programme before it was suspended.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 The Bank of Russia introduced a new facility for providing unsecured loans to certain private banks. Under this facility, from October 2008 through 2010, approximately 6.6 trillion roubles of unsecured loans were disbursed to banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \u8226\'20\'22 From 14 October 2008 until 31 December 2010, the Bank of Russia was given the\par authority to enter into agreements with credit organisations, pursuant to which\par the Bank of Russia was authorised to compensate such credit organisations for\par losses incurred on the interbank market as a result of loans made during the\par above-mentioned time period to credit organisations whose licences had been\par revoked. As of 31 December 2010, the Bank of Russia entered into such\par agreements with 15 banks as well as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which, through 31 December\par 2010, had concluded lending transactions with 346 credit organisations whose\par licences had been revoked. From 14 October 2008 through 31 December 2010,\par 41,081 transactions totalling approximately 9.4 billion roubles were eligible\par for compensation under this programme. During this period, there were only\par three instances when the Bank of Russia was required to compensate banks for\par overdue debt under eligible interbank lending transactions. All proceeds used\par for such compensation were subsequently repaid to the Bank of Russia.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 In October 2008, the Bank of Russia broadened the range of assets that banks\par can use as collateral in refinancing transactions and extended the terms of\par loans secured by non-market assets, such as promissory notes, guarantees and\par other receivables. As of 1 January 2010, indebtedness pursuant to transactions\par secured by this broader range of collateral equalled approximately\par 439.1 billion roubles.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 On 14 October 2008, the Bank of Russia reduced the reserve requirement to\par 0.5% for all types of financial obligations. The reserve requirement has since\par then been gradually increased: to 1% as of 1 May 2009, 1.5% as of 1 June 2009,\par 2% as of 1 July 2009 and 2.5% as of 1 August 2009. The reserve requirement\par currently is 5.5% for liabilities to non-resident legal entities, denominated\par in either roubles or foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and 4% for all other financial\par obligations in any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 On 27 October 2008, a federal law was passed to support the stability of the\par banking system and for the restructuring of credit organisations through\par 31 December 2011. Pursuant to this law, the Bank of Russia, together with the\par Deposit Insurance Agency, is permitted to undertake measures to support the\par financial condition of banks threatened by insolvency. Supporting measures\par include temporary receivership, mandatory capital decreases, the acquisition by\par the Deposit Insurance Agency of controlling interests in defaulting banks and\par the sale to private investors of assets or controlling interests in defaulting\par banks. This law also sets forth the sources of financing for the aforementioned\par measures.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 In November 2008, the Bank of Russia increased the refinancing rate from 11%\par to 13%. Since 24 April 2009, the Bank of Russia has implemented a series of\par reductions in the refinancing rate. On 28 February 2011, the Bank of Russia\par increased the refinancing rate from 7.75% to 8%, which was further increased on\par 3 May 2011 to 8.25%. In December 2011, the refinancing rate was lowered to its\par current rate of 8%.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Insurance coverage provided by the Deposit Insurance Agency for retail deposits has been expanded to 700,000 roubles per depositor per bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \u8226\'20\'22 In 2008, additional funding was provided to the Deposit Insurance Agency\par (200 billion roubles) and the Agency for Home Mortgage Loans was recapitalised\par (60 billion roubles). As part of its Anti-Crisis Programme for 2009, the\par Government allocated an additional 60 billion roubles (20 billion roubles for\par recapitalisation and 40 billion roubles in the form of loans) to the Agency for\par Home Mortgage Loans.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 As of 30 December 2008, the procedure required for foreclosures has been\par simplified. Pursuant to the amended procedures, subject to limited exceptions,\par parties to a pledge agreement may agree on an out-of-court enforcement\par procedure.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 In an effort to bolster the liquidity of the financial markets, the\par Government broadened the types of assets in which balances from the National\par Wealth Fund may be invested. For this purpose, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par transferred 175 billion roubles to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2   in the fourth quarter of\par 2008 for its onward investment in various Russian securities. In December 2009,\par the 175 billion roubles were repaid to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 To encourage consolidation in the banking sector, the procedures for bank mergers have been streamlined.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \u8226\'20\'22 In February 2009, a law requiring all banks to maintain at least 180 million\par roubles of own funds was adopted. However, pursuant to this law, a bank is\par permitted to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with less than 180 million roubles of own\par funds, provided the following conditions are met: (i) the bank had own funds of\par less than 180 million roubles as of 1 January 2007, and its amount of own funds\par did not subsequently decrease in a material fashion; (ii) the bank maintains\par own funds of at least 90 million roubles, beginning 1 January 2010; and (iii)\par  the bank increases its amount of own funds to 180 million roubles by 1 January\par 2012. Under a new law adopted in December 2011, the minimum amount of own funds\par that a bank is required to maintain has been increased to 300 million roubles.\par Banks with own funds of less than 300 million roubles are permitted to continue\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , provided that they meet the 300 million rouble requirement by\par 1 January 2015.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 Under federal law, effective 1 January 2009, certain entities, such as state\par corporations (gosudarstvennie korporatsii) and limited liability companies,\par that were previously prohibited from doing so, have been granted the right to\par issue exchange-traded debt securities.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 As part of its Anti-Crisis Programme for 2009, the Government earmarked 495 billion roubles for loans (and other forms of funding) to banks, a portion of which was provided by the Bank of Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 In July 2009, a federal law was passed to improve the capitalisation of\par banks. Under this law, until 31 December 2010, banks were entitled to issue\par non-voting preferred shares with veto rights on certain matters in exchange for\par receiving federal bonds (OFZs). Ultimately, the preferred shares may either be\par converted into ordinary shares after a 10-year grace period or be purchased by\par the bank or its shareholders.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 To ensure control over the disbursement of state funds, the Bank of Russia\par may appoint its own representatives to each bank that has received state\par funding either under the Financial System Support Law or the Bank of Russia's\par uncollateralised lending programme. In particular, until full repayment of\par state funds, the Bank of Russia's representatives are entitled to participate\par without voting rights in meetings of all governing bodies of the bank and to\par receive information about management remuneration and the bank's credit and\par liability management policies.\par \par \u8226\'20\'22 To support the real economy, the Government has simplified the process for\par granting state guarantees, and, in particular, has delegated to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the authority to provide state guarantees of up to 10 billion roubles\par per guarantee to certain corporate issuers.\par \par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Regulations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia has adopted Article 8 of the IMF's Articles of Agreement with respect to current account convertibility and has met all obligations imposed thereunder.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prior to June 2004, the convertibility of the rouble was heavily regulated. In\par June 2004, a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law came into force that liberalised the exchange\par control regime. It envisaged full convertibility of the rouble, although a\par transition period remained in effect for certain provisions until 1 July 2006\par and certain other provisions until 1 January 2007, during which time the Bank\par of Russia and the Government were authorised to impose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  control\par measures. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law distinguishes between two types of capital\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , those that are regulated by the Government (principally commercial\par credits granted by residents to non-residents) and those regulated by the Bank\par of Russia (loans, cash transfers and securities transactions). Under the new\par law, only enumerated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are subject to regulation, and neither\par the Bank of Russia nor the Government may adopt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulations except in\par cases expressly prescribed by the new law. For example, under the new law,\par several restrictions may still be imposed to prevent a significant reduction in\par Russia's gold or foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves or severe fluctuations in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and to support the stability of Russia's balance of payments. For\par instance, subject to certain exceptions, Russian residents are required to\par repatriate their export-related earnings, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with\par "external securities" or in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than the rouble between Russian\par residents are generally prohibited.\par \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law allows residents and non-residents to open bank accounts in\par Russia and conduct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  between one another without limitation\par and provides that all contradictions and ambiguities in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law or in\par any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulation are to be resolved in favour of residents and\par non-residents conducting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Currently, Russian residents may\par open accounts in foreign banks in any country that is a member of the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  and a party to the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Action Task Force\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Money Laundering, subject only to\par post-notification of the Russian tax authorities. Exporters are not required to\par convert any portion of their foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  revenues into roubles.\par \par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The largest share of Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading has historically\par occurred on the over-the-counter interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, with other trading\par conducted on the Moscow Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exchange ("MICEX"). The Bank of\par Russia buys and sells \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through the MICEX exchange when it acts to\par influence exchange rates. MICEX was established in 1991 as a department of the\par Bank of Russia's Soviet predecessor, and was transformed in January 1992 into a\par closed joint-stock company. In December 2011, MICEX merged with the Russian\par Trading System ("RTS") to create MICEX-RTS. The largest shareholder of\par MICEX-RTS is the Bank of Russia. Commercial banks and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Association of\par Russian Banks\f28 \uc2 \f2  are also shareholders. Only members of the MICEX exchange, which\par include commercial banks, financial institutions and certain other investment\par institutions, may participate in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange trading on the MICEX\par exchange.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded on the MICEX exchange is the dollar, accounting for over 90% of total turnover (excluding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ). The other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded on the MICEX exchange is the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The Bank of Russia and MICEX (now MICEX-RTS) have supported the establishment\par of a system of regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchanges within Russia. There are currently\par seven regional exchanges. The largest regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchanges are the\par St. Petersburg \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exchange ("SPCEX") and the Siberian Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par Exchange.\par \par Capital Markets\par \par Government Securities\par \par The market for rouble-denominated government securities comprises federal\par Government rouble-denominated bonds and rouble-denominated bonds of sub-federal\par entities. The Government's rouble-denominated securities include principally\par medium-term bonds (OFZs) and state savings bonds (GSOs). OFZs are initially\par issued through the MICEX exchange or via closed subscription. See "-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign\par Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market" above. Secondary trading of OFZs takes place on the MICEX\par exchange and its representative regional exchanges. OFZs are held in book-entry\par form, which minimises transaction risks. As of 1 January 2012, the par value of\par federal Government rouble-denominated bonds was approximately 3.55 trillion\par roubles (U.S.$110.14 billion, based on the 1 January 2012 exchange rate), of\par which 2.9 trillion roubles (U.S.$90.17 billion) were OFZs and 0.55 trillion\par roubles (U.S.$17.18 billion) were GSOs. For a detailed description of the\par various Government domestic debt instruments, see "Public Debt and Related\par Matters-Domestic Debt-Government Domestic Debt."\par \par The market for rouble-denominated bonds of sub-federal entities prior to 2002\par consisted mainly of City of Moscow bonds placed and traded on MICEX and City of\par St. Petersburg bonds placed and traded on SPCEX. Bond issuances by sub-federal\par entities have since increased. As of 1 January 2012, there were 90 outstanding\par rouble-denominated bond issuances by 35 sub-federal entities, with an aggregate\par principal amount of approximately 351.05 billion roubles (U.S.$10.9 billion,\par based on the 1 January 2012 exchange rate). Of this aggregate principal amount,\par approximately 343.88 billion roubles (U.S.$10.7 billion) were issued by\par Federation subjects, including 175.01 billion roubles (U.S.$5.44 billion) by\par the city of Moscow, and 7.18 billion roubles (U.S.$223 million) were issued\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by\par municipal entities.\par \par Corporate Securities\par \par The trading of privatisation vouchers issued in connection with Russia's mass\par privatisation programme, implemented between 1992 and 1994, played an important\par role in the development of the equity securities market in Russia. A\par certificateless securities system was subsequently adopted, largely in response\par to fraud encountered in vouchers trading. As a result, all common and preferred\par stock in Russia is held in book-entry form, with ownership recorded in the\par issuer's share register.\par \par The Russian stock market was originally based on RTS, an electronic\par over-the-counter trading system, which was introduced in 1995 and managed by\par the National Association of Stock Market Participants, a self-regulatory body\par responsible for the development of rules for trading on the RTS. There are\par currently four licensed stock exchanges in Russia, including MICEX-RTS, which\par completed their merger in December 2011. Historically, MICEX was the largest\par Russian exchange for equity and debt trading and RTS traded predominantly\par futures and other derivative products. In 2007, when the Russian equity markets\par experienced significant growth, the average daily stock turnover on MICEX\par reached approximately U.S.$4.9 billion. In 2009, 2010 and 2011, the average\par daily stock turnover on MICEX was approximately U.S.$3.4 billion,\par U.S.$4 billion and U.S.$2.2, respectively.\par \par The market capitalisation of companies listed on Russian stock markets\par increased significantly in the period before the global financial crisis, and\par then, as a result of the crisis, decreased in 2008. In 2009, both RTS and MICEX\par recovered a portion of their value lost in 2008. The total market\par capitalisation of the RTS was approximately U.S.$1,328.8 billion at the end of\par 2007, U.S.$375 billion at the end of 2008, U.S.$763.5 billion at the end of\par 2009 and U.S.$990.55 at the end of 2010. The total market capitalisation of\par MICEX was U.S.$1,221.2 billion, U.S.$337.4 billion, U.S.$738 billion and\par U.S.$950.7 at the end of 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively. The total\par market capitalisation of the combined MICEX-RTS Group as of 1 January 2012\par equalled U.S.$767 billion.\par \par The Russian corporate bond market has been a fast-growing segment of the\par Russian financial markets. The number of bond issues and their tenor increased\par significantly between 2002 and the first half of 2008, and the outstanding\par volume of corporate bonds continued to increase through 2009, 2010 and 2011.\par The outstanding volume of corporate bonds amounted to 905.6 billion roubles\par (U.S.$34.3 billion, based on the exchange rate then prevailing), 1,272 billion\par roubles (U.S.$51.8 billion), 1,815 billion roubles (U.S.$61.8 billion),\par 2,568.7 billion roubles (U.S.$84.9 billion), 2,965.4 billion roubles\par (U.S.$97.3 billion) and 3,437 billion roubles (U.S.$107.3 billion) at year-end\par 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010 and 2011, respectively. The average daily turnover\par of Russian corporate bonds on MICEX was approximately U.S.$0.4 billion in 2007\par and 2008 and, as a result of the global financial crisis, decreased to\par U.S.$0.3 billion in 2009. The average daily turnover of Russian corporate bonds\par on MICEX improved to approximately U.S.$0.6 billion in 2010 and U.S.$0.7\par billion in 2011.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regulation of the Capital Markets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The FSFM has primary responsibility for regulating the Russian securities\par market. The FSFM is a federal executive authority subordinated to the\par Government. Its functions include registration of securities issues, ensuring\par the disclosure of information on securities markets and control and supervision\par of securities issuers and professional securities market participants. Pursuant\par to its regulatory authority, as an anti-crisis measure, the FSFM prohibited the\par short-selling of stocks between 30 September 2008 and 9 April 2009 amid\par concerns that such sales during a period of global financial crisis would lead\par to a collapse in Russian stock prices and to the bankruptcy of Russian\par companies. Short-selling continues to be subject to strict regulation by the\par FSFM. The Bank of Russia oversees government securities transactions (as an\par instrument of monetary policy), foreign investment (as a matter of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par exchange control) and securities offerings by banks (as the primary regulator\par of banks).\par \par In 2009, the Government adopted a Concept for Creating an International\par Financial Centre in Russia. This Concept sets forth the following set of\par measures designed to prepare Moscow to become an international financial\par centre: (i) establishing a transparent and flexible regulatory system for the\par financial markets; (ii) developing an effective financial system, including\par attracting long-term institutional investors, offering a more diverse array of\par financial products and fostering a competitive financial-sector labour market;\par (iii) integrating Russia into the global financial markets; (iv) improving\par financial and business infrastructure as well as living conditions in Moscow;\par and (v) improving Russia's image as a reliable partner.\par \par                         PUBLIC DEBT AND RELATED MATTERS                        \par \par Overview\par \par Russia's Government debt was 9.3% of GDP in 2010 and 9.8% of GDP in 2011 and is\par expected to reach approximately 13.2%, 14.9% and 15.7% of GDP in 2012, 2013 and\par 2014, respectively. At year-end 2010, Russia's total public sector external\par debt (Government and state-owned enterprises) represented 41% of total external\par debt (13% of GDP), and at year-end 2011 total external debt (public and\par private) amounted to 29% of GDP. In 2011, Russia's domestic and external debt\par equalled 7.7% of GDP and 2.1% of GDP, respectively. As of 31 December 2011,\par total external and domestic Government debt equalled U.S.$166 billion. Based on\par current estimates, through 2014, the Government expects that domestic debt will\par assume a greater share of the Government's overall debt. In 2012, 2013 and\par 2014, external debt as a share of GDP is expected to equal 2.4%, 2.7% and 3%,\par respectively, and domestic debt as a share of GDP is expected to equal 10.8%,\par 12.2% and 12.7%, respectively.\par \par The Government intends to use its public debt policy to accelerate Russia's\par social and economic development. In pursuit of this endeavor, the Government\par has adopted a public debt policy that is designed to meet the following key\par objectives: (i) ensuring the performance of the Government's budgetary\par commitments, while at the same time maintaining the sustainability of its debt\par programme; (ii) creating the conditions for elevating Russia's credit ratings\par to the "A" category; (iii) further developing the domestic market for\par Government debt; (iv) creating favourable market conditions that facilitate\par both sovereign and corporate borrowings, including establishing benchmark\par issuances at various points on the yield curve; (v) ensuring an optimal balance\par between the yields and duration of soverign debt; and (vi) introducing policy\par measures to limit speculative capital inflows.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In order to effectively implement its overall public debt policy, the Government has put into place both an external debt policy and a domestic debt policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par The current objectives of the Government's external debt policy are to maintain\par Russia's presence as an active sovereign borrower on the international capital\par markets and to establish a representative yield curve for debt in different\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Russian authorities also aim to continue creating the\par conditions necessary to attract a diverse portfolio of long-term investors.\par \par According to the Public Debt Management Policy of the Russian Federation for\par 2012-2014, the development of the domestic debt market is a key priority of the\par Government. In particular, the Government expects domestic borrowing to be the\par main source of federal budget deficit financing through 2014, with domestic\par borrowing expected to be approximately 1.2 trillion roubles in each of 2012 and\par 2013 and approximately 1.1 trillion roubles in 2014. Demand for medium- and\par long-term debt instruments is expected to remain relatively high due to the\par pension reforms enacted in 2001 and amended in 2009, which established a\par "funded" component to the country's pension system, whereby a portion of\par pension contributions may be invested in domestic securities. See "The Russian\par Economy-Pension Reform."\par \par The authorities have implemented several measures to develop the domestic debt\par market, including the following: (i) the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has enhanced the\par transparency of the government bond market by publishing in advance and on a\par regular basis the quarterly schedules for OFZ auctions and notifying market\par participants in advance of the expected yields on government bonds issued\par through such auctions; (ii) the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has started to consult with\par key market participants on a regular basis; (iii) advance deposit requirements\par for auctions have been eliminated; (iv) the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to\par develop and extend the maturity of benchmark domestic bonds in volumes\par sufficient to enhance liquidity; (v) the Law on the Central Depository and the\par Law on Clearing and Central Counterparty have been adopted; (vi) restrictions\par on over-the-counter trading of OFZs have been lifted; and (vii) as of 1 January\par 2012, OFZs have been allowed to trade on the MICEX exchange alongside corporate\par and municipal securities. In recent years, OFZ turnover has tripled, OFZ market\par volume has doubled and, over the last two years, the OFZ yield curve has\par tightened substantially.\par \par As part of its plan to further develop the domestic debt market, the Government\par intends to extend the average duration of outstanding OFZs as well as introduce\par a number of measures aimed at improving the infrastructure of the domestic debt\par market. This includes working towards allowing OFZ settlement through\par international depositary and clearing systems, introducing a centralised\par registration of securities in order to increase the effectiveness and\par competitiveness of the registration and payment infrastructure, and running OFZ\par auctions in the same section of the MICEX exchange as corporate securities. The\par Government also intends to continue supporting the development of the\par derivatives market. In February 2012, the authorities extended the OFZ issuance\par programme to a 15-year tenor. At the end of 2011, the weighted average duration\par of OFZs was approximately 3.7 years. It is the strategy of the Government that\par the weighted average life of OFZs be increased to five years. The Government\par also believes that the provision of state guarantees will continue to play a\par significant role in its overall domestic debt policy and intends to introduce\par better management of such guarantees, as well as enhanced monitoring of private\par sector external debt.\par \par The Government also intends to create the Russian State Financial Agency, whose\par responsibilities are expected to include developing relations with investors,\par advising the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on matters related to the Government's debt\par policy, issuing securities on behalf of the Russian Federation and managing the\par Government's debt portfolio through secondary market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, due to changes in the Budget Code, as of 1 January 2011, sub-federal entities have the right to resume foreign borrowings for the purposes of budget deficit financing and external debt repayments, provided that certain conditions are met, including the requirement that the sub-federal entity obtain ratings from at least two leading international credit rating agencies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 External Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par As at 31 December 2011, the total outstanding external debt of the Government,\par or for which the Government has agreed to be responsible, amounted to\par approximately U.S.$34.8 billion. This amount included all loans contracted or\par guaranteed by the Government, loans contracted or guaranteed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par and debt contracted or guaranteed by other entities legally authorised to\par borrow on behalf of the government of the former Soviet Union. Undisbursed\par commitments and public or private sector borrowings not guaranteed by the\par Government are excluded from the U.S.$34.8 billion outstanding as of\par 31 December 2011. In 2010 and 2011, the Government's external debt was 2.7% and\par 2.1% of GDP, respectively, compared to 89.2% of GDP in 1999. External debt\par comprised approximately 29.0% of the Government's total debt in 2010 and\par approximately 21.1% of the Government's total debt in 2011. The Government's\par external debt as a share of the country's total public- and private-sector debt\par has declined since 1999.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information with respect to the Government's external debt as of the indicated dates:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par External Debt Stock of the Government of the Russian Federation by Creditor(1) \par \par                                  As of                                         \par  