ch, in 2010\par and in 2011, totalled U.S.$0.9 billion and U.S.$1.0 billion, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) Excludes contingent liabilities, which, as of 31 December 2011, amounted to approximately U.S.$1.0 billion, including in the form of guarantees.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3) The 2030 Eurobond has a step-up coupon which will remain at 7.5% until maturity in 2030.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) Includes FTO, London Club and other claims not submitted in previous exchange offers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some totals may not add due to rounding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the U.S.$34.8 billion outstanding at 31 December 2011, U.S.$2.5 billion\par represents loans from multilateral creditors, including the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Of the\par total U.S.$29.2 billion of Eurobonds outstanding at 31 December 2011,\par U.S.$2 billion represents the 2015 Eurobond maturing in April 2015,\par U.S.$3.5 billion represents the 2018 Eurobond maturing in July 2018,\par U.S.$3.5 billion represents the 2020 Eurobond maturing in April 2020,\par U.S.$2.5 billion represents the 2028 Eurobond maturing in June 2028 and\par U.S.$17.7 billion represents the 2030 Eurobond maturing between March 2012 and\par March 2030. An additional U.S.$328.2 million representing the 2010 Eurobond was\par outstanding at year-end 2009 and was repaid in full on 31 March 2010.\par U.S.$3.1 billion represents official government-to-government credits and\par export credits, of which U.S.$1.1 billion is owed to former member countries of\par the Council for Mutual Economic Assistance ("CMEA" or "COMECON"), China and the\par former Yugoslavia. See "-Other Former Soviet Union Debt" below. The last\par outstanding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bond, Series VII, was repaid in\par 2011.\par \par The Government prepaid approximately U.S.$44 billion of its external debt in\par 2005 and 2006. This included approximately U.S.$37 billion of rescheduled\par Soviet-era debt owed to the Paris Club, which was prepaid in August 2005 and\par August 2006.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia has in the past purchased Russian Government Eurobonds and other federal Government obligations in the open market and may do so in the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 External Debt Restructuring\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Paris Club\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par In January 1992, the Government concluded an initial annual rescheduling\par agreement with the Paris Club of official creditors, followed by three further\par annual reschedulings in 1993, 1994 and 1995, covering debt service falling due\par during the period from December 1991 to the end of 1995.\par \par In April 1996, a comprehensive rescheduling was agreed with the Paris Club\par covering approximately U.S.$33 billion of debt owed to Paris Club creditors,\par including debt service falling due between the beginning of January 1996 and\par the end of March 1999 and virtually all the debt rescheduled under the previous\par agreements.\par \par In August 1999, the Government concluded a further rescheduling agreement with\par the Paris Club. This agreement provided for the deferral of approximately\par U.S.$8.3 billion of debts owed to Paris Club creditors, including payment\par arrears outstanding at the end of June 1999 and debt service falling due\par between the beginning of July 1999 and the end of December 2000 in respect of\par both previously rescheduled and non-previously rescheduled Soviet-era\par obligations.\par \par In May 2005, the Government reached an agreement with the Paris Club under\par which approximately U.S.$15 billion of the debts rescheduled under the 1996 and\par 1999 rescheduling agreements was prepaid at face value. All of Russia's\par remaining Paris Club debt, in the approximate amount of U.S.$21.6 billion,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 was\par prepaid in August 2006.\par \par London Club\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A comprehensive restructuring agreement in respect of Soviet-era debt owed to\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's London Club commercial bank and other financial creditors was\par closed in December 1997. Under the terms of this agreement, the entire stock of\par outstanding principal owed to London Club creditors, amounting to approximately\par U.S.$22.2 billion, was restructured as interests in restructured loans maturing\par in 2002 to 2020 ("PRINs"), and the interest regarded as having accrued on this\par debt (net of a cash down-payment) was restructured into U.S.$6.7 billion\par principal amount of U.S.$ floating rate interest notes due 2002 to 2015\par ("IANs").\par \par In July 2000, the Russian Federation offered to exchange \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's\par PRINs and IANs for 2030 Bonds and the interest arrears on PRINs and IANs for a\par combination of 2010 Bonds and cash. For each U.S.$1,000 principal amount of\par PRINs tendered, a creditor received U.S.$625 principal amount of 2030 Bonds,\par and for each U.S.$1,000 principal amount of IANs tendered, a creditor received\par U.S.$670 of 2030 Bonds. In August 2000, U.S.$18.2 billion of 2030 Bonds and\par U.S.$2.8 billion of 2010 Bonds were issued in exchange for PRINs and IANs, and\par U.S.$275 million was paid in cash in exchange for PRIN and IAN interest\par arrears. A further U.S.$129.4 million of 2030 Bonds and U.S.$19.8 million of\par 2010 Bonds have been issued in subsequent re-openings of this exchange. At\par 31 December 2011, U.S.$0.1 million of PRINs and U.S.$32.9 million of IANs have\par not been tendered for exchange and remain outstanding.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FTO Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In November 2002, the Russian Federation offered to exchange 2030 Bonds and\par 2010 Bonds for eligible uninsured trade debt of the former Soviet Union for\par which the Government has agreed to be responsible ("FTO claims") on terms\par broadly comparable to the terms previously offered to PRIN and IAN holders. In\par December 2002, U.S.$1.19 billion principal amount of 2030 Bonds and\par U.S.$183.8 million principal amount of 2010 Bonds were issued in exchange for\par FTO claims and U.S.$171.5 million was paid in cash. An additional\par U.S.$1.075 billion of FTO claims were exchanged for U.S.$140.5 million\par principal amount of 2010 Bonds, U.S.$907.8 million principal amount of 2030\par Bonds and U.S.$383.4 million in cash in November 2006. In December 2009, a\par further U.S.$405.5 million of FTO claims were exchanged for U.S.$45.8 million\par principal amount of 2010 Bonds, U.S.$327.1 million principal amount of 2030\par Bonds and U.S.$262.8 million in cash. The bonds that were exchanged in 2009\par were previously issued by the Russian Federation and subsequently acquired in\par the secondary market. No new Eurobonds were issued in connection with the last\par exchange offer.\par \par As a result of these three exchange offers, a total of 12.7 thousand claims of\par foreign commercial creditors from 24 different countries, representing a total\par of U.S.$2.8 billion, have been settled, and, accordingly, all claims of foreign\par commercial creditors on Soviet-era debt have been satisfied.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Former Soviet Union Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par Following the dissolution of the former Soviet Union, the Government assumed\par responsibility for Soviet-era debts owed to the former member countries of the\par CMEA. These debts relate to the CMEA trade settlement systems that were in\par place between CMEA member countries. Most of these debts were incurred as a\par result of an imbalance in the settlements in favour of the creditor country at\par the time of the dissolution of the CMEA and the Soviet Union. As of 31 December\par 2011, debt to the former member countries of the CMEA, China and the former\par Yugoslavia amounted to U.S.$1.1 billion. A substantial portion of this debt\par will be repaid in the form of goods and services, with the balance to be repaid\par in cash.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government has also concluded negotiations with a number of non-Paris Club official creditors under which repayment is partly in the form of goods and services. As of 31 December 2011, debt to non-Paris Club official creditors amounted to U.S.$1.4 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 1993, the Government issued dollar denominated Internal Government Hard\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bonds ("OVVZs," known as "Taiga" bonds or "MinFins") to compensate\par Russian legal entities whose funds had been frozen in 1991 in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par accounts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . MinFins were initially issued in five series, with\par maturities ranging between one and 15 years. Two additional series were issued\par in May 1996 with maturities of 10 and 15 years, respectively. All of the\par MinFins pay a 3% coupon annually until redemption. OVVZs trade in the\par over-the-counter market. The MinFin Series VII, the last outstanding OVVZs\par which were issued in 1996, was repaid in 2011. Historically, OVVZs were\par regarded as domestic public debt. Starting in 2000, they were reclassified as\par external debt for budgetary purposes.\par \par On 11 May 1999, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  requested the holders of\par U.S.$1.3 billion aggregate principal amount of Series III MinFins maturing on\par 14 May 1999 not to present their bonds for redemption, pending the development\par of a restructuring proposal. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  did, however, pay all of\par the interest accrued on Series III MinFins and announced that interest on these\par bonds would continue to accrue at the original rate of 3% until they were\par restructured into new instruments. Following consultations with representative\par investor groups, the Government made a proposal in November 1999 to exchange\par all Series III MinFins for (i) new dollar-denominated Government bonds with an\par average life of seven and a half years and bearing interest at 3% payable\par semi-annually or (ii) rouble-denominated OFZs with maturities of four years,\par paying interest semi-annually at 15% in the first year, declining to 10% in\par subsequent periods. Under the Government's proposal, Series III MinFins were\par exchanged for new dollar-denominated bonds at par, and for new\par rouble-denominated bonds at par at the rate of 26.2 roubles per dollar, that\par being the average official exchange rate for the first week of November 1999.\par Between 2000 and 2005, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had received applications for\par the exchange of substantially all of the outstanding Series III MinFins, of\par which approximately 72% was exchanged for dollar instruments and the remaining\par 28% was exchanged for OFZs. In accordance with Government Decree No. 387 dated\par 20 June 2007, additional Series III MinFins principal and interest claims\par amounting to U.S.$1.4 million were settled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  between\par 2007 and 2009. In January 2010, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced the expiry of\par the deadline to submit further Series III MinFins for settlement.\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 External Debt Service Projection\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth a projection of the Government's contractual\par external debt service by type of credi