\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/MarketBeat: Russian Ruble Becomes Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Star\b0\par\par\uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 15:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Market Beat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Stephen L. Bernard and Erin McCarthy\par \par \uc2 Russian-ruble trading is suddenly the hottest corner of the ever-growing emerging-markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape.\par \par \uc2 Boosting the ruble's use is a burgeoning oil market and more flexibility from Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the bank's lighter touch in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market a primary force. Russian-ruble futures contracts traded on markets run by \uc2 CME Group\uc2  nearly tripled in 2011 from the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Among emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble now trails only the Mexican peso in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures trading volume on the \uc2 CME\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The ruble has become more flexible and [is] less predictable," said Dmitry Polevoy, economist for Russia and Kazakhstan at ING Commercial Banking in Moscow, noting what is attracting traders to the Russian unit.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the ruble has strengthened 9% against the dollar, which bought RUB29.263 late Wednesday, according to CQG.\par \par \uc2 This torrid pace of growth in volume has continued early in 2012. Volume through the first two months of the year is up 46% compared with the same period in 2011, according to \uc2 CME\uc2  data. Using futures as a proxy, the ruble even trades more heavily than the widely watched Brazilian real, popular for its large commodities market and high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Driving the increase, since the 2008 financial crisis, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance and also widened the ruble's trading band against a two-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket of the U.S. dollar and euro, drawing more market participants to the ruble.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "continues to make it more and more flexible, and that just encourages many exporters or importers on the other side to engage into more hedging activities," said Vladimir Kolychev, chief economist for Russia at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduced the amount it spends to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a looser exchange-rate policy has created wider swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have flocked to this higher volatility, and corporations often must hedge against that volatility, further adding to volume.\par \par \uc2 Rising crude oil prices, up 8% so far in 2012, have also boosted interest, with Russia now sitting as the world's second-largest crude oil producer, according to the U.S. Energy Information Administration.\par \par \uc2 "The main background driver is the oil price," said Carolin Hecht, emerging-market strategist at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "Currently correlation between the oil price and ruble is quite high."\par \par \uc2 Even so, volumes can grow still. Holding the ruble back somewhat, liquidity is still not yet deep enough for investors to use the ruble itself as a hedge for emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or commodities exposure, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The ruble's increasing liquidity and its play as a commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could draw in more investors in the near term, but what truly will be a turning point for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be when Russia liberalizes its local government bond market for foreign investors, market participants say. Its government is considering allowing ruble-denominated government bonds to be traded via foreign clearing-settlements systems like Euroclear.\par \par \uc2 That shift could happen by the end of this year and could lead to more ruble exposure for investors through higher-yielding local government bonds, said Alexander Kozhemiakin, portfolio manager at Standish in Boston.\par \par \uc2 "When that happens, the investors' interest in the Russian ruble would increase even more," he added.\par \par \uc2 -- For more \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economic news from our team, follow @djfxtrader\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-22-12 1005ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120322e83m0009s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
