\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble May Flounder As Investor Concerns On Russia Escalate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Prabha Natarajan and Ira Iosebashvili   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2011\par 22:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble has had a flustered run over the past few days as a whiff of citizens' unrest and political change have led investors to scramble for the exits.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is said to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent days to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  also curtailed the amount banks borrowed through its overnight repo auction to its lowest level in two months in a bid to shore up the ruble.\par \par \uc2 This has helped limit the ruble's fall, with the dollar gaining just 2.5% over the two weeks since the midterm election on Dec. 4 to RUB31.730, according to CQG data. In contrast, local bonds, which haven't seen much of an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have been routed. The benchmark bond is down 10% since the political unrest began.\par \par \uc2 The ruble is expected to flounder in 2012 as it deals with the dual onslaught of a slowdown in Europe and domestic unrest. Russia's troubles started with the weaker-than-expected showing by Prime Minister Vladimir Putin's party at the parliamentary polls held Dec. 4. Charges of vote rigging during the elections provoked massive protests last weekend, leading some investors to worry about an Arab spring-like uprising in Russia.\par \par \uc2 John Peta, portfolio manager at Acadian Asset Management, began shorting the ruble in the days following the election for that reason.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts and strategists caution against such an extreme read on the current situation. "The vote is not a mandate against Putin," said Neil Shearing, chief economist at Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, in the short term this scenario is expected to elevate political risks, which will accelerate capital outflows and prompt a potential sell-off of Russian assets, said Julia Tsepliaeva, head of market economics for Russia at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , in a note.\par \par \uc2 Immediately after the polls, \uc2 Vnesheconombank\uc2 , the state-run development bank, had to pull out of a euro bond sale as investors demanded higher risk premium to counter the increasing political risk. This was the first such instance since the 1990s, according to Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 Russia is expected to see $85 billion in capital outflows this year, according to the Finance Ministry. Some $64 billion had already left for the year to Oct. 31. Some analysts are now, however, estimating capital flight to be between $90 billion and $100 billion.\par \par \uc2 This is a perennial problem and concern for Russia as the country disproportionately depends on dollar borrowings to fund growth. In recent weeks, a couple of factors have escalated the exit of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Domestic companies are expected to pay out nearly $22 billion in the last two months of the year as part of their annual year-end debt repayments, according to an analysis by Moscow's Higher School of Economics. And, borrowing by euro-zone banks with a presence in Russia to lend to their foreign parents increased over the past few months as these institutions sought to shore up liquidity.\par \par \uc2 However, this steady outflow will have an adverse effect in limiting the options available to the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Monetary easing to support the weakening economy could backfire, and lead more investors to leave the country.\par \par \uc2 The one factor in favor of the ruble is the projected rise in oil prices, said Vladimir Kolychev, a director at Rosbank, a unit of \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in Russia, in a note. Earlier this week, Russia's economy ministry lowered its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectation, forecasting that the dollar would average at RUB31.1, up from the RUB28.7 previously expected.\par \par \uc2 But Citi analysts expect the dollar/ruble rate to rise to RUB36-37 by year end, but improve to RUB33-34 if oil prices hold in 2012.\par \par \uc2 However, even if Russian assets look cheap, capital outflows are not likely to reverse.\par \par \uc2 "The focus is now presidential elections, so a lot of people will tend to adopt a wait-and-see approach for next three months," said Natalia Orlova, the chief economist at \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Prabha Natarajan, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2468; prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Erin McCarthy contributed to this report. [ 12-13-11 1647ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111213e7cd000wq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
