\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Russia's vote bearish for rouble, bullish for consumer stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2011\par 12:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks, rouble ignore parliamentary elections\par \par \uc2 * Analysts expect fiscal spending increase\par \par \uc2 * Retailers seen winners from higher spending\par \par \uc2 (Adds inflation, latest prices)\par \par \uc2 By Megan Davies and Lidia Kelly\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Dec 5 (Reuters) - Sunday's electoral blow for Russian Prime Minister Vladimir Putin is bearish for the rouble as increased fiscal spending could follow, and the reduction in his party's power could hurt companies which are key beneficiaries of the status quo, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 However, they reckoned the news could boost stocks sensitive to consumer spending, such as retail stocks.\par \par \uc2 The fall in support on Sunday for Putin's United Russia to 49.5 percent of votes from 64 percent support four years ago was largely in line with what stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had expected, provoking little change in morning trading.\par \par \uc2 At 1424 GMT, the country's two main exchanges RTS and MICEX were up 0.9 percent and 0.8 percent respectively.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at U.S. bank Citi said they expected the equity market to be slightly weaker, partly because increased spending could ensue.\par \par \uc2 "While the result is only a little worse than expected for United Russia, some of the tactics used to bolster votes may cause concern," the analysts said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 "The fear is that a weaker party of power will resort to more spending and a more nationalist approach and be less inclined to indulge in liberal policies."\par \par \uc2 That could add to risks to macroeconomic stability if fiscal spending growth goes too far, said Yaroslav Lissovolik, chief economist and head of research at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 "While that sustains the growth outlook in the medium term, there are risks of higher inflation eventually, undermining the sustainability of growth," Lissovolik said.\par \par \uc2 Russia's headline annual inflation rate slowed in November to 6.8 percent, a sixth consecutive monthly fall, increasing chances the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may loosen monetary policy as the economy slows.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  said with a low budget deficit, they expected the government "can and will increase spending before the March presidential elections" and said it should be negative for fixed income and the rouble.\par \par \uc2 Retailers are seen winners from more spending, said analysts at Citi, although they expect "more weakness from companies like \uc2 Novatek\uc2 , \uc2 Uralkali\uc2  or \uc2 Mostotrest\uc2  which are key beneficiaries of the current system".\par \par \uc2 The rouble traded little changed on Monday, with investors staying away from making sharp moves and awaiting news from Europe, including Monday's meeting between the leaders of France and Germany, Thursday's meeting of the Governing Council of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Friday's European Union summit.\par \par \uc2 The rouble was flat at 30.85 versus the dollar\par \par \uc2 and level at 41.6 against the euro .\par \par \uc2 Versus the euro-dollar basket, the rouble was virtually unchanged at 35.68, remaining in the range of 33.70-35.70 where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease pressure on the rouble.\par \par \uc2 "The United \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  win was predictable, the question was only the numbers," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with a major foreign bank. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ \tab \par    Russia election digest:        \tab \par    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were looking towards Europe, with controlled optimism on a euro zone rescue seen across global markets. France and Germany on Monday agreed on a series of reforms to address the euro zone sovereign debt crisis that will be presented to EU President Herman Van Rompuy on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Roland Nash, chief strategist at hedge fund Verno Capital in Moscow, said that for investors the financial performance of companies in which they hold shares is far more important than the Sunday vote.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In our world the big news is that Russian companies are actually paying dividends -- that's more important than a result that wasn't a surprise," Nash said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That Russians are dissatisfied I think anyone could have told you months ago."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spread on Russia's dollar bonds over comparable U.S. securities narrowed to 269 basis points from nearly 320 points seen early last week, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's EMBI+ debt indexes, which also pointed to a lower intention to cut exposure towards risky assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on Russia's 30-year benchmark Eurobond fell to 4.21 percent, from around 4.65 percent seen late last month . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russian                             Latest   Net Change  % Change  % Change on year\par Markets                                                          \par                                                                  \par MICEX                               1517.89  12.12       0.80      -10.08\par RTS                                 1560.69  15.20       0.90      -100.00\par London ADRs                         877.68   4.02        0.46      -11.15\par Emrg Mkt Indx                       967.34