\par\par \b \uc2 Russian markets, rouble ignore elections outcome\b0\par\par\uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2011\par 09:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Dec 5 (Reuters) - Russian stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets remained hostage to global financial developments at Monday's opening, disregarding the results of the Sunday parliamentary elections that dealt Prime Minister Vladimir Putin his largest electorate blow to date.\par \par \uc2 At 0745 GMT, the country's two main exchanges RTS and MICEX were slightly up, 0.4 percent and 0.3 percent respectively, with controlled optimism on a euro zone rescue seen across global markets.\par \par \uc2 "News from Europe will be the main driver of the Russian markets today, even as the news of the Duma election result will dominate the domestic headlines," said Chris Weafer, chief strategist at Troika Dialog in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Roland Nash, chief strategist at hedge fund Verno Capital in Moscow, said that for investors the financial performance of companies in which they hold shares is far more important than the Sunday vote.\par \par \uc2 "In our world the big news is that Russian companies are actually paying dividends -- that's more important than a result that wasn't a surprise," Nash said.\par \par \uc2 "That Russians are dissatisfied I think anyone could have told you months ago."\par \par \uc2 The rouble also traded little changed, with investors staying away from making sharp moves and awaiting news from Europe, including Monday's meeting between the leaders of France and Germany, Thursday's meeting of the Governing Council of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Friday's European Union summit.\par \par \uc2 The rouble was down 0.06 percent at 30.87 versus the dollar but was up 0.36 percent at 41.46 against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Versus the euro-dollar basket, the rouble was up 0.2 percent at 35.64 , remaining in the range of 33.70-35.70 where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease pressure on the rouble.\par \par \uc2 "The United \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  win was predictable, the question was only the numbers," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with a major foreign bank.\par \par \uc2 With 95 percent of votes counted, Putin's United Russia had won 49.7 percent, compared with more than 64 percent four years ago, marking the largest electoral setback for Putin.\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Russia election digest:\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the domestic market is expected to pay some attention to a series of corporate results, which began on Monday with \uc2 Magnitogorsk Iron & Steel Works\uc2 , or MMK, Russia's third-largest steelmaker, posting third-quarter loss of $205 million.\par \par \uc2 MMK's shares were down 3.5 percent, significantly underperforming the broad MICEX index.\par \par \uc2 Later in the week, third quarter and nine month results are expected from \uc2 Severstal\uc2 , \uc2 Bashneft\uc2 , Dixy Group, VTB and \uc2 Rostelecom\par \par \uc2 The spread on Russia's dollar bonds over comparable U.S. securities narrowed to 278 basis points from nearly 320 points seen early last the week according to \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's EMBI+ debt indexes, which also pointed to a lower intention to cut exposure towards risky assets.\par \par \uc2 Yield on Russia's 30-year benchmark Eurobond fell to 4.25 percent, from around 4.65 percent seen late last month. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russian                           Latest      Net   % Change  % Change on \par Markets                                     Change                    year\par                                       