\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Russia's Ignatiev: RUB More Likely To Strengthen Than Weaken By End '11\b0\par\par\uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2011\par 15:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  Ignatiev: Banks Raised $12B In Foreign Assets In Oct, $4B In Sep\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  Ignatiev: Ctrl Bank Sold Around $1.5B In Nov \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Ignatiev: Cap Outflow Slowed In Nov\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  Ignatiev: Weaker Ruble May Spur Inflation Above 7% By End '11\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  Russia's Ignatiev: Does Not See Sharp Liquidity Deficit By End '11\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  Ignatiev: "Serious Reforms" Needed For Over 5% GDP Growth\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:30 EDT *DJ  Ignatiev: Fears ECB Will Have To Be Much More Active In Solving Problems\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 09:50 EDT DJ  Ignatiev: Fears ECB Will Have To Be Much More Active In Solving Problems\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Sergei Ignatiev said Friday he feared the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to do much more to solve the region's problems.\par \par \uc2 "I'm afraid the situation is so acute that the ECB will have to be much more active" in solving the region's debt problems, Ignatiev said. "I fear the volume of debt the ECB buys will have to grow."\par \par \uc2 Ignatiev said he didn't see sharp liquidity deficit in Russia by the end of 2011.\par \par \uc2 A weaker ruble may spur inflation above 7% by the year's end, Ignatiev said. He also said "serious reforms" were needed for the country's GDP to grow by more than 5%.\par \par \uc2 Ignatiev said he saw the ruble more likely strengthening rather than weakening by the end of 2011.\par \par \uc2 Russian banks raised $12 billion in foreign assets in October, after raising $4 billion in September, Ignatiev said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $1.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this month, Ignatiev said, adding the capital outflow has slowed down in November.\par \par \uc2 -By Ira Iosebashvili, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 232-9192, ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Nadia Popova in Moscow contributed to this report.)\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2011 16:23 EDT DJ  UPDATE: Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Head Sees Ruble Stronger Amid Capital Flight\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief says ruble more inclined to strengthen\par \par \uc2 --Weaker ruble may push year-end inflation above 7% target\par \par \uc2 --Sees local liquidity improving by mid-December\par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout to expand on Friday's comments by Ignatiev) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ira Iosebashvili \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The ruble is more likely to strengthen than weaken until the end of the year, even as capital continues to flee the country, Bank of Russia chairman Sergei Ignatiev said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A surge in risk aversion weakened the ruble by as much as 16% versus the dollar between Aug. 1 and Oct. 7, as investors fled emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid a sovereign debt crisis in Europe. Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has strengthened since then, although not back to levels seen in July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My personal point of view is that there is a greater chance the ruble will strengthen, rather than weaken," Ignatiev told journalists at a briefing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That strengthening may occur amid continued capital outflows--Russia has seen $64 billion in capital flight in the first 10 months of the year, and expects some $70 billion to leave the country by year-end. Outflows totaled $13 billion in October, down from $14 billion in the month before, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A large part of that outflow came from Russian banks, which raised their foreign assets by $12 billion in October to a total of $53 billion, after raising them by $4 billion in September, Ignatiev said, adding that the numbers were so far preliminary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in the day, Ignatiev told Russia's upper house of parliament he did not see the ruble's real effective exchange rate--which measures the ruble's value relative to a basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  accounted for inflation--strengthening by year end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ruble's weakness since the end of the summer may spark inflation and drive the consumer price index slightly above the 7% forecast the regulator forecasts for the end of 2011, Ignatiev said. That would still put Russian inflation at its lowest year-end reading since the demise of the Soviet Union.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ignatiev said he didn't see a "particularly acute" liquidity deficit by the end of the year, and the situation would likely improve by mid-December, when a wave of government budget spending hits the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ira Iosebashvili, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 232-9192; ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Nadia Popova in Moscow contributed to this report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 19, 2011 16:23 ET (21:23 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111118e7bi003m4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
