\par\par \b \uc2 DJ HEARD ON THE STREET: Kremlin Moves Rumble The Ruble\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Peaple   \par \uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2011\par 04:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 Russian voters were not consulted on the deal whereby President Dmitri Medvedev agreed to stand aside for Vladimir Putin in next year's presidential elections. But the markets are having their say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was this week forced into its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2009 as the ruble threatened to fall below its official trading band of RUB32.5 to RUB37.5 to a basket of euros and dollars. That could be a sign of things to come.\par \par \uc2 True, politics isn't the only thing destabilizing the ruble. The recent falls in oil and gas prices have exerted downward pressure, as they have on other resource-rich country \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as these commodities make up two-thirds of Russia's exports. When oil prices tanked during the global financial crisis so did the ruble; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent over $200 billion in 2008 defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was forced to hike interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the CBR has been reducing its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while widening the trading band. Such moves had encouraged hopes of Russia moving towards a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. That, in turn, would give it scope to run a more independent monetary policy, setting interest rates to suit domestic financial conditions.\par \par \uc2 But the current political uncertainty has put those ambitions on hold. Net capital outflows in the first half of this year reached over $30 billion. This week's sacking of Alexei Kudrin, the widely respected finance minister, has fuelled investor concerns; capital outflows could accelerate to $25-$30 billion in the fourth quarter alone, ING forecasts. If Russian households start switching their deposits into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they did in 2008, pressure on the ruble will grow.\par \par \uc2 The CBR has now decided to freeze the trading band, a setback for its liberalizing ambitions. Unless oil prices recover, or the political outlook suddenly improves, it faces digging into its reserves again to hold the ruble line.\par \par \uc2 (Andrew Peaple is a writer for Heard on the Street. He can be reached at +44 207 842 9277 or by email: andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110930e79t003uh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
