\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Kremlin Moves Rumble The Ruble\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Peaple \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN \par \uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2011\par 19:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Russian voters were not consulted on the deal whereby President Dmitri Medvedev agreed to stand aside for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in next year's presidential elections. But the markets are having their say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was this week forced into its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2009 as the ruble threatened to fall below its official trading band of RUB32.5 to RUB37.5 to a basket of euros and dollars. That could be a sign of things to come.\par \par \uc2 True, politics isn't the only thing destabilizing the ruble. The recent falls in oil and gas prices have exerted downward pressure, as they have on other resource-rich country \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as these commodities make up two-thirds of Russia's exports. When oil prices tanked during the global financial crisis so did the ruble; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent over $200 billion in 2008 defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was forced to hike interest rates.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Since then, the CBR has been reducing its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while widening the trading band. Such moves had encouraged hopes of Russia moving towards a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. That, in turn, would give it scope to run a more independent monetary policy, setting interest rates to suit domestic financial conditions.\par \par \uc2 But the current political uncertainty has put those ambitions on hold. Net capital outflows in the first half of this year reached over $30 billion. This week's sacking of \uc2 Alexei Kudrin\uc2 , the widely respected finance minister, has fuelled investor concerns; capital outflows could accelerate to $25-$30 billion in the fourth quarter alone, ING forecasts. If Russian households start switching their deposits into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they did in 2008, pressure on the ruble will grow.\par \par \uc2 The CBR has now decided to freeze the trading band, a setback for its liberalizing ambitions. Unless oil prices recover, or the political outlook suddenly improves, it faces digging into its reserves again to hold the ruble line.\par \par \uc2 (Andrew Peaple is a writer for Heard on the Street. He can be reached at +44 207 842 9277 or by email: andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 28, 2011 13:39 ET (17:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120110928e79s002y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
