\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Bank Of Russia Seen Defending Ruble On Tuesday - Bankers\b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2011\par 13:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pledge to support ruble\par \par \uc2 -- 1230 GMT briefing set with Chairman Sergei Ignatiev\par \par \uc2 -- Ruble weakness a concern among Russia's population\par \par \uc2 By William Mauldin\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Russia, at a briefing set for Tuesday, may voice support for the ailing ruble, pledging to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels to avoid a loss of confidence in an election year, economists and traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday scheduled a briefing with Chairman Sergei Ignatiev and journalists for 1230 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "It will be some statement that will reassure the population that they won't allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall much lower," said Peter Westin, strategist at Aton Capital in Moscow. "The population isn't happy with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling, that's for sure."\par \par \uc2 Besides addressing the sliding ruble, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may alter the way it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Currently the regulator uses a trading band to contain the ruble's volatility versus a basket of dollars and euros. The trading band is seen as between 32.50 and 37.50 rubles to the basket right now, according to Mikhail Anisimov, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at VTB Capital. On Tuesday the ruble strengthened to 37.29 versus the basket on the Micex exchange, following sharp declines last week and on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "They will confirm they have a full set of instruments ready to provide liquidity," Anisimov said. "I would expect them to confirm the current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism and readiness to act on interest rates to support asset-price stability through conventional and unconventional measures."\par \par \uc2 One option would be for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to widen or cancel the trading band and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only selectively in the ruble's exchange rate, according to Natalia Orlova, chief economist at \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  in Moscow. Another option would be to "publicly reserve the right to act independently" of the ruble trading band, said Jacob Nell, analyst at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's Moscow office.\par \par \uc2 The Russian population sees the ruble, especially the ruble-dollar exchange rate, as the main barometer of the country's economic health. With stock investments still unpopular, Russians easily convert between saving in rubles, dollars and euros.\par \par \uc2 -By William Mauldin, Dow Jones Newswires; +7 495 232-9192, william.mauldin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 27, 2011 07:17 ET (11:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110927e79r0031m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
