\par\par \b \uc2 VTB CEO: State Control Reduction May Take More Than Two Years\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexander Kolyandr   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2011\par 18:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ST. PETERSBURG, Russia (Dow Jones)--It may take Russia more than two years to reduce its stake in OAO VTB Group (VTBR.RS) below 50%, the bank's chief executive Andrei Kostin told Dow Jones Newswires late Friday.\par \par \uc2 A top aide to President Dmitry Medvedev had said Friday the country might look to sell completely out of VTB, Russia's second-largest bank by assets, over the next five years. But Kostin cast doubt on that as a realistic timeframe.\par \par \uc2 VTB, which sold 10% of its stake in February for $3.2 billion, plans to sell 10% stakes in 2012 and 2013 each, which would leave the state with 55% stake in the bank.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know whether we can reconsider that. It depends on the market," he said, adding that he expects the state to reduce its stake below 50% in the bank eventually.\par \par \uc2 In a keynote speech Friday, Medvedev had championed privatization as one of the solutions to raising Russia's growth rate. Kostin said he sees only "anemic growth" in Russia currently, with significant growth only in the sectors supported by the state, but said he hopes activity will pick up in the second half of 2011.\par \par \uc2 His words echoed those of the state officials, who say that Russia's economy is not growing up to its potential and that investment level is low, despite a windfall of cash due to high oil and other commodities prices.\par \par \uc2 "Russian investors prefer to keep the money they earn, to wait and not to invest in Russia. There is no political risk for them, mostly economical, but they are cautious about the global markets and growth outlook." Kostin said.\par \par \uc2 Kostin sees no political risk connected to the presidential elections next year, as both incumbent Dmitri Medvedev and Prime Minister Vladimir Putin, the former president, who are considered to be the most probable candidates, have very close views on the economy.\par \par \uc2 "There is a serious predictability now, unlike in 2008 when Medvedev was elected president for the first time", he said.\par \par \uc2 "Financial policy will be aimed at keeping the ruble stable," Kostin said, adding that the government "will try not to let ruble strengthen."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has purchased $2 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so far in June, its deputy chief Alexei Ulyukayev said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Kostin said there was no reason for the ruble to weaken in the near term.\par \par \uc2 "Some strengthening is possible, but volatility shouldn't be large," he said. "Russian financial policy makers would be happy with 3% to 5% inflation, compared to 8%-9% now."\par \par \uc2 "There is a strong government commitment to lower inflation, and social spending is not going through the roof even now ahead of the elections," he said.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Kostin said Russian banks may gain a competitive edge vis-a-vis foreign banks as the latter are forced to raise their capital buffers under new international regulation. The new Basel III capital adequacy requirements, to be phased in starting in 2013, require banks to hold a minimum 7% of risk-weighted assets in capital and reserves.\par \par \uc2 "It's not a problem for us as we (already) have a minimum 10% equity capital buffer requirement," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Alexander Kolyandr, Dow Jones Newswires; Alexander.Kolyandr@dowjones.com [ 06-18-11 1254ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110618e76i0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
