 Russia only in respect of certain multilateral facilities, through bond issues and through certain medium\u8211\'20\'13 and short-term financings. All other previous external borrowings of the Government have been implemented through \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vnesheconombank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vneshtorgbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (Russia's foreign trade bank), or Roseximbank (Russia's export\u8211\'20\'13import bank), which were all authorised on a case-by-case basis to borrow externally under the guarantee of the Government and are responsible for recording and monitoring these borrowings. Borrowings by these banks under the Government's guarantee are included in the external debt statistics of the Government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The provision of state guarantees is strictly regulated by the budget laws of the Russian Federation. State guarantees are provided solely on the basis of a Government resolution and the federal budget law for a particular year and for a particular target period. The 2011 Budget Law provides for the issuance of state guarantees, including for the purpose of implementing investment projects and promoting the export of industrial products. These guarantees may not exceed 420.5 billion roubles and U.S. $8 billion for 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government has in the past provided external guarantees to certain international financial institutions. As of 31 December 2010, U.S. $0.07 billion were outstanding under these guarantees.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government has paid in full all of the debt service due on borrowings contracted by the Government since 1 January 1992.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Domestic Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information with respect to Russia's domestic debt as of the indicated dates:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                   Domestic Debt of the Federal Government(1)                   \par \par                                                     As of 31 December           \par