                                                                       \par administration,                                                                \par defence and                                                                    \par mandatory social                                                               \par security             5.2    2.5  5.1    3.9  5.4    3.0  6.5    1.1  6.2    3.9\par                                                                                \par Education            2.7    0.5  2.7    1.1  2.8  (0.1)  3.4  (1.4)  3.1  (1.9)\par                                                                                \par Public health and                                                              \par social services      3.3    1.4  3.3    1.1  3.4    0.9  4.0  (0.2)  3.7    1.3\par                                                                                \par Communal and other                                                             \par services             1.8    7.5  1.8    8.6  1.8    1.4  1.7 (19.5)  1.6  (5.5)\par                                                                                \par                               -------------------                              \par \par Notes:\par \par (1)           Certain data presented in this table differ from previously\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 published data due to regular revisions by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Figures in this table are current as of 1 April 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (2)           Sectoral figures in this table are calculated pursuant to gross\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 added value, which does not take into account taxes on products, but does take into account subsidies on products. For this reason, sectoral contributions for each period total 100% of gross added value, but do not total 100% of GDP for that period. For the same reason, GDP growth rates and gross added value growth rates over the same period may also differ.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (3)           In constant prices. Percent changes reflect period-on-period\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 changes, based on figures for gross added value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4)           Includes hunting and forestry.\par \par (5)           Includes extraction of oil, gas, coal, metal ores and other\par minerals.\par \par (6)           Includes wholesale and retail trade and repairs of motor vehicles\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and personal and household goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From 2006 through 2010, the major trend in the composition of gross added value by sector was an increase in the contribution of real estate and a decrease in the share of the manufacturing and trade sectors. As a share of gross added value, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with real estate rose from 10% in 2006 to 11.4% in 2010. Manufacturing decreased from 17.9% of gross added value in 2006 to 16.4% of gross added value in 2010, and trade declined from 20.2% of gross added value in 2006 to 18.2% of gross added value in 2010. Electricity, water and gas production and distribution, construction, hotels and restaurants, financial services, public administration, education and public health increased their respective contributions to overall gross added value during this period whereas the share of overall gross added value attributable to agriculture, fishery, mining, transportation and communications and communal services declined.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These shifts in the composition of Russia's gross added value resulted from changes in performance in real terms and from changes in relative prices. Between 2006 and 2010, the fastest rate of growth among Russia's large sectors was recorded in real estate (35.1%), financial services (83.3%), trade (38%), construction (20.1%) and transportation and communications (19.2%), all of which exceeded the GDP growth rate during this period of 18.4%. Buoyant economic growth and the real appreciation of the rouble between 2006 and 2008 were the principal reasons for the improvement in these sectors. From 2006 through 2010, growth was recorded in all sectors except for the fishery sector, which contracted by 1.6%, education, which declined by 1.8%, and communal and other services, which declined by 9.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the country's largest sectors, manufacturing, construction, trade, real estate and transportation and communications were the worst affected by the global financial crisis in 2009. Compared to 2008, manufacturing declined by 14.9%, construction by 14.6%, trade by 6.2%, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with real estate by 7.2% and transportation and communications by 8.5%. Every industry with the exception of agriculture, fishery, financial services and public administration experienced a decline in 2009 compared to 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Due to improved economic conditions following the global financial crisis, key sectors of the Russian economy expanded in 2010 compared to 2009. For example, manufacturing grew by 12.3%, mining by 4.7%, transportation and communications by 7.7%, trade by 5% and public administration by 3.9%. In contrast, agriculture, construction, financial services and real estate experienced year\u8211\'20\'13 on\u8211\'20\'13year declines of 10.7%, 0.7%, 2.4% and 1.2%, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GDP by Use\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table illustrates Russia's GDP by use for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                        Gross Domestic Product by Use(1)                        \par \par                                  For the year ended 31 December                  \par                                                                                  \par                     2006         2007         2008         2009          2010    \par                                                                                  \par                         %            %            %             %            %   \par                      change       change       change        change       change \par                  %     (2)    %     (2)    %     (2)     %     (2)    %     (2)  \par                                                                                  \par GDP              100     8.2  100     8.5  100     5.2   100   (7.8)  100     4.0\par                                                                                  \par Consumption(3)  66.1     9.5 66.8    11.2 65.9     8.6  74.0   (3.5) 69.6     2.5\par                                                                                  \par Private         48.2    12.2 48.8    14.3 47.8    10.6  52.8   (4.8) 50.0     3.0\par                                                                                  \par Public          17.4     2.3 17.5     2.7 17.6     3.4  20.6     0.2 19.0     1.4\par                                                                                  \par Non-profit                                                                       \par institutions     0.5   (1.7)  0.5     2.7  0.5   (1.4)   0.6   (8.2)  0.6   (1.5)\par                                                                                  \par Gross                                                                            \par investment      21.2    17.7 24.4    22.0 25.1    10.5  18.6  (41.0) 22.3    28.4\par                                                                                  \par Fixed assets                                                                     \par accumulation    18.5    18.0 21.2    21.0 22.0    10.6  21.6  (14.4) 21.4     6.1\par                                                                                  \par Inventory                                                                        \par accumulation     2.7    15.5  3.2    29.3  3.1     9.5 (3.0)     n/a  0.9     n/a\par                                                                                  \par Net exports     12.7  (14.6)  8.8  (26.6)  9.0  (34.8)   7.4    56.6  8.1    42.8\par                                                                                  \par Exports         33.7     7.3 30.6     6.3 30.8     0.6  27.5   (4.7) 29.3     7.1\par                                                                                  \par Imports         21.0    21.3 21.8    26.2 21.8    14.8  20.1  (30.4) 21.2    25.6\par                                                                                  \par                               -------------------                              \par \par Notes:\par \par n/a = not available\par \par (1)           Certain data presented in this table differ from previously\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 published data due to regular revisions by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Figures in this table are current as of 1 April 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2)           In constant prices. Percent changes reflect period-on-period\par changes.\par \par (3)           Represents expenses on final consumption.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rosstat\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The composition of GDP by use was affected by the overall condition of the Russian economy-from its growth between 2006 and 2008, on the back of high commodity prices, to its contraction, particularly in 2009, as a result of the global financial crisis, and to its subsequent recovery in 2010. As a percentage of GDP, domestic demand, i.e., consumption and gross investment, increased from 87.3% in 2006 to 91.9% in 2010, and net exports decreased from 12.7% in 2006 to 8.1% in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Domestic consumption's contribution to GDP increased slightly between 2006 and 2007, before decreasing slightly in 2008 and then expanding to 74% of GDP in 2009. In 2010, domestic consumption grew by 2.5% relative to 2009, accounting for 69.6% of GDP. Between 2006 and 2010, domestic consumption grew by approximately 30.8%, despite a 3.5% decline in 2009 compared to 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 2006 and 2010, overall private consumption rose in real terms by 39.1%, outpacing the overall growth rate of GDP during the period, whereas public consumption increased in real terms by only 10.4%, which reflected the country's relatively conservative fiscal policy during the period. Between 2006 and 2008, private consumption increased in real terms by 41.8% due to the overall strength of the Russian economy during that period, including high oil prices and a rapid expansion of available credit. However, as a result of the global financial crisis and its negative effects on Russian consumers, private consumption contracted by 4.8% in 2009 compared to 2008. In 2010, private consumption grew by 3% relative to 2009, as the economy began to recover from the global financial crisis. The contribution to GDP made by private consumption fluctuated slightly between 2006 and 2008, averaging 48.3% per year, before increasing to 52.8% of GDP in 2009. Private consumption as a share of GDP equalled 50% in 2010. The contribution to GDP made by public consumption increased slightly between 2006 and 2008 (17.4% of GDP in 2006 compared to 17.6% of GDP in 2008), before rising to 20.6% in 2009 and 19% in 2010. The rise in the share of public consumption as a percentage of GDP in 2009 and 2010 was mainly a function of the Government's fiscal stimulus package.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 2006 and 2010, gross investment grew by 20.2% in real terms. This growth represents a significant slowdown from the 58.7% increase in gross investment that occurred between 2006 and 2008. This deterioration in growth was primarily the result of the 41% contraction in gross investment in 2009 compared to 2008, which was caused by the adverse effects of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy. Gross investments increased by 28.4% in 2010 compared to 2009 due, in part, to an increase in fixed asset investments. Between 2006 and 2010, investments in fixed assets grew by 43.4% in real terms, which included a 14.4% contraction in 2009 compared to 2008. The contribution of fixed asset investments to GDP increased from 18.5% in 2006 to 22.3% in 2008 before declining to 22% in 2009 and 21.9 in 2010. Inventory accumulation increased by 63.5% in real terms between 2006 and 2008, only to experience a depletion of inventories in 2009 compared to 2008. In 2010, inventory accumulation represented 0.9% of GDP in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 2006 and 2010, real growth of exports grew at a slower pace than overall GDP, while imports accelerated at a faster rate than GDP. During this period, the real value of exports grew by 17.1%, and the real value of imports grew by 53.6%. Exports as a share of GDP, however, contracted between 2006 and 2010. From 2006 through 2008, this contraction (from 33.7% of GDP in 2006 to 30.8% of GDP in 2008) was due to the rapid expansion of domestic demand, partially reflected in the rise of gross investment, and was supported by the real appreciation of the rouble. Exports declined in 2009 by 4.7% compared to 2008 and, as a share of GDP, from 30.8% in 2008 to 27.5% in 2009, due mainly to lower commodity prices and reduced global demand for Russian exports. Exports increased in 2010 by 7.1% compared to 2009, and, as a share of GDP, from 27.5% in 2009 to 29.3% in 2010, due mainly to an increase in the price and volume of Russia's principal exports. The high growth in imports between 2006 and 2008 was a result, generally, of high rates of economic growth in Russia during this period, and, in particular, of rising consumer incomes. Imports as a share of GDP increased slightly between 2006 and 2008, from 21% in 2006 to 21.8% in each of 2007 and 2008. The contraction in imports in 2009 compared to 2008 (30.4%) as well as the decline in imports as a share of GDP (from 21.8% in 2008 to 20.1% in 2009) was mainly due to the negative effects of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy. Imports grew by 25.6% in 2010 compared to 2009, and, as a share of GDP, from 20.1% in 2009 to 21.2% in 2010, due mainly to an expansion in import volumes, which, in turn, was the result of an improvement in domestic demand in 2010, as well as to a moderate rise in the price of Russia's principal imports. See "Balance of Payments and Foreign Trade-Balance of Payments-Current Account." Net exports increased in real terms by 91.4% between 2006 and 2010 and decreased as a share of GDP from 12.7% to 8.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Principal Sectors of the Economy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industry\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia is highly industrialised, with a large share of its industrial activity concentrated in manufacturing. Russia is the world leader in oil extraction, ranks second in the world in natural gas extraction and produces significant amounts of electricity, iron, steel, rolled products, mineral fertilisers and coal. Manufacturing activity is heavily concentrated in metallurgy, coke and petrochemicals, food processing, machine and equipment building, the production of electrical equipment and the production of transport vehicles and equipment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The following table illustrates the structure of industrial output and\par period-on-period changes in real industrial output by sector for the periods\par indicated:\par \par                         Industrial Output by Sector(1)                         \par \par                                 For the year ended 31 December                 \par                                                                                \par                     2006         2007         2008         2009        2010    \par                                                                                \par                         %            %          