rts to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries remained stable, accounting for 64-67% of Russia's total exports (as measured by value) in each year from 2006 through 2010. In 2009, Russian exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries declined by 38% compared to 2008 as a result of the contraction in worldwide demand resulting from the global financial crisis. Overall total exports in 2009 fell by 35.5%. Due to the economic recovery following the global financial crisis, in 2010 compared to 2009, Russia's exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries grew by 33.2%, while overall growth in total Russian exports equalled 31.5%. Exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries accounted for 65% of total exports in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2006 and 2007, Russian exports to CIS countries grew at a faster rate than exports to non-CIS countries (an increase of 29.7% and 24.5%, respectively, to CIS countries compared to an increase of 24.0% and 15.6%, respectively, to non-CIS countries.) This rise in exports was primarily due to growth of overall economic activity in the CIS countries. At a growth rate of 32.3%, exports to CIS countries grew in 2008 in parallel with exports to non-CIS countries. In 2009, Russia's exports to CIS countries fell by 32.8%, which was slightly less than the rate at which total Russian exports declined (35.5%). In 2010, Russia's exports to CIS countries increased by 27.5% relative to 2009, which was primarily due to an improvement in the economy following the global financial crisis. From 2006 through 2010, Russian exports to CIS countries recorded an overall growth rate of 83%. Exports to CIS countries accounted for 15.5% and 15% of Russia's total exports in 2009 and 2010, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in exports to transition economies, i.e., Bulgaria, Romania, Vietnam, China, North Korea, Mongolia and Cuba, generally followed trends in Russia's total exports, accounting for approximately 7-8% of total exports between 2006 and 2010. Exports to transition economies grew by approximately 18.6% in 2006, 5.5% in 2007 and 31.5% in 2008, before falling by 30.7% in 2009 due to the global financial crisis. Due to the subsequent economic recovery, exports to transition economies grew by 27.2% in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supported by a high rate of GDP growth, Russia's imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries grew by over 170% between 2006 and 2008, in line with overall import growth during the same period. Russian imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries equalled 62.6%, 61.9% and 62.7% of total Russian imports (as measured by value) in 2006, 2007 and 2008, respectively. In 2009, because of the contraction in domestic demand and consumer income resulting from the global financial crisis, Russian imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries dropped by 39.6%, while overall imports declined by 37.3% compared to 2008. Due to the economic recovery following the global financial crisis, Russia's imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries grew by 29% in 2010 compared to 2009, while overall growth in total Russian imports equalled 36.9%. Imports from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries accounted for 60.4% and 56.9% of total imports in 2009 and 2010, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 2006 and 2010, the share of total imports from transition economies followed an upward trend, primarily due to a rise in imports from China. In 2006, imports from transition economies accounted for 10.3% of total imports, compared with 13.1%, 14%, 14.9% and 18.4% in 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively. In 2009, imports from transition economies declined by 33.3% compared to 2008 due to weaker domestic demand, resulting from the global financial crisis. Due to the subsequent economic recovery-and significantly driven by a rise in imports from China-imports from transition economies grew by 68.7% in 2010 compared to 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's overall economic growth exhibited over the past decade led to a decline in the share of imports from CIS countries, as imports from other countries became more attractive to Russian consumers. In 2006, Russian imports from CIS countries accounted for 16.2% of total Russian imports, compared with 15%, 13.7%, 13% and 13.8% in 2007, 2008, 2009 and 2010, respectively. Imports from CIS countries declined by 40.4% in 2009 compared to 2008 due to the effects of the global financial crisis. As a result of the subsequent economic recovery, imports from CIS countries increased by 44.9% in 2010 compared to 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the non-CIS countries, Germany, the Netherlands, China, Italy, Turkey, United States, Poland and Japan are Russia's leading trading partners, as measured by value. Belarus, Ukraine and increasingly Kazakhstan are Russia's leading trading partners as measured by value within the CIS.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's largest export markets in 2008, 2009 and 2010 were the Netherlands (12.2%, 12.1% and 13.6%, respectively, of Russian exports), Italy (9%, 8.3% and 6.9%, respectively), Germany (7.1%, 6.2% and 6.3%, respectively), Turkey (5.9%, 5.4% and 5.1%, respectively), Belarus (5%, 5.4% and 4.5%, respectively), China (4.5%, 5.5% and 5.1%, respectively) and Ukraine (5%, 4.6% and 5.8%, respectively). In 2008, 2009 and 2010, the most important exporters to Russia were China (13%, 13.7% and 17%, respectively, of Russian imports), Germany (12.8%, 12.7% and 11.7%, respectively), Japan (7%, 4.3% and 4.5%, respectively), Ukraine (6.1%, 5.5% and 6.1%, respectively), the United States (5.2%, 5.5% and 4.9%, respectively), Italy (4.1%, 4.7% and 4.4%, respectively), Belarus (4% in both 2008 and 2009 and 4.3% in 2010), France (3.7%, 5% and 4.4%, respectively) and the Republic of Korea (4%, 2.9% and 3.2%, respectively). In 2008, 2009 and 2010, Ukraine and Belarus together accounted for 69.5%, 67.6% and 71.2%, respectively, of Russia's foreign trade with CIS countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Official Reserves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information with respect to the official reserves of the Russian Federation as of the indicated dates:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                              Official Reserves(1)                              \par \par                                                                         As at  \par                                           As at 31 December            30 April\par                                                                                \par                                 2006    2007    2008    2009    2010     2011  \par                                                                                \par                                   (millions of dollars, except as indicated)   \par                                                                                \par Official reserves (excluding                                                   \par gold)                          295,567 466,750 411,748 416,653 443,591  483,229\par                                                                                \par Special Drawing Rights               7       1       1   8,901   8,749    9,217\par                                                                                \par Reserve position in IMF            283     374   1,051   1,927   1,893    2,287\par                                                                                \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                295,277 466,376 410,695 405,825 432,948  471,725\par                                                                                \par Official gold reserves (fine                                                   \par troy ounces, million)             12.9    14.5    16.7    20.9    25.4  26.1(3)\par                                                                                \par U.S.$ million(2)                 8,164  12,012  14,533  22,798  35,788   40,720\par                                                                                \par Total official reserves                                                        \par (including gold)               303,732 478,762 426,281 439,450 479,379  523,950\par                                                                                \par                               -------------------                              \par \par Notes:\par \par (1)           Based on the official rouble/dollar exchange rates and reference\par price for gold, as established by the Bank of Russia on the relevant reporting\par date.\par \par (2)           At official Bank of Russia reference prices.\par \par (3)           As at 1 April 2011.\par \par Source: Bank of Russia.           \par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at 31 December 2006, total official reserves (including gold) stood at approximately U.S. $303.7 billion, which represents 17.4 months of import coverage and an increase of approximately U.S. $121.5 billion compared to year-end 2005. This improvement was due primarily to an increase in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves (backed by high commodity prices and the country's large trade surplus). As at 31 December 2006, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amounted to approximately U.S. $295.3 billion, and gold reserves stood at approximately U.S. $8.2 billion. The growth in reserves was also supported by net private sector foreign capital inflows of U.S. $41.8 billion, a significant expansion over net inflows in 2005.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The growth of official reserves continued in 2007 due to favourable world commodity prices and high inflows of foreign capital, including net private capital inflows of U.S. $83.1 billion. At 31 December 2007, total official reserves (including gold) stood at approximately U.S. $478.8 billion, representing approximately 20 months of import coverage. As of the same date, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves amounted to approximately U.S. $466.4 billion, and gold reserves stood at approximately U.S. $12 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 31 December 2008, total official reserves (including gold) were U.S. $426.3 billion, a decrease of approximately U.S. $52.5 billion compared with year-end 2007. These reserves represented approximately 13.9 months of import coverage. As of the same date, gold reserves equalled approximately U.S. $14.5 billion. The decrease in total reserves was due to an 11.9% drop in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to U.S. $410.7 billion. The decline in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, in turn, was primarily attributable to the onset of the global financial crisis in the second half of 2008 and its negative impact on world commodity prices and aggregate demand. As a result of the financial crisis, Russia experienced a significant outflow of private capital, amounting to U.S. $132.8 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 31 December 2009, total official reserves (including gold) stood at approximately U.S. $439.5 billion, an increase of approximately U.S. $13.2 billion compared to year-end 2008. These reserves represented nearly 21 months of import coverage. The increase in reserves was primarily attributable to a significant rise in Russia's balance of special drawing rights, i.e., international reserve assets created by the IMF, from U.S. $1 million in 2008 to U.S. $8.9 billion in 2009 and in the country's gold reserves from approximately U.S. $14.5 billion in 2008 to U.S. $22.8 billion in 2009. The increase in special drawing rights was due to an improvement in Russia's reserve position with the IMF. The increase in gold reserves was the result of Russian purchases of gold as a means to diversify and protect its reserves during the crisis. The overall rise in total reserves was partially offset by a reduction in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from U.S. $410.7 billion in 2008 to U.S. $405.8 billion in 2009. This decrease was primarily due to the continued impact of the global financial crisis on the Russian economy, particularly in the first quarter of 2009 when foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves dropped by 7% and net private capital outflows amounted to U.S. $35.1 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 31 December 2010, total official reserves (including gold) equalled U.S. $479.4 billion, an increase of approximately U.S. $13.9 billion compared to year-end 2009. Reserves as of 31 December 2010 represented approximately 24 months of import coverage. The increase in reserves was primarily attributable to a 6.7% rise in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from U.S. $405.8 billion at year-end 2009 to U.S. $432.9 billion at 31 December 2010 and a 21.5% rise in official gold reserves from U.S. $20.9 billion at year-end 2009 to U.S. $25.4 billion at 31 December 2010. The increase in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves was, in part, due to higher oil prices, and the increase in gold reserves reflected the Government's continuing policy of diversifying its reserves portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at 30 April 2011, total official reserves (including gold) equalled U.S. $524 billion, an increase of approximately U.S. $9.3 billion compared to year\u8211\'20\'13end 2010. The increase in reserves was primarily attributable to a 9% rise in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from U.S. $432.9 billion at 31 December 2010 to U.S. $471.7 billion at 30 April 2011. The increase in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves was largely due to an increase in oil prices. Reserves as of 31 March 2011 provided approximately 23.4 months of import coverage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 1 January 1999 and 31 December 2010, Russia's official reserves grew approximately 39-fold and have covered an increasing portion of the country's external debt. As of 31 December 2006, 2007, 2008 and 2009, Russia's total external debt (public and private) equalled U.S. $313.2 billion, U.S. $463.9 billion, U.S. $480.5 billion and U.S. $467.2 billion, respectively. As of 31 December 2010, Russia's total external debt equalled U.S. $488.7 billion, or 33.1% of GDP, with Russia's official reserves providing approximately 98.1% coverage. As of 31 December 2010, Russia's reserves were the second highest among the G-8 countries and the third highest in the world.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Government strictly regulates Russia's output, sale and export of precious stones and metals, including gold, platinum and diamonds. As part of this regulatory regime, the Government licences and has a right of first refusal to purchase Russia's precious metals output. The Government may add to its reserves any purchased output or any proceeds from the sale and export of precious metals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both the Bank of Russia and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  currently have official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and gold reserves. Reserves of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are allocated to either the National Wealth Fund or Reserve Fund. See "Public Finance-Deficit Financing" for more information on the National Wealth Fund and Reserve Fund. The percentage of Russia's total official reserves that are controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  varies year to year and depends on the reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  undertaken by the Bank of Russia and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the year. In 2007, for example, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held approximately one-third of the country's total official reserves, whereas, in 2008, it held nearly one-half. This change was generally due to the Bank of Russia's decision to defend the rouble at the end of 2008 and to the decision to accumulate reserves in the Funds controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At year-end 2009, the share of foreign reserves held by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declined to approximately one-third due to the depletion of the Reserve Fund, the proceeds of which were used to cover the budget deficit, and to the accumulation of reserves at the Bank of Russia. The Bank of Russia was able to increase its reserves because there was less need to support the rouble in 2009 than in 2008. As of 31 December 2010, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held approximately 24% of the country's total official reserves, with the remainder held by the Bank of Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par                                 PUBLIC FINANCE                                 \par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The information presented herein with respect to the federal budget has been prepared substantially in accordance with the guidelines and definitions set forth in the IMF's publication "Government Finance Statistics" (GFS-1986).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia maintained a strong fiscal position through 2008, running surpluses of 8.4%, 6% and 4.9% of GDP in 2006, 2007 and 2008, respectively. In 2009, Russia recorded a budget deficit of 6.3% of GDP. The federal budget has followed a similar trend, producing primary surpluses of 8.1%, 5.8% and 4.5% of GDP in 2006, 2007 and 2008, respectively, and a primary deficit of 5.5% in 2009. Russia's positive fiscal position through 2008 was attributable largely to favourable conditions in world energy markets, resulting in higher revenues from related production, export duties, excise and other taxes as well as higher profit tax revenues, combined with improvements in the federal budgetary process, allowing for greater control over expenditure, and more efficient tax policy. The reduction in the budget surpluses in 2006 and 2007 was caused primarily by increases in spending on the national economy and state administration as well as growth in the volumes of federal transfers to sub-federal budgets. The surplus continued to decrease in 2008 primarily because of a contraction in tax revenues (as a share of GDP), particularly domestic VAT proceeds, and a slight rise in expenditures mainly in the form of transfers to state extra\u8211\'20\'13budgetary funds. The federal budget deficit in 2009 was mainly due to the effects of the global financial crisis, which reduced revenues (particularly as a result of the decline in worldwide demand for Russia's commodity exports) and caused expenditures to rise as a consequence of the Government's fiscal stimulus package. In 2010, Russia ran an enlarged budget deficit of 3.5% of GDP and a federal budget primary deficit of 3.6% of GDP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth certain summary information regarding Russia's public finances for the periods indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                               Enlarged Budget(1)                               \par \par                                                                                \par \par                                              For the year                      \par                                           ended 31 December                    \par      